# The steps `pre-gate` runs at once, in front of a lane's gate: the kind that
# failed lane gates whose tests all passed (§380.1). In the justfile rather
# than in `.claude/agents/lane.md`, so that moving one reaches the next lane
# without a new `claude` process (§381). Every one is also a step of
# `gate_steps`; `check-pre-gate-selftest` holds that.
pre_gate_steps := "check-fmt lint check-wildcards"

# The steps that cost a runner money, and that a change touching only `docs/`
# and `*.md` cannot move either way. `gate-docs` runs `gate_steps` **minus**
# this list, so a new `check-*` recipe joins the docs arm by default and only an
# explicitly named step is left out — the same direction
# `check-gate-runs-every-check` guards, for the same reason. A list of what to
# keep would have quietly dropped each new check from the docs arm instead.
#
# Two reasons land a step here, and "metered" rather than "compiling" is the
# name because of the second:
#
#   It needs a build. `check`, `lint`, `test`, `test-slow`, `check-release-build`,
#   `check-fuzz-builds` and `check-systemd-units` obviously; `check-wildcards`
#   because it reads clippy's output; `deny` because it resolves the dependency
#   graph, and a prose commit cannot change `Cargo.lock`. `check-fmt` builds
#   nothing, but `cargo fmt` needs the pinned toolchain, which the docs arm
#   does not install, and a prose commit cannot change a `.rs` file.
#
#   It needs the STEP corpus. `check-corpus` is 0.16 s and compiles nothing, and
#   it is here anyway: it **fails** when `RCAD_STEP_CORPUS` is not a directory,
#   by design (§44.10), and putting a corpus there is a 119 MiB fetch. A prose
#   commit cannot change the corpus or `corpus/manifest.sha256`, so the arm that
#   pays for one is the arm that might have broken it.
#   `check-corpus-selftest` stays in the docs arm, because it builds its own
#   fixture and never reads the real corpus.
#
# On a metered runner this is 1m07s against 9m46s. `docs/decisions.md` §172.
gate_metered_steps := "check check-fuzz-builds check-wildcards check-fmt lint test test-slow check-release-build check-systemd-units deny check-corpus"

# The steps `gate` may serve from an earlier green of the same code instead of
# running (`docs/decisions.md` §337). The first nine were the slow ones: together
# about 215 of a 257-second gate, and nearly half of all green gates re-read code
# that had already gated green, because a `docs/queue.md` push had moved `main`.
# The tooling selftests after them were another 16 s of every served gate (§369).
#
# **A step is here only if nothing it reads is prose, or outside the tree.** An
# strace of every step settled that (§337.2, §369). `check-hooks` and
# `check-questions-selftest` are left out because each runs a plugin's own cases
# from its checkout. `deny` is left out because it fetches the advisory database,
# `check-corpus` because the corpus is outside the tree, `check-systemd-units`
# because it reads the installed systemd units, and `check-queue-merge-driver`
# because it reads git's config. Every step that reads `docs/` is left out as
# well. A step missing from this list runs every time, so a new step is never
# served by default.
gate_reusable_steps := "check \
    check-fuzz-builds \
    check-enums \
    check-wildcards-selftest \
    check-wildcards \
    check-ignored \
    check-skills-selftest \
    check-kache-off-on-ci \
    check-gate-runs-every-check \
    check-gate-summary-selftest \
    check-pre-gate-selftest \
    check-gate-docs-selftest \
    check-gate-verdicts-selftest \
    check-lanes-selftest \
    check-release-lanes-selftest \
    check-ff-main-selftest \
    check-land-selftest \
    check-verify-lane-selftest \
    check-status-selftest \
    check-plan-selftest \
    check-queue-selftest \
    check-queue-api-selftest \
    check-queue-page-selftest \
    check-hub-selftest \
    check-hub-align-selftest \
    check-clean-targets-selftest \
    check-prune-worktrees-selftest \
    check-decisions-selftest \
    check-claim-section-selftest \
    check-src-selftest \
    check-recipe-selftest \
    check-corpus-selftest \
    check-coverage-selftest \
    check-fuzz-overnight-selftest \
    check-timing-hooked-calls-selftest \
    check-fuzz-watermark-selftest \
    check-gate-fresh-selftest \
    check-gate-fresh-cleanup-selftest \
    check-fuzz-triage-selftest \
    check-systemd-units-selftest \
    check-fuzz-yield-selftest \
    check-spend-selftest \
    check-row-time-selftest \
    check-lineage-selftest \
    check-fmt \
    lint \
    test \
    test-slow \
    check-release-build \
    check-t-selftest"

# Where the whole run is kept, so the block at the end can be read back in full
# without running it again. A variable so `check-gate-summary-selftest` can
# point a nested run somewhere else: the selftest runs inside the gate, and a
# fixed path would have it truncate the log its own parent was still writing.
gate_log := "target/last-gate.log"

# The gate a prose commit needs: `gate_steps` minus `gate_metered_steps`,
# driven through `gate` itself so the summary block and `quiet` come for free.
#
# **This exists because the runner is metered and half of `main` is prose.** Of
# `main`'s last 150 commits, 43 touched only `docs/` and `*.md` — 42 of them
# `docs/decisions.md` — and each one bought a 13-minute compile of a tree whose
# source had not moved. Every step it keeps finishes in under a second:
# `check-decisions` 0.08 s, `check-plan` 0.14 s, `check-where-the-work-is`
# 0.21 s, `check-enums` 0.08 s, `check-ignored` 0.54 s, `check-corpus` 0.16 s.
#
# It is the arm `ci.yml` picks by path, and the choice is made there rather than
# here: a recipe cannot see what a push touched, and one that guessed from
# `git diff HEAD~1` would be wrong on the first merge commit it met.
#
# Running the docs arm by hand on a change that touches `.rs` is not guarded
# against, and deliberately: the steps it drops are the ones that cost money, so
# the way to be sure is `just gate`, and this recipe says which steps it dropped
# so that a reader of the output can see what was not asked.
[doc("The gate a docs-only change needs: everything that does not compile. `quiet` works here too.")]
gate-docs mode="loud":
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    steps=""
    dropped=""
    for step in {{ gate_steps }}; do
        skip=""
        for heavy in {{ gate_metered_steps }}; do
            if [ "$step" = "$heavy" ]; then skip=yes; break; fi
        done
        if [ -n "$skip" ]; then dropped="$dropped $step"; else steps="$steps $step"; fi
    done
    # An empty list would run nothing and exit 0, which is the one answer this
    # must never give: a gate that checked nothing reads exactly like a pass.
    if [ -z "$steps" ]; then
        echo "gate-docs would run no steps at all, which would pass by checking nothing" >&2
        exit 2
    fi
    echo "gate-docs: dropped${dropped:- nothing}"
    exec {{ just_executable() }} --set gate_steps "${steps# }" --set gate_log '{{ gate_log }}' gate '{{ mode }}'

# The gate a lane and its verifier run: `gate_steps` without `test-slow`, the
# tier `slow_tests` names, which the land gate runs (§397.4). A lane's test step
# then runs 383 test-seconds instead of 886, and its longest test takes 20 s
# instead of 67.
#
# **Only `lane.md`, `verifier.md` and `verify_lane.py` call it**, and
# `check-gate-lane-callers` holds that. Everything else runs the whole gate:
# `land.py`, the merger, CI, and a session landing its own work. A caller that
# forgets it pays time; a caller that took it up wrongly would put code on
# `main` that the slow tier never read.
#
# A recipe rather than a word to `gate`, as `gate-docs` is: it gives the check
# one name to look for, and `just pre-gate gate-lane` is two recipes in one
# call, so `pre-gate` has nothing to pass on. Always `quiet`, since only a tool
# call runs it.
[doc("The gate a lane and its verifier run: every step but `test-slow`, the slow tier, which the land gate runs. Always quiet; `just gate-lane fresh` serves nothing.")]
gate-lane verdicts="reuse":
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    steps=""
    left_out=""
    for step in {{ gate_steps }}; do
        if [ "$step" = test-slow ]; then left_out=yes; else steps="$steps $step"; fi
    done
    if [ -n "$left_out" ]; then
        echo "gate-lane: leaves out test-slow, which the land gate runs"
    else
        echo "gate-lane: gate_steps has no test-slow to leave out, so this is the whole gate"
    fi
    exec {{ just_executable() }} --set gate_steps "${steps# }" --set gate_log '{{ gate_log }}' gate quiet '{{ verdicts }}'

# The gate with what this machine supplies taken away, which is the one thing a
# local run cannot otherwise be: a fresh clone, in a container, with no `HOME`
# from here, no `CLAUDE_PLUGINS_ROOT`, no sibling hub and no warm `target/`.
#
# **This is `docs/decisions.md` §77.6's manoeuvre as a recipe.** Three red runs
# in 2026-09 were checks passing for a property of the environment they ran in
# rather than of the tree, and the way all three were found was to take away what
# the environment supplied and run it again. That was done by hand each time.
#
# It is not a replacement for the runner and does not pretend to be: it runs on
# the same kernel, the same docker daemon and the same disk, so it cannot catch
# what only a different machine would. What it does catch is the whole class the
# runner was bought for, and it costs no minutes — which is why it is the arm
# that survives a spent Actions allowance. §172.
#
# `check-plugins`, the plugin arm of `check-hooks` and `check-questions-selftest`
# all skip in here, because there is no Claude Code config directory. That is
# them working, not them broken: the predicate is that directory and never `CI`,
# `docs/decisions.md` §77.
#
# **The tools live in a named docker volume, the tree does not.** `mise install`
# builds eight `cargo:` tools from source, which is two minutes for
# `cargo-nextest` alone and dwarfs the gate it is setting up for. The volume
# keeps them across runs; the clone is thrown away every time. Taking `HOME`
# away is about this machine's `~/.claude` and its build cache, not about
# rebuilding `cargo-deny` on every run, and nothing of this repo's own
# compilation is in there. `docker volume rm rcad-gate-fresh-tools` resets it.
#
# Cold by construction, so expect the nine minutes the `gate` doc names rather
# than the median 98 seconds, and more than that on the first run of all. Pass
# `run_in_background` and be woken — and read the output file rather than piping
# it, because a pipe hands back the pipe's exit code and a failed run then reads
# as a pass. That happened on this recipe's own first run.
#
# **A pass here is written to the green record**, the same one a plain `just
# gate` writes and the one `just status` reads to say how far the tip is past
# the last green (§173.9). This is the strongest green this machine can produce —
# the whole gate, on a tree nothing of this environment has touched — so it is
# the last one that should have gone unrecorded, and it did.
[doc("The gate in a container on a fresh clone, with this machine's environment taken away.")]
gate-fresh image="rust:1-bookworm":
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    command -v docker >/dev/null || {
        echo "gate-fresh needs docker, which is not on PATH" >&2; exit 2; }
    corpus="$RCAD_STEP_CORPUS"
    [ -d "$corpus" ] || {
        echo "gate-fresh needs the STEP corpus at $corpus; just fetch-corpus builds one" >&2
        exit 2; }
    # **A clone carries commits, so uncommitted work is invisible to it.** Left
    # unguarded this is the worst answer a gate can give: a green for a tree
    # nobody asked about, indistinguishable from a green for the tree in front of
    # you. Refusing is the only honest option, because the alternative — copying
    # the dirty tree in — would stop this being a *fresh* clone, which is the one
    # thing the recipe is for.
    if [ -n "$(git status --porcelain --untracked-files=no)" ]; then
        echo "gate-fresh clones, so it would gate HEAD and not your changes:" >&2
        git status --short --untracked-files=no >&2
        echo "commit first — a WIP commit is enough, and the branch is still on disk" >&2
        exit 2
    fi
    # An untracked file is invisible to a clone for the same reason, but it is
    # also not part of the commit anyone is about to hand over, so gating without
    # it is right rather than wrong. What it must not do is pass *silently*: a
    # new `.rs` file nobody has `git add`ed is exactly the work a reader of this
    # green would assume was covered. So it is named, and the run carries on.
    untracked=$(git ls-files --others --exclude-standard)
    if [ -n "$untracked" ]; then
        echo "gate-fresh: not in the clone, because they are not committed —" >&2
        printf '  %s\n' $untracked >&2
        echo "  this gate is of HEAD; git add them if they were meant to be in it" >&2
    fi
    # The clone is made here rather than in the container so that the container
    # needs no credentials and no network for it. `--no-local` forces a real
    # object transfer: a local clone hardlinks into the hub's object store, and
    # a container writing through a hardlink would be writing into it.
    work=$(mktemp -d)
    # **A clean-up that cannot finish must not fail the gate.** The container is
    # root and writes `target/` as root, so the host cannot delete the tree it
    # has just gated — which turned a green run into an exit 1 the moment the
    # trap started firing at all. The `chown` at the end of the container script
    # is the fix; this is what is left when the container dies before reaching
    # it — killed, OOM, a docker error — and it still owns the same docker
    # daemon this recipe already required, so it reaches for a second, throwaway
    # container to do as root what the host cannot, before it names the tree and
    # gives up.
    cleanup() {
        rc=$?
        chmod -R u+w "$work" 2>/dev/null || true
        if ! rm -rf "$work" 2>/dev/null; then
            docker run --rm -v "$work:/w" '{{ image }}' rm -rf /w/rcad 2>/dev/null || true
            rm -rf "$work" 2>/dev/null || {
                echo "gate-fresh: $work is left behind, owned by the container's root" >&2
                echo "  docker run --rm -v $work:/w {{ image }} rm -rf /w/rcad  removes it" >&2
            }
        fi
        exit "$rc"
    }
    trap cleanup EXIT
    git clone --no-local --quiet --single-branch "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)" "$work/rcad"
    echo "gate-fresh: cloned $(git -C "$work/rcad" rev-parse --short HEAD) into a single-worktree tree"
    # `HOME` inside is a scratch directory, so nothing of this machine's
    # `~/.claude`, `~/.cargo` or `~/.rustup` is visible. `CI=true` is what
    # `mise.toml` reads to empty `RUSTC_WRAPPER`, so the build cache — which
    # lives in the `HOME` we just took away — is not asked for.
    #
    # **Run, not `exec`ed, because the green is written down after it.** `exec`
    # replaces this shell, so nothing could follow the container — and the
    # `trap` above could never fire either, leaving every run's clone behind.
    status=0
    docker run --rm -t \
        -v "$work/rcad:/rcad" \
        -v "$corpus:/corpus:ro" \
        -v rcad-gate-fresh-tools:/tmp/home/.local/share/mise \
        -w /rcad \
        -e HOME=/tmp/home \
        -e CI=true \
        -e RCAD_STEP_CORPUS=/corpus \
        -e CARGO_TERM_COLOR=always \
        -e RUST_BACKTRACE=1 \
        -e HOST_UID="$(id -u)" \
        -e HOST_GID="$(id -g)" \
        '{{ image }}' \
        bash -eu -c '
            mkdir -p /tmp/home
            # `check-corpus-selftest` refuses without a locale that collates by
            # dictionary order, because the collation bug it exists to catch is
            # invisible under a codepoint collation and three of its four cases
            # stay green with the bug in place. A bare image has only `C.UTF-8`,
            # which is exactly the wrong one. `ci.yml` installs this too; §79.
            apt-get update -qq
            apt-get install -y -qq locales >/dev/null
            sed -i "s/^# en_US.UTF-8/en_US.UTF-8/" /etc/locale.gen
            locale-gen >/dev/null
            # The clone is bind-mounted from the host and owned by the host
            # user, while this container is root, so git calls it "dubious
            # ownership" and refuses. That is not cosmetic here: the `justfile`
            # derives `RCAD_STEP_CORPUS` from a backtick `git rev-parse` at parse
            # time, and just evaluates it whether or not the variable is already
            # set — so with git refusing, *every* `just` command in this
            # container fails before any recipe runs.
            git config --global --add safe.directory /rcad
            curl -fsSL https://mise.run | sh
            export PATH="/tmp/home/.local/bin:$PATH"
            eval "$(mise activate bash --shims)"
            # Materialise the pinned toolchain before mise installs anything.
            # This is fault 1 from the top of `ci.yml`, and the first run of this
            # recipe reproduced it exactly: mise installs the eight `cargo:`
            # tools in parallel, each triggers rustup to fetch the version
            # `rust-toolchain.toml` pins, they race over one `.partial`, and
            # seven of the eight died — `cargo-nextest` won. That the fresh
            # environment found a fault this repo had already documented is the
            # best evidence the recipe does what it claims.
            rustup show
            # The gate below refuses a clone that anything has written to
            # (§415). The commands above write under /etc, /usr, /tmp/home and
            # the image rustup home, and this one is the one that can write
            # into /rcad: `mise.toml` sets `lockfile = true`, so a mise newer
            # than the one that wrote `mise.lock` may write it again, and then
            # the refusal names the file rather than gating.
            mise install
            # The gate runs as root and everything it writes is root-owned, so
            # the tree goes back to the invoking user before this container
            # exits. The status is carried by hand rather than left to `set -e`
            # so that the handover happens whether the gate passed or failed: a
            # red run whose tree cannot be deleted is the same mess as a green
            # one, and the host has no way to undo it afterwards.
            gate_status=0
            mise exec -- just gate quiet || gate_status=$?
            chown -R "$HOST_UID:$HOST_GID" /rcad
            exit $gate_status
        ' || status=$?
    [ "$status" -eq 0 ] || exit "$status"

    # **The green goes in the host's record, because the container's dies with
    # it.** `gate` in there reaches `_record-green` too, and writes into
    # `/rcad/.git` — the clone the `trap` deletes. Without this line the
    # strongest gate this machine can run would be the only one leaving no
    # trace, while a plain `just gate` in a warm tree counts.
    # `just status` reads that record to say how far the tip is past the last
    # green, and since §172 spent the Actions allowance it is the only source
    # that can still answer. §173.9.
    #
    # The sha is taken here and not carried out of the clone, and it is the same
    # sha: this recipe refuses a dirty tree and the clone is of HEAD.
    # `_record-green` re-reads that for itself, which is what catches a tree
    # written to during the ten minutes the container was running.
    #
    # Both directories named rather than left to `just`'s defaults, so the tree
    # the record describes is the one `git clone` above read, whichever
    # directory of it this recipe was invoked from.
    {{ just_executable() }} --justfile "{{ justfile() }}" \
        --working-directory "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)" \
        _record-green "{{ gate_steps }}"

# The gate's cheapest steps, run at once and in front of it: a lane gates with
# `mise exec -- just pre-gate gate-lane`. `just` runs the recipes it is given
# in turn and stops at the first that fails, so a misformatted file, a clippy
# warning or a new wildcard arm comes back in seconds instead of after a whole
# gate. `docs/decisions.md` §380 and §381.
#
# **Every step runs to its end, and a failure stops nothing.** Two of the four
# lane gates §380.1 counted broke `lint` and `check-wildcards` both, and
# stopping the other step at the first failure would have hidden the second
# until the next call. That would save at most 5 to 10 s, only on a failure,
# and would kill cargo mid-write (§381.3).
#
# A step that passes prints one line. A step that fails prints its first 60
# lines, first of the list first, because those are what the lane has to fix.
# Cargo's progress lines are left out of the 60, and the rest is kept in a log
# the result names. Not the whole output: one parse error in a doc comment
# made `check-fmt` print 1 136 lines and `lint` 3 445 (§381.3).
[doc("The gate's cheapest steps at once: formatting, clippy and the wildcard count. A lane runs `just pre-gate gate-lane`, so the gate starts only once these pass.")]
pre-gate:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -uo pipefail
    j='{{ just_executable() }}'
    names=({{ pre_gate_steps }})
    if [ "${#names[@]}" -eq 0 ]; then
        echo "pre_gate_steps has no steps, so pre-gate would pass having checked nothing"
        exit 1
    fi
    # A directory of its own, so a run nested in `check-pre-gate-selftest`
    # inside a gate cannot write over a real run's logs.
    logs=$(mktemp -d)
    # Keyed by index rather than by name: a fixture list can name a step twice.
    declare -A running=()
    began=()
    ended_with=()
    for i in "${!names[@]}"; do
        began[i]=${EPOCHREALTIME//[!0-9]/}
        "$j" "${names[$i]}" >"$logs/$i-${names[$i]}.log" 2>&1 &
        running[$!]=$i
    done
    while [ "${#running[@]}" -gt 0 ]; do
        wait -n -p pid
        step_status=$?
        i=${running[$pid]}
        unset "running[$pid]"
        ended_with[i]=$step_status
        tenths=$(( (${EPOCHREALTIME//[!0-9]/} - began[i]) / 100000 ))
        line="${names[$i]} ($((tenths / 10)).$((tenths % 10)) s)"
        [ "$step_status" -eq 0 ] || line="$line FAILED, exit $step_status"
        echo "· $line"
    done
    status=0
    cut=""
    for i in "${!names[@]}"; do
        [ "${ended_with[$i]}" -ne 0 ] || continue
        [ "$status" -ne 0 ] || status=${ended_with[$i]}
        log="$logs/$i-${names[$i]}.log"
        echo
        echo "=== ${names[$i]} ==="
        said=$(grep -vE '^ *(Compiling|Checking|Fresh|Blocking|Finished) ' "$log")
        head -60 <<<"$said"
        more=$(( $(grep -c '' <<<"$said") - 60 ))
        if [ "$more" -gt 0 ]; then
            echo "... $more more lines in $log"
            cut=1
        fi
    done
    # Kept only when the result names a log in it.
    [ -n "$cut" ] || rm -rf "$logs"
    exit $status

# The whole gate, and the same command CI runs. Named `gate` rather than
# `test-all` because it is two dozen checks, a lint and a `cargo-deny` around
# exactly one `test` — and this repo already says "gated green", "a gate agent",
# "the gate worktree". `test-all` would have shipped a false sentence on day one.
#
# **It ends with a summary block, and that is what the nine minutes are for.**
# In one week 145 of 415 gate runs were followed by a second call that grepped
# the redirected log for `Summary|FAIL|tests run` — a mean of 159k tokens of
# context to read back three numbers this recipe already had in hand. So it says
# them itself, last, on stdout: pass or fail, the counts, and the name of the
# first thing that broke.
#
# The exit code is untouched — CI reads it and nothing else.
#
# Each step's stderr is folded into stdout so that `tee` keeps one stream in the
# order it happened. That is the shape a redirected run already had, and the
# block is parsed back out of it.
#
# **`just gate quiet` is the mode a tool call wants; bare `gate` is the mode a
# terminal wants.** The block above was meant to end the second call that reads
# the log back, and it did not, because a whole run is 300 KB — 75k tokens — and
# no session can let that into a tool result. So 262 of the 272 gate runs in the
# week of 2026-09-12 redirected stdout to a file, which is the right thing to do
# with 300 KB and throws the block away with it; 122 of them then paid a second
# whole-context call to read back the eight lines already printed. `quiet` puts
# one line per step, the tail of whatever broke, and the block on stdout, and
# sends the rest to the log — so one unredirected call answers the question.
# `docs/decisions.md` §147.
#
# **Code that has already gated green on this machine is served, not run
# again.** If this commit's code, which is its tree without `docs/` and `*.md`,
# passed a gate here in the last day, the steps of `gate_reusable_steps` that
# gate ran are skipped and that pass stands in for them. Every other step still
# runs. The second word `fresh` serves nothing. A commit whose sha starts with
# `0` also serves nothing. That is the audit: it re-runs about one gate in
# sixteen. If a re-run fails a step the record says passed, that is reported as
# a violation and the record is deleted. `docs/decisions.md` §337.
#
# **The record names the steps it passed** (§397.4). A run that leaves some out,
# as `gate-lane` and `gate-docs` do, records the ones it ran, and a later gate
# of the same code is served those and runs the rest. Its pass adds what it ran
# to the record and keeps the record's date: a served verdict never gets a new
# day.
#
# **The steps run at once, as many as there are cores.** In turn they took 308 s
# on 24 idle cores; at once, with the steps that build with cargo run one after
# another beside the rest, 91 s (§372.3). Each step writes its own log, and a
# step's output reaches the terminal and `gate_log` whole, when it ends, under a
# `===` line naming it, so no two steps' lines interleave. **A failure stops
# nothing**: every step runs, the block names the first step of `gate_steps` that
# failed, and the others that failed after it. Stopping early would mean killing
# `test` and the slowest selftest, which start at once, and a lane would then
# find its second failure one gate later. The third word is how many steps run
# at once; `1` runs them in turn. `docs/decisions.md` §372.8 to §372.12.
#
# **A tree with anything uncommitted or untracked is refused before any step
# starts.** No sha names what such a gate reads, so its green can be neither
# served nor recorded, and whoever gates the commit after it runs every step
# again. Ignored files do not count. It is a refusal and not a step because a
# step would run beside the others to the end of the gate before it could say
# "commit first". Like the refusal of a misspelt word it prints no summary
# block: the block describes steps, and none ran. `docs/decisions.md` §415.
#
# **The steps run in a run of their own, and the call only follows it.** A
# `/clear` kills the command every agent has running, and a gate is the one it
# kills most (§429.1). So the call starts the run with a double fork, which
# leaves no agent's shell its ancestor, then prints what the run prints and
# exits with its code. The run's pid, output and exit code are files in
# `<gate_log>.run/`. The same gate run again in the same tree — the same
# commit and the same words — follows a run that is still going instead of
# starting another, so a merger whose gate came back 137 gets it back by
# running the same command. Any other gate refuses while one runs. A run that
# died without an exit code is reported once, as ended without finishing, and
# the gate after that starts afresh. A Ctrl-C is passed on to the run.
# `docs/decisions.md` §429.8.
[doc("Every step, as many at once as there are cores, on a tree with nothing uncommitted or untracked. `just gate quiet` prints one line a step and the summary; `just gate quiet fresh` serves nothing from an earlier green; a third word `1` runs the steps in turn.")]
gate mode="loud" verdicts="reuse" jobs="cores":
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -uo pipefail
    mode='{{ mode }}'
    verdicts='{{ verdicts }}'
    jobs='{{ jobs }}'
    # Named rather than defaulted, so a typo is a refusal and not a loud run.
    case "$mode" in
        loud|quiet) ;;
        *) echo "just gate takes 'quiet', 'loud' or nothing first, not '$mode'" >&2; exit 2 ;;
    esac
    case "$verdicts" in
        reuse|fresh) ;;
        *) echo "just gate takes 'fresh' or nothing second, not '$verdicts'" >&2; exit 2 ;;
    esac
    [ "$jobs" != cores ] || jobs={{ num_cpus() }}
    if ! [[ "$jobs" =~ ^[1-9][0-9]*$ ]]; then
        echo "just gate takes how many steps to run at once, or nothing, third, not '{{ jobs }}'" >&2
        exit 2
    fi
    log='{{ gate_log }}'
    # Absolute, so that `pgrep -af <tree>` finds the run by its script's path.
    case "$log" in
        /*) run="$log.run" ;;
        *) run="$PWD/$log.run" ;;
    esac
    if [ -z "${RCAD_GATE_RUN:-}" ]; then
        mkdir -p "$run"
        exec 9>"$run/lock"
        flock 9
        # What makes two calls the same gate: the commit, since the run reads
        # the tree it started on, and every word that changes what it runs or
        # prints. The run prints one form only, and `1` is asked for to rule
        # out steps getting in each other's way (§372.8).
        this=$(
            echo commit "$(git rev-parse HEAD 2>/dev/null)"
            echo steps {{ gate_steps }}
            echo reusable {{ gate_reusable_steps }}
            echo verdicts "$verdicts"
            echo mode "$mode"
            echo jobs "$jobs"
        )
        # A pid can be another process by the time it is read again, after a
        # reboot or once the pids wrap, so the run is known by its start too.
        # A zombie has ended.
        identity() {
            local stat
            read -r stat 2>/dev/null <"/proc/$1/stat" || return 1
            stat=${stat##*) }
            set -- $stat
            case "$1" in Z|X) return 1 ;; esac
            echo "$(</proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id) ${20}"
        }
        alive() { [ -n "$2" ] && [ "$(identity "$1")" = "$2" ]; }
        # Prints what the run prints until it ends, and exits with its code.
        # The run is no child of this call, so `wait` cannot reach it: its files
        # are all there is to go on.
        follow() {
            local pid want code
            pid=$(<"$run/pid")
            want=$(cat "$run/start" 2>/dev/null)
            # A Ctrl-C reaches this call and not the run, which leads a session
            # of its own and, as a background job, ignores SIGINT. So it is
            # passed on as SIGTERM, to the run's whole process group.
            trap 'stop "$pid" "$want"' INT
            exec 3<"$run/out"
            while :; do
                code=""
                [ ! -e "$run/exit" ] || code=$(<"$run/exit")
                cat <&3
                [ -z "$code" ] || exit "$code"
                if ! alive "$pid" "$want"; then
                    [ ! -e "$run/exit" ] || continue
                    : >"$run/lost"
                    echo "just gate: the run (pid $pid) ended without finishing; $log holds what it ran"
                    exit 1
                fi
                sleep 0.2
            done
        }
        stop() {
            ! alive "$1" "$2" || kill -TERM -- "-$1" 2>/dev/null
            : >"$run/stopped"
            echo "just gate: a Ctrl-C stopped the run (pid $1)" >&2
            exit 130
        }
        # A run that ended, or that a call already found dead or stopped, is
        # not followed: the gate starts afresh.
        if [ -e "$run/pid" ] && [ ! -e "$run/exit" ] && [ ! -e "$run/lost" ] && [ ! -e "$run/stopped" ]; then
            pid=$(<"$run/pid")
            if [ "$(cat "$run/gate" 2>/dev/null)" = "$this" ]; then
                flock -u 9
                exec 9>&-
                ! alive "$pid" "$(cat "$run/start" 2>/dev/null)" \
                    || echo "· following the gate already running in this tree, pid $pid, started $(cat "$run/started")"
                follow
            fi
            if alive "$pid" "$(cat "$run/start" 2>/dev/null)"; then
                {
                    echo "just gate will not start beside the gate already running in this tree, pid $pid," \
                        "started $(cat "$run/started"), since it is another gate:"
                    sed 's/^/  /' "$run/gate"
                    echo "wait for it to end, or run that same gate to follow it (docs/decisions.md §429.8)"
                } >&2
                exit 2
            fi
        fi
        # After the check for a run to follow, since following reads nothing
        # of the tree and a run can write into it, as a failing proptest does.
        # Before the log is emptied, so a refused run leaves the last real
        # gate's log where it was. `--untracked-files` is named because a
        # user's `status.showUntrackedFiles=no` would otherwise hide an
        # untracked `.rs` that cargo compiles all the same.
        dirty=$(git status --porcelain --untracked-files=normal) || {
            echo "just gate could not ask git whether this tree has uncommitted or untracked files" >&2
            exit 2
        }
        if [ -n "$dirty" ]; then
            echo "just gate refuses a tree with uncommitted or untracked files, since no sha names what it would read:" >&2
            git status --short --untracked-files=normal >&2
            echo "commit first — a WIP commit is enough, and the branch is still on disk (docs/decisions.md §415)" >&2
            exit 2
        fi
        # Removed rather than emptied, so that a run still exiting keeps the
        # files it has open, and the new run gets files of its own.
        rm -f "$run/pid" "$run/start" "$run/exit" "$run/lost" "$run/stopped" \
            "$run/gate" "$run/out" "$run/script" "$run/started"
        printf '%s\n' "$this" >"$run/gate"
        # `$0` is just's copy of this script, which goes when just does.
        cp "$0" "$run/script"
        : >"$run/out"
        date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ >"$run/started"
        # A double fork, as `setsid -f` does: the subshell starts the run and
        # exits, so the run is init's before this call follows it, and in a
        # session of its own.
        (
            RCAD_GATE_RUN=1 setsid bash "$run/script" "$@" </dev/null >>"$run/out" 2>&1 9>&- &
            echo "$!" >"$run/pid"
            identity "$!" >"$run/start"
        )
        flock -u 9
        exec 9>&-
        follow
    fi
    # The gates a selftest runs inside this one are calls, not runs.
    unset RCAD_GATE_RUN
    mkdir -p "$(dirname "$log")"
    : >"$log"
    # One log a step, so that a failing step's tail is its own however many
    # steps were writing beside it.
    steps_dir="$log.steps"
    rm -rf "$steps_dir"
    mkdir -p "$steps_dir"
    j='{{ just_executable() }}'
    note() { echo "$1" | tee -a "$log"; }

    # Which of this run's steps an earlier green could stand in for. None is
    # the usual answer inside the selftests, and then there is nothing to look
    # up and nothing to say.
    reusable=""
    for step in {{ gate_steps }}; do
        case " {{ gate_reusable_steps }} " in *" $step "*) reusable="$reusable $step" ;; esac
    done
    fingerprint="" record="" served=""
    head=$(git rev-parse HEAD 2>/dev/null)
    if [ -n "$reusable" ]; then
        fingerprint=$($j _code-fingerprint)
        if [[ "$fingerprint" =~ ^[0-9a-f]{64}$ ]]; then
            record="$(git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir)/rcad-verdicts/$fingerprint"
        else
            note "· verdicts: ${fingerprint:-no fingerprint could be taken}, so nothing is served or recorded"
            fingerprint=""
        fi
    fi
    # Read from a copy. The summary reads the record again at the end, and a
    # violation in another worktree can delete the record before then.
    served_copy="$log.served"
    rm -f "$served_copy"
    trusted=""
    if [ -n "$record" ] && cp "$record" "$served_copy" 2>/dev/null; then
        from=$(sed -n 's/^commit //p' "$served_copy")
        at=$(sed -n 's/^date //p' "$served_copy")
        passed=$(sed -n 's/^steps //p' "$served_copy")
        at_s=$(date -u -d "${at:-unreadable}" +%s 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
        age=$(( $(date +%s) - at_s ))
        # A record from before §397.4 names no steps, so it cannot say which
        # of them it passed.
        if [ -z "$from" ] || [ "$at_s" -eq 0 ] || [ -z "$passed" ]; then
            note "· verdicts: the record for this code cannot be read, so everything runs: $record"
        elif [ "$verdicts" = fresh ]; then
            note "· verdicts: fresh, so ${from:0:8}'s green for this code is run again, not served"
        elif [ "$age" -lt 0 ] || [ "$age" -ge 86400 ]; then
            note "· verdicts: ${from:0:8}'s green for this code is from $at, not the last day, so it is run again"
        elif [[ "$head" == 0* ]]; then
            note "· verdicts: audit, since this sha starts with 0: ${from:0:8}'s green for this code is run again"
        else
            trusted=yes
            for step in $reusable; do
                case " $passed " in *" $step "*) served="$served $step" ;; esac
            done
            [ -z "$served" ] || note "· served:$served from ${from:0:8}'s gate at $at"
        fi
    fi

    # The steps that build with cargo run one after another, in the order
    # `gate_steps` gives them, beside everything else. Cargo locks the build
    # directory, so two of them started together only wait on each other: all 56
    # steps started at once took 110 s, not 91, and `test` waited on the lock
    # seven times (§372.3). `check-t-selftest` also needs the binaries `test`
    # built, which is why it comes after `test` in the list.
    # `check-gate-runs-every-check` holds this line to the steps whose recipes
    # run cargo, so a new one cannot be left off it unnoticed.
    cargo_steps="check check-fuzz-builds check-wildcards check-ignored lint test test-slow check-release-build check-t-selftest"
    names=()
    chained=()
    unchained=()
    for step in {{ gate_steps }}; do
        case " $served " in *" $step "*) continue ;; esac
        case " $cargo_steps " in
            *" $step "*) chained+=("${#names[@]}") ;;
            *) unchained+=("${#names[@]}") ;;
        esac
        names+=("$step")
    done
    # Keyed by index rather than by name: a fixture list can name a step twice.
    declare -A running=()
    began=()
    ended_with=()
    chain_busy=""
    start() {
        began[$1]=${EPOCHREALTIME//[!0-9]/}
        "$j" "${names[$1]}" >"$steps_dir/$1-${names[$1]}.log" 2>&1 &
        running[$!]=$1
    }
    while :; do
        # The next cargo step takes a free slot first, because the chain is
        # the longest thing the gate waits on.
        while [ "${#running[@]}" -lt "$jobs" ]; do
            if [ -z "$chain_busy" ] && [ "${#chained[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
                chain_busy=${chained[0]}
                chained=("${chained[@]:1}")
                start "$chain_busy"
            elif [ "${#unchained[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
                start "${unchained[0]}"
                unchained=("${unchained[@]:1}")
            else
                break
            fi
        done
        [ "${#running[@]}" -gt 0 ] || break
        wait -n -p pid
        step_status=$?
        i=${running[$pid]}
        unset "running[$pid]"
        [ "$i" != "$chain_busy" ] || chain_busy=""
        ended_with[$i]=$step_status
        tenths=$(( (${EPOCHREALTIME//[!0-9]/} - began[i]) / 100000 ))
        line="${names[$i]} ($((tenths / 10)).$((tenths % 10)) s)"
        [ "$step_status" -eq 0 ] || line="$line FAILED, exit $step_status"
        if [ "$mode" = quiet ]; then
            # As each step ends rather than as it starts: with every step
            # started at once, a result cut short would otherwise list them all
            # and say nothing about how far the run got.
            echo "· $line"
            { echo "=== $line ==="; cat "$steps_dir/$i-${names[$i]}.log"; } >>"$log"
        else
            { echo "=== $line ==="; cat "$steps_dir/$i-${names[$i]}.log"; } | tee -a "$log"
        fi
    done

    # The first failure is the first in `gate_steps`, not the first to end, so
    # the name does not depend on which step happened to be quicker. That keeps
    # what `check-gate-runs-every-check` pins `check-plugins` first for: with no
    # plugins, it is `check-plugins` and its install command that are named.
    failed=""
    status=0
    first=""
    for i in "${!names[@]}"; do
        [ "${ended_with[$i]}" -ne 0 ] || continue
        failed="$failed ${names[$i]}"
        [ -n "$first" ] || { first=$i; status=${ended_with[$i]}; }
    done
    failed=${failed# }
    # Only in `quiet`, and only before the block: `loud` has already shown these
    # lines once, and the block stays last because `tail -6` is where a reader
    # looks for it. Capped, because a step can fail with a megabyte behind it
    # and `full log:` names where the rest is.
    if [ "$mode" = quiet ] && [ -n "$first" ]; then
        echo
        echo "=== last 60 lines of ${names[$first]} ==="
        tail -60 "$steps_dir/$first-${names[$first]}.log"
    fi
    summary=$($j _gate-summary "$log" "$status" "$failed" "${served:+$served_copy}")

    # The first failing step that the green on record for this code passed. Not
    # any reusable step: a land gate whose `test-slow` fails over a lane's
    # record, which never ran it, has found a failure and not a violation.
    violated=""
    if [ -n "$fingerprint" ] && [ -f "$record" ]; then
        passed=$(sed -n 's/^steps //p' "$record")
        for step in $failed; do
            case " $passed " in *" $step "*) violated=$step; break ;; esac
        done
    fi
    # The reusable steps this run ran, which are what it can add to the record.
    ran=""
    for step in $reusable; do
        case " $served " in *" $step "*) ;; *) ran="$ran $step" ;; esac
    done

    # What this run learned about the code, written down before the block is
    # printed so that the block stays last. A run served every step learned
    # nothing new.
    if [ -n "$fingerprint" ] && [ -n "$ran" ]; then
        if [ "$status" -eq 0 ]; then
            if [ "$($j _code-fingerprint)" != "$fingerprint" ]; then
                note "· verdicts: not recorded, because the tree changed while the gate ran"
            else
                store=$(dirname "$record")
                mkdir -p "$store"
                new=$(mktemp "$store/.new.XXXXXX")
                # The record as it is now and not the copy this run read: a
                # violation in another worktree may have deleted it since, and
                # what it was found wrong about must not come back.
                old=""
                [ -z "$trusted" ] || old=$(cat "$record" 2>/dev/null)
                own=$($j _gate-summary "$log" 0 | grep -E '^(tests|doc tests): ' | grep -v ': not reached$')
                {
                    if grep -q '^steps ' <<<"$old"; then
                        grep -E '^(commit|date|host) ' <<<"$old"
                        echo "$(grep '^steps ' <<<"$old")$ran"
                        grep -E '^(tests|doc tests): ' <<<"$old"
                    else
                        echo "commit $head"
                        echo "date $(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)"
                        echo "host $(hostname)"
                        echo "steps$ran"
                    fi
                    [ -z "$own" ] || echo "$own"
                } >"$new"
                mv "$new" "$record"
                # Nothing is served past a day, and a week keeps enough to
                # catch a violation against an expired record.
                find "$store" -maxdepth 1 -type f -mtime +7 -delete
                if grep -q '^steps ' <<<"$old"; then
                    note "· verdicts:$ran added to this code's green, ${fingerprint:0:12}"
                else
                    note "· verdicts: this code's green is recorded as ${fingerprint:0:12}"
                fi
            fi
        elif [ -n "$violated" ]; then
            # The same code and the same environment gave a different answer,
            # so the fingerprint is missing an input or a step is not
            # deterministic. Either way the record is wrong and must go before
            # anything serves it.
            from=$(sed -n 's/^commit //p' "$record")
            at=$(sed -n 's/^date //p' "$record")
            violations="$(dirname "$(dirname "$record")")/rcad-verdict-violations"
            echo "$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ) $fingerprint $violated $head passed-at=$from $(hostname)" >>"$violations"
            rm -f "$record"
            note "!!! verdict violated: $violated failed on code that ${from:0:8}'s gate passed at $at."
            note "!!! Same code, same environment, different answer: the fingerprint is missing an"
            note "!!! input, or the step is not deterministic (docs/decisions.md §337.6). The record is"
            note "!!! deleted, and the case is kept in $violations"
        fi
    fi
    echo "$summary" | tee -a "$log"
    # After the block, so that a reader's `tail -6` still lands on the summary,
    # and only on a pass: what it records is that this commit was read end to
    # end. §173.9 — it is what moves the overnight fuzz now that no push can be
    # CI-green.
    if [ "$status" -eq 0 ]; then
        {{ just_executable() }} _record-green "{{ gate_steps }}"
    fi
    # Moved into place whole, so a follower never reads half of it.
    echo "$status" >"$run/exit.new"
    mv "$run/exit.new" "$run/exit"
    exit $status

# A name for the code a gate reads. It prints a sha256, or one sentence saying
# why there is none. `docs/decisions.md` §337.
#
# **The files are HEAD's tree without `docs/` and `*.md`.** Those are exactly the
# paths `ci.yml` leaves to `ci-docs.yml`, and the reason is the same: no prose
# reaches a build or a test here. `check-gate-verdicts-selftest` holds the two
# lists together, so the claim cannot change in one place only.
#
# **The environment is the variables the steps read that the tree does not
# pin.** The tests read `RCAD_*`, proptest reads `PROPTEST_*`, and the rest
# choose the compiler, its flags, or nextest's retries. `RCAD_STEP_CORPUS` is
# left out. It only says where the corpus is. What the corpus holds is pinned by
# `corpus/manifest.sha256`, which is in the tree, and `check-corpus` checks it
# on every gate and is never served.
#
# **A tree with anything uncommitted or untracked has no name.** The name is
# read from HEAD, and an untracked `.rs` file under `crates/*/tests/` gets
# compiled anyway. `gate` refuses such a tree before it starts (§415), so it
# meets this sentence when the tree changes while it runs, and its pass is then
# not recorded; `check-gate-verdicts-selftest` drives that case.
[private]
_code-fingerprint:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    git rev-parse --verify --quiet HEAD >/dev/null || { echo "there is no commit here"; exit 0; }
    if [ -n "$(git status --porcelain --untracked-files=normal)" ]; then
        echo "the tree has uncommitted or untracked files"
        exit 0
    fi
    {
        git ls-tree -r --full-tree HEAD | awk -F'\t' '$2 !~ /^docs\// && $2 !~ /\.md$/'
        echo "-- environment"
        for name in $(compgen -e | LC_ALL=C sort); do
            case "$name" in
                RCAD_STEP_CORPUS) ;;
                RCAD_*|PROPTEST_*|NEXTEST_*|CARGO_BUILD_*|CARGO_PROFILE_*|RUSTFLAGS|RUSTDOCFLAGS|CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTFLAGS|RUSTC|RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN|RUST_MIN_STACK)
                    printf '%s=%s\0' "$name" "${!name}" ;;
            esac
        done
    } | sha256sum | cut -d' ' -f1

# The block itself, kept apart from the run so `check-gate-summary-selftest` can
# hold it against logs whose answer is already known — the same split `t` and
# `_test-digest` have, and for the same reason.
#
# **The escape codes come off before anything is grepped.** `ci.yml` sets
# `CARGO_TERM_COLOR: always`, so on a runner nextest's summary line arrives as
# `\e[1m     Summary\e[0m ...` and an anchored pattern sees a colour code where
# it wanted a space. That is not a guess: it is what turned two CI runs red in
# `check-wildcards`, which anchored its grep the same way (docs/decisions.md
# §77.6).
#
# **A missing count reads as `not reached`, never as zero.** A gate that died in
# `check-enums` never got to `test`, and a block that printed `0 tests run`
# there would be describing the gate's own failure as a clean empty suite.
#
# **Every count line is reported, never the last one.** The gate has two nextest
# runs — `test` and `check-release-build` — and eight `test result:` lines, one
# per crate, out of `cargo test --doc`. Reading the last of each got `3 tests
# run` over a suite of 1869 and `2 passed` over 24 doc tests, on a real green
# gate. A number that small and that wrong is worse than none, because it reads
# as a real total. So the `Summary` lines are all printed, each under its own
# `tests:` label, and the doc tests are added up.
#
# **A served run prints the counts of the gate that served it**, marked as such.
# `test` did not run, so the log has no counts, and `tests: not reached` would
# describe a pass as a gate that died early. `served` is that gate's record
# (§337). If that record is gone by the end, the summary says so. The first real
# served gate printed no counts at all, because a nested run had deleted it.
# **A partly served run prints the record's counts and then its own**: a land
# gate served everything but `test-slow` from a lane's record has both (§397.4).
# `not reached` is printed only for a kind of count that neither has.
#
# **`failed` is every step that failed, first of `gate_steps` first.** The steps
# run at once and a failure stops none of them (§372.9), so a gate can end with
# several. The first is named as the first failure, the rest on a line of their
# own.
[private]
_gate-summary log status failed="" served="":
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -uo pipefail
    plain=$(sed 's/\x1b\[[0-9;]*m//g' '{{ log }}' 2>/dev/null)
    tests=$(grep -E '^ *Summary ' <<<"$plain")
    doctests=$(awk '/^test result: /{passed += $4; failed += $6; crates += 1} \
                    END {if (crates) printf "%d passed, %d failed, across %d crate(s)", passed, failed, crates}' \
                    <<<"$plain")
    echo
    echo "=== gate summary ==="
    if [ '{{ status }}' = "0" ]; then
        echo "gate: PASS"
    else
        read -r first others <<<'{{ failed }}'
        echo "gate: FAIL  first failure: $first (exit {{ status }})"
        [ -z "$others" ] || echo "also failed: $others"
        first=$(grep -m1 -E '^ *FAIL \[' <<<"$plain" | sed 's/^ *//')
        [ -z "$first" ] || echo "first failing test: $first"
    fi
    kept=""
    if [ -n '{{ served }}' ] && [ ! -r '{{ served }}' ]; then
        echo "tests: served, but the record it was served from is gone: {{ served }}"
        kept=gone
    elif [ -n '{{ served }}' ]; then
        from=$(sed -n 's/^commit //p' '{{ served }}')
        kept=$(grep -E '^(tests|doc tests): ' '{{ served }}')
        [ -z "$kept" ] || sed "s/\$/ (served from ${from:0:8})/" <<<"$kept"
    fi
    if [ -n "$tests" ]; then
        sed 's/^ *//; s/^/tests: /' <<<"$tests"
    elif [ "$kept" != gone ] && ! grep -q '^tests: ' <<<"$kept"; then
        echo "tests: not reached"
    fi
    if [ -n "$doctests" ]; then
        echo "doc tests: $doctests"
    elif [ "$kept" != gone ] && ! grep -q '^doc tests: ' <<<"$kept"; then
        echo "doc tests: not reached"
    fi
    echo "full log: {{ log }}"

# Writes down that this commit has been read end to end, so that something other
# than GitHub can say so.
#
# Written for `fuzz-watermark`, which until §186 moved the overnight run only
# onto a green commit, when §172's spent Actions allowance meant no commit could
# be CI-green any more. The night follows the tip now and reads none of this;
# what still reads it is `just status`'s green row. **A passing `just gate` is a
# reading of the tree end to end**, and CI was never the only thing that could
# supply that fact.
#
# Three conditions, and each is a way the record could otherwise lie:
#
#   - **the metered steps all ran.** `gate-docs` re-enters `gate` with a trimmed
#     list, so it lands here as a pass having compiled nothing at all. A green
#     that skipped `test` is not a reading of the code, and this is the one
#     mistake that would be invisible afterwards — the file would just name a
#     commit, with no trace of how little was run for it. `gate-lane` leaves
#     out `test-slow`, and its pass is not a reading end to end either.
#   - **the tree is clean**, because the sha is the whole record. A green
#     recorded against a dirty tree names something nobody can get back.
#     `gate` refuses a dirty tree before it starts (§415), so from there this
#     catches a tree written to while the gate ran; from `gate-fresh`, a tree
#     written to while the container ran.
#   - **it never fails the gate.** Bookkeeping that can turn a green run red is
#     worse than no bookkeeping; every giving-up path here says why and exits 0.
#
# The file sits in the common git dir, so every worktree writes to one list and
# the hub does not need to be checked out to have one. Untracked by construction:
# it is a fact about this machine, and a commit's greenness is not the sort of
# thing to carry in the tree it describes. `docs/decisions.md` §173.9.
[private]
_record-green steps:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -uo pipefail
    for heavy in {{ gate_metered_steps }}; do
        case " {{ steps }} " in
            *" $heavy "*) ;;
            *) echo "green not recorded: this run skipped $heavy, so it did not read the code end to end"; exit 0 ;;
        esac
    done
    git diff --quiet 2>/dev/null && git diff --cached --quiet 2>/dev/null || {
        echo "green not recorded: the tree has uncommitted changes, so a sha would not name it"
        exit 0
    }
    sha=$(git rev-parse HEAD 2>/dev/null) || { echo "green not recorded: no HEAD here"; exit 0; }
    common=$(git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir 2>/dev/null) \
        || { echo "green not recorded: no git dir here"; exit 0; }
    file="$common/rcad-greens"
    if grep -q "^$sha " "$file" 2>/dev/null; then
        echo "green already recorded for $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)"
        exit 0
    fi
    printf '%s %s %s\n' "$sha" "$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)" "$(hostname)" >>"$file"
    # Bounded, because nothing ever deletes from it and the readers are loops.
    # The tail is what a watermark could still move onto; the head is history no
    # night will ever walk back to.
    if [ "$(wc -l <"$file")" -gt 500 ]; then
        tail -200 "$file" >"$file.new" && mv "$file.new" "$file"
    fi
    echo "green recorded for $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) in $file"

# Holds the block and the loop that drives it against fixtures whose right
# answer is known, rather than against whatever the gate happens to do today.
#
# **The loop is exercised by moving `gate_steps`, not by running the real gate.**
# This selftest is itself a step of the gate, so a nested real run would not
# terminate. `--set` hands the driver a step list one of whose words is not a
# recipe at all, which is the cheapest failing step there is and needs no
# fixture recipe of its own.
#
# The five ways this can be wrong each get a fixture: a pass that does not print
# the block, a failure that prints no block at all (which is the whole defect
# this recipe exists to remove), a failure that does not name the step that
# broke, a log whose counts are behind colour codes, and a doc-test total read
# off one crate's line rather than added up.
#
# `quiet` gets four more, because the thing it promises is what it leaves out: a
# quiet pass that carried a step's own output into the result would be no better
# than a loud one, and a quiet failure that left the failing lines in the log
# would send the reader back for the second call this whole mode exists to
# remove. The fourth is the typo — a mode that is neither word must refuse,
# because a `quiet` misspelt into a 300 KB result is the defect wearing a
# different hat.
#
# Running the steps at once gets four (§372): a failure that stops the steps
# after it, a second failure the block does not name, steps that run in turn
# when they were told they could run at once, and a third word `1` that does not
# run them in turn. The two about time use `_gate-selftest-sleep`, and they
# measure what no load can shorten: three two-second sleeps in turn take six
# seconds at least, so the one that wants them at once asks for under five.
[private]
check-gate-summary-selftest:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    dir=$(mktemp -d)
    trap 'rm -rf "$dir"' EXIT
    j='{{ just_executable() }}'

    fail() {
        echo "the gate summary drifted from its own fixtures: $1"
        echo "--- got ---"; echo "$got"
        exit 1
    }

    # Every nested gate asks git about a scratch repository and not the real
    # tree, because `gate` refuses a tree with anything uncommitted or untracked
    # (§415). The real tree is dirty whenever this is run by hand mid-edit, and
    # a failing proptest writes its seed into it while the real gate runs, which
    # would add this step to a red gate's `also failed:` line for nothing.
    repo="$dir/repo"
    mkdir -p "$repo"
    export GIT_DIR="$repo/.git" GIT_WORK_TREE="$repo"
    git init -q -b main
    git -c user.name=selftest -c user.email=selftest@invalid commit -q --allow-empty -m 'a clean tree'

    # A pass. `list` is a real recipe that costs nothing, so the driver runs
    # something real without the gate running itself.
    got=$($j --set gate_steps list --set gate_log "$dir/pass.log" gate 2>&1) \
        || fail "a gate whose every step passed exited non-zero"
    [ "$(tail -6 <<<"$got" | grep -c '=== gate summary ===')" -eq 1 ] \
        || fail "the block is not at the tail, which is the one place a redirected run is read"
    grep -q '^gate: PASS$' <<<"$got" || fail "a passing gate did not say so"
    grep -q '^tests: not reached$' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a run that never reached the suite reported a count anyway"

    # A failure. The step list names something that is not a recipe, so `just`
    # exits non-zero on it, and the step after it still runs: a failure stops
    # nothing, so that a second one is found by this gate and not the next.
    got=$($j --set gate_steps 'list no-such-step list' --set gate_log "$dir/fail.log" gate 2>&1) \
        && fail "a gate with a failing step exited 0"
    grep -q '=== gate summary ===' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a failing gate printed no block, which is the defect this exists to remove"
    grep -q '^gate: FAIL  first failure: no-such-step' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the block does not name the step that broke"
    [ "$(grep -c 'Everything this repo can do' "$dir/fail.log")" -eq 2 ] \
        || fail "a failing step stopped the steps after it"
    grep -q '^also failed' <<<"$got" && fail "a gate with one failure named another"

    # Two failures. The first of the list is named as the first failure, and
    # the other is named too.
    got=$($j --set gate_steps 'no-such-step list no-such-other-step' --set gate_log "$dir/two.log" gate quiet 2>&1) \
        && fail "a gate with two failing steps exited 0"
    grep -q '^gate: FAIL  first failure: no-such-step (exit ' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a gate with two failures did not name the first of the list first"
    grep -qx 'also failed: no-such-other-step' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a gate with two failures named only one"

    # At once. Three steps that each take two seconds, given three slots.
    began=$(date +%s%N)
    got=$($j --set gate_steps '_gate-selftest-sleep _gate-selftest-sleep _gate-selftest-sleep' \
            --set gate_log "$dir/once.log" gate quiet reuse 3 2>&1) \
        || fail "three steps that sleep failed"
    took=$(( ($(date +%s%N) - began) / 1000000 ))
    [ "$took" -lt 5000 ] || fail "three two-second steps given three slots took $took ms, so they ran in turn"
    # And in turn, when told to.
    began=$(date +%s%N)
    got=$($j --set gate_steps '_gate-selftest-sleep _gate-selftest-sleep' \
            --set gate_log "$dir/turn.log" gate quiet reuse 1 2>&1) \
        || fail "two steps that sleep failed"
    took=$(( ($(date +%s%N) - began) / 1000000 ))
    [ "$took" -ge 4000 ] || fail "two two-second steps given one slot took $took ms, so they ran at once"
    got=$($j --set gate_steps list --set gate_log "$dir/jobs.log" gate quiet reuse many 2>&1) \
        && fail "a gate told to run 'many' steps at once ran anyway"
    grep -q "not 'many'" <<<"$got" || fail "a gate given a bad count did not say what it was given"

    # A quiet pass. The step still runs and the log still gets everything; what
    # must not appear on stdout is the step's own output.
    got=$($j --set gate_steps list --set gate_log "$dir/quiet-pass.log" gate quiet 2>&1) \
        || fail "a quiet gate whose every step passed exited non-zero"
    grep -q '^gate: PASS$' <<<"$got" || fail "a passing quiet gate did not say so"
    grep -qE '^· list \([0-9]+\.[0-9] s\)$' <<<"$got" || fail "a quiet gate did not say which step it had run"
    grep -q 'Everything this repo can do' <<<"$got" \
        && fail "a quiet gate put a step's own output in the result, which is the whole thing it exists not to do"
    grep -q 'Everything this repo can do' "$dir/quiet-pass.log" \
        || fail "a quiet gate dropped a step's output instead of logging it"

    # A quiet failure. Naming the step is not enough here: loud mode has already
    # shown the lines that explain it and quiet mode has not, so they come out
    # with the block — and the block stays last.
    got=$($j --set gate_steps 'list no-such-step list' --set gate_log "$dir/quiet-fail.log" gate quiet 2>&1) \
        && fail "a quiet gate with a failing step exited 0"
    grep -q '^gate: FAIL  first failure: no-such-step' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a quiet failing gate does not name the step that broke"
    grep -q '=== last 60 lines of no-such-step ===' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a quiet failure sent the reader back to the log for the lines that explain it"
    grep -q "Justfile does not contain recipe" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the failing tail named the step but carried none of its output"
    grep -q 'Everything this repo can do' <<<"$got" \
        && fail "the failing tail spent its lines on the step before the one that broke"
    [ "$(tail -6 <<<"$got" | grep -c '=== gate summary ===')" -eq 1 ] \
        || fail "the failing tail pushed the block off the end, where no reader looks for it"

    # A mode that is neither word. Defaulting it to `loud` would answer a typed
    # `quite` with the 300 KB this mode exists to avoid.
    got=$($j --set gate_steps list --set gate_log "$dir/mode.log" gate quite 2>&1) \
        && fail "a misspelt mode ran the gate anyway"
    grep -q "not 'quite'" <<<"$got" || fail "a misspelt mode did not say what it was given"

    # The counts, read back out of a log written the way a runner writes one:
    # `CARGO_TERM_COLOR: always`, so every line arrives wrapped in escape codes.
    printf '\033[1m     Summary\033[0m [  12.345s] 1811 tests run: 1811 passed, 0 skipped\n' >"$dir/counts.log"
    printf 'test result: ok. 15 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out\n' >>"$dir/counts.log"
    printf 'test result: ok. 9 passed; 1 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out\n' >>"$dir/counts.log"
    printf '     Summary [   0.007s] 3 tests run: 3 passed, 0 skipped\n' >>"$dir/counts.log"
    got=$($j _gate-summary "$dir/counts.log" 0)
    grep -q '^tests: Summary \[  12.345s\] 1811 tests run: 1811 passed, 0 skipped$' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the test counts did not survive the colour codes a runner writes"
    grep -q '^tests: Summary \[   0.007s\] 3 tests run: 3 passed, 0 skipped$' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "only one of the gate's two nextest runs was reported"
    grep -q '^doc tests: 24 passed, 1 failed, across 2 crate(s)$' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the doc tests were read off one crate's line instead of added up over all of them"

    # A failing suite names the first failing test as well as the step, because
    # `test` is one step and the answer wanted under it is which test.
    printf '        FAIL [   0.011s] rcad-geom a_plane_and_a_sphere\n' >>"$dir/counts.log"
    got=$($j _gate-summary "$dir/counts.log" 1 test)
    grep -q '^first failing test: FAIL \[   0.011s\] rcad-geom a_plane_and_a_sphere$' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a failing suite did not name the first test that failed"

# Holds `pre-gate` to what a lane leans on it for, against fixture step lists
# moved in with `--set`, as `check-gate-summary-selftest` does for `gate`.
#
# Eight ways it can be wrong. A pass that prints a step's own output, which
# would put clippy's replayed warnings in every lane's result. A failure whose
# output is not printed, which would send the lane to a log for the lines it
# needs. A failure printed whole, which a parse error turns into thousands of
# lines, or cut without naming where the rest is. A failure that stops the
# steps after it, which would hide the second of two failures until the next
# call (§381.3). Two failures printed in the order they ended rather than the
# order of the list. Steps run in turn. A failing `pre-gate` that lets `just`
# go on to the next recipe, which is what `just pre-gate gate-lane` relies on.
# And an empty list, which would pass having checked nothing.
#
# The real list is held too: every step of it must be a step of the gate, since
# `pre-gate` is the gate's cheapest steps run early and never a check of its
# own, and `lint` must not check formatting again (§381.2).
[private]
check-pre-gate-selftest:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    j='{{ just_executable() }}'
    got=""

    fail() {
        echo "pre-gate drifted from its own fixtures: $1"
        [ -z "$got" ] || { echo "--- got ---"; echo "$got"; }
        exit 1
    }

    all=$(printf '%s\n' {{ gate_steps }})
    early=$(printf '%s\n' {{ pre_gate_steps }})
    [ "$(grep -c . <<<"$early")" -ge 3 ] || fail "pre_gate_steps came back with fewer than its three steps"
    while read -r step; do
        grep -qx "$step" <<<"$all" || fail "pre_gate_steps names '$step', which is not a step of the gate"
    done <<<"$early"
    lint_body=$($j --show lint | grep -E '^[[:space:]]+[^#[:space:]]')
    [ -n "$lint_body" ] || fail "just --show lint gave no recipe body, so the arm below would read nothing"
    grep -q 'fmt' <<<"$lint_body" \
        && fail "lint checks formatting, so a misformatted file stops it before clippy says anything, and check-fmt already checks it beside it"

    got=$($j --set pre_gate_steps list pre-gate 2>&1) || fail "a pre-gate whose every step passed exited non-zero"
    grep -qE '^· list \([0-9]+\.[0-9] s\)$' <<<"$got" || fail "a passing step was not named with its time"
    grep -q 'Everything this repo can do' <<<"$got" \
        && fail "a passing step's own output reached the result"

    got=$($j --set pre_gate_steps 'no-such-step list no-such-other-step' pre-gate 2>&1) \
        && fail "a pre-gate with two failing steps exited 0"
    grep -qE '^· list \([0-9]+\.[0-9] s\)$' <<<"$got" || fail "a failing step stopped the steps beside it"
    grep -qE '^· no-such-step \([0-9]+\.[0-9] s\) FAILED, exit [1-9]' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a failing step was not named as failed"
    [ "$(grep -c 'Justfile does not contain recipe' <<<"$got")" -eq 2 ] \
        || fail "the two failing steps' own output was not both printed"
    order=$(grep -E '^=== no-such(-other)?-step ===$' <<<"$got" | paste -sd' ')
    [ "$order" = "=== no-such-step === === no-such-other-step ===" ] \
        || fail "the failures were printed as '$order', not in the order of the list"
    grep -q 'Everything this repo can do' <<<"$got" \
        && fail "a passing step's output was printed beside the failures"

    got=$($j --set pre_gate_steps _pre-gate-selftest-long pre-gate 2>&1) \
        && fail "a step that exits 1 left pre-gate passing"
    grep -q 'Compiling' <<<"$got" && fail "cargo's progress lines took up the lines meant for the failure"
    grep -qx 'line 60' <<<"$got" || fail "a long failure was cut before its sixtieth line"
    grep -qx 'line 61' <<<"$got" && fail "a long failure was printed past its sixtieth line"
    kept=$(sed -nE 's/^\.\.\. [0-9]+ more lines in //p' <<<"$got")
    [ -n "$kept" ] || fail "a long failure did not say how much was cut and where the rest is"
    [ -f "$kept" ] || fail "a long failure named a log, $kept, that it then deleted"
    grep -qx 'line 100' "$kept" || fail "the log a cut failure names does not hold the rest"
    rm -rf "$(dirname "$kept")"

    began=$(date +%s%N)
    got=$($j --set pre_gate_steps '_gate-selftest-sleep _gate-selftest-sleep _gate-selftest-sleep' pre-gate 2>&1) \
        || fail "three steps that sleep failed"
    took=$(( ($(date +%s%N) - began) / 1000000 ))
    [ "$took" -lt 5000 ] || fail "three two-second steps took $took ms, so they ran in turn"

    got=$($j --set pre_gate_steps no-such-step pre-gate list 2>&1) \
        && fail "a failing pre-gate let the recipe after it run and exit 0"
    grep -q 'Everything this repo can do' <<<"$got" \
        && fail "a failing pre-gate let the recipe after it run"

    got=$($j --set pre_gate_steps '' pre-gate 2>&1) && fail "an empty pre-gate passed having checked nothing"
    grep -q 'no steps' <<<"$got" || fail "an empty pre-gate failed without saying it had no steps"

# A step that fails with more lines than `pre-gate` prints, behind three of
# cargo's progress lines, so `check-pre-gate-selftest` can see what is cut.
[private]
_pre-gate-selftest-long:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    printf '   Compiling fixture v0.0.0\n%.0s' 1 2 3
    seq -f 'line %g' 1 100
    exit 1

# A gate step that takes two seconds and does nothing else, so that
# `check-gate-summary-selftest` and `check-pre-gate-selftest` can tell steps run
# at once from steps run in turn by the clock.
#
# Given `GATE_SELFTEST_HOLD`, a path, it does not sleep: it adds a line to
# `<path>.ran` as it starts and then waits while the path is there, so that
# `check-gate-verdicts-selftest` can stop a gate's follower while the gate is
# still running and count how many runs reached the step (§429.8). Two minutes
# held is a selftest that never let it go, and fails.
[private]
_gate-selftest-sleep:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    hold=${GATE_SELFTEST_HOLD:-}
    if [ -z "$hold" ]; then
        sleep 2
        exit 0
    fi
    echo "$$" >>"$hold.ran"
    for _ in $(seq 1200); do
        [ -e "$hold" ] || exit 0
        sleep 0.1
    done
    echo "held for two minutes: nothing removed $hold" >&2
    exit 1

# Holds `gate-docs` to the two things it claims: that it drops the compiles, and
# that it still runs everything a prose commit can break.
#
# **The second is the arm that matters, and it is the one nothing else guards.**
# Dropping a step too few costs money. Dropping a step too many means a
# `docs/decisions.md` commit lands on `main` having been checked by nothing that
# reads `docs/decisions.md` — and it would pass, loudly, printing a summary
# block. So `check-decisions`, `check-plan` and `check-where-the-work-is` are
# named here: move one of them into `gate_metered_steps` and this fails.
#
# The filter is driven through `--set` against fixture lists rather than against
# the real ones, for the same reason `check-gate-summary-selftest` is: this
# recipe is itself a step of the docs arm, so a nested real run would not
# terminate. Both nested calls run against a scratch repository, the way
# `check-gate-summary-selftest`'s does, so that `gate`'s §415 refusal of an
# uncommitted or untracked real tree never fires on the selftest itself.
#
# Both directions are exercised. A filter that dropped nothing and a filter that
# dropped everything are both "the list came out wrong", and only one of them is
# visible from a run that passes.
#
# `gate-lane` is the other gate that trims the list, and is held here the same
# way: it drops `test-slow` and nothing else, and passes its word on to `gate`.
# So is `check-gate-lane-callers`, which keeps it to its callers, against a
# repository built here, and the split of `test` and `test-slow`, against a stub
# `cargo` (§397.4).
[private]
check-gate-docs-selftest:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    dir=$(mktemp -d)
    trap 'rm -rf "$dir"' EXIT
    j='{{ just_executable() }}'
    got=""
    held=gate-docs

    fail() {
        echo "$held drifted from what it promises: $1"
        [ -n "$got" ] && { echo "--- got ---"; echo "$got"; }
        exit 1
    }

    # Interpolated rather than grepped out of the justfile. `gate_steps` is one
    # step per source line now (§177), and a regex anchored at `^` reads the
    # first name and stops — which is exactly what it did, loudly, the moment
    # the list was split. What `just` hands over cannot disagree with what
    # `gate-docs` reads, because it is the same substitution.
    all=$(printf '%s\n' {{ gate_steps }})
    heavy=$(printf '%s\n' {{ gate_metered_steps }})
    # An empty list would make every comparison below pass against nothing.
    # `check-gate-runs-every-check` says the same thing about itself.
    [ "$(grep -c . <<<"$all")" -ge 30 ] || fail "gate_steps came back empty"
    [ "$(grep -c . <<<"$heavy")" -ge 5 ] \
        || fail "gate_metered_steps came back empty"

    # A name in the exclusion list that is not a step excludes nothing, and says
    # so nowhere. That is how this list goes stale when a step is renamed.
    while read -r step; do
        grep -qx "$step" <<<"$all" \
            || fail "gate_metered_steps names '$step', which is not a step of the gate"
    done <<<"$heavy"

    docs_arm=$(grep -vxF -f <(printf '%s\n' "$heavy") <<<"$all")

    # What a prose commit can actually break. Named one by one rather than
    # counted, because the count would survive the swap that matters.
    for needed in check-decisions check-decisions-selftest check-claim-section-selftest \
                  check-plan check-plan-selftest check-queue check-queue-selftest \
                  check-where-the-work-is check-gate-lane-callers; do
        grep -qx "$needed" <<<"$docs_arm" \
            || fail "$needed is not in the docs arm, so a docs commit would be checked by nothing that reads docs"
    done

    # And the other half of the bargain: if these survived, the arm buys nothing.
    #
    # `check-corpus` is in this list for a different reason from the rest, and it
    # is the one that would not have been noticed by reading. It compiles nothing
    # and takes 0.16 s, and it **fails** when `RCAD_STEP_CORPUS` is not a
    # directory (§44.10) — so a docs arm that ran it would have gone red on the
    # runner, every time, until somebody added a 119 MiB fetch to the cheap job.
    for costly in check lint test test-slow check-release-build check-corpus; do
        grep -qx "$costly" <<<"$docs_arm" \
            && fail "$costly is still in the docs arm, which is the whole cost it exists to drop"
    done
    # The selftest beside it must stay, though: it builds its own fixture, so it
    # is the arm that would catch the collation bug in a prose-only commit.
    grep -qx check-corpus-selftest <<<"$docs_arm" \
        || fail "check-corpus-selftest left the docs arm, and it needs no corpus to run"

    # Every nested `gate-docs` and `gate-lane` call below asks git about a
    # scratch repository and not the real tree, because `gate` refuses a tree
    # with anything uncommitted or untracked (§415). The real tree is dirty
    # whenever this is run by hand mid-edit, and a failing proptest writes its
    # seed into it while the real gate runs, which would add this step to a
    # red gate's `also failed:` line for nothing.
    repo="$dir/repo"
    mkdir -p "$repo"
    export GIT_DIR="$repo/.git" GIT_WORK_TREE="$repo"
    git init -q -b main
    git -c user.name=selftest -c user.email=selftest@invalid commit -q --allow-empty -m 'a clean tree'

    # The filter itself. `list` is a real recipe that costs nothing; the word
    # beside it is not a recipe at all, which is the cheapest failing step there
    # is and needs no fixture of its own.
    got=$($j --set gate_steps 'list no-such-step' --set gate_metered_steps 'no-such-step' \
            --set gate_log "$dir/drop.log" gate-docs 2>&1) \
        || fail "the docs arm did not drop the step it was told to drop"
    grep -q '^gate-docs: dropped no-such-step$' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the docs arm dropped a step without saying which"

    # The same fixture with the lists swapped. A non-zero exit alone would prove
    # nothing here — dropping every step also exits non-zero — so this checks
    # which step was dropped and which one the summary blames.
    got=$($j --set gate_steps 'list no-such-step' --set gate_metered_steps 'list' \
            --set gate_log "$dir/keep.log" gate-docs 2>&1) \
        && fail "the docs arm kept a failing step and exited 0, so it is filtering nothing"
    grep -q '^gate-docs: dropped list$' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the docs arm dropped something other than the one step it was told to"
    grep -q '^gate: FAIL  first failure: no-such-step' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the step the docs arm was told to keep never ran"
    # The log it was given, not the default. The default is the log of the real
    # gate this selftest runs inside, and writing there empties it and deletes
    # the record a served gate is still holding (§337.8).
    grep -q '^gate: FAIL  first failure: no-such-step' "$dir/keep.log" \
        || fail "the docs arm wrote its log to the default path, which is the real gate's, instead of the gate_log it was given"

    # Every step excluded. Running no steps exits 0 through the loop in `gate`,
    # prints a passing summary block, and has checked nothing whatsoever.
    got=$($j --set gate_steps 'list' --set gate_metered_steps 'list' \
            --set gate_log "$dir/empty.log" gate-docs 2>&1) \
        && fail "an empty docs arm passed by checking nothing"
    grep -q 'no steps at all' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "an empty docs arm failed without saying that it had nothing to run"

    # `gate-lane` drops `test-slow` and nothing else. Any other line naming
    # `test-slow` fails, and not only its step line: the outer `--set` does not
    # reach the inner `just`, so the real `gate_reusable_steps` applies there,
    # and a filter that kept the step could serve it from the real store.
    held=gate-lane
    got=$($j --set gate_steps 'list test-slow' --set gate_log "$dir/lane.log" gate-lane 2>&1) \
        || fail "a lane gate of one passing step failed"
    grep -qx 'gate-lane: leaves out test-slow, which the land gate runs' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the lane gate did not say it left out test-slow"
    grep -q '^· list (' <<<"$got" || fail "the lane gate did not run the step it keeps"
    grep -v '^gate-lane: leaves out test-slow' <<<"$got" | grep -q 'test-slow' \
        && fail "the lane gate ran or served test-slow"
    # The word is passed on, so `gate` refuses a misspelt one.
    got=$($j --set gate_steps 'list test-slow' --set gate_log "$dir/lane.log" gate-lane quite 2>&1) \
        && fail "a lane gate given 'quite' ran"
    grep -q "not 'quite'" <<<"$got" || fail "a lane gate given 'quite' was refused without gate's own word"

    # `GIT_DIR` wins over `-C`, and the section below runs `git -C "$repo"`
    # against a second, unrelated repository of its own, and so does
    # `check-gate-lane-callers` itself, through its own `git -C dir grep`. Left
    # exported, every one of those calls would read the scratch repository
    # above instead, and this section would test nothing.
    unset GIT_DIR GIT_WORK_TREE

    # `check-gate-lane-callers`, in a repository built here.
    held=check-gate-lane-callers
    repo="$dir/callers"
    mkdir -p "$repo/.claude/agents" "$repo/tooling/verify-lane"
    git init -q "$repo"
    printf 'gate-lane:\n    echo gate-lane\n' >"$repo/justfile"
    printf 'Gate with `just pre-gate gate-lane`.\n' >"$repo/.claude/agents/lane.md"
    printf 'run(JUST, "gate-lane")\n' >"$repo/tooling/verify-lane/verify_lane.py"
    printf 'run: just check-gate-lane-callers\n' >"$repo/ci.yml"
    git -C "$repo" add -A
    got=$($j check-gate-lane-callers "$repo" 2>&1) \
        || fail "it refused the three callers, or took check-gate-lane-callers for a call"
    printf 'run(JUST, "gate-lane")\n' >"$repo/land.py"
    git -C "$repo" add -A
    got=$($j check-gate-lane-callers "$repo" 2>&1) && fail "a fifth file calling gate-lane passed"
    grep -q '^land.py names gate-lane' <<<"$got" || fail "a fifth file calling gate-lane was refused without its name"
    git -C "$repo" rm -q -f land.py
    printf 'land:\n    just gate-lane\n' >>"$repo/justfile"
    git -C "$repo" add -A
    got=$($j check-gate-lane-callers "$repo" 2>&1) && fail "a justfile recipe calling gate-lane passed"
    grep -q 'justfile:4, in land: ' <<<"$got" || fail "a justfile recipe calling gate-lane was refused without its line"
    printf 'gate-lane:\n    echo gate-lane\n[doc("not gate-lane")]\nland:\n    # just gate-lane\nland-selftest:\n    just gate-lane\n' \
        >"$repo/justfile"
    git -C "$repo" add -A
    got=$($j check-gate-lane-callers "$repo" 2>&1) \
        || fail "a comment, an attribute or a selftest naming gate-lane was refused"

    # `test` and `test-slow`, with a stub `cargo` whose `nextest list` prints a
    # fixture as what the filter selected, and which logs every other call. A
    # bench binary's id holds a `/`, so the split must be at the last one.
    held='test and test-slow'
    mkdir -p "$dir/bin"
    printf '%s\n' '#!/usr/bin/env bash' \
        'if [ "$1 $2" = "nextest list" ]; then cat "$STUB_LISTED"; else echo "cargo $*" >>"$STUB_LOG"; fi' \
        >"$dir/bin/cargo"
    chmod +x "$dir/bin/cargo"
    stub() {
        : >"$dir/cargo.log"
        PATH="$dir/bin:$PATH" STUB_LISTED="$dir/listed" STUB_LOG="$dir/cargo.log" "$j" --set slow_tests "$@" 2>&1
    }
    printf '%s\n' 'rcad-x::a t1' 'rcad-x::bench/b t2' >"$dir/listed"
    filter='(binary_id(=rcad-x::a) & test(=t1)) | (binary_id(=rcad-x::bench/b) & test(=t2))'
    got=$(stub 'rcad-x::a/t1 rcad-x::bench/b/t2' test) || fail "a slow tier whose names each select one test failed"
    [ "$(cat "$dir/cargo.log")" = "cargo nextest run --workspace -E not ($filter)"$'\n'"cargo test --workspace --doc" ] \
        || fail "test did not run every test but the slow tier, then the doc tests: $(cat "$dir/cargo.log")"
    got=$(stub 'rcad-x::a/t1 rcad-x::bench/b/t2' test-slow) || fail "test-slow failed"
    [ "$(cat "$dir/cargo.log")" = "cargo nextest run --workspace --no-tests fail -E $filter" ] \
        || fail "test-slow did not run only the slow tier: $(cat "$dir/cargo.log")"
    printf '%s\n' 'rcad-x::a t1' >"$dir/listed"
    got=$(stub 'rcad-x::a/t1 rcad-x::a/gone rcad-x::a/t1' test) && fail "a name that selects nothing and a name written twice passed"
    grep -qx 'slow_tests names 3 tests and selects 1:' <<<"$got" || fail "the count did not say what it counted"
    grep -qx '  rcad-x::a/gone selects no test' <<<"$got" || fail "a name that selects nothing was not named"
    grep -qx '  named twice: rcad-x::a/t1' <<<"$got" || fail "a name written twice was not named"
    [ ! -s "$dir/cargo.log" ] || fail "test ran tests after its count failed: $(cat "$dir/cargo.log")"
    got=$(stub 'rcad-x::a' test-slow) && fail "a name with no binary passed"
    grep -q "names 'rcad-x::a', which is not binary_id/test_name" <<<"$got" || fail "a name with no binary was refused without its name"
    [ ! -s "$dir/cargo.log" ] || fail "test-slow ran with a name it could not split: $(cat "$dir/cargo.log")"

# Holds the verdict cache to what §337 says it does, in a scratch repository.
#
# `GIT_DIR` and `GIT_WORK_TREE` point every git call at the scratch repository,
# while `just` still reads this justfile. The store is kept in that repository's
# git dir, so this never touches the real one. The gate is driven with `--set`,
# as in `check-gate-summary-selftest`: `list` is the step that passes and
# `no-such-step` the one that fails.
#
# The commit dates are fixed, so every sha here is the same on every run. The
# audit is chosen by HEAD's first hex digit. With real dates, a scratch commit
# starting with `0` would turn a serving case into an audit one run in sixteen.
[private]
check-gate-verdicts-selftest:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    dir=$(mktemp -d)
    trap 'rm -rf "$dir"' EXIT
    j='{{ just_executable() }}'
    got=""
    drifted='the verdict cache drifted from what docs/decisions.md §337 says'
    fail() {
        echo "$drifted: $1"
        [ -z "$got" ] || { echo "--- got ---"; echo "$got"; }
        exit 1
    }

    # The lists, before anything is driven. A name that is not a step is never
    # served and says so nowhere, which is how a rename leaves this list stale.
    all=$(printf '%s\n' {{ gate_steps }})
    reusable=$(printf '%s\n' {{ gate_reusable_steps }})
    [ "$(grep -c . <<<"$reusable")" -ge 5 ] || fail "gate_reusable_steps came back empty"
    while read -r step; do
        grep -qx "$step" <<<"$all" || fail "gate_reusable_steps names '$step', which is not a step of the gate"
    done <<<"$reusable"
    # These read prose, or something outside the tree that no fingerprint of
    # it can see, which is what the traces behind §337.2 and §369 found.
    for never in check-plugins check-hooks check-hub check-corpus check-systemd-units deny \
                 check-decisions check-plan check-queue check-where-the-work-is \
                 check-skills check-lanes-workflow check-questions-selftest \
                 check-queue-merge-driver check-gate-lane-callers; do
        grep -qx "$never" <<<"$reusable" \
            && fail "$never reads prose or something outside the tree, and gate_reusable_steps would serve it"
    done
    # The fingerprint leaves out what CI's docs split leaves out, for the same
    # reason. If one of the two claims changes, the other has to change with it.
    wf='{{ justfile_directory() }}/.github/workflows'
    [ "$(grep -cxF "    paths-ignore: ['docs/**', '**.md']" "$wf/ci.yml")" = 2 ] \
        || fail "ci.yml no longer sends exactly docs/** and **.md to the docs arm, and _code-fingerprint still leaves exactly those out"
    [ "$(grep -cxF "    paths: ['docs/**', '**.md']" "$wf/ci-docs.yml")" = 2 ] \
        || fail "ci-docs.yml no longer takes exactly docs/** and **.md, and _code-fingerprint still leaves exactly those out"

    repo="$dir/repo"
    store="$repo/.git/rcad-verdicts"
    mkdir -p "$repo/crates" "$repo/docs"
    export GIT_DIR="$repo/.git" GIT_WORK_TREE="$repo"
    export GIT_AUTHOR_DATE='2026-01-01T00:00:00Z' GIT_COMMITTER_DATE='2026-01-01T00:00:00Z'
    unset RCAD_SOAK_SEED
    g() { git -c user.name=selftest -c user.email=selftest@invalid "$@"; }
    fingerprint() { "$j" _code-fingerprint; }
    gate() {
        "$j" --set gate_steps "$1" --set gate_reusable_steps "$2" \
            --set gate_log "$dir/gate.log" gate quiet "${@:3}" 2>&1
    }
    g init -q -b main
    printf 'fn main() {}\n' >"$repo/crates/a.rs"
    printf 'notes\n' >"$repo/docs/notes.txt"
    printf '# a readme\n' >"$repo/README.md"
    g add -A
    g commit -q -m 'the code'
    base=$(g rev-parse HEAD)
    [[ "$base" != 0* ]] || fail "the scratch commit starts with 0, so every serving case below is an audit"
    f0=$(fingerprint)
    [[ "$f0" =~ ^[0-9a-f]{64}$ ]] || fail "a clean tree had no fingerprint: $f0"

    # Prose moves nothing: a file under docs/, and a .md anywhere.
    printf 'more\n' >>"$repo/docs/notes.txt"
    printf 'more\n' >>"$repo/README.md"
    mkdir -p "$repo/crates/x"
    printf 'notes\n' >"$repo/crates/x/NOTES.md"
    g add -A
    g commit -q -m prose
    prose=$(g rev-parse HEAD)
    [ "$(fingerprint)" = "$f0" ] || fail "a commit touching only docs/ and .md files changed the fingerprint"
    g reset -q --hard "$base"

    # Code moves it, including the two paths a loose pattern would call prose:
    # a root file whose name starts with `docs`, and a `docs/` below the root.
    for path in crates/a.rs docs.rs crates/docs/b.rs; do
        mkdir -p "$repo/$(dirname "$path")"
        printf '// moved\n' >>"$repo/$path"
        g add -A
        g commit -q -m "$path"
        [ "$(fingerprint)" != "$f0" ] || fail "a commit touching $path left the fingerprint where it was"
        g reset -q --hard "$base"
    done

    # A seed the suite reads moves it. Where the corpus lives does not, since
    # what it holds is pinned in the tree and checked on every gate.
    [ "$(RCAD_SOAK_SEED=7 fingerprint)" != "$f0" ] \
        || fail "RCAD_SOAK_SEED changes what the suite draws, and the fingerprint did not move"
    [ "$(RCAD_STEP_CORPUS=/elsewhere fingerprint)" = "$f0" ] \
        || fail "moving RCAD_STEP_CORPUS changed the fingerprint, so no two machines could share a verdict"

    # The first gate of this code runs its step and records a green.
    got=$(gate list list) || fail "a gate of one passing step failed"
    grep -q '^· list (' <<<"$got" || fail "the first gate of this code did not run its step"
    [ -f "$store/$f0" ] || fail "a passing gate that ran every reusable step recorded nothing"
    grep -qx "commit $base" "$store/$f0" || fail "the record does not name the commit that was gated"

    # The second is served: the step does not run, and the summary says whose
    # counts it is showing.
    got=$(gate list list) || fail "a served gate failed"
    grep -q "^· served: list from ${base:0:8}'s gate at " <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a gate of code that was already green did not serve it"
    grep -q '^· list (' <<<"$got" && fail "a served step ran anyway"
    grep -qx 'gate: PASS' <<<"$got" || fail "a served gate did not say it passed"
    grep -qx 'tests: not reached' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a served gate whose record holds no counts did not say no tests were reached"

    # A commit on top that touches only prose is the case all of this is for.
    g reset -q --hard "$prose"
    [[ "$prose" != 0* ]] || fail "the prose commit starts with 0, so this case would be an audit"
    got=$(gate list list) || fail "a served gate of a prose commit failed"
    grep -q "^· served: list from ${base:0:8}'s gate" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a commit that touched only docs/ was not served the green of the code under it"
    g reset -q --hard "$base"

    # A step that fails but is not in the reusable list says nothing about the
    # verdict, and the record stays. `fresh`, because a served run records
    # nothing and reports nothing, and would pass this case without reaching it.
    got=$(gate 'list no-such-step' list fresh) && fail "a gate with a failing step exited 0"
    grep -q '^· list (' <<<"$got" || fail "fresh served a step"
    grep -q 'verdict violated' <<<"$got" && fail "a step outside the reusable list was taken as a violation"
    [ -f "$store/$f0" ] || fail "a failing step outside the reusable list deleted the record"

    # `fresh` runs everything, and its pass writes the record again.
    got=$(gate list list fresh) || fail "a fresh gate failed"
    grep -q '^· list (' <<<"$got" || fail "fresh served a step"
    grep -q '^· verdicts: fresh' <<<"$got" || fail "fresh did not say why it ran a step it had a green for"

    # A tree with anything uncommitted or untracked is refused before any step
    # starts (§415). An untracked file is not in HEAD and gets compiled all the
    # same, and a changed or staged file is not what HEAD names either. An
    # ignored file is no part of the code, and the gate runs.
    refused() {
        local rc=0
        got=$("${@:2}" 2>&1) || rc=$?
        [ "$rc" -eq 2 ] || fail "a gate of a tree with $1 exited $rc, where a refusal exits 2"
        grep -q '^just gate refuses a tree with uncommitted or untracked files' <<<"$got" \
            || fail "a gate of a tree with $1 did not say why it was refused"
        grep -q '^commit first — a WIP commit is enough' <<<"$got" \
            || fail "the refusal of a tree with $1 did not say what to do"
        grep -q '^· ' <<<"$got" && fail "a gate of a tree with $1 started a step"
        grep -q '=== gate summary ===' <<<"$got" && fail "a refused gate printed a summary of steps it never ran"
        return 0
    }
    got=$(gate list list) || fail "a gate of a clean tree failed"
    grep -q 'refuses' <<<"$got" && fail "a clean tree was refused"
    grep -qx 'gate: PASS' <<<"$got" || fail "a gate of a clean tree did not pass"
    printf 'fn new() {}\n' >"$repo/crates/new.rs"
    refused "an untracked .rs file" gate list list
    grep -qx '?? crates/new.rs' <<<"$got" || fail "the refusal did not name the untracked file"
    refused "an untracked file that git config hides" \
        env GIT_CONFIG_COUNT=1 GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0=status.showUntrackedFiles GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0=no \
        "$j" --set gate_steps list --set gate_reusable_steps list --set gate_log "$dir/gate.log" gate quiet
    grep -qx '?? crates/new.rs' <<<"$got" || fail "a refusal under status.showUntrackedFiles=no did not name the untracked file"
    hidden=$(GIT_CONFIG_COUNT=1 GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0=status.showUntrackedFiles GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0=no fingerprint)
    [[ "$hidden" =~ ^[0-9a-f]{64}$ ]] && fail "a tree with an untracked file that git config hides got a fingerprint"
    g add crates/new.rs
    refused "a staged new file" gate list list
    grep -qx 'A  crates/new.rs' <<<"$got" || fail "the refusal did not name the staged file"
    g rm -q --cached crates/new.rs
    rm "$repo/crates/new.rs"
    printf '// changed\n' >>"$repo/crates/a.rs"
    refused "a changed tracked file" gate list list
    grep -qx ' M crates/a.rs' <<<"$got" || fail "the refusal did not name the changed file"
    g checkout -q -- crates/a.rs
    mkdir -p "$GIT_DIR/info"
    printf '/target/\n' >>"$GIT_DIR/info/exclude"
    mkdir -p "$repo/target"
    printf 'fn built() {}\n' >"$repo/target/built.rs"
    got=$(gate list list) || fail "a gate of a tree whose only extra file is ignored failed"
    grep -q 'refuses' <<<"$got" && fail "a tree whose only extra file is ignored was refused"
    grep -q "^· served: list from ${base:0:8}'s gate" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "an ignored file kept a tree from being served the green of its code"
    rm -r "$repo/target"

    # A day is the whole of what is served. Every repeat measured came within
    # it (§337.1).
    old=$(date -u -d '2 days ago' +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)
    sed -i "s/^date .*/date $old/" "$store/$f0"
    got=$(gate list list) || fail "a gate over an expired record failed"
    grep -q '^· list (' <<<"$got" || fail "a record two days old was served"
    grep -q 'not the last day' <<<"$got" || fail "an expired record did not say why it was not served"
    grep -qx "date $old" "$store/$f0" && fail "a pass over an expired record did not write it again"

    # The audit. Amending the message keeps the code and moves the sha, so this
    # walks to a sha starting with 0 on the same fingerprint.
    n=0
    while [[ "$(g rev-parse HEAD)" != 0* ]]; do
        n=$((n + 1))
        [ "$n" -le 400 ] || fail "400 amended commits found no sha starting with 0"
        g commit -q --amend -m "audit $n"
    done
    [ "$(fingerprint)" = "$f0" ] || fail "amending the message moved the fingerprint"
    got=$(gate list list) || fail "an audited gate failed"
    grep -q '^· list (' <<<"$got" || fail "a commit whose sha starts with 0 was served"
    grep -q '^· verdicts: audit' <<<"$got" || fail "an audit did not say it was one"
    g reset -q --hard "$base"

    # A violation: a step the record says passed fails on the same code. No
    # step passes and fails here, so the record is told it passed.
    sed -i 's/^steps .*/steps no-such-step/' "$store/$f0"
    got=$(gate no-such-step no-such-step fresh) && fail "a gate whose only step fails exited 0"
    grep -q '^!!! verdict violated: no-such-step failed' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a reusable step failed on code with a green on record, and nothing said so"
    [ -f "$store/$f0" ] && fail "a violated record was kept, so the next gate would serve it"
    grep -q " $f0 no-such-step $base " "$repo/.git/rcad-verdict-violations" \
        || fail "the violation was not written down"
    [ "$(tail -6 <<<"$got" | grep -c '=== gate summary ===')" -eq 1 ] \
        || fail "the violation pushed the summary block off the end"

    # A failure stops no step (§372.9), so a run can pass every reusable step
    # and still fail. It records nothing.
    got=$(gate 'no-such-other-step list' list) && fail "a gate with a failing step exited 0"
    [ -f "$store/$f0" ] && fail "a failing run recorded a green because every reusable step in it passed"
    # And a step the record names that fails after another failure is still a
    # violation.
    got=$(gate list list) || fail "a gate of one passing step failed"
    [ -f "$store/$f0" ] || fail "a passing gate that ran every reusable step recorded nothing"
    sed -i 's/^steps .*/steps no-such-step/' "$store/$f0"
    got=$(gate 'no-such-other-step no-such-step' no-such-step fresh) && fail "a gate with two failing steps exited 0"
    grep -q '^!!! verdict violated: no-such-step failed' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a step the record names failed after a step it does not, and the violation went unsaid"
    [ -f "$store/$f0" ] && fail "a violated record was kept, so the next gate would serve it"

    # A run that leaves a reusable step out records the steps it ran, as a lane's
    # gate leaves out the slow tier (§397.4). Each fixture step prints a nextest
    # summary, so the counts can be followed into the record and out of it.
    fast=_gate-selftest-tests
    slow=_gate-selftest-slow-tests
    fast_count='tests: Summary [   0.100s] 3 tests run: 3 passed, 0 skipped'
    slow_count='tests: Summary [   0.200s] 2 tests run: 2 passed, 0 skipped'
    got=$(gate "$fast" "$fast $slow") || fail "a gate of one passing step failed"
    grep -qx "steps $fast" "$store/$f0" || fail "a run that left out a reusable step did not record the one it ran"
    grep -qxF "$fast_count" "$store/$f0" || fail "a record does not hold the counts of the run it records"

    # A failure of a step the record does not name is a failure, not a
    # violation: a land gate's slow tier failing over a lane's record.
    got=$(gate "$fast no-such-step" "$fast no-such-step") && fail "a gate with a failing step exited 0"
    grep -q 'verdict violated' <<<"$got" && fail "a step the record never passed was taken as a violation"
    grep -qx "steps $fast" "$store/$f0" || fail "a failure of a step the record does not name changed or deleted the record"

    # The whole list is served what the record names and runs the rest. Its
    # pass adds to the record and keeps its date: a served verdict gets no new
    # day.
    hour_ago=$(date -u -d '1 hour ago' +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)
    sed -i "s/^date .*/date $hour_ago/" "$store/$f0"
    got=$(gate "$fast $slow" "$fast $slow") || fail "a partly served gate failed"
    grep -q "^· served: $fast from ${base:0:8}'s gate at " <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a gate was not served the step its record names"
    grep -q "^· $fast (" <<<"$got" && fail "a step the record names ran anyway"
    grep -q "^· $slow (" <<<"$got" || fail "a step the record does not name was not run"
    grep -qxF "$fast_count (served from ${base:0:8})" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a partly served summary did not show the record's counts as served"
    grep -qxF "$slow_count" <<<"$got" || fail "a partly served summary did not show its own counts"
    grep -qx 'tests: not reached' <<<"$got" && fail "a summary with counts said no tests were reached"
    grep -q "^· verdicts: $slow added to this code's green" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a partly served pass did not say it added to the record"
    grep -qx "date $hour_ago" "$store/$f0" || fail "a partly served pass gave the record a new date"
    grep -qx "steps $fast $slow" "$store/$f0" || fail "the record does not name both runs' steps"
    grep -qxF "$fast_count" "$store/$f0" && grep -qxF "$slow_count" "$store/$f0" \
        || fail "the record does not hold both runs' counts"
    grep -q 'served from' "$store/$f0" && fail "the record took a count with its served suffix"

    # A run served every step learned nothing, and leaves the record alone.
    cp "$store/$f0" "$dir/before"
    got=$(gate "$fast $slow" "$fast $slow") || fail "a served gate failed"
    grep -q "^· served: $fast $slow from " <<<"$got" || fail "a gate was not served both steps its record names"
    cmp -s "$dir/before" "$store/$f0" || fail "a gate served every step wrote the record again"

    # A record from before §397.4 names no steps, so nothing in it is served.
    sed -i '/^steps /d' "$store/$f0"
    got=$(gate "$fast $slow" "$fast $slow") || fail "a gate over a record without steps failed"
    grep -q 'cannot be read, so everything runs' <<<"$got" || fail "a record without steps did not say why nothing was served"
    grep -q "^· $fast (" <<<"$got" && grep -q "^· $slow (" <<<"$got" || fail "a record without steps served a step"

    # The summary of a served run, from a record holding the counts a real
    # gate writes.
    printf 'commit %s\ndate 2026-01-01T00:00:00Z\nhost h\n' "$base" >"$dir/record"
    printf 'tests: Summary [  94.982s] 2672 tests run: 2672 passed\n' >>"$dir/record"
    printf 'doc tests: 25 passed, 0 failed, across 9 crate(s)\n' >>"$dir/record"
    : >"$dir/empty.log"
    got=$("$j" _gate-summary "$dir/empty.log" 0 "" "$dir/record")
    grep -qxF "tests: Summary [  94.982s] 2672 tests run: 2672 passed (served from ${base:0:8})" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a served summary did not carry the counts of the gate that served it"
    grep -qxF "doc tests: 25 passed, 0 failed, across 9 crate(s) (served from ${base:0:8})" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a served summary did not carry the doc tests of the gate that served it"
    grep -q 'not reached' <<<"$got" && fail "a served summary said the tests were not reached"
    got=$("$j" _gate-summary "$dir/empty.log" 0 "" "$dir/gone" 2>&1)
    grep -qxF "tests: served, but the record it was served from is gone: $dir/gone" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a served summary whose record had gone said nothing about the counts"

    # A partly served run: the record's counts, then its own.
    printf '\e[1m     Summary\e[0m [  21.004s] 30 tests run: 30 passed, 0 skipped\n' >"$dir/own.log"
    got=$("$j" _gate-summary "$dir/own.log" 0 "" "$dir/record")
    want=$(printf '%s\n' "tests: Summary [  94.982s] 2672 tests run: 2672 passed (served from ${base:0:8})" \
        "doc tests: 25 passed, 0 failed, across 9 crate(s) (served from ${base:0:8})" \
        'tests: Summary [  21.004s] 30 tests run: 30 passed, 0 skipped')
    [ "$(grep -E '^(tests|doc tests): ' <<<"$got")" = "$want" ] \
        || fail "a partly served summary did not print the record's counts and then its own"
    got=$("$j" _gate-summary "$dir/own.log" 0 "" "$dir/gone" 2>&1)
    grep -qxF 'tests: Summary [  21.004s] 30 tests run: 30 passed, 0 skipped' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a summary whose record had gone dropped its own counts"
    grep -q 'not reached' <<<"$got" && fail "a summary whose record had gone said the tests were not reached"

    # A clean tree can still change while its gate runs, and then no sha names
    # what the gate read, so its pass is not recorded. This is the path that
    # still reaches `_code-fingerprint`'s sentence for a dirty tree (§415). The
    # file is written once the two-second step has started.
    rm -f "$store/$f0"
    rm -rf "$dir/gate.log.steps"
    gate _gate-selftest-sleep _gate-selftest-sleep >"$dir/during" &
    during=$!
    for _ in $(seq 100); do
        [ ! -e "$dir/gate.log.steps/0-_gate-selftest-sleep.log" ] || break
        sleep 0.1
    done
    [ -e "$dir/gate.log.steps/0-_gate-selftest-sleep.log" ] || fail "the gate never started its step"
    printf 'fn late() {}\n' >"$repo/crates/late.rs"
    wait "$during" || { got=$(cat "$dir/during"); fail "a gate whose tree changed while it ran failed"; }
    got=$(cat "$dir/during")
    grep -qx '· verdicts: not recorded, because the tree changed while the gate ran' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a pass whose tree changed while it ran did not say why it recorded nothing"
    [ -f "$store/$f0" ] && fail "a pass whose tree changed while it ran recorded a green"
    rm "$repo/crates/late.rs"

    # A `/clear` stops the command every agent has running, and a gate is the
    # command it stops most (§429.1). So the gate runs its steps in a run begun
    # with a double fork, and the call only follows it: stopping the call stops
    # nothing else, and the same command run again follows the run rather than
    # starting another (§429.8). The follower is stopped as §429.2 says Claude
    # Code stops a command, its process group and then every descendant, found
    # before the first signal and not after it: found after it, even a run that
    # was the follower's own child has been handed to init, and the arm passes
    # on the code it is meant to fail on (§429.7).
    drifted='a gate whose call was stopped drifted from what docs/decisions.md §429.8 says'
    descendants_of() {
        local root=$1
        ps -A -o pid=,ppid= | awk -v root="$root" '
            { parent[$1] = $2 }
            END {
                for (pid in parent) {
                    cur = pid
                    for (i = 0; i < 1000 && (cur in parent); i++) {
                        if (parent[cur] == root) { print pid; break }
                        cur = parent[cur]
                    }
                }
            }'
    }
    stop_like_a_clear() {
        local group=$1 pid before
        before=$(descendants_of "$group")
        kill -TERM -- "-$group" 2>/dev/null || true
        for pid in $before; do
            kill -TERM "$pid" 2>/dev/null || true
        done
        sleep 0.2
        kill -KILL -- "-$group" 2>/dev/null || true
        for pid in $before; do
            kill -KILL "$pid" 2>/dev/null || true
        done
    }
    within_ten_seconds() {
        for _ in $(seq 100); do
            ! eval "$1" || return 0
            sleep 0.1
        done
        return 1
    }
    gone() {
        local stat
        read -r stat 2>/dev/null <"/proc/$1/stat" || return 0
        stat=${stat##*) }
        [ "${stat:0:1}" = Z ]
    }
    # `_gate-selftest-sleep` adds a line to `$hold.ran` each time a run starts
    # it, and waits while `$hold` is there.
    hold="$dir/hold"
    run="$dir/gate.log.run"
    export GATE_SELFTEST_HOLD="$hold"
    # In a group of its own, as Claude Code starts a command, so that it can be
    # stopped as Claude Code stops one. With SIGINT as a terminal leaves it: a
    # background job starts with SIGINT ignored, and bash cannot trap a signal
    # that was ignored when it started.
    started_gate() {
        env --default-signal=INT setsid "$j" --set gate_steps _gate-selftest-sleep --set gate_reusable_steps '' \
            --set gate_log "$dir/gate.log" gate quiet >"$1" 2>&1 &
    }

    touch "$hold"
    started_gate "$dir/stopped"
    follower=$!
    within_ten_seconds '[ -e "$hold.ran" ]' || fail "the gate never started its step"
    stop_like_a_clear "$follower"
    wait "$follower" 2>/dev/null || true
    "$j" --set gate_steps _gate-selftest-sleep --set gate_reusable_steps '' \
        --set gate_log "$dir/gate.log" gate quiet >"$dir/again" 2>&1 &
    again=$!
    within_ten_seconds 'grep -q "^· following " "$dir/again"' \
        || { got=$(cat "$dir/again"); fail "the same gate run again on the same commit did not follow the run whose call was stopped"; }

    # Another gate beside it refuses and names the one running: other steps, as
    # a lane's gate is beside a whole one, and the same steps with `fresh`.
    rc=0
    got=$(gate list '') || rc=$?
    [ "$rc" -eq 2 ] || fail "a gate of other steps exited $rc beside a running gate, where a refusal exits 2"
    grep -q '^just gate will not start beside the gate already running in this tree' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a gate of other steps beside a running one did not say why it would not start"
    grep -qx "  commit $base" <<<"$got" || fail "the refusal did not name the running gate's commit"
    grep -qx '  steps _gate-selftest-sleep' <<<"$got" || fail "the refusal did not name the running gate's steps"
    grep -q '^· ' <<<"$got" && fail "a refused gate started a step"
    rc=0
    got=$(gate _gate-selftest-sleep '' fresh) || rc=$?
    [ "$rc" -eq 2 ] || fail "a fresh gate exited $rc beside a gate of the same steps that was not, where a refusal exits 2"
    grep -qx '  verdicts reuse' <<<"$got" || fail "the refusal of a fresh gate did not say the running one serves"

    rm "$hold"
    rc=0
    wait "$again" || rc=$?
    got=$(cat "$dir/again")
    [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] || fail "a gate that followed a passing run exited $rc"
    grep -qE '^· _gate-selftest-sleep \([0-9]+\.[0-9] s\)$' <<<"$got" || fail "a gate that followed a run did not print its step's line"
    grep -qx 'gate: PASS' <<<"$got" || fail "a gate that followed a passing run did not say it passed"
    [ "$(tail -6 <<<"$got" | grep -c '=== gate summary ===')" -eq 1 ] \
        || fail "a gate that followed a run did not end on its summary block"
    [ "$(grep -c . "$hold.ran")" -eq 1 ] \
        || fail "the step started $(grep -c . "$hold.ran") times, so the run whose call was stopped did not finish and another started"

    # A run that dies without writing its exit code, killed outright here, is
    # not followed forever: the next gate says it ended without finishing and
    # fails, and the one after that starts a new run.
    rm "$hold.ran"
    touch "$hold"
    started_gate "$dir/stopped"
    follower=$!
    within_ten_seconds '[ -e "$hold.ran" ]' || fail "the gate never started its step"
    stop_like_a_clear "$follower"
    wait "$follower" 2>/dev/null || true
    kill -KILL -- "-$(cat "$run/pid")"
    rm "$hold"
    rc=0
    got=$(gate _gate-selftest-sleep '') || rc=$?
    [ "$rc" -ne 0 ] || fail "a gate exited 0 on a run that was killed before it finished"
    grep -q "^just gate: the run (pid [0-9]*) ended without finishing; " <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a gate whose run was killed did not say it ended without finishing"
    rc=0
    got=$(gate _gate-selftest-sleep '') || rc=$?
    [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] || fail "the gate after the one that found a dead run exited $rc"
    grep -q '^· following ' <<<"$got" && fail "the gate after the one that found a dead run followed it again"
    grep -qx 'gate: PASS' <<<"$got" || fail "the gate after the one that found a dead run did not pass"
    [ "$(grep -c . "$hold.ran")" -eq 2 ] || fail "the gate after the one that found a dead run did not run the step itself"

    # A Ctrl-C in a terminal reaches the follower's group, and the follower
    # passes it on to the run, so it stops the steps as it did when they were
    # the follower's own. Stopped on purpose, the run is not reported as one
    # that ended without finishing.
    rm "$hold.ran"
    touch "$hold"
    started_gate "$dir/interrupted"
    follower=$!
    within_ten_seconds '[ -e "$hold.ran" ]' || fail "the gate never started its step"
    pid=$(cat "$run/pid")
    kill -INT -- "-$follower"
    rc=0
    wait "$follower" || rc=$?
    [ "$rc" -ne 0 ] || { got=$(cat "$dir/interrupted"); fail "a gate stopped with a Ctrl-C exited 0"; }
    within_ten_seconds 'gone "$pid"' || fail "a Ctrl-C to the gate's call left its run going"
    rm "$hold"
    rc=0
    got=$(gate _gate-selftest-sleep '') || rc=$?
    [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] || fail "the gate after one stopped with a Ctrl-C exited $rc"
    grep -q 'ended without finishing' <<<"$got" && fail "a run stopped with a Ctrl-C was reported as one that died"
    echo "a gate whose call is stopped as a /clear stops it finishes, and the same gate follows it to its summary;" \
        "another gate beside it refuses; a run killed outright is reported once as ended without finishing," \
        "and the gate after that starts a new run; a Ctrl-C stops the run"
    exit 0

# Two gate steps that print the line a nextest run ends with, one for 3 tests
# and one for 2, so `check-gate-verdicts-selftest` can follow each step's counts
# through a partly served gate.
[private]
_gate-selftest-tests:
    @echo '     Summary [   0.100s] 3 tests run: 3 passed, 0 skipped'

[private]
_gate-selftest-slow-tests:
    @echo '     Summary [   0.200s] 2 tests run: 2 passed, 0 skipped'

# The STEP corpus lives outside the tree, because it is other people's CAD files
# and this repo has no business carrying them. Seven test files in `rcad-step`
# read this variable and **fail** rather than skip when the corpus is not there
# (§44.10): a green has to mean the corpus was read.
#
# Because this file defaults the variable, nothing that runs through `just` ever
# reaches those tests' "not set" panic — it trips their `root.is_dir()` assertion
# one line later, naming a path that looks plausible and is not there. A bare
# `cargo nextest run` gets the panic instead. Both are failures, which is the
# point; they just name different things, and the plausible path is the one that
# wastes an afternoon.
#
# Derived rather than hardcoded so that every worktree gets the same answer:
# `--git-common-dir` points at the *main* repo's `.git` from inside a worktree,
# so two `parent_directory` calls land beside the repo wherever it is run from.
# Before this, lanes exported it by hand — 412 times on 2026-09-05 — and the
# ones that forgot got four silent skips rather than a failure.
export RCAD_STEP_CORPUS := env_var_or_default("RCAD_STEP_CORPUS", parent_directory(parent_directory(`git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir`)) / "rcad-step-corpus")

[doc("Type-check the whole workspace, tests and benches included.")]
check:
    cargo check --workspace --all-targets

# The fuzz targets compile, which `check` above cannot tell you.
#
# `fuzz/Cargo.toml` declares its own empty `[workspace]`, deliberately: building
# a target needs a nightly compiler and a sanitizer, and `rust-toolchain.toml`
# pins stable. The cost of that was silence. `no_step_file_makes_the_reader_panic.rs`
# stopped compiling when §86 made `Model::solid` return a `Result`, and nothing
# said so until a night of fuzzing had already been spent — the §77.6 shape, a
# check passing for a property of the environment it ran in.
#
# **Type-checking needs none of that.** The nightly compiler and the sanitizer
# are for *linking* an instrumented binary; `cargo check` on stable reads the
# same four targets and finds the same break, in six seconds. So the gap was
# never that this could not be checked, only that nothing checked it.
#
# Run from `fuzz/` because it is its own workspace; `--all-targets` to reach the
# `fuzz_targets/` binaries rather than just the empty lib.
[doc("The four fuzz targets still compile, which the workspace check cannot see.")]
check-fuzz-builds:
    cd fuzz && cargo check --all-targets

# Every public enum either grows or says why it does not (docs/decisions.md §40).
#
# `#[non_exhaustive]`, or a doc comment saying "Exhaustive on purpose" — which
# `Orientation` is, and is the only one (§3.6). A new public enum written
# without either compiles, passes every test, and nothing else would notice.
#
# A `pub enum` is judged against what stands between it and the blank line above
# it, which is where rustfmt puts the item's own attributes and doc comment.
# How many wildcard match arms stand over one of our own enums, or over an
# array, a tuple, or a reference of them (docs/decisions.md §40). Raise or
# lower it only with the commit that does.
#
# Two mechanisms sit behind this one number, because they see different
# things. `clippy::wildcard_enum_match_arm` asks only what the scrutinee's
# own type is, so a match over `Surface` is seen and a match over
# `[Surface; 2]` or `(&Surface, &Surface)` is not — the scrutinee there is an
# array or a tuple, and clippy asks nothing about what is nested inside one.
# Two real defects hid in exactly that gap this milestone: a panic reachable
# from a real KiCad file through `Import::read`, and a fourth list of
# surface pairs that had drifted out of step with the other three. Both
# matched over a composite rather than over one enum.
#
# `crates/rcad-step/tools/composite_wildcards.awk` is the second mechanism: a
# line-based scan for an arm that names one of our enum's variants inside a
# `[`, a `(`, or a `&`, standing in the same match as a bare `_`. It counts how
# many of that enum's own variants the match never names anywhere in its arms
# — not the match itself — so a fully-named composite match, the same as a
# fully-named single-enum one, counts zero. The proper mechanism for this is
# rustc's own `non_exhaustive_omitted_patterns`, unstable on the 1.96 this repo
# pins; the script's own header comment says so first.
#
# `check-wildcards-selftest`, below, pins two small fixtures the scan must
# catch and two it must not, against a made-up enum rather than one of ours —
# so the scan keeps being proven even on a tree where nothing real trips it.
#
# A count and not a denial: rustc resolves a lint level at the enclosing
# expression, so `#[allow]` on a match arm never reaches it and the only
# granularity clippy leaves is the whole function; the text scan has no
# per-site silencing at all.
#
# The number moves for three reasons and all three want looking at. A new
# wildcard raises it, of either kind. So does a new *variant*, because a site
# that names every variant there is today starts matching a known one
# tomorrow — which is the whole point of naming them. That used to be true of
# the clippy half only; §43.8 item 8 found the composite half counted sites
# and not variants, so a fourth surface-pair list could drift out of step with
# the other three and this number would never say so. It counts uncovered
# variants now, and moves with a new one on the composite side too.
#
# It falls when a refusal becomes an answer, which is the fourth reason and the
# happiest one: a test that matched an `Error` it expected, with `other =>
# panic!` for the rest, stops matching anything once the case it named is
# answered. That is what took it from 88 to 87 — a block corner on a ball's
# skin, which the mesher used to refuse.
#
# **A fifth reason, and the one that makes this number not a ratchet: the
# instrument changed.** 90 → 87 over the unit called "the door" looks like three
# arms going away and is not. Measured both ways on both trees:
#
#     old awk, before   82 direct + 8 composite = 90
#     new awk, before   82 direct + 6 composite = 88
#     old awk, after    81 direct + 8 composite = 89
#     new awk, after    81 direct + 6 composite = 87
#
# So the composite half moved **entirely** because the scan started counting
# uncovered variants instead of sites, and not at all because the tree did. Only
# one point of the three — the direct half's 82 → 81 — is a real arm going away.
# A ratchet that resets its own instrument in the same commit range as the tree
# has lost its baseline, so the bridging figures are written here rather than
# left to be re-derived. Whoever changes the scan again owes the same four rows.
#
# **87 → 89 over the unit called "the endless bend", and every point of it was
# read.** Measured on three commits with one instrument:
#
#     06bc048  the unit's own starting main   81 direct + 6 composite = 87
#     198de07  after E, F and C merged        81 direct + 5 composite = 86
#     2c236f9  after A and B merged           84 direct + 5 composite = 89
#
# The composite point is the happy fourth reason: `is_on_a_knife_edge` in
# `the_same_pair_turned_names_the_same_kind_of_crossing.rs` grew a ball-and-pipe
# arm, so that match leaves two of `Surface`'s variants unnamed where it left
# three. The three direct points are all in `intersect.rs` and all belong to
# naming the coaxial cone and pipe: two are the `other => unreachable!("a sum of
# squares is never below zero")` arms `#[non_exhaustive]` forces at the two `ask`
# call sites in `where_a_cone_and_a_pipe_cross`, which is what twenty-three of the
# other twenty-four direct arms in that file already are — the scan counts 26 in
# `intersect.rs`, 25 of them `unreachable!` or `panic!`; the third is
# `leaning => leaning` in `whether_a_cone_and_a_pipe_share_an_axis`, a
# pass-through that hands any answer but `Zero` straight back. It is safe by its
# caller's shape and not by the enum's: `walk`'s cone-and-pipe cut asks
# `!= Decision::Zero` and refuses, so a variant `Decision` grows later lands on
# the refusing side. `same => same` in `Picker::side_of_point`
# (`half_space.rs:441`) is **not** the precedent for it — there `Zero` and
# `Undecidable` both genuinely mean "not on a side", and here they do not.
#
#     a2d4df5  after the measurement lanes   85 direct + 5 composite = 90
#
# The one new point is a **test-only label mapper**, and it is the case where a
# wildcard is right rather than tolerated: `what_the_refusal_was_called` in
# `whether_a_coarser_deflection_always_meshes.rs` turns a refusal into a short
# string for a report, and `rcad_topo::Error` has **64 variants it does not
# name**. Naming all 64 to satisfy the letter of §40 would be worse code than the
# wildcard, and the arm decides nothing — it returns "an unexpected refusal" and
# the test's real assertions are on the refusal kinds it *does* name. §40's
# hazard is a wildcard swallowing a variant that should have changed an answer;
# nothing here answers anything.
#
#     b51b39d  after the reach fix            86 direct + 5 composite = 91
#
# The one new point is `how_far_along_the_axis_the_other_surface_reaches` in
# `mesh.rs`. It asks the surface on the other side of a crossing how far along
# our own axis a point on both of them can be, and it names all five `Surface`
# variants there are — the `_` is only there because `#[non_exhaustive]` forces
# it. It answers `None`, which the caller reads as "the corners are all there
# is": a floor that is too low on a surface kind nobody has taught it yet, which
# is what every surface got before this function existed. A `panic!` there would
# refuse to mesh a shape the kernel could have cut, and that is the worse of the
# two.
#     lane-non-monotone  after the placement pin  87 direct + 5 composite = 92
#
# The one new point is `what_it_refused` in
# `how_far_out_a_ball_can_sit_and_still_mesh.rs`, and it is the same case as the
# one at 90: a test-only label mapper over `rcad_topo::Error`, naming the five
# refusals that file has actually seen and answering "an unexpected refusal" for
# the rest. Reaching the arm is the finding, not the pass.
#     lane-record-enclosure  after §73's narrowing  83 direct + 5 composite = 88
#
# Four go away and none arrive. Three are `holds.rs` 318, 377 and 550, where a
# `Decision` reached `gave_up_…` through a wildcard; `Record::new` takes an
# `f64` now, so each arm has to name `Undecidable` to get one, and the wildcard
# below it can only be reached by a variant that does not exist yet
# (`docs/decisions.md` §73.3). The fourth is `validate.rs:281`, and it was a
# false positive the ledger was carrying: the arm above it read
# `decision @ Decision::Undecidable { .. }`, and clippy does not see a `@`
# binding as covering the variant. Written plainly as
# `Decision::Undecidable { enclosure }` — which is what the narrowing wanted
# anyway — the same match stops warning. The arm itself did not move.
#
#     ef7cf664  the pipe-convention conversion  83 direct + 3 composite = 86
#
# Two go away and none arrive. **Both are composite and neither is a new kind of
# movement**: each was a `_ => return None` over `Decision` inside a tuple, in
# `intersect.rs`, leaving `Undecidable` the one variant its match never named.
# `how_two_squares_compare_exactly` is gone outright — with a pipe `r` wide
# rather than `r/‖w‖` wide, what two leaning pipes ask clears to `(r₁ ± r₂)²K`
# against `T²` and no longer needs the five-hundred-order expansion that
# function existed to square (`docs/decisions.md` §118.11). The other is in
# `how_two_radii_and_a_gap_compare_exactly`, where the radii's sign was read off
# an `Exact` difference and is now `other.radius().partial_cmp(&one.radius())`:
# both radii mean the same thing, so the sign of the difference *is* the sign of
# the comparison. `Ordering` is not one of ours, so the scan stops counting that
# site at all. The direct half does not move.
#
#     lane-settle-the-turned-pair  after the resting ball  83 direct + 4 composite = 87
#
# The one new point is the mirror of the composite point at 89: `is_on_a_knife_edge`
# in `the_same_pair_turned_names_the_same_kind_of_crossing.rs` **loses** the
# ball-and-pipe arm it grew there, so that match leaves three of `Surface`'s
# variants unnamed where it left two. A ball resting all the way round the
# inside of a pipe is not a knife edge any more — §118.11 leaves both radii
# untouched by a turn, so the resting survives it — and an arm that skips a pair
# the property can now compare is a hole in the property. Two more quartic pairs
# get compared for the point.
#
#     §191's three test lanes  after the merge  84 direct + 4 composite = 88
#
# One new direct arm, in test code. `fuses_that_touch.rs`'s `named` grew one
# that only singled out `Undecidable`, and it is a `let … else` now; what is
# left is a match that names the three variants it is asked about and hands
# everything else on — `Rule`'s three fuses in `name_of`, or
# `a_turned_curved_refusal` handing every other `Error` to `a_curved_refusal`,
# where the upright families' names live.
#
#     §194's ball-and-block lane  after the merge  85 direct + 7 composite = 92
#
# Four more, all in the cut of a ball-and-plane circle that starts at a pole.
# The direct one is `which_half_of_a_circle_cut_at_a_pole`'s material side,
# which panics on `Zero` or `Undecidable` as `which_arc_this_is` beside it does.
# The composite three are one site, `across_a_circle_that_starts_at_a_pole`'s
# pair of surfaces, which names the ball and the plane and leaves `Surface`'s
# other three kinds to `_`. A pair that is not a ball and a plane is not cut,
# and `None` there leaves the fuse where it was before — refusing by
# `AClosedEdgeOfTheAnswerWouldStartAtAPole` when the start is a pole.
#
#     §195's polar-axis lane  after the merge  84 direct + 7 composite = 91
#
# One fewer. `a_ball_cut_by_a_plane_down_its_polar_axis_is_refused_by_name`
# matched its refusal and sent every other `Error` to `panic!`; the cut down the
# polar axis now answers, and the test that replaced it has no match at all.
#
#     the cone box lane  97 -> 98
#
# One more: `fuses_that_touch.rs`'s
# `a_pointed_cone_on_its_apex_whose_rim_touches_two_walls_fuses_by_every_rule`
# names every `Containment` it refuses and keeps the arm for ones not yet made.
#
#     Q649's lane  98 -> 97
#
# One fewer. `degenerate_curved_fuses.rs`'s wall lying on a bar's skin matched
# its `Undecidable` and sent every other `Error` to `panic!`; that fuse now
# answers every rule, and the test that replaced it has no match on a refusal.
#
#     Q930's lane  97 -> 95
#
# Two fewer. `degenerate_curved_fuses.rs`'s block corner on a bar's wall and
# block on a bar's cap each matched `TwoCurvesTouchWithoutCrossing` and sent
# every other `Error` to `panic!`; the fold of a straight cut along a straight
# crossing answers both, and the tests that replaced them match no refusal.
#
#     Q1272's lane  95 -> 96
#
# One more. `the_two_places_along_it` in `three_surfaces.rs` takes `A`'s sign,
# which its caller has already certified as `Pos` or `Neg`, and names those
# two; the arm for every other `Decision` is `unreachable!`.
#
#     Q1598's lane  96 -> 95
#
# One fewer. `degenerate_curved_fuses.rs`'s ball one rounding off a bar's axis
# matched each fuse's `CannotMeshAFace` by its rule and sent every other pair
# of rule and order to `CornersCoincide`; every fuse of it now answers, and the
# test that replaced it reads each entry's faces off its table, with no match.
#
#     Q2131's lane  95 -> 101
#
# Six more, in three new tests in `writer.rs`:
# `a_bends_seam_picked_inside_a_pipe_is_the_same_run_with_a_subnormal_axis`,
# `a_bends_seam_picked_inside_a_pipe_is_the_same_run_with_a_radius_an_ulp_off`
# and
# `a_bends_seam_picked_outside_its_pipe_is_a_different_run_from_the_one_picked_inside_it`.
# Each rebuilds one `Picker::InsideAPipe` arm — the one Q1293's bend pins need —
# and keeps every other `Picker` as it is with `other => *other`; that is a
# real catch-all over the non_exhaustive `Picker`, not a match that must name
# one variant, so it is not the `let … else` case. Each of the three counts
# twice because `writer.rs` builds into both `rcad-step`'s own tests and
# `rcad-fuzz-explain`.
#
#     Q2171's lane  101 -> 99
#
# Two fewer, both composite, at the same site named at line 2530 above.
# `across_a_circle_that_starts_at_a_pole`'s pair of surfaces now names
# `Cylinder` and `Torus` beside the ball and the plane it already named, so a
# circle through a ball's pole gets its second node against a pipe or a ring
# too, and only `Cone` is left to `_`.
#
#     Q2024's lane  99 -> 100
#
# One more, composite, in `a_bar_lying_along_a_plane_or_a_bar.rs`'s
# `a_thousand_turns_of_a_cube_and_a_bar`: its match on `SurfaceMeet` names
# `Nothing` and `Curves` of one run or two, and `other => panic!` takes the
# rest, since a plane holding a pipe's axis direction meets it in nothing else.
#
#     Q2329's lane  100 -> 101
#
# One more, in `fuses_that_must_agree.rs`'s `a_three_balls_crossing_refusal`:
# it names `Error::CannotMeshAFace` for three balls that cross at the same two
# points and sends every other `Error` to `a_curved_refusal` with `_`, the
# same shape `a_turned_curved_refusal` already has, since `a_curved_refusal`
# itself names the rest.
wildcard_arms := "101"

[doc("Count wildcard match arms over our own enums; fails when the count moves.")]
check-wildcards:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    # The mtime snapshot below is taken before `set -uo pipefail` rather than
    # after, on purpose: that is the one spot nothing can land in front of by
    # being written in as "the recipe's first command", which is where the
    # touch this recipe used to open with would be put back.
    before=$(find crates -type f -printf '%p %T@\n' | sort -k1,1)
    set -uo pipefail
    mkdir -p target
    log=target/last-wildcard-clippy.log
    # No touch of the sources before this (there used to be one, with no
    # reason written beside it, and it left every crate needing a relink on
    # whatever cargo step ran next in the gate — Q708, 383 CPU-seconds on
    # `check-ignored` alone). Two things were checked and both hold: a crate
    # left exactly as the last identical clippy invocation saw it still has
    # its warnings replayed, not swallowed (two runs back to back, the second
    # recompiling nothing, printed the same count both times); and the
    # arguments after clippy's `--` are themselves part of the fingerprint, so
    # a crate last linted by `lint`'s `-D warnings` would be re-checked here
    # rather than answered from that unrelated run. Nothing a touch bought was
    # not already covered by cargo's own fingerprint.
    #
    # **A target directory of its own, since §381.** In `lint`'s, the two sets
    # of flags took turns in cargo's cache, and each turn re-checked all 8
    # crates of the workspace whether the edit reached them or not; run at
    # once, one of the two waited on the other's lock. Here each re-checks only
    # what the edit reached. The directory costs 276 MB on its first run, and
    # about 1.1 GB once an edit has gone through it (§381.4).
    if ! CARGO_TARGET_DIR=target/wildcards CARGO_TERM_COLOR=never cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- \
            -W clippy::wildcard_enum_match_arm > "$log" 2>&1; then
        grep '^error' "$log" | head -5
        echo "clippy did not finish, so this counted nothing"
        echo "full log: $log"
        exit 1
    fi
    after=$(find crates -type f -printf '%p %T@\n' | sort -k1,1)
    touched=$(join -j1 <(echo "$before") <(echo "$after") \
            | awk '$3 > $2 { print $1; exit }')
    if [ -n "$touched" ]; then
        echo "check-wildcards left $touched newer than it found it"
        echo "a count must not touch what it inspects"
        exit 1
    fi
    direct=$({{ just_executable() }} _wildcard-direct-count "$log")
    files=$(find crates -name '*.rs' \( -path '*/src/*' -o -path '*/tests/*' \
            -o -path '*/benches/*' -o -path '*/examples/*' \))
    enums=$(grep -hoE '^[ \t]*pub enum [A-Za-z0-9_]+' $(find crates -path '*/src/*' -name '*.rs') \
            | awk '{print $NF}' | sort -u | paste -sd'|')
    composite=$(awk -v enums="$enums" -f crates/rcad-step/tools/composite_wildcards.awk $files \
            | grep '^COUNT=' | cut -d= -f2)
    found=$((direct + composite))
    if [ "$found" = "{{wildcard_arms}}" ]; then
        exit 0
    fi
    echo "wildcard arms over our own enums: $found ($direct direct, $composite composite), expected {{wildcard_arms}}"
    if [ "$direct" = "0" ] && [ "{{wildcard_arms}}" != "0" ]; then
        echo "0 direct is the parse breaking, not the arms going away: clippy ran and"
        echo "nothing it printed was matched. Read $log before touching the ledger."
        exit 1
    fi
    echo "a new one, or a new variant reaching an old one — see docs/decisions.md §40"
    exit 1

# The direct half of the count, kept apart from the clippy run so
# `check-wildcards-selftest` can hold it against logs whose answer is already
# known — the same split as `t` and `_test-digest`, for the same reason.
#
# ANSI escapes come off before anything is matched. `ci.yml` sets
# `CARGO_TERM_COLOR=always`, which puts an escape in front of every `warning:`,
# and every `^`-anchored pattern here then matches nothing at all. Run
# 34264306999 counted 0 of 83 that way and took `main` red while this machine
# stayed green, because cargo colours nothing when its stdout is a `$(...)`
# rather than a terminal. Stripping is what makes the count the same answer
# either way; `CARGO_TERM_COLOR=never` above is what keeps the saved log
# readable when a human opens it.
_wildcard-direct-count log:
    @sed 's/\x1b\[[0-9;]*m//g' '{{ log }}' | grep -c '^warning: wildcard match' || true

# Pins the composite-wildcard scan against fixtures it does not read off the
# tree, so it stays proven even on a tree where nothing real trips it.
#
# `Toy` is not one of our enums — the fixtures pass it to the script by name
# so the test is about the *script*, not about what today's `crates` happen
# to hold, and `toy_enum.rs` gives it a two-variant body of its own so the
# scan has a real total to judge the others against. `commented.rs` pins the
# bug the script had while this recipe was being written: a doc comment that
# says the word "match" opened a match this script never closed, and every
# line after it stopped being read. Every fixture below names only `Toy::A`
# and never `Toy::B`, so each is one variant short — 1 apiece, 3 in all — not
# because three sites were found, but because three fixtures each leave one
# of `Toy`'s two variants uncovered.
[private]
check-wildcards-selftest:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    dir=$(mktemp -d)
    trap 'rm -rf "$dir"' EXIT
    cat > "$dir/bad_array.rs" <<'RS'
    fn f(pair: [Toy; 2]) -> bool {
        match pair {
            [Toy::A, Toy::A] => true,
            _ => false,
        }
    }
    RS
    cat > "$dir/bad_tuple_ref.rs" <<'RS'
    fn f(one: &Toy, other: &Toy) -> bool {
        match (one, other) {
            (&Toy::A, &Toy::A) => true,
            _ => false,
        }
    }
    RS
    cat > "$dir/good_named.rs" <<'RS'
    fn f(pair: [Toy; 2]) -> bool {
        match pair {
            [Toy::A, Toy::A] => true,
            [Toy::A, Toy::B] | [Toy::B, Toy::A] | [Toy::B, Toy::B] => false,
        }
    }
    RS
    cat > "$dir/good_single_enum_wildcard.rs" <<'RS'
    fn f(one: Toy) -> bool {
        match one {
            Toy::A => true,
            _ => false,
        }
    }
    RS
    cat > "$dir/commented.rs" <<'RS'
    // Whatever the caller does, match it against Toy::A here.
    fn f(pair: [Toy; 2]) -> bool {
        match pair {
            [Toy::A, Toy::A] => true,
            _ => false,
        }
    }
    RS
    cat > "$dir/toy_enum.rs" <<'RS'
    pub enum Toy {
        A,
        B,
    }
    RS
    want=$(printf '%s\n' \
        "$dir/bad_array.rs:2" \
        "$dir/bad_tuple_ref.rs:2" \
        "$dir/commented.rs:3" \
        "COUNT=3" | sort)
    # Under **every** awk on this machine and not just the default one. The
    # script is not the gate's answer; what the gate counts is whatever the awk
    # in front of it says, and two of them disagreed by four arms —
    # `docs/decisions.md` §173. A bare container has mawk alone and this arm is
    # then one implementation wide, which is why it says which ones it ran.
    ran=
    for a in awk gawk mawk nawk "busybox awk"; do
        command -v ${a%% *} >/dev/null || continue
        got=$($a -v enums="Toy" -f crates/rcad-step/tools/composite_wildcards.awk \
            "$dir/bad_array.rs" "$dir/bad_tuple_ref.rs" "$dir/good_named.rs" \
            "$dir/good_single_enum_wildcard.rs" "$dir/commented.rs" "$dir/toy_enum.rs" | sort)
        if [ "$got" != "$want" ]; then
            echo "composite_wildcards.awk drifted from its own fixtures under $a:"
            echo "--- got ---"; echo "$got"
            echo "--- want ---"; echo "$want"
            echo "an answer that depends on which awk ran is not an answer —"
            echo "gawk's own regex extensions are the way this happened before"
            exit 1
        fi
        ran="$ran $a"
    done
    echo "composite scan agrees under:$ran"

    # The direct half, against the same line with and without cargo's colours.
    # The escapes below are the bytes `cargo clippy` actually emitted under
    # `CARGO_TERM_COLOR=always` on 2026-09-08, not a guess at their shape.
    w='wildcard match will also match any future added variants'
    printf 'warning: %s\n' "$w" "$w" > "$dir/plain.log"
    printf '\033[1m\033[33mwarning\033[0m\033[1m: %s\033[0m\n' "$w" "$w" > "$dir/coloured.log"
    # Its clippy keeps a target directory of its own. In `lint`'s, the two
    # recipes' flags take turns in cargo's cache, and each turn re-checks every
    # crate of the workspace whether the edit reached it or not (§381.1).
    clippy_target=$({{ just_executable() }} --show check-wildcards | grep -v '^ *#' \
        | sed -n 's/.*CARGO_TARGET_DIR=\([^ ]*\) .*cargo clippy.*/\1/p')
    case "$clippy_target" in
        ""|target|target/|./target)
            echo "check-wildcards runs its clippy in lint's target directory ('${clippy_target:-the default}'),"
            echo "so the two take turns re-checking every crate (docs/decisions.md §381.1)"
            exit 1 ;;
    esac

    for f in plain coloured; do
        got=$({{ just_executable() }} _wildcard-direct-count "$dir/$f.log")
        if [ "$got" != "2" ]; then
            echo "_wildcard-direct-count read $got warnings from $f.log, expected 2"
            echo "a count that only works on one of these is a count that only"
            echo "works off a runner — see the comment on the recipe"
            exit 1
        fi
    done

# How many tests are marked `#[ignore]` today. Move it with the commit that
# moves the tree, the same way `wildcard_arms` above works.
#
# Zero is a legitimate answer, and it is where this should end up: it says the
# last gap somebody parked behind an `#[ignore]` has been closed, and
# `test-ignored` then has nothing to run and says so.
ignored_tests := "3"

# An `#[ignore]`d test is a gap somebody parked, and this is what stops it being
# forgotten.
#
# Nothing ran the ignored tests at all before this: `cargo nextest run
# --workspace` skips them silently, so the one `#[ignore]` in the tree could be
# fixed and un-ignored, or a second one added, and no recipe here would say a
# word either way.
#
# **The count is pinned rather than the run being left to speak for itself**,
# because a run that selects nothing is the failure this recipe exists to
# prevent and an empty selection is exactly what a passing run looks like. A
# typo in the filter, a flag this nextest does not have, a crate that did not
# build — each of those selects nothing, and `cargo nextest run --run-ignored
# ignored-only` printing "0 tests run" would be read as good news. A number that
# has to match says which of the two happened.
#
# `test-ignored` below is what actually runs them, and it is not part of
# `gate`: today's one ignored test **fails**, which is why it is ignored, so
# a `gate` that ran it would be red on purpose.
[doc("How many tests are marked #[ignore]; fails when the count moves.")]
check-ignored:
    @out=$(cargo nextest list --workspace --run-ignored ignored-only 2>/dev/null) \
        || { echo "nextest could not list the ignored tests, so this counted nothing"; exit 1; }; \
    listed=$(echo "$out" | grep -c .); \
    if [ "$listed" != "{{ignored_tests}}" ]; then \
        echo "$out"; \
        echo "^ tests marked #[ignore]: $listed, expected {{ignored_tests}}"; \
        echo "a new one raises this number; one fixed and un-ignored lowers it"; \
        exit 1; \
    fi

# Run them. Fails loudly if the selection is empty rather than reporting a run
# of no tests as a pass.
[doc("Run the #[ignore]d tests. They fail; that is why they are ignored.")]
test-ignored:
    @if [ "{{ignored_tests}}" = "0" ]; then \
        echo "no test is marked #[ignore], so there is nothing here to run"; \
        exit 0; \
    fi; \
    cargo nextest run --workspace --run-ignored ignored-only --no-tests fail

# The hook that refuses shell patching of a .rs file, against its own cases
# (docs/decisions.md §50.8).
#
# It is in `gate` because the hook fails in the direction that is hard to
# notice: a pattern that stops matching lets the thing through silently, which
# is the same failure mode the hook exists to prevent. Two of its cases are
# drafts of itself that were wrong — a commit message describing the rule, and
# a recipe whose own body writes .rs fixtures — and five more came from the
# 2026-09-05 session review: three shapes it let through, two it wrongly
# refused in the scratchpad.
#
# The hook and most of its cases now live in the `guardrails` plugin, shared
# with `rscene`. What stayed here is `patching-cases.local.json`: the cases that
# name *this* repo — a `crates/rcad-*` path, `docs/decisions.md`, a `just`
# recipe of ours. The plugin's runner appends it and fails loudly if the file is
# named and missing, because a repo's own cases quietly not running is exactly
# the drift the plugin was made to end.
#
# The plugin's half is skipped where there is no Claude Code, which since
# 2026-09-13 includes CI: the runner used to clone the marketplace to get that
# script, and the hook it tests is a **PreToolUse** hook, which cannot fire in a
# job with no session. What is lost is this repo's three overlay cases, and it
# is named here rather than hidden. The shared 24 have a runner of their own in
# the marketplace repo now.
#
# The four hooks below it are rcad's own, need no plugin, and run either way —
# which is why the skip wraps one line and not the recipe. The fourth is the
# guard that sends a write to `docs/queue.md` through `just queue` (§263.7): its
# cases hold each writing form refused, each reading form allowed, and each
# form that runs `pin` or `unpin` asked about, since only Mattias pins (§430).
[doc("The hooks against their own cases: shell patching, context budget, worktrees.")]
check-hooks:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    if [ -d "{{claude_config}}" ]; then
        {{guardrails_plugin}}/bin/selftest.sh .claude/patching-cases.local.json
    else
        echo "no {{claude_config}}, so no Claude Code and no plugin here: the patching cases are skipped, not passed"
    fi
    .claude/hooks/check-worktree-create.sh
    .claude/hooks/check-session-start-hub.sh
    .claude/hooks/check-decisions-zero.sh
    .claude/hooks/check-queue-write-guard.sh

# Every `just` recipe and every repo path a project skill names still exists.
#
# A skill is prose that tells a session which commands to run, so it rots the
# way every document naming a recipe rots — and this repo has already paid for
# that once: `default` became `gate` on 2026-09-08 and every sentence naming it
# went quietly wrong, including ones in the shared skills. `check-decisions`
# holds that file to its own numbering for the same reason; this is that rule
# pointed at `.claude/skills/`, which is where a skill too repo-specific to give
# away has to live (§64.4).
#
# **Only code spans are read.** "just" is an ordinary English word, and a prose
# sentence containing it would otherwise invent a recipe name and fail here for
# nothing. A command a skill means literally is one it wrote in backticks or in
# a fenced block — the same rule the reader follows, so the check and the reader
# cannot disagree about what was a claim.
#
# A path is checked only when it carries a slash and an extension and no `<`, so
# `crates/<c>/src` is left alone: a placeholder is not a claim about the disk.
#
# Only public recipes are known, because `--summary` is what lists them. A skill
# naming a `[private]` recipe fails here, which is the right answer — those are
# the selftests, and a skill has no business sending a session into one.
[doc("Every recipe and path a .claude/skills SKILL.md names still exists.")]
check-skills:
    @{{ just_executable() }} _skills-check '.claude/skills'

# Held apart from the recipe above so the selftest can run it over fixtures
# whose answer is known, rather than over whatever the skills happen to say.
[private]
_skills-check dir:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -uo pipefail
    dir='{{dir}}'
    if [ ! -d "$dir" ]; then
        echo "no $dir, so there are no project skills to check"
        exit 0
    fi
    files=$(find "$dir" -name SKILL.md | sort)
    if [ -z "$files" ]; then
        echo "no SKILL.md under $dir, so there are no project skills to check"
        exit 0
    fi
    recipes=$({{ just_executable() }} --justfile {{ justfile() }} --summary 2>/dev/null \
        | tr ' ' '\n' | grep . | sort -u)
    if [ -z "$recipes" ]; then
        echo "just --summary listed no recipes, so every name a skill gave would pass"
        exit 1
    fi
    spans=0
    bad=0
    for f in $files; do
        claims=$(awk '
            /^[ \t]*```/ { fence = !fence; next }
            fence { print; next }
            {
                line = $0
                while (match(line, /`[^`]+`/)) {
                    print substr(line, RSTART + 1, RLENGTH - 2)
                    line = substr(line, RSTART + RLENGTH)
                }
            }
        ' "$f")
        spans=$((spans + $(printf '%s\n' "$claims" | grep -c .)))
        named=$(printf '%s\n' "$claims" \
            | awk '{ for (i = 1; i < NF; i++) \
                if ($i == "just" && $(i + 1) ~ /^[a-z][a-z0-9-]*$/) print $(i + 1) }' \
            | sort -u)
        for r in $named; do
            grep -qxF -- "$r" <<<"$recipes" && continue
            echo "$f says \`just $r\`, which this justfile does not define"
            bad=1
        done
        paths=$(printf '%s\n' "$claims" \
            | awk '{ for (i = 1; i <= NF; i++) { t = $i; sub(/[,;:)]+$/, "", t); \
                if (t ~ /^[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+\/[A-Za-z0-9_.\/-]*\.[a-z]+$/ && t !~ /</) print t } }' \
            | sort -u)
        for p in $paths; do
            [ -e "$p" ] && continue
            echo "$f names $p, which is not in the tree"
            bad=1
        done
    done
    if [ "$spans" -eq 0 ]; then
        echo "no code spans in any SKILL.md under $dir, so this scan compared nothing"
        echo "a skill with no backticks and no fenced block is not how any of them are written"
        exit 1
    fi
    [ "$bad" -eq 0 ] || exit 1

# Pins the scan against fixtures rather than against the skills in this tree, so
# it stays proven on a day they are rewritten.
#
# The passing fixture carries the two cases that would make this check worse than
# nothing if the code-span rule broke: an ordinary English "just" before a word
# that is not a recipe, and a `<`-placeholder path. Both are in the file that has
# to pass, so either one failing fails here rather than in a lane's brief.
[private]
check-skills-selftest:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    dir=$(mktemp -d)
    trap 'rm -rf "$dir"' EXIT
    j="{{ just_executable() }} --justfile {{ justfile() }}"

    mkdir -p "$dir/good"
    cat > "$dir/good/SKILL.md" <<'MD'
    Run `mise exec -- just gate quiet`, then read .claude/workflows/lanes.js.
    You can just read it first. The sources are crates/<c>/src/thing.rs.
    MD
    $j _skills-check "$dir" || { echo "a skill naming only real things failed"; exit 1; }

    mkdir -p "$dir/bad-recipe"
    printf 'Run `just no-such-recipe`.\n' > "$dir/bad-recipe/SKILL.md"
    if $j _skills-check "$dir" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        echo "a skill naming a recipe this justfile does not define passed"; exit 1
    fi
    rm -rf "$dir/bad-recipe"

    # Not a docs/ path: this step is served, and a prose commit that added the
    # file would fail this case without moving the fingerprint (§369).
    mkdir -p "$dir/bad-path"
    printf 'Read `crates/no-such-file.rs` first.\n' > "$dir/bad-path/SKILL.md"
    if $j _skills-check "$dir" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        echo "a skill naming a path that is not in the tree passed"; exit 1
    fi
    rm -rf "$dir/bad-path"

    rm -rf "$dir/good"
    mkdir -p "$dir/silent"
    printf 'No code spans at all in this one.\n' > "$dir/silent/SKILL.md"
    if $j _skills-check "$dir" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        echo "a scan that found no code spans passed, having compared nothing"; exit 1
    fi

    if ! $j _skills-check "$dir/no-such-dir" | grep -q 'no project skills'; then
        echo "a missing skills directory is not the same as a broken scan"; exit 1
    fi

# Every lane the `lanes` workflow sends out creates its own branch, nothing in
# the workflow resets one, and it lands its lanes on `main` one at a time.
#
# `.claude/workflows/lanes.js` hands each lane its first commands, and until
# §237 one of them was `git checkout -B <branch> <base>`. That resets a branch
# which already exists, and rscene's `235ccaf` is what it costs when an older
# worktree still has that branch checked out: the old tree shows a staged revert
# of everything since, and a commit there would undo it. `git switch -c` refuses
# a name already taken, and the lane is told to stop as blocked.
#
# **It reads the source, not the prompt a lane is sent.** rscene holds its
# workflow with a node harness that renders every prompt; this repo pins no node,
# and the line is literal text in a template string. Three arms, each of which
# alone would let the reset back in: the `switch -c` line is there exactly once;
# every line naming `checkout -B` or `switch -C` is the one warning against them;
# and the lane is told what to do when the switch refuses — without that, the
# nearest fix a lane knows is the reset.
#
# **Since §266 it also merges, and three more arms hold that half.** Each lane
# is followed by its verifier and then an Opus merger that pushes to `main`, so
# a mistake here now lands on `main` rather than in a report:
#
# - Every `agent(` call names an `agentType`, the three roles are all there, and
#   each type is a file in `.claude/agents/` whose `name:` says so. Until §266
#   none did, so `lane.md` and `verifier.md` never loaded (§261.6).
# - The merger is started on one line behind the merge before it, through
#   `lastMerge`, so merges inside one run never overlap. The other runs and
#   sessions are what `ff-main` and the ancestor check settle (§266.4).
# - `merger.md` fetches, checks that `origin/main` is an ancestor of `HEAD`,
#   pushes and then runs `ff-main`, in that order and once each, and no other
#   role is told to `git push`.
#
# None of it runs the workflow, pushes, or touches `docs/queue.md`: it reads
# the script and the agent files as text, for the same reason as above.
#
# **Since §266 a seventh arm holds the shell rule the three roles share.** It
# used to live once in `lanes.js`, sent inline in every brief; §266 moved it
# into `CLAUDE.md`, which every role reads anyway. The arm checks both
# directions: `CLAUDE.md` still carries the `mise exec --` prefix rule and the
# Bash guard's rule against a path it cannot resolve, and `lanes.js` carries no
# second copy of either.
#
# **It proves each arm by cutting the real files**, not against fixtures kept
# beside them, because these files are the only ones it will ever scan. Each
# cut must change a file and must be caught by the arm it was made for, so a
# rewrite of `lanes.js` that moves a line cannot leave an arm passing having
# compared nothing.
#
# **An eighth arm holds the verifier's own tree apart from the lane's.**
# `VERIFY`'s prompt used to name only the lane's worktree, and the schema's
# `worktree_path` said just "your own worktree" -- so a verifier echoed the
# lane's path back as its own, and its real tree stayed locked, holding a
# slot, until a merger found it by hand (§263.4). The arm checks that both the
# prompt and the schema tell the verifier to find its own tree with
# `git rev-parse --show-toplevel`, and that `verifier.md` says so too.
#
# **A ninth arm holds the mutation that can stand in for a revert.** A revert
# can't fail for a fix that only moves a floor or a bound -- Q160's verifier
# reproduced a halving mutation the new 0.5 floor caught and the old 0.3 one
# didn't, and `lanes.js` still refused the merge because `tests_fail_without_fix`
# was false (§266). The arm counts `reproduced_mutation` across the file --
# `grep -c 'reproduced_mutation' .claude/workflows/lanes.js` shows 0 on main and
# 4 here -- and fails under 3, which is what naming it in the readiness check,
# `VERIFY`'s prompt and `verdictSchema` alone adds up to.
#
# **A tenth arm holds the pins-only case explicit.** Three test-only rows --
# Q126, Q127 and Q203, each a verdict, a pinned test and a decisions section
# with no `src/` path in the write set -- came back "verifier said merge" and
# were merged by hand, because `unproven` demanded a revert that had nothing
# to revert. `lane.pinsOnly` says so; it is never inferred from an empty
# `sourcePaths`, because that emptiness is also what a tooling lane shows when
# its dispatcher forgot to pass `sourcePaths` by hand, and that must keep
# refusing. The arm checks four exact lines: the one that gates `pinsOnly` on
# `sourcePathsOf(lane)` being empty, so the field cannot be swapped for that
# inference; the refusal a lane with no field and no source still gets; the
# refusal a `pinsOnly` lane with a declared source gets instead, since there is
# something to revert then; and the line in the verifier's own prompt that
# tells it which lane this is, so it is never asked for a revert that has
# nothing to revert (§287).
#
# **An eleventh arm holds the foreground rule in each agent file.** Q206's
# first lane came back empty because the harness forced its report before a
# backgrounded test run had printed anything, and Q148's merger reported three
# steps into the gate and never pushed. The arm checks the same literal
# sentence appears in `lane.md`, `verifier.md` and `merger.md`; it is not
# shared through `CLAUDE.md` because that file's own cold-tree paragraph now
# carves these three roles out of it (Q216). It also fails when one of those
# files still says to pass `run_in_background: true`: the sentence alone let
# `merger.md` keep telling a merger to background a cold gate, four paragraphs
# below the rule, which is the Q148 failure itself.
#
# **A twelfth arm holds the run's own clean-up (§315.2).** 101 lane trees were
# removed by hand on 2026-09-28 with `/` full, because nothing else ever removed
# one. Three lines keep it working. The pruner is started only behind a merge,
# because a run that did not land is the dispatcher's to judge. The command it
# is sent is the exact text `.claude/settings.json` allows, because any other
# form stops to ask and nobody is there to answer. And the merger is told to
# mark its run's trees finished, because without the mark `prune-worktrees`
# keeps all three for an hour.
#
# **A thirteenth arm holds a verified row's own line when it still did not
# merge (§304.4).** Q124 and Q128 sat held five and a half hours because
# `merged()` and `handedBack()` could return `merged: false` for a row whose
# verifier had already said `merge` or `fix_first` -- the merger returned
# nothing, or declined for its own reason -- and that only showed in the log,
# not in the row's own returned text, so no dispatcher noticed. The arm checks
# two things against `lanes.js` itself, the only fixture it ever cuts: the exact
# line that defines `verified` from `prior.check.verdict` appears twice, once in
# `merged()` and once in `handedBack()`, so a row is only ever called verified
# when the verifier said `merge` or `fix_first`; and the ternary that reads it
# into the row's own line appears in all three places a row can end up
# `merged: false` after that -- the merger returning nothing, the merger
# declining, and the lane being handed back -- so each of those, for a row the
# verifier called either `merge` or `fix_first`, reads "verified, not merged"
# rather than plain "not merged".
#
# **A fourteenth arm keeps a catch-up over docs alone apart from a push attempt
# (§367).** Q574's merger gated green three times on 09-29 and never pushed: it
# lost its last attempt to six `docs/queue.md` commits, when the gate after them
# would have served its slow steps and taken about a minute. The arm checks the
# three places the rule lives. `merger.md` carries, on a line of its own and
# once, the diff that tells a merger what `origin/main` gained. `lanes.js`
# defines `DOCS_CATCH_UPS` and hands it to the merger in its brief, so the
# catch-ups have a bound and a merger cannot loop. And the merger's schema
# requires `docs_catch_ups`, so every merger says how many it used.
#
# **A fifteenth arm holds a verifier to one run of the tests, not two (§370).**
# Q574's verifier re-ran the lane's tests straight after the gate that had just
# run, or served, the very same code -- about five minutes for nothing step 3
# had not already shown. `verifier.md`'s step 6 now takes the pass half of the
# proof from step 3's gate summary instead, and runs a test or a recipe again
# only when it is not one of `gate_steps`. The arm checks the one sentence that
# says so appears in both `lanes.js` -- once in `VERIFY`'s prompt and once in
# the `pinsOnly` note, so neither path keeps the old double run -- and in
# `verifier.md` itself.
#
# **A sixteenth arm holds the road for a change that alters nothing that runs
# (§375).** Q519, Q601 and the bundle Q440,Q619,Q720,Q624,Q684 waited on
# Mattias's hand: a doc-comment edit in a `.rs` file always met the moved-text
# step's "any file not ending in `.md`" bar, and a pinned test inside `src/`
# met `unproven`'s demand for a revert. The `lines-that-run` recipe now prints
# every changed line that could change what runs -- none from `.md` files,
# comments, or test code -- and then how many there are, and a count of 0 is
# what waives the revert (§382 moved it out of `lanes.js` and gave it the
# count). The arm runs that awk program, cut out of the recipe itself, on a
# small repo it builds, and wants back exactly the lines that run: a code line
# in a plain `mod`, a function after a `#[cfg(test)]` module closes, a shebang
# in a recipe that is not a `check-*`, and a source file renamed, then `lines
# that run: 7`. It skips a comment, a doc comment, a test inside
# `#[cfg(test)]`, a file under `tests/`, a fuzz seed renamed, a `check-*`
# recipe's body and a `.md` file. The arm also checks the lines that use the
# print: the recipe reads the whole-file diff with its flags spelled out,
# `unproven` reads the count off the print's last line, waives the revert only
# on a count of 0 and refuses a lane with no test whose count is not 0, a print
# with no count or from another command is refused, the schema requires both,
# `VERIFY` hands the command over, and `verifier.md` carries the new moved-text
# rule and not the old one.
#
# **A seventeenth arm holds the verifier's report short (§382).** Writing its
# report took a verifier about 40 s, and two fields were three quarters of it:
# the lines-that-run print, which ran to 199 lines, and `notes`, a step-by-step
# account of what the other fields already held. The arm runs the recipe's awk
# program on a second repo, whose one commit adds fifteen functions, and wants
# back the first ten and then `lines that run: 15`. It also checks that the
# verdict schema's `notes` says at most five lines, and that `verifier.md` says
# so too.
#
# **An eighteenth arm holds who merges (§383.4 item 3, §384).** A `merge` on a
# lane that wrote its rows goes to the Haiku `lander`, typed as one, which the
# fourth arm now wants beside the other roles; a `fix_first`, and a lane whose
# rows `just land` cannot take, go to the Opus merger; so does every merge the
# recipe stopped on, and a lander that throws, which is what a `claude` process
# started before `lander.md` existed does. The arm checks those four exact lines,
# the merger's own branch after a stop, since the lander's `merge-<label>` may
# exist, the `origin/main` check that brief and `merger.md` both carry, and the
# lander's command starting with the text `.claude/settings.json` allows. The
# fifth arm now holds the whole of that, lander and merger, behind `lastMerge`.
#
# **A nineteenth arm holds the verifier to `just verify-lane` (§383.4 item 4,
# §385).** `VERIFY`'s prompt hands over the recipe's start and its wait, each as
# a line of its own, and `verifier.md` has the verifier run both first. The
# recipe looks for each number's value in what its command prints, so
# `numberSchema` and `lane.md` both say the value is that text. And each record
# the prompt writes into the recipe's input is one the script reads, so a
# rename on one side cannot leave the other refusing every input.
#
# **A twentieth arm holds the road for a change that keeps the behaviour
# (§399).** Q854 dropped one of two spellings of one question for the other, so
# no test could fail without it, and its 25 lines that run met the moved-text
# refusal; its dispatcher did the row in its own tree. `lane.sameBehaviour` is
# that road, and only that field opens it: the arm checks the line that picks
# the steps from the field, the three refusals a marked lane can meet before its
# verifier runs, and the two lines and the helper that hold it to its tests
# passing with the source reverted and one failing under a mutation of the
# changed lines. Without the mutation, tests that never run the changed lines
# would pass both. It checks too that an unmarked lane's refusal names the road,
# since that refusal is all the next dispatcher holding such a row sees.
# `verifier.md` carries the steps, and the nineteenth arm now holds the
# `same-behaviour` record on both sides.
#
# **A twenty-first arm holds a red gate to the Opus merger too (§414).** Five
# lanes in the week of 2026-10-01 to 10-08 came back `merge` with `fixes: []`
# on a verifier whose own gate had gone red, and the eighteenth arm's check did
# not ask about the gate at all, so `landable()` sent them to the Haiku lander
# and `just land`'s shared gate went red with them. The arm checks that
# `landable()` also calls a helper that reads `gate_summary` for an exact
# `gate: PASS` line -- never a substring, since `gate: FAIL` text can hold that
# phrase inside its own tail -- and that the Opus merger's brief, for a row
# that never reached the lander, names the gate as a reason beside the other
# two.
#
# **A twenty-second arm holds the relay (§416).** From 10-01 to 10-08, 283 of
# 354 Opus lanes passed the 150k budget, and a lane's first commit came at a
# median of 203k, so a lane stopped at the warning handed on nothing and briefs
# told lanes to carry on instead. Now a writing lane past the warning commits,
# lets go of its branch and reports `relay`, and `lanes.js` starts a fresh lane
# on that branch. The arm checks that a row's relays are bounded by
# `MAX_RELAYS`; that a row asking for one past the bound, or whose relay was
# refused, is handed back rather than verified; that a relay switches onto the
# lane's own branch and is checked to start where the lane before it stopped;
# that the trees a row was relayed from are counted among its trees, so they
# are unlocked and marked finished; and that `relay` is an outcome a writing
# lane may report. In `lane.md` it checks the rule to hand over, the `git
# switch --detach` that frees the branch, and the rule to commit the failing
# test first, which is what a relay starts from. And it checks that the lane's
# brief and a relay's both carry the hand-over rule themselves, because a
# `claude` process keeps its agent types through a `/clear`, so a dispatcher
# cleared after §416 would send lanes whose `lane.md` has no relay (Q1422).
#
# **Arms twenty-three to twenty-seven hold the prep stage (§396.4).** A
# dispatcher used to check and brief every row itself, at every fill. A lane
# sent as `{ label, queueId, notes }` now goes first to `prep`, a read-only
# Sonnet type, which answers `ok` with the write set, model, marks and facts,
# or `needs` with the files the row is missing. The twenty-third arm checks
# that `lanes.js` sends one agent of that type and that `prep.md` gives it the
# Sonnet model and no tool that writes, since it runs in the dispatcher's own
# tree; the fourth already wants the file. The twenty-fourth checks that the
# stage runs before the lane, that an answer other than `ok` starts no lane,
# and that the files prep said the row needs come back in the row's lines. The
# twenty-fifth checks that the lane takes its write set, model and marks from
# prep's answer, and that an answer is refused when its write set is not its
# rows', its model is not theirs, or it gives both marks. The twenty-sixth
# checks that a lane giving fields of both forms is refused. The twenty-seventh
# checks that a brief built from a row carries the two notes every brief the
# dispatcher wrote carried (Q1047, Q1505, Q1347), and that a writing lane gets
# the `for_the_verifier` field by default. Unlike the arms above, these five
# do not stop at the first that fails, so the base's `lanes.js` names them all.
#
# **Arms twenty-eight to thirty hold a row whose fault no longer shows
# (§433.8).** A lane whose row names the crash files, corpus files or shape
# that show its fault runs them on its base first, and when none shows it, the
# lane commits nothing and reports `does_not_reproduce`. The twenty-eighth arm
# checks that a writing lane may report that outcome and that `lane.md` gives
# the rule. The twenty-ninth checks that such a lane goes to its verifier
# before `verify: false` is read, so no row closes on its lane's word alone;
# that a confirmed one goes to a merger that closes it before the lander is
# weighed, since there is no branch for `just land` to merge; and that
# `verifier.md` has the steps. The thirtieth checks that a verifier that does
# not confirm stops the row, and that `merger.md` closes a confirmed one at the
# base. Like the five before them, these three do not stop at the first that
# fails.
#
# **Arm thirty-one checks that a read-only report carries the same `rows`
# field a writing one does, required the same way** (§418.3). Without it a
# read-only lane has no way to hand back the rows its work leaves, and pastes
# them into `remaining` instead, for a dispatcher to file by hand.
#
# **Arm thirty-two checks that `lanes.js` refuses a run of several rows
# (§230.4).** Five times from 2026-10-03 to 2026-10-05 a dispatcher put a fill
# of several rows into one `lanes` run, as several entries of `args.lanes`,
# though the dispatch-lanes skill, the handoff and a memory note each said one
# Workflow call per row, and prose alone did not stop it. `lanes.js` now
# throws at the start when `args.lanes` has more than one entry unless
# `args.shareOneRun` is `true`. The arm checks that the exact throw line is
# there, and that its message names one Workflow call per row.
#
# **Arm thirty-three checks that the rows a fix lane also answers close on its
# verifier's word, in the merge's own `queue apply`** (§450.3 rule 4). Before
# it, `17532d10` closed Q1965's family by hand. `lanes.js` must give the
# verifier `also_answers_confirmed`, ask it to run the lane's `also_answers`
# again, pass what it confirms to `just land --answers` and tell the Opus
# merger; `prep.md` must list the open rows on the row's test file, `lane.md`
# tell a fix lane to run them, `verifier.md` hold the Also answers steps, and
# `merger.md` give each confirmed row a `done` step.
#
# **Arm thirty-four checks that `gone()` stops a does_not_reproduce report
# that still holds rows, naming them, instead of sending it on to the GONE
# verifier and the close at the base** (§433.8). Q2185's lane reported
# does_not_reproduce with an `after` row that Q654's refusal had stopped
# before Q2185's target ever ran; lane.md's rule forbade that add, but nothing
# in `lanes.js` stopped the report itself, so the merger filed the row by hand
# as Q2322 and closed Q2185 done, a pin never written. The arm checks the
# guard in `lanes.js` and that `lane.md` tells a lane that a row whose shape
# another open row's fault stops first reports `blocked`, naming that row,
# instead of `does_not_reproduce` with rows.
[doc("The lanes workflow makes each lane's branch with switch -c and resets none, types every agent, merges one lane at a time, refuses to share a run across rows unless asked, and prunes only what merged.")]
