    Q1897 1    after:Q1892    opus    crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/,crates/rcad-topo/src/mesh.rs,crates/rcad-geom/src/walk.rs,crates/rcad-topo/tests/two_equal_bars_leaning_at_each_other.rs   refuses/B from/Q1892 Two bars of radius 1/4 cut off 1 either side of where their axes meet, on (3,4,0) and (4,-3,0), square to each other in z = 0, each built by add_bar_cut_off_by with its axis origin at that point (as two_bars_crossing_at in fuse/arrange.rs mod curved builds them), are refused in all six fuses wherever the axes meet. At the origin and (1,0,0), where intersect picks the halves by half-spaces, and at (11,0,0), where it picks them by surfaces holding the middle since Q1892, the refusals are AWireWillNotLieFlatOnItsFace, ACurvedFaceGoesRightRoundWithNoSeam or CannotMeshAFace, the same six in the same order at (1,0,0) and (11,0,0). At (11,-3.25,0.5), picked by surfaces holding the middle, all six are refused CannotNameWhichPieceOfACrossingThisIs, as before the arrangement change of Q1892, though intersect names every pair of the two bars surfaces there in both orders with no error. Untraced; the first refusals may share the cause of the row for the bars on (3,4,0) and (4,0,3), not run. Make them answer §31
    Q1929 1    todo    opus    crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/,crates/rcad-topo/tests/two_equal_bars_leaning_at_each_other.rs   refuses/B from/Q1908 Two bars of radius 1/4, the second 2 long starting where the axes meet at (11,-3.25,0.5) turned 120 degrees about z, its stored direction a hair off z = 0: since Q1908 all six fuses get past the second bar near cap and are refused, the union and both differences AWireWillNotLieFlatOnItsFace and the intersection ACurvedFaceGoesRightRoundWithNoSeam (a_bar_starting_where_it_crosses_another_at_120_degrees_is_refused_building_a_wire in two_equal_bars_leaning_at_each_other.rs pins it), where at 50, 60, 70 and 100 degrees every fuse answers with 1/(24 sin a) shared. Untraced; Q1793, Q1724, Q1896 and Q1897 meet the same refusals and may share the cause, not run. Make them answer and pin the volumes §31
    Q1646 1    done:cdb4d6fe52cc131100b86be1f4ddd91483d3b5e3    opus    crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/arrange.rs,crates/rcad-topo/tests/two_equal_bars_leaning_at_each_other.rs   refuses/B Two bars of radius 1/4 whose axes meet at a slant are named in four halves by intersect since Q1625, with pickers facing (0,1,0) and (1,0,0) rather than the pipes' stored directions, and every fuse of them is refused in the arrangement. The bars: one from (9,0,0), length 4, turned onto x by Isometry::quarter_turns(y,1); the other turned the same way and then by Isometry::rotation(z, a), a = 50, 60, 70 or 100 degrees, from (11,0,0) - 16d, length 32, d its direction. All six fuses of each refuse Geometry(WhereThreeSurfacesMeetNeedsTwoOfThemFlat); with the second bar starting at (11,-3.25,0.5), length 2, and the first from (9,-3.25,0.5), they refuse that or Undecidable; at 72cc2f6c all were refused CannotNameWhichPieceOfACrossingThisIs at intersect. a_half_of_an_ellipse in fuse/arrange.rs knows a half only by picker normals bit-equal to plus or minus the stored directions, so these halves get no node where they meet: place_the_meets falls to place_where_two_rulings_cross. Recognise a half by which pipe each picker leans towards, asked exactly, take the two axis directions from the pipes in place_the_middle_of_each_half, and fix the docs of both that say the pickers face along the stored directions. That may not be all: the bar turned 120 degrees through (11,0,0), whose pickers are its stored directions, is refused WhereThreeSurfacesMeetNeedsTwoOfThemFlat before and after Q1625, untraced; trace it (Q1292 is a lead, not run). Pin the six fuses of each in the new test file §31
    Q1656 1    dropped:§31    opus    crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/,crates/rcad-topo/tests/two_equal_bars_leaning_at_each_other.rs   refuses/B Two bars of radius 1/4 through (11,0,0), the second 32 long turned 120 degrees about z, stored direction (-0.5,0.866,-2.2e-16) (a_long_bar_through_another_at_120_degrees_is_refused_meshing_or_building_a_wire in two_equal_bars_leaning_at_each_other.rs), get past the arrangement since Q1646. All six fuses are refused CannotMeshAFace CornersCoincide, AWireWillNotLieFlatOnItsFace or ACurvedFaceGoesRightRoundWithNoSeam. Untraced. A lead, not run: the way out of a point where the two pipes touch is read in assemble.rs the_way_across_the_chord_into_the_arc off grad S x grad T, which is nought there but for rounding, the step Q1622 names. Trace it after Q1622 lands §31
    Q1990  5  parked:§450  opus  crates/rcad-topo/tests/two_equal_bars_leaning_at_each_other.rs  refuses/C from/- parked on the family's file, so no member §450
    Q1991  2  todo  opus  crates/rcad-topo/tests/fuses_that_must_agree.rs#a_pair,crates/rcad-topo/tests/fuses_that_must_agree.rs#another_pair  from/- one file named twice, one row §450
    Q1992  2  todo  opus  crates/rcad-topo/tests/fuses_that_must_agree.rs  from/- a second row on the file §450
    MD
    family='4 crates/rcad-topo/tests/two_equal_bars_leaning_at_each_other.rs Q1793(1) Q1896(1) Q1897(1) Q1929(1)'
    drive families families
    grep -qxF "$family" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "queue families did not find 16eab121's family on two_equal_bars_leaning_at_each_other.rs, its four open rows and no closed or parked one"
    grep -qF 'fuses_that_must_agree.rs' <<<"$got" \
        && fail "queue families counted a row naming one file by two items as two rows"
    grep -qxF '1 family with no trace (§450.3)' <<<"$got" || fail "queue families did not end with how many families it found"
    drive families families --count
    [ "$got" = 1 ] || fail "queue families --count did not print the number of families alone"
    drive families
    grep -qxF 'families with no trace: 1 (§450.3); just queue families lists them' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the plain queue does not count the families"

    more() { sed '$d' "$dir/$1.md" > "$dir/$2.md"; cat >> "$dir/$2.md"; echo '```' >> "$dir/$2.md"; }
    more families families-traced <<'MD'
    Q1953  1  done:abcdef0  opus  -  from/- Trace the family on crates/rcad-topo/tests/two_equal_bars_leaning_at_each_other.rs: Q1896, Q1897, Q1929 and Q1793, under Policy::certified() §450
    MD
    drive families-traced families
    [ "$got" = '0 families with no trace (§450.3)' ] || fail "a closed trace naming the family's four rows did not take the family out"
    drive families-traced
    grep -qxF 'families with no trace: 0 (§450.3); just queue families lists them' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the plain queue still counts a family a trace has covered"

    more families-traced families-again <<'MD'
    Q2001  1  todo  opus  crates/rcad-topo/tests/two_equal_bars_leaning_at_each_other.rs  from/- a new row on the traced file §450
    Q2002  1  todo  opus  crates/rcad-topo/tests/two_equal_bars_leaning_at_each_other.rs#a_test  from/- another §450
    Q2003  1  todo  opus  crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/,crates/rcad-topo/tests/two_equal_bars_leaning_at_each_other.rs  from/- a third §450
    Q2004  1  todo  opus  crates/rcad-topo/tests/two_equal_bars_leaning_at_each_other.rs,crates/rcad-topo/tests/a_third_file.rs  from/- a row a trace on another file named §450
    Q2005  1  todo  opus  crates/rcad-topo/tests/a_third_file.rs  from/- the third file's second row §450
    Q2006  1  todo  opus  crates/rcad-topo/tests/a_third_file.rs  from/- the third file's third row §450
    Q2007  1  todo  opus  -  pinned/1 from/- Trace the family on crates/rcad-topo/tests/another_file.rs: Q2004 §450
    MD
    drive families-again families
    [ "$got" = "$(printf '%s\n' \
        '4 crates/rcad-topo/tests/two_equal_bars_leaning_at_each_other.rs Q2001(1) Q2002(1) Q2003(1) Q2004(1)' \
        '3 crates/rcad-topo/tests/a_third_file.rs Q2004(1) Q2005(1) Q2006(1)' \
        '2 families with no trace (§450.3)')" ] \
        || fail "a traced file was not a family again with the three rows no trace named, plus the row only a trace on another file named, which a trace on another file does not take out of any file's family"

    # A row's own from/ or was/ link can reach a trace several hops back, not
    # just directly: the trace's own fix row, a member it re-filed, a chain of
    # four links, and a chain that lands on a trace on a different file, which
    # is no match and leaves the row a member (§450.3 rule 4).
    fence familieslinks <<'MD'
    Q3001  1  done:abcdef0  opus  -  from/- Trace the family on crates/rcad-topo/tests/a_chain_of_links_reaches_its_trace.rs: Q3002 §450
    Q3002  1  dropped:§450  opus  -  refuses/B the original member, since re-filed as Q3003 §450
    Q3003  1  todo  opus  crates/rcad-topo/tests/a_chain_of_links_reaches_its_trace.rs  was/Q3002 the member the trace re-filed §450
    Q3005  1  todo  opus  crates/rcad-topo/tests/a_chain_of_links_reaches_its_trace.rs  from/Q3001 the trace's own fix row §450
    Q3009  1  todo  opus  -  from/Q3001 one hop from the trace §450
    Q3008  1  todo  opus  -  from/Q3009 two hops from the trace §450
    Q3007  1  todo  opus  -  from/Q3008 three hops from the trace §450
    Q3006  1  todo  opus  crates/rcad-topo/tests/a_chain_of_links_reaches_its_trace.rs  from/Q3007 four hops from the trace §450
    Q3010  1  done:bbbbbbb  opus  -  from/- Trace the family on crates/rcad-topo/tests/a_trace_on_a_different_file.rs: Q3011 §450
    Q3012  1  todo  opus  crates/rcad-topo/tests/a_chain_of_links_reaches_its_trace.rs  from/Q3010 reaches a trace on another file and stays a member §450
    Q3013  1  todo  opus  crates/rcad-topo/tests/a_chain_of_links_reaches_its_trace.rs  from/- a plain new row on the file §450
    Q3014  1  todo  opus  crates/rcad-topo/tests/a_chain_of_links_reaches_its_trace.rs  from/- another plain new row on the file §450
    MD
    drive familieslinks families
    [ "$got" = "$(printf '%s\n' \
        '3 crates/rcad-topo/tests/a_chain_of_links_reaches_its_trace.rs Q3012(1) Q3013(1) Q3014(1)' \
        '1 family with no trace (§450.3)')" ] \
        || fail "a from/ or was/ chain did not reach the trace it should, or excluded a row that only reaches a trace on another file"

    bundled() {
        got=$("$j" --set queue_file "$dir/$1.md" --set queue_running 1 --set queue_bundle "$2" queue "${@:3}" 2>&1) \
            || fail "queue ${*:3} failed on the $1 fixture with bundles of $2"
    }

    fence full <<'MD'
    T1  1  todo  opus  docs/a.md  wrong/A a wrong answer a real file met §230
    Q1  2  todo  sonnet  docs/b.md  small one test §230
    Q2  2.5  todo  sonnet  docs/b.md  small a stale claim in the same file §230
    Q3  3  todo  sonnet  docs/c.md  small a few-line fix §230
    Q4  3  todo  sonnet  docs/d.md  small a fourth, past the bundle §230
    MD
    bundled full 3 next 2
    [ "$(takes)" = "Q1,Q2,Q3 T1 " ] || fail "a full bundle of small rows did not go ahead of tier 1"
    grep -qxF 'take Q1,Q2,Q3 (a bundle of 3 small rows, one lane)' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a bundle's take line does not name its rows and say it is one lane"
    grep -qxF '    Q2  2.5  todo  sonnet  docs/b.md  small a stale claim in the same file §230' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a bundle does not print each of its rows, indented, under its take line"
    grep -qx 'took 2 of 2' <<<"$got" || fail "a bundle was not counted as one slot"
    bundled full 3 next 3
    [ "$(takes)" = "Q1,Q2,Q3 T1 Q4 " ] || fail "the small row left over was not taken once the walk reached it"
    grep -qxF 'take Q4 (a bundle of 1 small row, one lane)' <<<"$got" || fail "a small row alone was not printed as a bundle"

    fence pinned <<'MD'
    Q1  1  todo  opus  docs/a.md  wrong/A a wrong answer a real file met §230
    Q2  1  todo  opus  docs/b.md  pinned/2 the second pin, written first §230
    Q3  1  todo  opus  docs/c.md  pinned/1 wrong/A the first pin, written second §230
    Q6  1  lane:lane-q6  opus  docs/f.md  running §230
    Q7  1  todo  opus  docs/f.md  pinned/3 a pin the running lane holds back §230
    Q4  2  todo  sonnet  docs/d.md  small one test §230
    Q5  3  todo  sonnet  docs/e.md  small another test §230
    MD
    bundled pinned 2 next 4
    [ "$(takes)" = "Q3 Q2 Q4,Q5 Q1 " ] \
        || fail "the pinned rows were not taken first, by number and not by line, ahead of the full bundle and of wrong/A"
    grep -qxF "skip Q7 (tier 1): its docs/f.md meets Q6's docs/f.md, held by lane:lane-q6" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a pinned row a running lane holds back was not skipped with the lane named"
    bundled pinned 2
    grep -qxF 'tier 1, open and not parked: 2 in A or B (2 wrong, 0 crash, 0 refuses), 3 with no class; tier 5: 0 in C, 0 in D, 0 with no class; 2 small rows ready, and a bundle is 2' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the measure line did not read the class after a pin"

    # §430.7 (Q2328): Q729, pinned on all of a tests/ directory, was skipped
    # four fills running for a lane on one file there, while each fill took
    # another row on a file there. Every row the fill reaches after a skipped
    # pinned row now stays out of its write set.
    fence pinwait <<'MD'
    Q1  1  lane:lane-q1  opus  crates/t/tests/a.rs  running, on one file in the pinned row's directory §430
    Q2  1  todo  opus  crates/t/tests/,crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/arrange.rs  pinned/1 a pin on the whole directory, which the running lane holds back §430
    Q3  1  todo  opus  crates/t/tests/b.rs  pinned/2 a later pin on another file in the directory §430
    Q4  1  todo  opus  crates/t/tests/c.rs  wrong/A a lower row on a third file in it §430
    Q5  1  todo  opus  crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/arrange.rs  the first lower row on a shared file the pin names §430
    Q6  1  todo  opus  crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/arrange.rs  a second, once the pin and Q5 hold the file §430
    Q7  2  todo  sonnet  crates/t/tests/d.rs  small a small row in the directory, which would complete a full bundle §430
    Q8  2  todo  sonnet  docs/a.md  small a small row elsewhere §430
    Q9  3  todo  opus  docs/b.md  a lower row that meets no pin §430
    MD
    bundled pinwait 2 next 4
    [ "$(takes)" = "Q5 Q8 Q9 " ] \
        || fail "a row the fill reaches after a pinned row a running lane holds back was taken into that pinned row's write set, or one that meets no pin was not taken"
    [ "$(grep '^skip ' <<<"$got")" = "$(printf '%s\n' \
        "skip Q2 (tier 1): its crates/t/tests/ meets Q1's crates/t/tests/a.rs, held by lane:lane-q1" \
        "skip Q3 (tier 1): its crates/t/tests/b.rs meets Q2's crates/t/tests/, pinned and skipped above" \
        "skip Q4 (tier 1): its crates/t/tests/c.rs meets Q2's crates/t/tests/, pinned and skipped above" \
        "skip Q6 (tier 1): its crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/arrange.rs is shared, and Q5 (taken above) and Q2 (pinned and skipped above) already hold it" \
        "skip Q7 (tier 2): its crates/t/tests/d.rs meets Q2's crates/t/tests/, pinned and skipped above")" ] \
        || fail "the rows a skipped pinned row holds back were not each skipped with the pinned row named, a later pin and a third holder of a shared file it names among them"
    grep -qx 'took 3 of 4: every other ready row meets a running lane, a row taken above or a pinned row skipped above' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a fill a skipped pinned row cut short did not say a pinned row is among the reasons"
    drive pinwait blocked
    [ "$got" = "skip Q2 (tier 1): its crates/t/tests/ meets Q1's crates/t/tests/a.rs, held by lane:lane-q1" ] \
        || fail "blocked no longer names the running lane alone, the two line shapes the queue page reads"

    fence partial <<'MD'
    Q1  1  todo  opus  docs/a.md  tier one §230
    Q2  2  todo  sonnet  docs/b.md  small one test §230
    Q3  2  todo  opus  docs/c.md  tier two, between the small rows §230
    Q4  3  todo  sonnet  docs/d.md  small a stale claim §230
    Q5  4  todo  sonnet  docs/b.md  small another test in Q2's file §230
    MD
    drive partial next 3
    [ "$(takes)" = "Q1 Q2,Q4,Q5 Q3 " ] \
        || fail "three small rows, short of a bundle of 5, did not wait for tier 2 and then go together"

    fence smallheld <<'MD'
    Q1  1  lane:lane-q1  opus  docs/a.md  running §230
    T2  1  todo  opus  docs/c.md  tier one §230
    Q2  2  todo  sonnet  docs/a.md  small a test in the running lane's file §230
    Q3  2  todo  sonnet  docs/b.md  small a test elsewhere §230
    MD
    bundled smallheld 2 next 2
    [ "$(takes)" = "T2 Q3 " ] || fail "a small row a running lane blocks completed a bundle or joined one"
    grep -qxF "skip Q2 (tier 2): its docs/a.md meets Q1's docs/a.md, held by lane:lane-q1" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a small row meeting a running lane was not skipped with the lane named"

    # Q1932, §447.3: one of the free slots is kept for a Sonnet row while one
    # is ready and no running lane is a Sonnet row. Without the rule, three
    # ready Opus rows across three tiers fill all three slots; with it, the
    # last slot goes to the ready Sonnet row instead.
    fence sonnetkept <<'MD'
    Q1  1  todo  opus  docs/a.md  tier one §230
    Q2  2  todo  opus  docs/b.md  tier two §230
    Q3  3  todo  opus  docs/c.md  tier three §230
    Q4  4  todo  sonnet  docs/d.md  tier four, the sonnet row §230
    MD
    drive sonnetkept next 3
    [ "$(takes)" = "Q1 Q2 Q4 " ] \
        || fail "the last free slot did not go to the ready Sonnet row ahead of the third Opus row"

    fence nosonnetkept <<'MD'
    Q1  1  todo  opus  docs/a.md  tier one §230
    Q2  2  todo  opus  docs/b.md  tier two §230
    Q3  3  todo  opus  docs/c.md  tier three §230
    MD
    drive nosonnetkept next 3
    [ "$(takes)" = "Q1 Q2 Q3 " ] \
        || fail "with no Sonnet row ready, the old all-Opus fill was not restored"

    # A lane already running a Sonnet row satisfies 447.3 on its own; the rule
    # does not then reserve a second slot for another ready Sonnet row.
    fence sonnetrunning <<'MD'
    Q1  1  lane:lane-q1  sonnet  docs/a.md  running §230
    Q2  2  todo  opus  docs/b.md  tier two §230
    Q3  3  todo  opus  docs/c.md  tier three §230
    Q4  4  todo  sonnet  docs/d.md  tier four, the sonnet row §230
    MD
    drive sonnetrunning next 2
    [ "$(takes)" = "Q2 Q3 " ] \
        || fail "a running Sonnet lane still had a second slot reserved for a ready Sonnet row"

    # The same rule applies to a full bundle taken ahead of tier order: a
    # bundle of small Opus rows that would exactly fill the single free slot
    # does not take it while a Sonnet row is ready and no running lane is one.
    fence sonnetfullbundle <<'MD'
    Q1  2  todo  opus  docs/a.md  small one test §230
    Q2  2  todo  opus  docs/b.md  small another test §230
    Q3  2  todo  opus  docs/c.md  small a third test §230
    Q4  3  todo  sonnet  docs/d.md  tier three, the sonnet row §230
    MD
    bundled sonnetfullbundle 3 next 1
    [ "$(takes)" = "Q4 " ] \
        || fail "a full bundle of small Opus rows took the one free slot ahead of a ready Sonnet row"

    fence sonnetfullbundlepure <<'MD'
    Q1  2  todo  sonnet  docs/a.md  small one test §230
    Q2  2  todo  sonnet  docs/b.md  small another test §230
    Q3  2  todo  sonnet  docs/c.md  small a third test §230
    MD
    bundled sonnetfullbundlepure 3 next 1
    [ "$(takes)" = "Q1,Q2,Q3 " ] \
        || fail "a full bundle that is itself all Sonnet was not taken for the one free slot"

    fence badtier <<'MD'
    Q1  1  todo  opus  docs/a.md  a row the fill knows §230
    Q2  7  todo  opus  docs/b.md  a tier the fill does not know §230
    MD
    got=$("$j" --set queue_file "$dir/badtier.md" --set queue_running 1 queue next 2 2>&1) \
        && fail "the fill took from a file holding a row of tier 7, rather than refuse it"
    grep -qF 'cannot read the tier "7"' <<<"$got" || fail "the fill did not say which tier it could not read"

    fence lane <<'MD'
    Q1  1  lane:lane-q1  opus  justfile#queue  running §230
    Q2  1  todo  opus  justfile#queue  meets the running lane §230
    Q3  2  todo  opus  docs/  fits beside it §230
    MD
    drive lane next 1
    grep -qxF "skip Q2 (tier 1): its justfile#queue meets Q1's justfile#queue, held by lane:lane-q1" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a row meeting a running lane was not skipped with the lane named"
    [ "$(takes)" = "Q3 " ] || fail "the slot a blocked tier 1 row left did not go to tier 2"
    drive lane next 6
    grep -qx 'note: 1 running and 6 more is past the 4 slots of §397.7' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a fill past the slots did not say so, citing the section that sets them"

    fence items <<'MD'
    Q1  1  todo  opus  crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/select.rs#a  one function §230
    Q2  1  todo  opus  crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/select.rs#b  another function §230
    Q3  1  todo  opus  crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/select.rs#a  the first function again §230
    MD
    drive items next 3
    [ "$(takes)" = "Q1 Q2 " ] || fail "two different items of one file were not both taken"
    grep -qxF "skip Q3 (tier 1): its crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/select.rs#a meets Q1's crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/select.rs#a, taken above" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the same item twice was not skipped against the row taken above"
    grep -q '^took 2 of 3: every other ready row meets' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a short fill did not say why it was short"

    # §424.4: `arrange.rs` and `assemble.rs` are on `queue_shared_files`, so a
    # second lane into either is not a meet, and only the third is.
    fence shared <<'MD'
    Q1  1  lane:lane-q1  opus  crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/arrange.rs  the first lane on a shared file §230
    Q2  1  todo  opus  crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/arrange.rs  a second lane into the same shared file §230
    Q3  1  todo  opus  crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/arrange.rs  a third, once two already hold it §230
    MD
    drive shared next 2
    [ "$(takes)" = "Q2 " ] || fail "a shared file with one running lane did not let a second lane in"
    grep -qxF "skip Q3 (tier 1): its crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/arrange.rs is shared, and Q1 (held by lane:lane-q1) and Q2 (taken above) already hold it" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a shared file already held by a running lane and a row taken above did not keep a third out, naming both"

    # §424.8's bug (Q1850): a shared meet on the first entry a candidate lists
    # must not stop the fill short of a plain meet on a later entry. Both rows
    # hold `arrange.rs` and the same test file, `arrange.rs` listed first.
    fence sharedplus <<'MD'
    Q1  1  lane:lane-q1  opus  crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/arrange.rs,crates/rcad-topo/tests/two_arcs_where_two_curved_surfaces_cross.rs  the first lane, on the shared file and a test file §424
    Q2  1  todo  opus  crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/arrange.rs,crates/rcad-topo/tests/two_arcs_where_two_curved_surfaces_cross.rs  lists the shared file first, but shares the test file too §424
    MD
    drive sharedplus next 1
    [ "$(takes)" = "" ] || fail "a shared meet on the first entry hid a plain meet on an entry later in the list"
    grep -qxF "skip Q2 (tier 1): its crates/rcad-topo/tests/two_arcs_where_two_curved_surfaces_cross.rs meets Q1's crates/rcad-topo/tests/two_arcs_where_two_curved_surfaces_cross.rs, held by lane:lane-q1" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a shared meet did not let the fill go on to check the write sets' other entries"

    # A directory above a shared file is not itself a shared entry: it still
    # meets every row under it at once, both files on the list included.
    fence shareddir <<'MD'
    Q1  1  lane:lane-dir  opus  crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/  a directory holding two shared files §230
    Q2  1  todo  opus  crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/arrange.rs  blocked by the directory, not shared §230
    Q3  1  todo  opus  crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/assemble.rs  blocked by the directory too §230
    MD
    drive shareddir next 2
    [ "$(takes)" = "" ] || fail "a directory holding shared files let a row into one of them anyway"
    grep -qxF "skip Q2 (tier 1): its crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/arrange.rs meets Q1's crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/, held by lane:lane-dir" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a directory holding a shared file did not block a row naming the file at once"
    grep -qxF "skip Q3 (tier 1): its crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/assemble.rs meets Q1's crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/, held by lane:lane-dir" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a directory holding a shared file did not block a second row naming the other shared file too"

    # A file left off queue_shared_files keeps §230.3's rule: one holder blocks
    # the next.
    fence notshared <<'MD'
    Q1  1  lane:lane-q1  opus  crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/select.rs  a lane on a file not on the shared list §230
    Q2  1  todo  opus  crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/select.rs  blocked by the one lane already holding it §230
    MD
    drive notshared next 1
    [ "$(takes)" = "" ] || fail "a file left off queue_shared_files let a second lane in after only one holder"
    grep -qxF "skip Q2 (tier 1): its crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/select.rs meets Q1's crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/select.rs, held by lane:lane-q1" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a file not on the shared list did not keep one holder blocking the next"

    fence dirs <<'MD'
    Q1  1  lane:lane-dir  opus  crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/  holds a directory §230
    Q2  1  lane:lane-file  opus  docs/plan.md  holds a file §230
    Q3  1  todo  opus  crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/arrange.rs  a file in the held directory §230
    Q4  1  todo  opus  docs/  the directory of a held file §230
    Q5  1  todo  opus  crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse-extra/x.rs  a name that only starts the same §230
    MD
    drive dirs next 3
    grep -q '^skip Q3 .* held by lane:lane-dir$' <<<"$got" || fail "a held directory did not block a file in it"
    grep -q '^skip Q4 .* held by lane:lane-file$' <<<"$got" || fail "a held file did not block its directory"
    [ "$(takes)" = "Q5 " ] || fail "a directory blocked a path that only shares the start of its name"

    fence dash <<'MD'
    Q1  1  lane:lane-docs  opus  docs/  holds a directory §230
    Q2  1  todo  opus  -  writes only a section of its own §230
    Q3  1  todo  opus  -  and so does this one §230
    MD
    drive dash next 2
    [ "$(takes)" = "Q2 Q3 " ] || fail "a row writing nothing tracked did not fit beside everything"

    fence after <<'MD'
    Q1  1  lane:lane-y  opus  docs/a.md  running §230
    Q9  1  done:abcdef0  -  -  finished §230
    Q2  1  after:Q1  opus  docs/b.md  waits on the running one §230
    Q3  2  after:Q9  opus  docs/c.md  waits on the finished one §230
    MD
    drive after ready
    [ "$got" = "Q3  2  after:Q9  opus  docs/c.md  waits on the finished one §230" ] \
        || fail "ready is not exactly the row waiting on a done one"
    drive after
    grep -qxF '      waits on Q1 (lane:lane-y)' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a waiting row does not say what it waits on and that row's state"

    fence blocked <<'MD'
    Q1  1  lane:lane-q1  opus  docs/a.md  running §230
    Q2  2  todo  opus  docs/a.md  meets the running lane §230
    Q3  2  todo  opus  docs/b.md  meets nothing, so a fill takes it §230
    Q4  3  todo  opus  docs/b.md  meets only Q3, which a fill takes above it §230
    Q5  3  after:Q1  opus  docs/a.md  waits, so it is not ready §230
    Q6  3  parked:§230  opus  docs/a.md  parked, so not ready either §230
    T7  1  todo  opus  docs/  the directory of the running lane's file §230
    MD
    drive blocked next 4
    grep -qxF "skip Q4 (tier 3): its docs/b.md meets Q3's docs/b.md, taken above" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "next does not pass Q4 over as taken above, so blocked leaving it out would prove nothing"
    drive blocked blocked
    [ "$got" = "$(printf '%s\n' \
        "skip T7 (tier 1): its docs/ meets Q1's docs/a.md, held by lane:lane-q1" \
        "skip Q2 (tier 2): its docs/a.md meets Q1's docs/a.md, held by lane:lane-q1")" ] \
        || fail "blocked is not exactly the skip line of each ready row the running lane holds back, in the fill's order"

    # Q1505: `add` refuses a description whose first word is a class (§352)
    # with punctuation stuck on after it, and still tags a row whose first
    # word is exactly a class, with nothing after it, as today.
    cp "$dir/good.md" "$dir/addfix.md"
    qf() { "$j" --justfile '{{ justfile() }}' --working-directory "$repo" --set queue_file "$dir/addfix.md" queue "$@"; }

    got=$(qf add 1 opus - 'wrong/B: a wrong answer with a colon stuck on §230' 2>&1) \
        && fail "add accepted 'wrong/B:' as a class with punctuation after it"
    grep -qF "'wrong/B:' is a class with punctuation after it; write exactly 'wrong/B', with nothing after it" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "add did not explain the colon refusal"

    got=$(qf add 5 opus - 'refuses/C, a refusal with a comma stuck on §230' 2>&1) \
        && fail "add accepted 'refuses/C,' as a class with punctuation after it"
    grep -qF "'refuses/C,' is a class with punctuation after it; write exactly 'refuses/C', with nothing after it" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "add did not explain the comma refusal"

    got=$(qf add 1 opus - 'wrong/B from/- a tier one row with its class exactly §230' 2>&1) \
        || fail "add refused a class with nothing stuck after it"
    grep -q "^$got .*wrong/B from/- a tier one row with its class exactly §230\$" "$dir/addfix.md" \
        || fail "add did not write the row with its class still tagging it"

    # §432: a new row says where it came from, and the word is written after
    # the tag whichever of the two came first, since just land puts it in front.
    got=$(qf add 2 sonnet - 'a row that says nothing of where it came from §230' 2>&1) \
        && fail "add accepted a row that does not say where it came from"
    grep -qF 'a new row says where it came from' <<<"$got" || fail "add did not say why it refused a row with no from/"
    got=$(qf add 1 opus - 'from/T9 refuses/B a defect the first row found §230' 2>&1) \
        || fail "add refused a row whose from/ came before its class"
    grep -q "^$got .*  refuses/B from/T9 a defect the first row found §230\$" "$dir/addfix.md" \
        || fail "add did not move from/T9 to after the class"
    got=$(qf add 2 sonnet - 'small was/D1 the dropped row filed again §230' 2>&1) \
        || fail "add refused a re-file written after its tag"
    grep -q "^$got .*  small was/D1 the dropped row filed again §230\$" "$dir/addfix.md" \
        || fail "add did not keep was/D1 after small"
    got=$(qf add 2 sonnet - 'from/Q404 a row from a row that exists nowhere §230' 2>&1) \
        && fail "add accepted from/ naming a row that exists nowhere"
    grep -qF "'from/Q404' names Q404, which exists nowhere" <<<"$got" || fail "add did not name the missing row"
    got=$(qf add 2 sonnet - 'was/- a re-file of nothing §230' 2>&1) \
        && fail "add accepted was/-"
    grep -qF "'was/-' is not where a row came from" <<<"$got" || fail "add did not refuse was/- by name"
    got=$(qf add 1 opus - 'from/- refuses/C: a class with a colon behind the word §230' 2>&1) \
        && fail "add accepted 'refuses/C:' behind from/-"
    grep -qF "'refuses/C:' is a class with punctuation after it" <<<"$got" || fail "add did not check the class behind from/-"
    got=$(qf add 1 opus - 'from/- pinned/9 a pin behind the word §230' 2>&1) \
        && fail "add accepted a pin behind from/-"
    grep -qF 'only Mattias pins a row' <<<"$got" || fail "add did not refuse the pin behind from/-"

    # §433: a row can come from a fuzz target or a file of the STEP corpus, and
    # `add` checks the name against this tree (§433.4). The scratch repository
    # gets one target and three corpus files, two of them one NIST name used
    # twice, under directories with a space in their names.
    mkdir -p "$repo/fuzz/fuzz_targets" "$repo/corpus"
    touch "$repo/fuzz/fuzz_targets/where_two_surfaces_cross.rs"
    printf '0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef  %s\n' \
        'kicad/LED_D5.0mm.step' \
        'nist/ftc-ctc/NIST-FTC-CTC-PMI-CAD-models/CTC Definitions/nist_ctc_01_asme1_rd.stp' \
        'nist/pmi-step-files/NIST-PMI-STEP-Files/AP203 geometry only/nist_ctc_01_asme1_rd.stp' \
        > "$repo/corpus/manifest.sha256"
    got=$(qf add 1 opus - 'from/fuzz:where_two_surfaces_cross crash/A a panic a crash file shows §230' 2>&1) \
        || fail "add refused a row from a fuzz target that is there"
    grep -q "^$got .*  crash/A from/fuzz:where_two_surfaces_cross a panic a crash file shows §230\$" "$dir/addfix.md" \
        || fail "add did not move from/fuzz: to after the class"
    got=$(qf add 1 opus - 'wrong/B from/step:LED_D5.0mm.step a wrong answer a corpus file shows §230' 2>&1) \
        || fail "add refused a row from a corpus file named by its own name, or the colon in it tripped the refusal of a class with punctuation (Q1505)"
    grep -q "^$got .*  wrong/B from/step:LED_D5.0mm.step a wrong answer a corpus file shows §230\$" "$dir/addfix.md" \
        || fail "add did not keep from/step: after the class"
    got=$(qf add 2 sonnet - 'from/step:CTC_Definitions/nist_ctc_01_asme1_rd.stp a NIST name used twice keeps its directory §230' 2>&1) \
        || fail "add refused a corpus name that keeps one directory, with the space in it written _"
    got=$(qf add 2 sonnet - 'from/fuzz:where_no_target_is a target with no .rs §230' 2>&1) \
        && fail "add accepted a fuzz target with no .rs under fuzz/fuzz_targets/"
    grep -qF "'from/fuzz:where_no_target_is' names no fuzz target" <<<"$got" || fail "add did not say the target is not there"
    got=$(qf add 2 sonnet - 'from/night:10-11 a kind of input the queue does not know §230' 2>&1) \
        && fail "add accepted a kind of input it does not know"
    grep -qF "'from/night:10-11' is not where a row came from" <<<"$got" || fail "add did not refuse the unknown kind by name"
    got=$(qf add 2 sonnet - 'from/step:nist_ctc_01_asme1_rd.stp a NIST name without its directory §230' 2>&1) \
        && fail "add accepted a corpus name that ends two paths"
    grep -qF "'from/step:nist_ctc_01_asme1_rd.stp' names 2 files of the STEP corpus" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "add did not say the name ends two paths"
    grep -qF 'from/step:AP203_geometry_only/nist_ctc_01_asme1_rd.stp' <<<"$got" || fail "add did not name a name that would do"
    got=$(qf add 2 sonnet - 'from/step:D5.0mm.step the end of a file name, not cut after a / §230' 2>&1) \
        && fail "add accepted a corpus name that does not start just after a /"
    grep -qF "'from/step:D5.0mm.step' names no file of the STEP corpus" <<<"$got" || fail "add did not refuse a name cut inside a file name"
    got=$(qf add 2 sonnet - 'from/step:kicad/LED_D5.0mm.step more of the path than names the file §230' 2>&1) \
        && fail "add accepted a corpus name longer than it needs"
    grep -qF 'write it as from/step:LED_D5.0mm.step' <<<"$got" || fail "add did not give the shorter name"
    got=$(qf add 2 sonnet - 'was/fuzz:where_two_surfaces_cross a re-file of a target §230' 2>&1) \
        && fail "add accepted was/ naming a fuzz target"
    grep -qF "'was/fuzz:where_two_surfaces_cross' is not where a row came from" <<<"$got" || fail "add did not refuse was/fuzz: by name"
    got=$(qf add 1 opus - 'from/fuzz:where_two_surfaces_cross refuses/C: a class with a colon behind the word §230' 2>&1) \
        && fail "add accepted 'refuses/C:' behind from/fuzz:"
    grep -qF "'refuses/C:' is a class with punctuation after it" <<<"$got" || fail "add did not check the class behind from/fuzz:"

    # Q1436: `take` lets a stale `lane:` state in another worktree's copy
    # through when main's own history of the queue file already held that
    # exact state for the row, and refuses one main's history never showed
    # for it, which looks like a take racing this one.
    hub="$dir/q1436-hub"
    g() { git -C "$1" -c user.name=selftest -c user.email=selftest@invalid "${@:2}"; }
    mkdir -p "$hub/docs/decisions.d"
    printf '## 230. One queue\n' > "$hub/docs/decisions.d/sections.md"
    cat > "$hub/docs/queue.md" <<'MD'
    # Queue

    ## Tier 1

    ```
    Q1  1  todo  opus  docs/a.md  a row to take §230
    ```

    ## Closed

    ```
    ```
    MD
    touch "$hub/docs/a.md"
    g "$hub" init -q -b main
    g "$hub" add -A
    g "$hub" commit -q -m root
    sed -i 's/^Q1  1  todo /Q1  1  lane:lane-old /' "$hub/docs/queue.md"
    g "$hub" add -A
    g "$hub" commit -q -m take
    sed -i 's/^Q1  1  lane:lane-old /Q1  1  todo /' "$hub/docs/queue.md"
    g "$hub" add -A
    g "$hub" commit -q -m release

    a="$dir/q1436-a"
    b="$dir/q1436-b"
    stale="$dir/q1436-stale"
    racer="$dir/q1436-racer"
    g "$hub" worktree add -q -b q1436-a "$a" main
    g "$hub" worktree add -q -b q1436-b "$b" main
    g "$hub" worktree add -q -b q1436-stale "$stale" main
    g "$hub" worktree add -q -b q1436-racer "$racer" main
    sed -i 's/^Q1  1  todo /Q1  1  lane:lane-old /' "$stale/docs/queue.md"
    sed -i 's/^Q1  1  todo /Q1  1  lane:lane-racer /' "$racer/docs/queue.md"

    got=$("$j" --justfile '{{ justfile() }}' --working-directory "$b" queue take Q1 lane-new-b 2>&1) \
        && fail "take let through lane:lane-racer, a state main's history never held for Q1"
    grep -qF "Q1 is lane:lane-racer in $racer's copy, a branch main has never shown for it, so this looks like a take racing this one" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "take did not name the racing copy and the state main never showed"
    grep -q '^Q1  1  todo ' "$b/docs/queue.md" || fail "a refused take wrote Q1's file anyway"

    g "$hub" worktree remove --force "$racer"

    got=$("$j" --justfile '{{ justfile() }}' --working-directory "$a" queue take Q1 lane-new-a 2>&1) \
        || fail "take refused a stale copy showing lane:lane-old, a state main's history already held for Q1"
    grep -q '^Q1  1  lane:lane-new-a ' "$a/docs/queue.md" \
        || fail "take did not write lane:lane-new-a once the only other lane: state left was one main's history held"

    # Q2315: a wait on a row held only by another branch, never merged into
    # this copy's own history, still gets §435.4's refusal or §435.6's move.
    # `exists_elsewhere` lets `after` name such a row at all; `open_blockers`
    # must then read its tier from wherever that found it, not from this
    # copy alone, or the refusal and the move both go missing.
    hub2="$dir/q2315-hub"
    mkdir -p "$hub2/docs/decisions.d"
    printf '## 230. One queue\n' > "$hub2/docs/decisions.d/sections.md"
    cat > "$hub2/docs/queue.md" <<'MD'
    # Queue

    ## Tier 1

    ```
    T1  1  todo  opus  docs/a.md  refuses/B from/- a classed row that would wait across tiers §230
    T2  1  todo  opus  docs/a.md  from/- an unclassed row that would wait across tiers §230
    ```

    ## Closed

    ```
    ```
    MD
    touch "$hub2/docs/a.md"
    g "$hub2" init -q -b main
    g "$hub2" add -A
    g "$hub2" commit -q -m root

    tier2_wt="$dir/q2315-tier2"
    g "$hub2" worktree add -q -b q2315-tier2 "$tier2_wt" main
    cat > "$tier2_wt/docs/queue.md" <<'MD'
    # Queue

    ## Tier 1

    ```
    T1  1  todo  opus  docs/a.md  refuses/B from/- a classed row that would wait across tiers §230
    T2  1  todo  opus  docs/a.md  from/- an unclassed row that would wait across tiers §230
    ```

    ## Tier 2

    ```
    Q1  2  todo  opus  docs/a.md  from/- a tier 2 row held only on another branch §230
    ```

    ## Closed

    ```
    ```
    MD
    g "$tier2_wt" add -A
    g "$tier2_wt" commit -q -m "add Q1, never merged into main"
    g "$hub2" worktree remove --force "$tier2_wt"

    got=$("$j" --justfile '{{ justfile() }}' --working-directory "$hub2" queue after T1 Q1 2>&1) \
        && fail "after moved a classed row to wait on a row held only by another branch"
    grep -qxF "queue after: T1 carries the class refuses/B, which keeps it in tier 1, and it would wait on Q1, which is tier 2: which of the two moves is Mattias's to set (§435.4)" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "after did not read Q1's tier from the branch that holds it"
    grep -q '^T1  1  todo ' "$hub2/docs/queue.md" || fail "a refused after wrote hub2's file anyway"
    got=$("$j" --justfile '{{ justfile() }}' --working-directory "$hub2" queue after T2 Q1 2>&1) \
        || fail "after refused an unclassed row waiting on a row held only by another branch"
    grep -qxF "T2: todo -> after:Q1, tier 1 -> 2, beside Q1, which it waits on" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "after did not move the unclassed row down to Q1's tier"
    grep -q '^T2  2  after:Q1 ' "$hub2/docs/queue.md" \
        || fail "after did not leave T2 at tier 2 in hub2's own file"

    # Q817: `queue help` names every mode and verb the recipe's own case
    # dispatches, read from that case itself, so a verb added later with no
    # help line fails here rather than only going undocumented.
    got=$(q queue help 2>&1) || fail "queue help failed"
    modes=$(sed -n '/^queue mode="" \*args:/,/^[[:space:]]*esac$/p' '{{ justfile() }}' \
        | grep -E '^[[:space:]]*[A-Za-z"][A-Za-z0-9_"|]*\)([[:space:]]*;;)?[[:space:]]*$' \
        | sed -E 's/^[[:space:]]*//; s/\)[[:space:]]*;;[[:space:]]*$//; s/\)[[:space:]]*$//' \
        | tr '|' '\n' \
        | sed -E 's/^"+$//' \
        | sed '/^$/d')
    [ -n "$modes" ] || fail "no verb or mode was read from the queue recipe's own case"
    for m in $modes; do
        grep -qF "queue $m " <<<"$got" || fail "queue help names no line for '$m', which the recipe dispatches"
    done

    # §424.7 (Q1530): `narrow` writes a held row's narrower write set to the
    # claims file, and only inside the set the row was taken with. It reads a
    # claims file of its own here, so no hold of this machine's reaches it.
    claims="$dir/narrow-claims"
    printf 'take\tQ1\t2026-10-12T00:00:00Z\t/a/dispatcher\tlane-q1\ttooling/,justfile\t1\n' > "$claims"
    nq() {
        QUEUE_CLAIMS="$claims" "$j" --justfile '{{ justfile() }}' --working-directory "$repo" \
            --set queue_file "$dir/good.md" queue "$@"
    }
    got=$(nq narrow Q1 tooling/queue/queue.py 2>&1) || fail "narrow refused a file under the directory Q1 was taken with"
    [ "$(tail -n 1 "$claims" | cut -f 1,2,5)" = "$(printf 'narrow\tQ1\ttooling/queue/queue.py')" ] \
        || fail "narrow did not append its event to the claims file"
    got=$(nq running 2>&1) || fail "running failed on a narrowed hold"
    grep -qxF 'Q1 narrowed to tooling/queue/queue.py (taken with tooling/,justfile)' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "running did not say what Q1 was narrowed to"
    got=$(nq show Q1 2>&1) || fail "show failed on a narrowed hold"
    grep -qxF 'narrowed to tooling/queue/queue.py, taken with tooling/,justfile' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "show did not say what Q1 was narrowed to"
    got=$(nq narrow Q9 justfile 2>&1) && fail "narrow accepted Q9, which nobody holds"
    grep -qF 'Q9 is not held, so it has no write set to narrow' <<<"$got" || fail "narrow did not say Q9 is not held"
    got=$(nq narrow Q1 tooling/queue/queue.py,docs/decisions.d/sections.md 2>&1) \
        && fail "narrow accepted an entry outside the set Q1 was taken with"
    grep -qF 'docs/decisions.d/sections.md is outside tooling/,justfile, the write set Q1 was taken with' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "narrow did not name the entry outside the taken set"
    got=$(nq narrow Q1 tooling/queue 2>&1) && fail "narrow accepted tooling/queue, a directory written without its /"
    grep -qF '"tooling/queue" is a directory; write it as tooling/queue/' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "narrow did not say tooling/queue needs its /"
    printf 'take\tQ2\t2026-10-12T00:00:01Z\t/a/dispatcher\tlane-q2\tjustfile#gate\t1\n' >> "$claims"
    got=$(nq narrow Q1 tooling/queue/queue.py,justfile 2>&1) \
        && fail "narrow widened Q1 back to the whole justfile while Q2 holds an item in it"
    grep -qF "skip Q1 (tier -): its justfile meets Q2's justfile#gate, held elsewhere by lane:lane-q2" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "narrow did not quote the fill's line for the hold the widening meets"
    [ "$(tail -n 1 "$claims" | cut -f 1,2)" = "$(printf 'take\tQ2')" ] || fail "a refused narrow wrote the claims file anyway"
    got=$(nq narrow Q1 tooling/queue/queue.py,justfile#queue 2>&1) \
        || fail "narrow refused to widen Q1 back to an item no other hold meets"
    [ "$(tail -n 1 "$claims" | cut -f 1,2,5)" = "$(printf 'narrow\tQ1\ttooling/queue/queue.py,justfile#queue')" ] \
        || fail "narrow did not append the widening it passed"

    # §418.2, §418.3: `queue next` refuses while the latest fuzz night has
    # left a group no row names and no triage row is open, naming
    # `just fuzz-triage-row`; it fills as usual once a triage row is open, or
    # when the night has no verdicts file. `RCAD_FUZZ_LOGS` points at this
    # fixture tree only for this section, never at the real machine's.
    triage_logs="$dir/triage-fuzz-logs"
    main_sha=$(git rev-parse HEAD)
    point_triage_at() {
        printf '2026-10-11 08:15  abc1234 (0 days old, 0 behind main)  5 targets  %s\n' "$triage_logs/$1" >"$triage_logs/latest"
    }
    triage_night() {
        local night=$1
        mkdir -p "$triage_logs/$night"
        printf 'target\tfiles\tstill_fails\tsample\tgroup\n' >"$triage_logs/$night/fuzz-triage-verdicts.tsv"
        printf 't\t3\tyes\t/night/artifacts/t/crash-aaaabbbbccccddddeeeeffff00001111\tpanicked at crates/rcad-geom/src/foo.rs:10:5: a group no row names\n' \
            >>"$triage_logs/$night/fuzz-triage-verdicts.tsv"
        printf 'target\tgroup\tfile\n' >"$triage_logs/$night/fuzz-triage-members.tsv"
        printf 't\tpanicked at crates/rcad-geom/src/foo.rs:10:5: a group no row names\t/night/artifacts/t/crash-aaaabbbbccccddddeeeeffff00001111\n' \
            >>"$triage_logs/$night/fuzz-triage-members.tsv"
        printf '%s\n' "$main_sha" >"$triage_logs/$night/fuzz-triage-replayed-main.txt"
        point_triage_at "$night"
    }

    fence triage-none <<'MD'
    Q90  1  todo  opus  docs/a.md  an unrelated open row §230
    MD
    fence triage-open <<'MD'
    Q90  1  todo  opus  docs/a.md  an unrelated open row §230
    Q91  1  todo  opus  -  from/- Triage the latest fuzz night's groups that still fail on main and that no row names by a crash file, as section 418.3 says. §418.3
    MD

    export RCAD_FUZZ_LOGS="$triage_logs"
    triage_night refuses-night
    got=$("$j" --set queue_file "$dir/triage-none.md" --set queue_running 1 queue next 1 2>&1) \
        && fail "queue next filled while a group no row names stood and no triage row was open"
    grep -qF 'fuzz-triage-row' <<<"$got" || fail "the refusal did not name fuzz-triage-row"
    grep -qF 'named by no row' <<<"$got" || fail "the refusal did not say a group is named by no row"

    drive triage-open next 1
    [ "$(takes)" = "Q90 " ] || fail "queue next did not fill once a triage row (Q91) was already open"

    mkdir -p "$triage_logs/untriaged"
    point_triage_at untriaged
    drive triage-none next 1
    [ "$(takes)" = "Q90 " ] || fail "queue next did not fill when the latest night has no verdicts file"
    grep -qF 'has no fuzz-triage-verdicts.tsv' <<<"$got" || fail "the fill did not say the night has not been triaged"

    point_triage_at refuses-night
    cat > "$dir/triage-filed.md" <<'MD'
    ## Tier 1

    ```
    Q90  1  todo  opus  docs/a.md  an unrelated open row §230
    ```

    ## Closed

    ```
    ```
    MD
    got=$("$j" --justfile '{{ justfile() }}' --working-directory "$repo" fuzz-triage-row "$triage_logs" "$dir/triage-filed.md" 2>&1) \
        || fail "fuzz-triage-row failed when a group no row names stood and no triage row was open"
    filed=$(grep -o 'filed Q[0-9]*' <<<"$got" | cut -d' ' -f2)
    [ -n "$filed" ] || fail "fuzz-triage-row did not report filing a row"
    fields=$("$j" --justfile '{{ justfile() }}' _queue-rows "$dir/triage-filed.md" \
        | awk -F'\037' -v id="$filed" '$1 == "R" && $5 == id { print $6 "\037" $7 "\037" $8 "\037" $9; exit }')
    [ "$fields" = $'1\037todo\037opus\037-' ] \
        || fail "fuzz-triage-row's filed row is not tier 1, todo, opus, write-set - ($fields)"
    grep -qF 'from/- Triage the latest fuzz night' "$dir/triage-filed.md" \
        || fail "fuzz-triage-row did not write a row starting from/- Triage the latest fuzz night"

    got=$("$j" --justfile '{{ justfile() }}' --working-directory "$repo" fuzz-triage-row "$triage_logs" "$dir/triage-filed.md" 2>&1) \
        || fail "fuzz-triage-row failed on its second call, with a triage row already open"
    grep -qF "$filed is already open" <<<"$got" || fail "fuzz-triage-row did not say $filed was already open"
    [ "$(grep -c 'from/- Triage the latest fuzz night' "$dir/triage-filed.md")" = 1 ] \
        || fail "fuzz-triage-row filed a second triage row while one was already open"

    point_triage_at untriaged
    got=$("$j" --justfile '{{ justfile() }}' --working-directory "$repo" fuzz-triage-row "$triage_logs" "$dir/triage-none.md" 2>&1) \
        || fail "fuzz-triage-row failed on a night with no verdicts file"
    grep -qxF 'fuzz-triage-row: nothing to file' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "fuzz-triage-row did not say it had nothing to file when the night has no verdicts"
    [ "$(grep -c 'from/- Triage the latest fuzz night' "$dir/triage-none.md")" = 0 ] \
        || fail "fuzz-triage-row wrote a row though it said it had nothing to file"

    exit 0

# The merge driver `.gitattributes` names for `docs/queue.md`, spelt as this
# clone's git config must hold it. The path goes through `rev-parse` because
# every worktree shares the one config and git runs the driver from the top of
# whichever worktree is merging (§263.6).
queue_merge_driver := 'python3 "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)/tooling/queue/merge.py" %O %A %B %P'

# Registers the `docs/queue.md` merge driver in this clone's git config. Once
# per clone is enough, since every worktree reads the same config;
# `check-queue-merge-driver` names this recipe when the entry is missing or
# spelt differently.
[doc("Register the docs/queue.md merge driver (§263) in this clone's git config, which every worktree shares.")]
queue-merge-driver:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    git config merge.queue-rows.name 'docs/queue.md, merged by row id (docs/decisions.md §263)'
    git config merge.queue-rows.driver '{{ queue_merge_driver }}'
    echo 'merge.queue-rows is registered in this clone'

# **git ignores a merge driver that `.gitattributes` names and its config does
# not define**, and merges the file line by line without a word. That is the
# failure this exists for: the two dispatchers would be back to one text
# conflict per concurrent edit, and nothing would say why.
#
# **Skipped, not passed, where there is no Claude Code** — CI and `gate-fresh`,
# the same test as `check-plugins` (§77). A fresh clone has no config from here,
# and registering the driver there would prove only that this recipe can write
# a config. What is being asked is whether *this* clone merges the queue by row,
# and only a clone a dispatcher merges in can be asked that. The driver itself
# is not skipped anywhere: `check-queue-api-selftest` registers it in a scratch
# repository and merges through it, on the runner as here.
[doc("This clone's git config registers the docs/queue.md merge driver that .gitattributes names; skipped where there is no Claude Code.")]
check-queue-merge-driver:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -uo pipefail
    grep -qxF 'docs/queue.md merge=queue-rows' .gitattributes \
        || { echo ".gitattributes does not name the queue-rows driver for docs/queue.md"; exit 1; }
    if [ ! -d "{{claude_config}}" ]; then
        echo "no {{claude_config}}, so no Claude Code and no dispatcher merging here: the queue merge driver's registration is skipped, not passed"
        exit 0
    fi
    got=$(git config --get merge.queue-rows.driver || true)
    if [ "$got" != '{{ queue_merge_driver }}' ]; then
        echo "git config merge.queue-rows.driver is '${got:-unset}', so git merges docs/queue.md line by line and says nothing; register it with: mise exec -- just queue-merge-driver"
        exit 1
    fi
    echo "merge.queue-rows is registered, and .gitattributes names it for docs/queue.md"

# `just queue`'s verbs, its count of the machine, and the merge driver, against
# scratch repositories under `target/` — a hub, two session trees, lane trees
# locked the way the worktree hook locks them — and never the live queue.
#
# Each case is one the brief behind §263 named: two worktrees taking one row,
# two adding rows at once, two branches changing different rows merging
# cleanly, one row changed two ways conflicting loudly, `after:` on an id only
# another worktree holds, and on one that exists nowhere. §263.9's two gaps are
# here too: `rank` moving a row within its tier, and a row another copy already
# shows `done:` staying out of `next`'s offers and out of the write-set
# meeting. §307's verb is here too: `tier` moving a row to tier 2.2, which the
# fixture has no heading for, writing that heading in its place, and refusing a
# move to the row's own tier, to a tier that does not exist, and of a closed
# row; `add` writing the heading the same way; and `tier` moving a row up from
# tier 3 into 2.2, whose new heading lands above the row (§403.7). §352's are
# here too: `tag` setting, replacing and removing a class, refusing one outside
# tiers 1 and 5 and a typo, `tier` refusing a row that carries one, `add`
# refused a class on tier 2, and two small rows taken onto one branch holding
# one slot, not two. §403's: a C class moving its row to tier 5 and writing the
# heading, a B class moving it back, `tier` taking a C row to tier 5 and
# refusing a B row there, and `add` refused a C class on tier 1. §430's: `pin`
# putting a tier 1 row and a tier 2 row first in tier 1, numbered in the order
# given, and `ready` taking them ahead of wrong/A even once that row is ranked
# above them; `rank`, `tier`, `tag`, `park`, `drop` and `after` each refusing a
# pinned row, `add` refusing a pin, and `pin` refusing a pinned, a parked and a
# C row; `unpin` leaving its row where it stood; and `take` and `done` closing a
# pinned row with its pin. §432's: every `add` saying where its row came from,
# `was/T1` naming a closed row and `from/Q1` an open one; `tag` putting a class
# or `small` in front of that word and `tag -` taking it off again; `unpin`
# leaving the word first; and `add` refusing a pin before it looks for the
# word. §435's: the merged queue refused for Q3, which waits on a tier 2 row
# from the other side, until `tier` moves it down; `after` moving a row with no
# class down beside the latest row it names, and taking its own waiters along,
# and refusing a B row; `tier` and `tag` taking the rows with no class that wait
# on a row down the chain with it, refusing to move a row up past a row it waits
# on, and refusing a B row that would have to follow; `tag` taking up out of tier
# 5 only the waiters whose `after:` names no other tier 5 row; and `pin`
# refusing a row that waits outside tier 1 and taking a tier 5 row's waiters up
# with it. §424.7's: a row taken on a directory holding back a row on a file
# under it, in the copy that took it and in another, until `narrow` takes the
# hold down to another file; then `next` offers the row in both copies,
# `blocked` drops it, and the plain listing, `elsewhere` and the page say what
# the hold was narrowed to. §451's: a drop refused while two rows wait on it,
# naming the `after` step for each, and going through with those steps in one
# `apply`, one row back to `todo` and the other still waiting on Q2; and `-`
# refused on a row that does not wait and beside an id. The
# hook's cases are `check-hooks`'s. Without `tooling/queue/` the
# first take already fails, and with the text merge in place of the driver the
# clean merge conflicts: Q1 and Q2 sit on neighbouring lines, and both sides
# add at the foot of Tier 2.
[private]
[doc("just queue's verbs, its machine count and the merge driver agree with scratch repositories under target/.")]
check-queue-api-selftest:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -uo pipefail
    mkdir -p '{{ justfile_directory() }}/target'
    root=$(mktemp -d '{{ justfile_directory() }}/target/queue-selftest.XXXXXX')
    trap 'rm -rf "$root"' EXIT
    j='{{ just_executable() }}'
    page_dir="$root/queue-page"
    export RCAD_QUEUE_PAGE_DIR="$page_dir"
    export RCAD_FUZZ_LOGS="$root/no-fuzz-logs"
    got=""
    fail() {
        echo "just queue drifted from its selftest: $1"
        echo "--- got ---"; echo "$got"
        exit 1
    }
    g() { git -C "$1" -c user.name=selftest -c user.email=selftest@invalid "${@:2}"; }
    # Six slots of its own, whatever the cap is: the machine count's case holds
    # three and needs slots left over for two ready rows. `check-queue-selftest`
    # is where the real cap is pinned (§397.7).
    q() { "$j" --justfile '{{ justfile() }}' --working-directory "$1" --set queue_slots 6 queue "${@:2}"; }
    check() { "$j" --justfile '{{ justfile() }}' --working-directory "$1" check-queue 2>&1; }
    lock() { g "$hub" worktree lock --reason "agent $(basename "$1") since 2026-09-26T00:00:00Z" "$1"; }

    hub="$root/rcad"
    mkdir -p "$hub/docs/decisions.d" "$hub/tooling/queue"
    cp '{{ justfile_directory() }}'/tooling/queue/*.py "$hub/tooling/queue/"
    cp '{{ justfile_directory() }}/.gitattributes' "$hub/.gitattributes"
    printf '## 230. One queue\n' > "$hub/docs/decisions.d/230-one-queue.md"
    printf 'a\n' > "$hub/docs/a.md"
    printf 'b\n' > "$hub/docs/b.md"
    cat > "$hub/docs/queue.md" <<'MD'
    # Queue

    ## Tier 1

    ```
    Q1  1  todo  opus  docs/a.md  the first row §230
    Q2  1  todo  opus  docs/b.md  the second row §230
    Q3  1  todo  opus  -  the third row §230
    ```

    ## Tier 2

    ```
    Q4  2  after:Q1  opus  -  waits on the first §230
    ```

    ## Tier 2.5

    ```
    ```

    ## Tier 3

    ```
    ```

    ## Tier 4

    ```
    ```

    ## Closed

    ```
    T1  1  done:abcdef0  -  -  a closed row §230
    ```
    MD
    g "$hub" init -q -b main
    g "$hub" add -A
    g "$hub" commit -q -m root
    "$j" --justfile '{{ justfile() }}' --working-directory "$hub" queue-merge-driver >/dev/null \
        || fail "queue-merge-driver could not register the driver"
    one="$root/rcad-worktrees/one"
    two="$root/rcad-worktrees/two"
    g "$hub" worktree add -q -b one "$one" main && lock "$one"
    g "$hub" worktree add -q -b two "$two" main && lock "$two"
    claims_file=$(g "$hub" rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir)/rcad-queue-claims

    got=$(q "$one" take Q1 lane-q1 2>&1) || fail "the first take of Q1 was refused"
    grep -q '^Q1  1  lane:lane-q1 ' "$one/docs/queue.md" || fail "take did not write lane:lane-q1"
    got=$(awk -F '\t' '$1 == "take" && $2 == "Q1"' "$claims_file")
    [ "$(cut -f 5- <<<"$got")" = "$(printf 'lane-q1\tdocs/a.md\t1')" ] \
        || fail "take did not write Q1's branch, write set and tier to the claims file"
    [ -f "$page_dir/index.html" ] || fail "queue take did not render queue-page to RCAD_QUEUE_PAGE_DIR"
    got=$(q "$two" take Q1 lane-q1-again 2>&1) && fail "a second worktree took Q1 while the first held it"
    grep -qF 'Q1 is held by lane-q1 from' <<<"$got" || fail "the second take did not name who holds Q1"
    grep -q '^Q1  1  todo ' "$two/docs/queue.md" || fail "a refused take wrote the file anyway"
    got=$(q "$two" take Q4 lane-q4 2>&1) && fail "take dispatched Q4, which waits on Q1"
    grep -qF 'Q4 is not ready: it waits on Q1 (todo)' <<<"$got" || fail "take did not say why Q4 is not ready"

    for n in 1 2; do
        q "$one" add 2 opus - "from/- a row added in one, $n §230" > "$root/add-one-$n" 2> "$root/err-one-$n" &
        q "$two" add 2 opus - "from/- a row added in two, $n §230" > "$root/add-two-$n" 2> "$root/err-two-$n" &
    done
    wait
    got=$(cat "$root"/add-* "$root"/err-* | sort | tr '\n' ' ')
    [ "$got" = "Q5 Q6 Q7 Q8 " ] || fail "four adds at once from two worktrees did not get Q5 to Q8 once each"
    in_two=$(awk '/a row added in two, 1 / { print $1 }' "$two/docs/queue.md")
    [ -n "$in_two" ] || fail "an add from two did not reach two's copy"
    grep -q "^$in_two " "$one/docs/queue.md" && fail "an add from two reached one's copy, so the next case proves nothing"

    got=$(q "$one" after Q3 "$in_two" 2>&1) || fail "after refused $in_two, which two handed out and one does not hold yet"
    grep -qxF "Q3: todo -> after:$in_two, tier 1 -> 2, beside $in_two, which it waits on" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "after did not move Q3 down to $in_two's tier, once it could read it from the claims file two wrote"
    grep -q "^Q3  2  after:$in_two " "$one/docs/queue.md" || fail "after did not write Q3 at tier 2"
    got=$(check "$one") || fail "check-queue refused a wait on a Q row only two holds"
    grep -qF '1 wait on a Q row this copy does not hold yet' <<<"$got" || fail "check-queue did not count the wait on a row it cannot see"
    got=$(q "$one" after Q2 Q99 2>&1) && fail "after accepted Q99, which exists nowhere"
    grep -qF 'Q99 exists nowhere' <<<"$got" || fail "after did not say Q99 exists nowhere"

    lanes="$root/rcad-worktrees/one-worktrees"
    g "$hub" worktree add -q -b lane-q1 "$lanes/lane" main && lock "$lanes/lane"
    g "$hub" worktree add -q -b lane-q1-verify "$lanes/verify" main && lock "$lanes/verify"
    g "$hub" worktree add -q -b idle "$lanes/idle" main
    got=$(q "$lanes/idle" rank Q3 before Q1 2>&1) || fail "rank refused to move Q3 before Q1, in its own tier"
    got=$(awk '/^Q[0-9]+  1  / { printf "%s ", $1 }' "$lanes/idle/docs/queue.md")
    [ "$got" = "Q3 Q1 Q2 " ] || fail "rank did not put Q3 just before Q1"
    got=$(q "$lanes/idle" ready 2>&1) || fail "ready failed"
    [ "$(awk 'NR == 1 { print $1 }' <<<"$got")" = Q3 ] || fail "ready did not take the ranked Q3 ahead of Q2"
    got=$(q "$lanes/idle" rank Q3 before Q4 2>&1) && fail "rank moved Q3 into tier 2"
    grep -qF 'rank only moves a row within its own tier' <<<"$got" || fail "rank did not say why it refused to move Q3 into tier 2"

    got=$(q "$lanes/idle" tier Q4 2.2 2>&1) || fail "tier refused to move Q4 to tier 2.2, whose heading the file does not have yet"
    got=$(awk '/^## / { h = $0 } /^Q4 / { print h ": " $2 " " $3 }' "$lanes/idle/docs/queue.md")
    [ "$got" = "## Tier 2.2: 2.2 after:Q1" ] || fail "tier did not put Q4 under ## Tier 2.2 as tier 2.2 with its state kept"
    got=$(awk '/^## / { printf "%s|", $0 }' "$lanes/idle/docs/queue.md")
    [ "$got" = "## Tier 1|## Tier 2|## Tier 2.2|## Tier 2.5|## Tier 3|## Tier 4|## Closed|" ] \
        || fail "tier did not write ## Tier 2.2 between ## Tier 2 and ## Tier 2.5"
    got=$(check "$lanes/idle") || fail "the file tier wrote fails check-queue"
    got=$(q "$lanes/idle" tier Q4 2.2 2>&1) && fail "tier moved Q4 to the tier it is in already"
    grep -qF 'Q4 is tier 2.2 already' <<<"$got" || fail "tier did not say Q4 is in tier 2.2 already"
    got=$(q "$lanes/idle" tier Q2 7 2>&1) && fail "tier moved Q2 to tier 7, which does not exist"
    grep -qF "'7' is not a tier" <<<"$got" || fail "tier did not say 7 is not a tier"
    got=$(q "$lanes/idle" tier T1 2 2>&1) && fail "tier moved T1, which is closed"
    grep -qF 'T1 is closed' <<<"$got" || fail "tier did not say T1 is closed"
    g "$hub" worktree add -q -b spare "$lanes/spare" main
    got=$(q "$lanes/spare" add 2.2 sonnet - "was/T1 a row for a tier with no heading yet §230" 2>&1) \
        || fail "add refused tier 2.2, whose heading the file does not have yet"
    got=$(awk -v id="$got" '/^## / { h = $0 } $1 == id { print h }' "$lanes/spare/docs/queue.md")
    [ "$got" = "## Tier 2.2" ] || fail "add did not write ## Tier 2.2 over the row it added"
    g "$hub" worktree add -q -b up "$lanes/up" main
    grep -q '^## Tier 2.2' "$lanes/up/docs/queue.md" && fail "the tree for moving a row up has ## Tier 2.2 already"
    up=$(q "$lanes/up" add 3 opus - "from/Q4 a row moved up into a tier with no heading yet §230") || fail "add for the row to move up failed"
    got=$(q "$lanes/up" tier "$up" 2.2 2>&1) || fail "tier refused to move $up up from tier 3 to tier 2.2, whose heading goes above it"
    got=$(awk -v id="$up" '/^## / { h = $0 } $1 == id { print h ": " $2 }' "$lanes/up/docs/queue.md")
    [ "$got" = "## Tier 2.2: 2.2" ] || fail "tier did not leave $up once, under ## Tier 2.2"
    got=$(check "$lanes/up") || fail "the file tier wrote moving a row up fails check-queue"

    got=$(q "$two" take Q2 lane-q2 2>&1) || fail "two could not take Q2"
    got=$(q "$one" running 2>&1) || fail "running failed"
    grep -qx '3 running' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a lane, its verifier and a take with no tree are three slots, and two session trees and an unlocked tree none"
    got=$(q "$one" next 2>&1) || fail "next failed"
    grep -q '^take Q2 ' <<<"$got" && fail "next offered Q2, which two holds"
    grep -qx 'took 2 of 3: no other row is ready' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "next did not offer the three slots the machine leaves to one's two ready rows"

    sha=$(g "$one" rev-parse HEAD)
    got=$(q "$one" done Q1 "$sha" 2>&1) || fail "done refused Q1"
    awk '/^## Closed/ { c = 1 } c && /^Q1  1  done:/ { f = 1 } END { exit !f }' "$one/docs/queue.md" \
        || fail "done did not move Q1 under ## Closed"
    got=$(q "$one" running 2>&1) || fail "running failed"
    grep -qx '3 running' <<<"$got" || fail "done released a hold whose lane tree still stands, or counted it twice"
    got=$(q "$two" add 2.5 opus docs/a.md "from/Q1 a row on Q1's file, added in two §230" 2>&1) || fail "two could not add a row on docs/a.md"
    on_a=$got
    got=$(q "$two" next 9 2>&1) || fail "next failed"
    grep -q '^take Q1 ' <<<"$got" && fail "next offered Q1, which one's copy already shows done"
    grep -q "^take $on_a " <<<"$got" || fail "Q1, done in one's copy, still kept docs/a.md busy in two's fill"
    got=$(q "$two" 2>&1) || fail "the plain queue failed"
    grep -qx "closed elsewhere: Q1 (done:$sha)" <<<"$got" || fail "the plain queue did not name Q1 as closed in one's copy"
    g "$one" commit -qam "one's queue"
    g "$two" commit -qam "two's queue"
    g "$hub" merge -q --no-edit one || fail "main did not take one"
    got=$(g "$hub" merge --no-edit two 2>&1) || fail "one and two changed different rows and did not merge through the driver"
    for row in '^Q1  1  done:' '^Q2  1  lane:lane-q2 ' "^Q3  2  after:$in_two " 'added in one, 1 ' 'added in one, 2 ' 'added in two, 1 ' 'added in two, 2 '; do
        grep -q "$row" "$hub/docs/queue.md" || fail "the merge lost the row matching $row"
    done
    # §435 (Q2315): one copy could not see the tier of the row Q3 waits on, but
    # `after` reads it from the claims file two wrote, and already moved Q3
    # down to tier 2 beside it, so the merge carries no violation to fix by hand.
    got=$(check "$hub") || fail "the merged queue fails check-queue"

    g "$hub" switch -q -c three
    got=$(q "$hub" park Q3 §230 2>&1) || fail "park refused Q3"
    g "$hub" commit -qam park
    g "$hub" switch -q -c four main
    got=$(q "$hub" drop Q3 230 2>&1) || fail "drop refused Q3"
    g "$hub" commit -qam drop
    g "$hub" switch -q main
    g "$hub" merge -q --no-edit three
    got=$(g "$hub" merge --no-edit four 2>&1) && fail "Q3 parked on one side and dropped on the other merged without a conflict"
    grep -qF 'Q3 changed on both sides' <<<"$got" || fail "the driver did not name the row that conflicted"
    [ -n "$(g "$hub" ls-files -u docs/queue.md)" ] || fail "git did not mark docs/queue.md unmerged"
    grep -q '^<<<<<<< ours: Q3$' "$hub/docs/queue.md" || fail "the row in conflict is not marked in the file"
    got=$(q "$hub" park Q2 §230 2>&1) && fail "a verb wrote a file that still holds a conflict"
    got=$(q "$hub" pick Q3 theirs 2>&1) || fail "pick could not settle Q3"
    awk '/^## Closed/ { c = 1 } c && /^Q3  2  dropped:§230 / { f = 1 } END { exit !f }' "$hub/docs/queue.md" \
        || fail "pick theirs did not keep four's drop under ## Closed"
    got=$(check "$hub") || fail "the file pick settled fails check-queue"

    # §299: once a lane hands off, its own tree can be gone while its
    # verifier's (detached, no branch) is still held; `running` must count
    # that tree once for the row, not once for the tree and again as "no tree
    # yet" (§263.4's fallback, meant for before any tree exists).
    gone="$lanes/gone-work"
    g "$hub" worktree add -q -b lane-gone "$gone" main
    g "$gone" commit -q --allow-empty -m "lane-gone's own commit"
    g "$hub" worktree remove --force "$gone"
    verify_gone="$lanes/verify-gone"
    g "$hub" worktree add -q --detach "$verify_gone" lane-gone && lock "$verify_gone"
    got=$(q "$hub" add 3 opus - "from/- a row whose lane tree is gone §230" 2>&1) || fail "add for the gone-lane row failed"
    q_gone=$got
    got=$(q "$hub" take "$q_gone" lane-gone 2>&1) || fail "take could not hold $q_gone on lane-gone"
    got=$(q "$hub" running 2>&1) || fail "running failed with a lane tree gone and its verifier held"
    grep -qxF "(detached)  $verify_gone" <<<"$got" || fail "the verifier's detached tree was not counted"
    grep -q "^lane-gone  .*no tree yet" <<<"$got" && fail "a row whose verifier is still held was also counted as treeless"

    # The reverse miscount: a second row in the same state is its own slot,
    # not the first row's tree counted again.
    gone2="$lanes/gone-work-2"
    g "$hub" worktree add -q -b lane-gone-2 "$gone2" main
    g "$gone2" commit -q --allow-empty -m "lane-gone-2's own commit"
    g "$hub" worktree remove --force "$gone2"
    verify_gone2="$lanes/verify-gone-2"
    g "$hub" worktree add -q --detach "$verify_gone2" lane-gone-2 && lock "$verify_gone2"
    got=$(q "$hub" add 3 opus - "from/- a second row whose lane tree is gone §230" 2>&1) || fail "add for the second gone-lane row failed"
    q_gone2=$got
    got=$(q "$hub" take "$q_gone2" lane-gone-2 2>&1) || fail "take could not hold $q_gone2 on lane-gone-2"
    got=$(q "$hub" running 2>&1) || fail "running failed with two rows each missing their lane tree"
    grep -qxF "(detached)  $verify_gone" <<<"$got" || fail "the first row's verifier tree dropped out of running"
    grep -qxF "(detached)  $verify_gone2" <<<"$got" || fail "the second row's verifier tree dropped out of running"
    grep -q "^lane-gone  .*no tree yet" <<<"$got" && fail "the first held row was still counted as treeless beside its verifier"
    grep -q "^lane-gone-2  .*no tree yet" <<<"$got" && fail "two rows whose runs are each held collapsed onto one slot"

    # §299.3's gap: the branch is deleted outright, not just its worktree
    # removed, while its verifier's detached tree is still held. `has_a_tree`
    # can no longer read a tip to test ancestry against, so `running` must
    # still count this row's tree once -- not the tree, and again as "no tree
    # yet" because the branch it was looking for is gone.
    gone3="$lanes/gone-work-3"
    g "$hub" worktree add -q -b lane-gone-3 "$gone3" main
    g "$gone3" commit -q --allow-empty -m "lane-gone-3's own commit"
    g "$hub" worktree remove --force "$gone3"
    verify_gone3="$lanes/verify-gone-3"
    g "$hub" worktree add -q --detach "$verify_gone3" lane-gone-3 && lock "$verify_gone3"
    g "$hub" branch -D lane-gone-3 >/dev/null
    got=$(q "$hub" add 3 opus - "from/- a row whose lane branch is deleted outright §230" 2>&1) || fail "add for the deleted-branch row failed"
    q_gone3=$got
    got=$(q "$hub" take "$q_gone3" lane-gone-3 2>&1) || fail "take could not hold $q_gone3 on a branch that is about to be deleted"
    got=$(q "$hub" running 2>&1) || fail "running failed with a lane branch deleted outright and its verifier held"
    grep -qxF "(detached)  $verify_gone3" <<<"$got" || fail "the verifier's detached tree was not counted once its branch was deleted"
    grep -q "^lane-gone-3  .*no tree yet" <<<"$got" && fail "a row whose branch is deleted outright was counted again as treeless"

    # The reverse miscount, once more: two rows in this same state (branch
    # gone outright) must not collapse onto one slot either.
    gone4="$lanes/gone-work-4"
    g "$hub" worktree add -q -b lane-gone-4 "$gone4" main
    g "$gone4" commit -q --allow-empty -m "lane-gone-4's own commit"
    g "$hub" worktree remove --force "$gone4"
    verify_gone4="$lanes/verify-gone-4"
    g "$hub" worktree add -q --detach "$verify_gone4" lane-gone-4 && lock "$verify_gone4"
    g "$hub" branch -D lane-gone-4 >/dev/null
    got=$(q "$hub" add 3 opus - "from/- a second row whose lane branch is deleted outright §230" 2>&1) || fail "add for the second deleted-branch row failed"
    q_gone4=$got
    got=$(q "$hub" take "$q_gone4" lane-gone-4 2>&1) || fail "take could not hold $q_gone4 on a branch that is about to be deleted"
    got=$(q "$hub" running 2>&1) || fail "running failed with two rows each deleted outright"
    grep -qxF "(detached)  $verify_gone3" <<<"$got" || fail "the first deleted-branch row's verifier tree dropped out of running"
    grep -qxF "(detached)  $verify_gone4" <<<"$got" || fail "the second deleted-branch row's verifier tree dropped out of running"
    grep -q "^lane-gone-3  .*no tree yet" <<<"$got" && fail "the first deleted-branch row was still counted as treeless beside its verifier"
    grep -q "^lane-gone-4  .*no tree yet" <<<"$got" && fail "two deleted-branch rows collapsed onto one slot"

    # §299.6 (Q1511): a read-only lane that leaves its branch to build an old
    # commit. Its branch tip stays at its base, already on main, so the tip reads
    # as a stale hold, and the tree is detached at a commit the tip is not an
    # ancestor of. The tree's own reflog names the branch it left, so `running`
    # must count the row once, as that tree, and not again as "no tree yet".
    left="$lanes/left-its-branch"
    g "$hub" worktree add -q -b worktree-left-its-branch "$left" main && lock "$left"
    g "$left" switch -q -c lane-left main
    g "$left" switch -q --detach "$(g "$hub" rev-list --max-parents=0 main)"
    got=$(q "$hub" add 3 opus - "from/- a read-only row whose lane left its branch §230" 2>&1) || fail "add for the read-only row failed"
    q_left=$got
    got=$(q "$hub" take "$q_left" lane-left 2>&1) || fail "take could not hold $q_left on lane-left"
    got=$(q "$hub" running 2>&1) || fail "running failed with a read-only lane off its branch"
    grep -qxF "(detached)  $left" <<<"$got" || fail "the tree that left lane-left was not counted"
    grep -q "^lane-left  .*no tree yet" <<<"$got" && fail "a read-only lane that left its branch was counted again as treeless"

    # The other side of §299.2: a held branch already on main that no tree
    # left is still a stale hold, with a tree that left another branch beside it.
    g "$hub" branch lane-stale main
    got=$(q "$hub" add 3 opus - "from/- a row held on a branch already on main §230" 2>&1) || fail "add for the stale row failed"
    q_stale=$got
    got=$(q "$hub" take "$q_stale" lane-stale 2>&1) || fail "take could not hold $q_stale on lane-stale"
    got=$(q "$hub" running 2>&1) || fail "running failed with a stale hold"
    grep -q "^lane-stale  .*no tree yet" <<<"$got" || fail "a hold on a branch already on main, which no tree left, was not counted"

    # §309.4: `apply`, all or nothing, and its own tests, last and in a fresh
    # worktree off the script's own main -- everything above has already taken,
    # closed or renumbered rows enough that reusing 'one' or 'two' here, or
    # running any earlier, would make the ids and states below unpredictable.
    # Q2 is the one row every case above leaves permanently held (by "two", as
    # lane-q2, never done/dropped/parked), so it is what a batch's refused step
    # names here.
    batch_wt="$root/rcad-worktrees/batch"
    g "$hub" worktree add -q -b apply-batch "$batch_wt" main
    row_a=$(q "$batch_wt" add 2 opus - "from/- a row this batch takes in step 1 §230") || fail "add for row_a failed"
    row_b=$(q "$batch_wt" add 2 opus - "from/- a row this batch takes in step 2 §230") || fail "add for row_b failed"

    before_queue=$(md5sum "$batch_wt/docs/queue.md" | cut -d' ' -f1)
    before_claims=$(md5sum "$claims_file" | cut -d' ' -f1)
    got=$(q "$batch_wt" apply take "$row_a" apply-a + take "$row_b" apply-b + take Q2 apply-c 2>&1) \
        && fail "a batch whose third take names Q2, held by lane-q2, went through"
    grep -qF "step 3 (take Q2 apply-c) is refused" <<<"$got" || fail "the refusal did not name step 3 and its own text"
    grep -qF 'Q2 is held by lane-q2 from' <<<"$got" || fail "the refusal did not carry Q2's own reason"
    after_queue=$(md5sum "$batch_wt/docs/queue.md" | cut -d' ' -f1)
    after_claims=$(md5sum "$claims_file" | cut -d' ' -f1)
    [ "$before_queue" = "$after_queue" ] || fail "a batch refused at its third step still changed docs/queue.md"
    [ "$before_claims" = "$after_claims" ] || fail "a batch refused at its third step still appended to the claims file"

    got=$(q "$batch_wt" apply add 2 opus - "from/- a row an after step points at §230" + after @1 Q2 2>&1) \
        || fail "add + after @1 was refused"
    new_id=$(sed -n '1p' <<<"$got")
    sed -n '2p' <<<"$got" | grep -qF "$new_id: todo -> after:Q2" || fail "after @1 did not print the new row waiting on Q2"
    got=$(awk -v id="$new_id" '$1 == id { print $2, $3 }' "$batch_wt/docs/queue.md")
    [ "$got" = "2 after:Q2" ] || fail "after @1 did not write tier 2, after:Q2 on the row the batch just added"

    got=$(q "$batch_wt" apply add 2 opus - "from/- a row dropped with two rows waiting on it §230" \
        + add 2 opus - "from/- waits on the dropped row alone §230" + after @2 @1 \
        + add 2 opus - "from/- waits on the dropped row and on Q2 §230" + after @3 @1,Q2 2>&1) \
        || fail "the rows for the drop with waiters were refused"
    dropped=$(sed -n '1p' <<<"$got")
    alone=$(sed -n '2p' <<<"$got")
    beside=$(sed -n '4p' <<<"$got")
    got=$(q "$batch_wt" drop "$dropped" §230 2>&1) && fail "drop went through with $alone and $beside waiting on $dropped"
    grep -qF "$alone: waits on $dropped, which was dropped and will never be done; just queue after $alone - frees it" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the refused drop did not name the step that frees $alone"
    grep -qF "$beside: waits on $dropped, which was dropped and will never be done; just queue after $beside Q2 keeps its other waits" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the refused drop did not name the step that keeps $beside waiting on Q2"
    got=$(q "$batch_wt" apply drop "$dropped" §230 + after "$alone" - + after "$beside" Q2 2>&1) \
        || fail "the drop with the steps its refusal named, in one apply, was refused"
    grep -qxF "$alone: after:$dropped -> todo" <<<"$got" || fail "after - did not print $alone going back to todo"
    got=$(awk -v a="$alone" -v b="$beside" '$1 == a || $1 == b { print $1, $3 }' "$batch_wt/docs/queue.md")
    [ "$got" = "$(printf '%s todo\n%s after:Q2' "$alone" "$beside")" ] \
        || fail "the drop's apply did not leave $alone todo and $beside after:Q2"
    got=$(q "$batch_wt" after "$alone" - 2>&1) && fail "after - freed $alone, which waits on nothing"
    grep -qF "$alone is todo, and '-' frees only a row whose state is after:" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "after - did not say $alone waits on nothing"
    got=$(q "$batch_wt" after "$beside" -,Q2 2>&1) && fail "after took '-' beside an id"
    grep -qF "'-' frees $beside of every row it waits on, so it stands alone" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "after did not say '-' stands alone"

    got=$(q "$batch_wt" apply add 2 opus - "from/- the first of a pair §230" + add 2 opus - "from/- the second of a pair §230" 2>&1) \
        || fail "two adds in one batch were refused"
    first_id=$(sed -n '1p' <<<"$got")
    second_id=$(sed -n '2p' <<<"$got")
    [ "$((${second_id#Q} - ${first_id#Q}))" = 1 ] || fail "two adds in one batch, $first_id and $second_id, are not consecutive"

    # §309.3.2: one hold of the claims lock for the whole batch. A second
    # worktree adds rows one at a time in a tight loop while this worktree runs
    # a three-step add batch; if the batch let go of the lock between steps,
    # the loop could append between the batch's own claims events. It cannot:
    # they land as one contiguous run, regardless of how the loop and the batch
    # interleave, because only one process ever holds the flock at a time and
    # the batch does not release it until every step has run.
    spam_wt="$root/rcad-worktrees/spam"
    g "$hub" worktree add -q -b spam "$spam_wt" main
    (for i in $(seq 1 40); do q "$spam_wt" add 2.5 opus - "from/- a spammed row $i §230" >/dev/null 2>&1; done) &
    spam_pid=$!
    got=$(q "$batch_wt" apply add 2.5 opus - "from/- a contiguous probe row 1 §230" \
        + add 2.5 opus - "from/- a contiguous probe row 2 §230" \
        + add 2.5 opus - "from/- a contiguous probe row 3 §230" 2>&1) \
        || fail "the three-step probe batch was refused"
    wait "$spam_pid"
    lines=$(grep -n 'a contiguous probe row' "$claims_file" | cut -d: -f1)
    n=$(wc -l <<<"$lines")
    [ "$n" = 3 ] || fail "the probe batch's three add events do not all appear exactly once in the claims file"
    first=$(sed -n '1p' <<<"$lines")
    third=$(sed -n '3p' <<<"$lines")
    [ "$((third - first))" = 2 ] || fail "another worktree's adds landed between this batch's own three claims events"
    total_lines=$(wc -l < "$claims_file")
    [ "$first" -gt 1 ] || fail "the probe batch ran before any spammed row landed, so this case raced against nothing"
    [ "$third" -lt "$total_lines" ] || fail "the probe batch ran after every spammed row, so this case raced against nothing"

    # §352: a tag is the first word of the description, set through `tag` only.
    tag_wt="$root/rcad-worktrees/tag"
    g "$hub" worktree add -q -b tag "$tag_wt" main
    words() { awk -v id="$1" '$1 == id { print $6, $7 }' "$tag_wt/docs/queue.md"; }
    ranked=$(q "$tag_wt" add 1 opus - "from/- a defect the tag verb ranks §230") || fail "add for the row to tag failed"
    got=$(q "$tag_wt" tag "$ranked" refuses/B 2>&1) || fail "tag refused a class on a tier 1 row"
    [ "$(words "$ranked")" = "refuses/B from/-" ] \
        || fail "tag did not put refuses/B in front of the description, ahead of the word that says where the row came from (§432)"
    got=$(q "$tag_wt" tag "$ranked" wrong/A 2>&1) || fail "tag refused to replace one class with another"
    [ "$(words "$ranked")" = "wrong/A from/-" ] || fail "tag did not replace refuses/B with wrong/A"
    got=$(q "$tag_wt" tag "$ranked" wrong/A 2>&1) && fail "tag set a class the row carries already"
    grep -qF "$ranked is tagged wrong/A already" <<<"$got" || fail "tag did not say the row carries wrong/A already"
    got=$(q "$tag_wt" tag "$ranked" wrong/E 2>&1) && fail "tag accepted wrong/E, which is no class"
    grep -qF "'wrong/E' is not a tag" <<<"$got" || fail "tag did not say wrong/E is not a tag"
    under() { awk -v id="$1" '/^## / { h = $0 } $1 == id { print h ": " $2 " " $6 }' "$tag_wt/docs/queue.md"; }
    got=$(q "$tag_wt" tag "$ranked" refuses/C 2>&1) || fail "tag refused a C class on a tier 1 row"
    grep -qxF "$ranked: tag wrong/A -> refuses/C, tier 1 -> 5, at the foot of ## Tier 5" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "tag did not say the C class moved the row to tier 5"
    [ "$(under "$ranked")" = "## Tier 5: 5 refuses/C" ] || fail "tag refuses/C did not move the row under ## Tier 5"
    got=$(awk '/^## / { printf "%s|", $0 }' "$tag_wt/docs/queue.md")
    [[ "$got" == *"|## Tier 4|## Tier 5|## Closed|" ]] || fail "tag did not write ## Tier 5 between ## Tier 4 and ## Closed"
    got=$(check "$tag_wt") || fail "the file tag wrote moving a row to tier 5 fails check-queue"
    got=$(q "$tag_wt" tag "$ranked" wrong/B 2>&1) || fail "tag refused a B class on a tier 5 row"
    [ "$(under "$ranked")" = "## Tier 1: 1 wrong/B" ] || fail "tag wrong/B did not move the row back under ## Tier 1"
    got=$(q "$tag_wt" tier "$ranked" 5 2>&1) && fail "tier moved a row of class B to tier 5"
    grep -qF "carries the class wrong/B, which puts it in tier 1" <<<"$got" || fail "tier did not say a B row belongs in tier 1"
    got=$(q "$tag_wt" add 1 opus - "refuses/C from/- a class add writes onto a tier 1 row §230" 2>&1) \
        && fail "add wrote a C class onto a tier 1 row"
    grep -qF 'the class refuses/C puts an open row in tier 5, and this row is tier 1' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "add's refusal of a C row in tier 1 did not carry check-queue's reason"
    got=$(q "$tag_wt" apply add 1 opus - "refuses/C from/- a C row filed in tier 1 and moved §230" + tier @1 5 2>&1) \
        || fail "tier refused to move a C row to tier 5, the tier its class names"
    [ "$(under "$(sed -n '1p' <<<"$got")")" = "## Tier 5: 5 refuses/C" ] || fail "tier did not move the C row under ## Tier 5"
    got=$(q "$tag_wt" tier "$ranked" 2 2>&1) && fail "tier moved a row carrying a class out of tier 1"
    grep -qF "remove it first with \`tag $ranked -\`" <<<"$got" || fail "tier did not name the tag step that frees the row"
    got=$(q "$tag_wt" tag "$ranked" - 2>&1) || fail "tag refused to remove the class"
    [ "$(words "$ranked")" = "from/- a" ] || fail "tag - did not leave the description as add wrote it"
    got=$(q "$tag_wt" tier "$ranked" 2 2>&1) || fail "tier refused a row whose class was removed"
    got=$(q "$tag_wt" tag "$ranked" crash/C 2>&1) && fail "tag put a class on a tier 2 row"
    grep -qF "$ranked is tier 2, and a class ranks tier 1 and tier 5 rows only" <<<"$got" || fail "tag did not say a class ranks tiers 1 and 5 only"
    got=$(q "$tag_wt" tag "$ranked" small 2>&1) || fail "tag refused small on a tier 2 row"
    [ "$(words "$ranked")" = "small from/-" ] || fail "tag did not put small in front of the description"
    got=$(check "$tag_wt") || fail "the file tag wrote fails check-queue"
    got=$(q "$tag_wt" add 2 sonnet - "wrong/B from/- a class add writes onto a tier 2 row §230" 2>&1) \
        && fail "add wrote a class onto a tier 2 row"
    grep -qF 'the class wrong/B ranks tier 1 and tier 5 rows only, and this row is tier 2' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "add's refusal did not carry check-queue's reason"

    first_small=$(q "$tag_wt" add 2 sonnet - "small from/- the first row of a bundle §230") || fail "add for the first small row failed"
    second_small=$(q "$tag_wt" add 2 sonnet - "small from/- the second row of a bundle §230") || fail "add for the second small row failed"
    before=$(q "$tag_wt" running --count) || fail "running --count failed before the bundle was taken"
    got=$(q "$tag_wt" apply take "$first_small" lane-bundle + take "$second_small" lane-bundle 2>&1) \
        || fail "one branch could not take two small rows"
    after=$(q "$tag_wt" running --count) || fail "running --count failed after the bundle was taken"
    [ "$after" = "$((before + 1))" ] || fail "two small rows on one branch held $((after - before)) slots, and a bundle is one lane"
    got=$(q "$tag_wt" running 2>&1) || fail "running failed with a bundle held"
    grep -qxF "lane-bundle  $first_small,$second_small taken from $tag_wt, no tree yet" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "running did not name a bundle's rows together on one line"

    # The same, for a bundle whose branch exists with no tree on it: `has_a_tree`
    # answers False there rather than None, and the two are counted apart.
    unlanded=$(g "$hub" commit-tree -p main -m "a bundle's own commit" 'main^{tree}') || fail "commit-tree failed"
    g "$hub" branch lane-bundle-2 "$unlanded"
    third_small=$(q "$tag_wt" add 2 sonnet - "small from/- the first row of a second bundle §230") || fail "add for the third small row failed"
    fourth_small=$(q "$tag_wt" add 2 sonnet - "small from/- the second row of a second bundle §230") || fail "add for the fourth small row failed"
    got=$(q "$tag_wt" apply take "$third_small" lane-bundle-2 + take "$fourth_small" lane-bundle-2 2>&1) \
        || fail "a branch with no tree could not take two small rows"
    again=$(q "$tag_wt" running --count) || fail "running --count failed after the second bundle was taken"
    [ "$again" = "$((after + 1))" ] || fail "two small rows on a branch with no tree held $((again - after)) slots, and a bundle is one lane"

    # §430: `pin` puts rows first, by its own number, and no dispatcher's verb moves them.
    pin_wt="$root/rcad-worktrees/pin"
    g "$hub" worktree add -q -b pin "$pin_wt" main
    tier1() { awk '/^## / { h = $0 } h == "## Tier 1" && /^[TDQ][0-9]/ { printf "%s ", $1 }' "$pin_wt/docs/queue.md"; }
    pinword() { awk -v id="$1" '$1 == id { print $2, $6 }' "$pin_wt/docs/queue.md"; }
    first_three() { awk 'NR <= 3 { printf "%s ", $1 }' <<<"$got"; }
    classed=$(q "$pin_wt" add 1 opus - "wrong/A from/- a defect the pinned rows go ahead of §230") || fail "add for the wrong/A row failed"
    first=$(q "$pin_wt" add 1 opus - "from/- a tier 1 row Mattias pins first §230") || fail "add for the first row to pin failed"
    second=$(q "$pin_wt" add 2 opus - "from/- a tier 2 row Mattias pins second §230") || fail "add for the second row to pin failed"
    got=$(q "$pin_wt" pin "$first" "$second" 2>&1) || fail "pin refused a tier 1 row and a tier 2 row"
    [ "$(pinword "$first")" = "1 pinned/1" ] || fail "pin did not put pinned/1 on $first"
    [ "$(pinword "$second")" = "1 pinned/2" ] || fail "pin did not move $second to tier 1 as pinned/2"
    [ "$(tier1)" = "$first $second Q2 $classed " ] || fail "pin did not put the pinned rows first in tier 1, in the order pinned"
    got=$(q "$pin_wt" ready 2>&1) || fail "ready failed with pinned rows"
    [ "$(first_three)" = "$first $second $classed " ] || fail "ready did not list the pinned rows first, by number, ahead of wrong/A"
    got=$(q "$pin_wt" rank "$classed" before "$first" 2>&1) || fail "rank refused to move a row that is not pinned above a pinned one"
    got=$(q "$pin_wt" ready 2>&1) || fail "ready failed with a row ranked above the pinned ones"
    [ "$(first_three)" = "$first $second $classed " ] || fail "a row ranked above the pinned rows in the file was taken ahead of them"
    for step in "rank $first before $classed" "tier $first 2" "tag $first small" "park $first §230" "drop $first §230" "after $first Q2"; do
        got=$(q "$pin_wt" $step 2>&1) && fail "$step went through on a row Mattias pinned"
        grep -qF "$first is pinned/1: Mattias placed it, so \`${step%% *}\` leaves it alone. Take it, close it with \`done\`, or ask him to \`unpin\` it (§430)" <<<"$got" \
            || fail "$step did not say the pinned row is Mattias's to move"
    done
    got=$(q "$pin_wt" add 1 opus - "pinned/9 a pin add writes §230" 2>&1) && fail "add wrote a row with a pin on it"
    grep -qF 'only Mattias pins a row' <<<"$got" || fail "add did not say a pin is Mattias's to set"
    got=$(q "$pin_wt" pin "$first" 2>&1) && fail "pin numbered a row that is pinned already"
    grep -qF "$first is pinned/1 already; unpin it first to re-order" <<<"$got" || fail "pin did not say to unpin a pinned row first"
    got=$(q "$pin_wt" pin Q3 2>&1) && fail "pin put a parked row first"
    grep -qF 'Q3 is parked:§230, and the fill never takes a parked row' <<<"$got" || fail "pin did not say Q3 is parked"
    fifth=$(q "$pin_wt" add 5 opus - "crash/C from/- a crash only a probe builds §230") || fail "add for the tier 5 row failed"
    got=$(q "$pin_wt" pin "$fifth" 2>&1) && fail "pin moved a C row out of tier 5"
    grep -qF "$fifth carries the class crash/C, which puts it in tier 5" <<<"$got" || fail "pin did not say a C row belongs in tier 5"
    got=$(q "$pin_wt" unpin "$second" 2>&1) || fail "unpin refused $second"
    [ "$(pinword "$second")" = "1 from/-" ] || fail "unpin did not take the pin off and leave $second in tier 1"
    [ "$(tier1)" = "$classed $first $second Q2 " ] || fail "unpin moved $second from where it stood"
    got=$(q "$pin_wt" take "$first" lane-pinned 2>&1) || fail "take refused a pinned row"
    pinned_sha=$(g "$pin_wt" rev-parse HEAD)
    got=$(q "$pin_wt" done "$first" "$pinned_sha" 2>&1) || fail "done refused a pinned row"
    awk -v id="$first" '/^## Closed/ { c = 1 } c && $1 == id && $6 == "pinned/1" { f = 1 } END { exit !f }' "$pin_wt/docs/queue.md" \
        || fail "done did not move the pinned row under ## Closed with its pin"
    got=$(check "$pin_wt") || fail "the file the pin verbs wrote fails check-queue"

    # §435.6: the verbs keep a row that waits in no tier the fill reaches before
    # an open row it waits on.
    wait_wt="$root/rcad-worktrees/wait"
    g "$hub" worktree add -q -b wait "$wait_wt" main
    sits() { awk -v id="$1" '/^## / { h = $0 } $1 == id { print h ": " $2 " " $3 }' "$wait_wt/docs/queue.md"; }
    mattias="which of the two moves is Mattias's to set (§435.4)"
    x=$(q "$wait_wt" add 1 opus - "from/- a row that comes to wait on a tier 5 row §230") || fail "add for the row to wait failed"
    c5=$(q "$wait_wt" add 5 opus - "refuses/C from/- a tier 5 row other rows wait on §230") || fail "add for the tier 5 row failed"
    got=$(q "$wait_wt" after "$x" "$c5" 2>&1) || fail "after refused a row with no class waiting on a tier 5 row"
    grep -qxF "$x: todo -> after:$c5, tier 1 -> 5, beside $c5, which it waits on" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "after did not say it moved the row down beside the tier 5 row it waits on"
    [ "$(sits "$x")" = "## Tier 5: 5 after:$c5" ] || fail "after did not move the row under ## Tier 5"
    w=$(q "$wait_wt" add 1 opus - "wrong/B from/- a defect that may not wait on a tier 5 row §230") || fail "add for the B row failed"
    got=$(q "$wait_wt" after "$w" "$c5" 2>&1) && fail "after moved a row of class B to wait on a tier 5 row"
    grep -qxF "queue after: $w carries the class wrong/B, which keeps it in tier 1, and it would wait on $c5, which is tier 5: $mattias" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "after did not name both rows and leave the order to Mattias"
    [ "$(sits "$w")" = "## Tier 1: 1 todo" ] || fail "a refused after wrote the file anyway"

    p=$(q "$wait_wt" add 2 opus - "from/- a tier 2 row two rows wait on in a chain §230") || fail "add for the head of the chain failed"
    f=$(q "$wait_wt" add 2 opus - "from/- a tier 2 row that waits on the head §230") || fail "add for the second of the chain failed"
    gg=$(q "$wait_wt" add 2 opus - "from/- a tier 2 row that waits on the second §230") || fail "add for the third of the chain failed"
    got=$(q "$wait_wt" apply after "$f" "$p" + after "$gg" "$f" 2>&1) || fail "after refused a wait within one tier"
    grep -qxF "$f: todo -> after:$p" <<<"$got" || fail "after moved a row that waits on a row of its own tier"
    got=$(q "$wait_wt" tier "$p" 3 2>&1) || fail "tier refused to move the head of a chain down"
    grep -qxF "$p: tier 2 -> 3, at the foot of ## Tier 3, takes $f along, tier 2 -> 3, takes $gg along, tier 2 -> 3" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "tier did not take the rows that wait on the moved row along, down the chain"
    [ "$(sits "$gg")" = "## Tier 3: 3 after:$f" ] || fail "tier did not move the foot of the chain under ## Tier 3"

    r=$(q "$wait_wt" add 1 opus - "from/- a tier 1 row a tag moves to tier 5 and back §230") || fail "add for the row to tag failed"
    s=$(q "$wait_wt" add 1 opus - "from/- a tier 1 row that waits on the row a tag moves §230") || fail "add for its follower failed"
    got=$(q "$wait_wt" after "$s" "$r" 2>&1) || fail "after refused a wait within tier 1"
    got=$(q "$wait_wt" tag "$r" crash/C 2>&1) || fail "tag refused a C class on a row another row waits on"
    grep -qxF "$r: tag - -> crash/C, tier 1 -> 5, at the foot of ## Tier 5, takes $s along, tier 1 -> 5" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "tag did not take the row that waits on it along to tier 5"
    both=$(q "$wait_wt" add 1 opus - "from/- a tier 1 row that waits on two tier 5 rows §230") || fail "add for the row to wait on two failed"
    got=$(q "$wait_wt" after "$both" "$r,$c5" 2>&1) || fail "after refused a wait on two tier 5 rows"
    grep -qxF "$both: todo -> after:$r,$c5, tier 1 -> 5, beside $r and $c5, which it waits on" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "after did not name both tier 5 rows it moved the row beside"
    got=$(q "$wait_wt" tag "$r" wrong/B 2>&1) || fail "tag refused a B class on a tier 5 row others wait on"
    grep -qxF "$r: tag crash/C -> wrong/B, tier 5 -> 1, at the foot of ## Tier 1, takes $s along, tier 5 -> 1" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "tag did not take up only the row whose after: names no other tier 5 row"
    [ "$(sits "$both")" = "## Tier 5: 5 after:$r,$c5" ] || fail "tag took up a row that still waits on a tier 5 row"

    got=$(q "$wait_wt" tier "$x" 2 2>&1) && fail "tier moved a row up past the tier 5 row it waits on"
    grep -qxF "queue tier: $x waits on $c5, which is tier 5, so in tier 2 it would sit in a tier the fill reaches first (§435)" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "tier did not name the row the moved row waits on"
    got=$(q "$wait_wt" tag "$x" refuses/B 2>&1) && fail "tag moved a row up past the tier 5 row it waits on"
    grep -qxF "queue tag: $x waits on $c5, which is tier 5, so in tier 1 it would sit in a tier the fill reaches first (§435): $mattias" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "tag did not leave a class against a wait to Mattias"
    [ "$(sits "$x")" = "## Tier 5: 5 after:$c5" ] || fail "a refused tag or tier moved the row anyway"

    t=$(q "$wait_wt" add 1 opus - "from/- a tier 1 row the B row waits on §230") || fail "add for the B row's blocker failed"
    got=$(q "$wait_wt" after "$w" "$t" 2>&1) || fail "after refused a B row waiting on a tier 1 row"
    got=$(q "$wait_wt" tag "$t" crash/C 2>&1) && fail "tag moved a row to tier 5 past a B row that waits on it"
    grep -qxF "queue tag: $w waits on $t and carries the class wrong/B, so it cannot follow $t to tier 5: $mattias" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "tag did not name the B row that cannot follow"
    [ "$(sits "$t")" = "## Tier 1: 1 todo" ] || fail "a refused tag moved the row anyway"

    z=$(q "$wait_wt" add 2 opus - "from/- a tier 2 row that waits on a tier 3 row §230") || fail "add for the row to pin failed"
    got=$(q "$wait_wt" after "$z" "$p" 2>&1) || fail "after refused a tier 2 row waiting on a tier 3 row"
    grep -qxF "$z: todo -> after:$p, tier 2 -> 3, beside $p, which it waits on" <<<"$got" || fail "after did not move the row down to tier 3"
    got=$(q "$wait_wt" pin "$z" 2>&1) && fail "pin moved a row to tier 1 past the tier 3 row it waits on"
    grep -qxF "queue pin: $z waits on $p, which is tier 3, and a pinned row is tier 1, which the fill reaches first (§435)" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "pin did not name the row the pinned row would wait on"

    gone5=$(q "$wait_wt" add 5 opus - "crash/C from/- a tier 5 row done while a row waits on it §230") || fail "add for the tier 5 row to close failed"
    y=$(q "$wait_wt" add 1 opus - "from/- a row that sits in tier 5 with no class until a pin §230") || fail "add for the row to pin out of tier 5 failed"
    y2=$(q "$wait_wt" add 1 opus - "from/- a row that follows it out of tier 5 §230") || fail "add for its follower failed"
    gone5_sha=$(g "$wait_wt" rev-parse HEAD)
    got=$(q "$wait_wt" apply after "$y2" "$y" + after "$y" "$gone5" + done "$gone5" "$gone5_sha" 2>&1) || fail "the steps that leave a tier 5 row with no class failed"
    grep -qxF "$y: todo -> after:$gone5, tier 1 -> 5, beside $gone5, which it waits on, takes $y2 along, tier 1 -> 5" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "after did not take the row that waits on the moved row along"
    [ "$(sits "$y2")" = "## Tier 5: 5 after:$y" ] || fail "the follower did not go to tier 5 with the row it waits on"
    got=$(q "$wait_wt" pin "$y" 2>&1) || fail "pin refused a tier 5 row with no class that waits on a closed row"
    grep -qxF "$y: pinned/1, tier 5 -> 1, takes $y2 along, tier 5 -> 1, taken ahead of every row not pinned" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "pin did not take the row that waits on it up out of tier 5"
    got=$(check "$wait_wt") || fail "the file the verbs of §435 wrote fails check-queue"

    # §424.7 (Q1530): a hold narrowed to the files its lane changes frees the
    # rest of the set it was taken with, for the fill in the copy that took it
    # and in another copy that sees it only through the claims file.
    narrow_wt="$root/rcad-worktrees/narrow"
    g "$hub" worktree add -q -b narrow "$narrow_wt" main
    wide=$(q "$narrow_wt" add 2 opus tooling/queue/ "from/- a row taken on a whole directory §230") \
        || fail "add for the row to narrow failed"
    beside=$(q "$narrow_wt" add 2 opus tooling/queue/rows.py "from/- a row on one file under that directory §230") \
        || fail "add for the row beside it failed"
    g "$narrow_wt" commit -qam "two rows, one on a directory and one on a file under it"
    far="$root/rcad-worktrees/narrow-far"
    g "$hub" worktree add -q -b narrow-far "$far" narrow
    got=$(q "$narrow_wt" take "$wide" lane-wide 2>&1) || fail "take of the row on tooling/queue/ was refused"
    got=$(q "$narrow_wt" next 20 2>&1) || fail "next failed in the copy that took the wide row"
    grep -qxF "skip $beside (tier 2): its tooling/queue/rows.py meets $wide's tooling/queue/, held by lane:lane-wide" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "next in the copy that took the wide row did not skip the row on a file under it"
    got=$(q "$far" next 20 2>&1) || fail "next failed in the other copy"
    grep -qxF "skip $beside (tier 2): its tooling/queue/rows.py meets $wide's tooling/queue/, held elsewhere by lane:lane-wide" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "next in the other copy did not skip the row on a file under the wide hold"
    got=$(q "$narrow_wt" narrow "$wide" tooling/queue/queue.py 2>&1) \
        || fail "narrow refused a file under the directory the row was taken with"
    got=$(q "$narrow_wt" next 20 2>&1) || fail "next failed in the taking copy after the narrow"
    grep -q "^take $beside " <<<"$got" || fail "next in the taking copy still skipped a row the narrowed hold no longer meets"
    got=$(q "$far" next 20 2>&1) || fail "next failed in the other copy after the narrow"
    grep -q "^take $beside " <<<"$got" || fail "next in the other copy still skipped a row the narrowed hold no longer meets"
    got=$(q "$narrow_wt" blocked 2>&1) || fail "blocked failed after the narrow"
    grep -q "^skip $beside " <<<"$got" && fail "blocked still holds back a row the narrowed hold no longer meets"
    got=$(q "$narrow_wt" 2>&1) || fail "the plain listing failed after the narrow"
    grep -qxF "      narrowed to tooling/queue/queue.py" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the plain listing did not say what the taking copy's own lane: row was narrowed to"
    got=$(q "$far" 2>&1) || fail "the plain listing failed in the other copy after the narrow"
    grep -qF "  -  lane:lane-wide  -  tooling/queue/queue.py  (held in " <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the other copy's listing did not show the narrowed write set of the hold"
    grep -qF ", narrowed from tooling/queue/)" <<<"$got" || fail "the other copy's listing did not say what the hold was taken with"
    grep -qF "narrowed to <code>tooling/queue/queue.py</code>" "$page_dir/index.html" \
        || fail "the queue page did not say what the hold was narrowed to"
    exit 0

# The fuzz half of `status`: for the night `latest` names, how many of the
# groups `fuzz-triage` found still failing on `main` no row names yet (§418.4).
#
# **Triage files nothing** (§410.5). The 08:30 timer writes the night's
# verdicts and stops, so a group still failing on `main` reached the queue only
# when somebody thought to read the file.
#
# **A row names a group by any one of its crash files** (§418.4), read from
# `fuzz-triage-members.tsv`: a `crash-<hex>` of at least 8 digits, matched as a
# prefix of a member file's own hash. A prefix that matches files in two
# different groups names neither, and refuses rather than guessing. This
# replaced naming by sample, because the sample is the first file `fuzz-triage`
# happened to read and changes as a group grows — 2 of 14 groups failing on
# both 10-07 and 10-08 had a different sample the second morning.
#
# **Open, dropped, and a done row not yet in the replayed `main`, all count the
# same.** A dropped row keeps a fault from being refiled every morning; a done
# row whose `done:<sha>` is not an ancestor of `fuzz-triage-replayed-main.txt`
# was not actually fixed by the `main` that still fails, so it still names its
# group. One that is an ancestor drops out — the fix landed and the row is
# closed for good.
#
# **The ancestor check runs only for a done row naming a crash file.** A place
# only flags a group for a look, it never un-names one (next paragraph), so a
# done row with no crash file changes nothing whether its fix landed or not —
# checking it anyway cost two `git` calls apiece, 39.7s over the 803 done rows
# against `origin/main` on 2026-10-08 at 22:10Z, for every `just status` and
# every `queue next`, against the 22 of those 803 that carry one. A done row
# with no crash file always survives this walk, and one whose sha does not
# resolve no longer refuses: nothing was going to be weighed against it anyway.
#
# **A place is flagged apart, needing a look, but never un-names a group.**
# `writer.rs:226` is the assertion every reader refusal in the writer fuzzer
# reaches, whatever the refusal: on 2026-10-08 four groups failed there, and
# Q1364, which is about one of them, holds that place. A row naming only a
# place does not name its group (§418.4): every group no row names by a crash
# file counts as not named, whether or not a row sits on its place, shared by
# another failing group or not. The ones among those whose place a row does
# name are flagged again, to say which group the row might be about. A group
# named by a file is never flagged this way.
#
# A night with no verdicts file says so, because not triaged is a different
# answer from nothing failing — the rule `tooling/fuzz-yield/nights.py` keeps.
# A night with a verdicts file but not the two §418.4 files beside it predates
# this row: it says so and names the replay that would rewrite all three.
[private]
_status-triage root queue:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -uo pipefail
    latest='{{ root }}/latest'
    if [ ! -f "$latest" ]; then
        echo "no night has finished: $latest does not exist"
        exit 0
    fi
    night=$(awk 'END { print $NF }' "$latest")
    verdicts="$night/fuzz-triage-verdicts.tsv"
    if [ ! -f "$verdicts" ]; then
        echo "$night has no fuzz-triage-verdicts.tsv, so it has not been triaged: \`just fuzz-triage\` replays it against main"
        exit 0
    fi
    members="$night/fuzz-triage-members.tsv"
    replayed_file="$night/fuzz-triage-replayed-main.txt"
    if [ ! -f "$members" ] || [ ! -f "$replayed_file" ]; then
        echo "$night has fuzz-triage-verdicts.tsv but not fuzz-triage-members.tsv and fuzz-triage-replayed-main.txt, so it predates §418.4: \`just fuzz-triage ${night##*/}\` rewrites all three"
        exit 0
    fi
    replayed=$(cat "$replayed_file")
    if ! git rev-parse --verify --quiet "${replayed}^{commit}" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        echo "$replayed_file names $replayed, which does not resolve to a commit here, so nothing could be weighed against it"
        exit 1
    fi

    rows=$(mktemp)
    surviving=$(mktemp)
    trap 'rm -f "$rows" "$surviving"' EXIT
    {{ just_executable() }} --justfile '{{ justfile() }}' _queue-rows '{{ queue }}' \
        | awk -F'\037' '$1 == "R" { print $5 "\037" $7 "\037" $11 }' >"$rows" \
        || { echo "could not read the rows of {{ queue }}, so no group was matched to one"; exit 1; }

    # A done row drops out when its fix already reached the main this night
    # was replayed on; git is needed for that, so this walk happens in bash,
    # ahead of the one awk pass below.
    while IFS=$'\037' read -r id state text; do
        case "$state" in
            done:*)
                if [[ "$text" =~ crash-[0-9a-fA-F]{8} ]]; then
                    sha=${state#done:}
                    if ! git rev-parse --verify --quiet "${sha}^{commit}" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
                        echo "$id: done:$sha does not resolve to a commit here, so this group's status could not be told"
                        exit 1
                    fi
                    git merge-base --is-ancestor "$sha" "$replayed"
                    rc=$?
                    if [ "$rc" -eq 0 ]; then
                        continue
                    elif [ "$rc" -ne 1 ]; then
                        echo "$id: git merge-base --is-ancestor $sha $replayed exited $rc, which this does not know how to read"
                        exit 1
                    fi
                fi
                ;;
        esac
        printf '%s\037%s\n' "$id" "$text" >>"$surviving"
    done <"$rows"

    LC_ALL=C awk -F'\t' -v rows_file="$surviving" -v night="${night##*/}" -v verdicts="$verdicts" -v members="$members" '
        function place_of(group,    rest, at, place) {
            if (substr(group, 1, 12) != "panicked at ") return ""
            rest = substr(group, 13)
            at = index(rest, ": ")
            place = at ? substr(rest, 1, at - 1) : rest
            sub(/:$/, "", place)
            if (place !~ /^[^ ]+:[0-9]+:[0-9]+$/) return "!"
            sub(/:[0-9]+$/, "", place)
            while (sub(/[^\/.][^\/]*\/\.\.\//, "", place)) {}
            return place
        }
        # A row naming writer.rs:2260 does not name writer.rs:226.
        function names_place(row, place,    at, after) {
            while ((at = index(row, place)) > 0) {
                after = substr(row, at + length(place), 1)
                if (after !~ /[0-9]/) return 1
                row = substr(row, at + 1)
            }
            return 0
        }
        function refuse(why) {
            print why
            refused = 1
            exit 1
        }
        FILENAME == rows_file {
            n = split($0, parts, "\037")
            nrows++
            row_id[nrows] = parts[1]
            row_text[nrows] = parts[2]
            next
        }
        FILENAME == members {
            if (FNR == 1) {
                if ($0 != "target\tgroup\tfile")
                    refuse(members ": the header is not target, group, file, so no column can be read by name")
                next
            }
            key = $1 SUBSEP $2
            if (!(key in member_group_seen)) { member_group_seen[key] = 1; member_groups[++nmembergroups] = key }
            base = $3
            sub(/.*\//, "", base)
            if (substr(base, 1, 6) != "crash-")
                refuse(members ": " $3 " does not start with crash-, so no hash could be read from it")
            nhashes++
            hash_list[nhashes] = tolower(substr(base, 7))
            hash_key[nhashes] = key
            next
        }
        FNR == 1 {
            if ($0 != "target\tfiles\tstill_fails\tsample\tgroup")
                refuse(verdicts ": the header is not target, files, still_fails, sample, group, so no column can be read by name")
            next
        }
        $3 == "no" { next }
        $3 != "yes" { refuse(verdicts ": line " FNR " says still_fails is \"" $3 "\", which is neither yes nor no") }
        {
            failing++
            group_key[failing] = $1 SUBSEP $5
            verdict_group_seen[$1 SUBSEP $5] = 1
            place[failing] = place_of($5)
            if (place[failing] == "!") refuse(verdicts ": line " FNR " is a panic whose place this cannot read: " $5)
        }
        END {
            if (refused) exit 1
            for (i = 1; i <= nmembergroups; i++) {
                if (!(member_groups[i] in verdict_group_seen))
                    refuse(members ": names a group that " verdicts " does not list as still failing; `just fuzz-triage " night "` would refresh it")
            }
            for (i = 1; i <= failing; i++) {
                if (!(group_key[i] in member_group_seen))
                    refuse(members ": is missing a still-failing group that " verdicts " lists; `just fuzz-triage " night "` would refresh it")
            }
            if (!failing) {
                printf "%s: no group still fails on main\n", night
                exit 0
            }
            for (r = 1; r <= nrows; r++) {
                s = row_text[r]
                while (match(s, /crash-[0-9a-fA-F]{8,}/)) {
                    tok = tolower(substr(s, RSTART + 6, RLENGTH - 6))
                    s = substr(s, RSTART + RLENGTH)
                    matched_key = ""
                    ambiguous = 0
                    for (h = 1; h <= nhashes; h++) {
                        if (substr(hash_list[h], 1, length(tok)) == tok) {
                            if (matched_key == "") matched_key = hash_key[h]
                            else if (matched_key != hash_key[h]) ambiguous = 1
                        }
                    }
                    if (ambiguous) refuse(row_id[r] ": names crash-" tok ", which is a prefix of crash files in more than one still-failing group")
                    if (matched_key != "") named_by_file[matched_key] = 1
                }
            }
            if (refused) exit 1
            for (i = 1; i <= failing; i++) {
                key = group_key[i]
                if (key in named_by_file) continue
                unnamed++
                by_place = 0
                for (r = 1; r <= nrows; r++) {
                    if (place[i] != "" && names_place(row_text[r], place[i])) by_place = 1
                }
                if (by_place) shared_only++
            }
            printf "%s: %d group(s) still fail on main: %d named by no row, and %d of those named only by a place, needing a look\n", night, failing, unnamed + 0, shared_only + 0
            printf "(read from %s: a row names a group by one of its crash files, or by the place of its panic)\n", verdicts
        }
    ' "$surviving" "$members" "$verdicts"

# §418.2: `queue next` refuses while a night has left groups no row names and
# no triage row is open, naming `just fuzz-triage-row`. Any other answer of
# `_status-triage` — no night, no verdicts file, zero failing groups, a hard
# error — is not this recipe's to escalate: it is echoed and this exits 0.
#
# A triage row is told apart from any other by its text alone, since no
# structural marker exists for one (§418.3): an open row (`todo`, `after:` or
# `lane:`) whose write set is `-` and whose description holds the literal
# words "Triage the latest fuzz night".
[private]
_triage-refusal root queue:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -uo pipefail
    status=$({{ just_executable() }} --justfile '{{ justfile() }}' _status-triage '{{ root }}' '{{ queue }}')
    echo "$status"
    unnamed=$(sed -n 's/.*: [0-9][0-9]* group(s) still fail on main: \([0-9][0-9]*\) named by no row.*/\1/p' <<<"$status" | tail -1)
    [ -n "$unnamed" ] && [ "$unnamed" -gt 0 ] || exit 0
    open=$({{ just_executable() }} --justfile '{{ justfile() }}' _queue-rows '{{ queue }}' 2>/dev/null \
        | awk -F'\037' '$1 == "R" && $9 == "-" && ($7 == "todo" || $7 ~ /^after:/ || $7 ~ /^lane:/) && index($11, "Triage the latest fuzz night") > 0 { print $5; exit }')
    [ -z "$open" ] || exit 0
    echo "queue next: $unnamed group(s) still fail on main that no row names, and no triage row is open; run \`just fuzz-triage-row\` to file one (§418.2, §418.3)"
    exit 1

[doc("Files the triage row of §418.3 when a night's fuzz replay leaves groups no row names, and says which it did.")]
fuzz-triage-row root=fuzz_log_root queue=queue_file:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -uo pipefail
    status=$({{ just_executable() }} --justfile '{{ justfile() }}' _status-triage '{{ root }}' '{{ queue }}')
    echo "$status"
    unnamed=$(sed -n 's/.*: [0-9][0-9]* group(s) still fail on main: \([0-9][0-9]*\) named by no row.*/\1/p' <<<"$status" | tail -1)
    if [ -z "$unnamed" ] || [ "$unnamed" -eq 0 ]; then
        echo "fuzz-triage-row: nothing to file"
        exit 0
    fi
    open=$({{ just_executable() }} --justfile '{{ justfile() }}' _queue-rows '{{ queue }}' 2>/dev/null \
        | awk -F'\037' '$1 == "R" && $9 == "-" && ($7 == "todo" || $7 ~ /^after:/ || $7 ~ /^lane:/) && index($11, "Triage the latest fuzz night") > 0 { print $5; exit }')
    if [ -n "$open" ]; then
        echo "fuzz-triage-row: $open is already open"
        exit 0
    fi
    id=$({{ just_executable() }} --justfile '{{ justfile() }}' --working-directory "$PWD" --set queue_file '{{ queue }}' queue add 1 opus - \
        "from/- Triage the latest fuzz night's groups that still fail on main and that no row names by a crash file, as section 418.3 says: read the night's verdicts when the lane starts, report one row per fault with its class naming one of the group's crash files, starting each from/fuzz:<target>, and file again any open row that names a group by place alone. §418.3") \
        || exit 1
    echo "fuzz-triage-row: filed $id"

# The branch half of `status`, kept apart so `check-status-selftest` can hold it
# against a repository whose right answer is known.
#
# **`git cherry` and not `rev-list main..<branch>`.** Ahead-of-main counts what
# is reachable; what a reader wants is what is *unlanded*, and the two disagree
# on every branch whose commits reached `main` by a cherry-pick, a rebase or a
# split into two commits. On 2026-09-14 that was three of the five branches the
# count called unmerged — one of them `merge/justfile`, whose recipe had been on
# `main` for days. `cherry` compares patch ids and marks those `-`, so counting
# only its `+` lines turns a five-branch answer into a two-branch one without a
# human reading five diffs. It skips merge commits, which is the same thing
# wanted here: a lane merging `main` into itself has landed nothing.
#
# **A branch with nothing new is counted, not listed.** The count is what says
# the sweep ran; listing twenty-three names nobody has to act on is how a survey
# stops being read.
[private]
_status-branches dir base:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -uo pipefail
    repo=$(git -C '{{dir}}' rev-parse --show-toplevel) || exit 1
    if ! git -C "$repo" rev-parse --verify --quiet '{{base}}^{commit}' >/dev/null; then
        echo "there is no branch named {{base}} here, so nothing could be weighed against it"
        exit 1
    fi

    trees=$(git -C "$repo" worktree list --porcelain \
        | awk '/^worktree /{t=substr($0,10)} /^branch /{print substr($0,19) "\t" t}')

    branches=$(git -C "$repo" for-each-ref --format='%(refname:short)' refs/heads/)
    if [ -z "$branches" ]; then
        echo "for-each-ref printed no branches, so this counted nothing"
        exit 1
    fi

    carrying=0
    quiet=0
    while IFS= read -r branch; do
        [ "$branch" != '{{base}}' ] || continue
        new=$(git -C "$repo" cherry '{{base}}' "$branch" 2>/dev/null | grep -c '^+')
        if [ "$new" -eq 0 ]; then
            quiet=$((quiet + 1))
            continue
        fi
        carrying=$((carrying + 1))
        where=$(awk -F'\t' -v b="$branch" '$1 == b { print $2 }' <<<"$trees")
        printf '%-46s %2d commit(s)  %s\n' "$branch" "$new" "${where:-no worktree}"
    done <<<"$branches"

    if [ "$carrying" -eq 0 ]; then
        echo "nothing: every branch here is already on {{base}}"
    fi
    echo "$quiet branch(es) carry nothing {{base}} does not already have"

# The green half of `status`, kept apart for the same reason as the branch half:
# the live repository's answer is whatever happened to it this week, so the ways
# this row goes wrong can only be pinned against a fixture.
#
# **Two sources, because since §172 one of them can no longer answer.** The
# Actions allowance is spent, so no push to `main` can go green on the runner,
# and a row that asks only GitHub reports a tip drifting further from a green
# that cannot move — 22 commits within a day, while a full `just gate` had in
# fact read the tip end to end. That is the same staleness §173.9 fixed for
# `fuzz-watermark`, and this was the other place it showed: the row a session
# reads first. `_record-green` writes each local gate down; this reads that file
# beside `gh`, and §173.9's rule about what may be written there — every metered
# step ran, on a clean tree — is what makes the two comparable at all.
#
# **It says which source answered, and that is the point rather than a detail.**
# A CI green is a different machine; a local green is *this* machine, the one
# §173 spent a day finding four environment-dependent faults in, so the two are
# not the same evidence and a row that blurred them would be the more expensive
# error now that almost every green is local. §173.9 left that caveat owed; this
# is where it gets paid, next to the number it qualifies.
[private]
_status-green dir base:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -uo pipefail

    file=""
    common=$(git -C '{{dir}}' rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir 2>/dev/null) \
        && file="$common/rcad-greens"

    # Both sources normalised to full shas in *this* checkout, so that the
    # source test below is a string compare it cannot get subtly wrong, and so
    # a sha naming no commit here drops out before it can be chosen.
    keep() {
        for sha in $1; do
            git -C '{{dir}}' rev-parse --verify --quiet "${sha}^{commit}" 2>/dev/null
        done
    }
    raw_ci=""
    if command -v gh >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        raw_ci=$(gh run list --workflow ci.yml --branch '{{base}}' --status success \
            --limit 20 --json headSha --jq '.[].headSha' 2>/dev/null) || raw_ci=""
    fi
    raw_local=$(cut -d' ' -f1 "$file" 2>/dev/null) || raw_local=""
    ci=$(keep "$raw_ci")
    local_greens=$(keep "$raw_local")

    if [ -z "$raw_ci$raw_local" ]; then
        if command -v gh >/dev/null 2>&1; then
            echo "unknown: neither gh nor the local gate record names a green commit on {{base}}"
        else
            echo "unknown: gh is not on PATH and no local gate has been recorded"
        fi
        exit 0
    fi

    # The furthest forward of the candidates, not the first that qualifies: two
    # sources merged arrive in no useful order, so the walk establishes it here.
    best=""
    for sha in $ci $local_greens; do
        git -C '{{dir}}' merge-base --is-ancestor "$sha" '{{base}}' 2>/dev/null || continue
        if [ -z "$best" ] || git -C '{{dir}}' merge-base --is-ancestor "$best" "$sha" 2>/dev/null; then
            best=$sha
        fi
    done
    if [ -z "$best" ]; then
        echo "no green commit is on {{base}} in this checkout; fetch, or gate it here"
        exit 0
    fi

    case " $ci " in *" $best "*) from_ci=yes ;; *) from_ci=no ;; esac
    case " $local_greens " in *" $best "*) from_local=yes ;; *) from_local=no ;; esac
    if [ "$from_ci" = yes ] && [ "$from_local" = yes ]; then
        how="CI and a local gate"
    elif [ "$from_ci" = yes ]; then
        how="CI"
    else
        how="a local gate$(awk -v s="$best" '$1 == s { printf " on %s, %s", $3, $2 }' "$file" 2>/dev/null)"
    fi

    ahead=$(git -C '{{dir}}' rev-list --count "$best"..'{{base}}' 2>/dev/null)
    printf '%s tip %s, last green %s (%s), %s commit(s) on %s not yet green\n' \
        '{{base}}' "$(git -C '{{dir}}' rev-parse --short '{{base}}')" \
        "$(git -C '{{dir}}' rev-parse --short "$best")" "$how" "${ahead:-?}" '{{base}}'
    [ "$from_ci" = yes ] \
        || echo "  nothing independent has read it: a local gate is this machine, §173 and §173.9"

# §0 is the one row of `status` that a recipe cannot compute, so this is what
# stands behind it. It cannot check the judgement — whether the unit named there
# is still the unit — and it does not pretend to. It checks the three ways the
# block goes wrong without anyone noticing.
#
# **A cited section that does not exist.** §0 points at the sections that defer
# the work it lists, and those numbers get renumbered (§71 is four such moves in
# one session). A block pointing at a §43.8 that has become §44.8 is worse than
# no block, because it reads as current.
#
# **A `reviewed:` date that does not parse.** `status` prints how old the block
# is, and a date `date -d` cannot read makes that line silently vanish — which
# looks exactly like a block reviewed today.
#
# **A block that has grown into an essay.** The cap is the whole reason this
# stays true: `decisions.md` is 27 000 lines because nothing ever capped it, and
# that is correct for a record and fatal for a summary. Fifty lines is about a
# screen, which is the length something gets re-read at. The refusal names how
# many lines to cut, because the 21 trims of 2026-09-17..20 took 34 rounds
# between them — 67 to 56 to 54 to 51 is one of them — and a trim by eye aims
# short.
#
# **It reads the concatenation, not §0's own file** (§179). Only half of what it
# checks lives in §0: the `reviewed:` date and the 50-line cap do, but the last
# arm walks every `§N` and `§N.M` the block cites and asks whether that heading
# exists — and after the split those headings are in 177 other files. Pointing
# this at `docs/decisions.d/000-*.md` alone would have turned every correct
# citation in §0 into "cites sections that do not exist", which is louder than
# the failure it was written for and wrong every time. `decisions-all` is the
# same bytes the single file held, so the check below is the one that ran
# against it, unchanged, and its fixtures are unchanged with it.
[doc("docs/decisions.md §0 exists, parses, and points only at sections that do.")]
check-where-the-work-is:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -uo pipefail
    all=$(mktemp)
    trap 'rm -f "$all"' EXIT
    {{ just_executable() }} decisions-all > "$all" || exit 1
    {{ just_executable() }} _where-the-work-is-check "$all" '{{ decisions_file }}'

# Held apart from the recipe above so the selftest can run it over fixtures
# whose answer is known, rather than over whatever §0 happens to say today.
#
# `name` is what a failure calls the file, so the recipe above can hand over a
# temporary concatenation and still have the message name `docs/decisions.md`,
# which is the path a reader has to go and edit. It defaults to `file`, which is
# what the fixtures pass.
[private]
_where-the-work-is-check file name="":
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -uo pipefail
    file='{{file}}'
    name='{{name}}'
    [ -n "$name" ] || name="$file"

    block=$(awk '/^## 0\. /{on=1} on && /^## [1-9]/{exit} on{print}' "$file")
    if [ -z "$block" ]; then
        echo "$name has no '## 0.' section, so nothing says where the work is"
        exit 1
    fi
    if ! head -1 <<<"$block" | grep -qx '## 0\. Where the work is'; then
        echo "$(head -1 <<<"$block")"
        echo "^ §0 is reserved for 'Where the work is'; \`just status\` prints it by that name"
        exit 1
    fi

    lines=$(grep -c . <<<"$block")
    if [ "$lines" -gt 50 ]; then
        echo "§0 is $lines non-blank lines; the cap is 50, because a summary nobody re-reads is not one"
        echo "^ cut $((lines - 50)) non-blank lines, in one pass: trimming by eye takes two to four (§102.3)"
        exit 1
    fi

    reviewed=$(sed -n 's/^reviewed: *//p' <<<"$block" | head -1)
    if [ -z "$reviewed" ]; then
        echo "§0 has no 'reviewed: <date>' line, so \`just status\` cannot say how stale it is"
        exit 1
    fi
    if ! date -d "$reviewed" +%s >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        echo "§0's reviewed date '$reviewed' is not one \`date -d\` can read, so its age would print as nothing"
        exit 1
    fi

    missing=""
    for id in $(grep -oE '§[0-9]+(\.[0-9]+)?' <<<"$block" | sed 's/^§//' | sort -u); do
        case "$id" in
        *.*) pattern="^### ${id//./\\.} " ;;
        *)   pattern="^## ${id}\. " ;;
        esac
        grep -qE "$pattern" "$file" || missing="$missing $id"
    done
    if [ -n "$missing" ]; then
        echo "§0 cites sections that do not exist:$missing"
        echo "^ a renumbering moved them; §0 reads as current whether or not it is (docs/decisions.md §71)"
        exit 1
    fi

    # Headroom, not a verdict (§178.4): a session that knows it has three lines
    # left writes the trim into the edit it is already making, where "clean"
    # alone sends it to find out at the gate instead. Printed on every clean run
    # of this check, including from `just gate` — `decisions-zero.sh` is what
    # this line was written for, but there is no reason `gate` should see less.
    echo "§0 is $lines of 50 non-blank lines, reviewed $reviewed, every section it cites exists."

# Pins the computed rows of `status` against fixtures, because the live repository
# cannot exercise any of them. Its §0 is right today by construction, and its
# branches are whatever happens to be lying around — a `_status-branches` that
# had lost the ability to tell a cherry-picked branch from an unlanded one would
# pass on most mornings and fail on the one that mattered.
#
# The branch fixture is the 2026-09-14 tree in miniature: a branch whose commit
# was cherry-picked onto main (ahead by the count, carrying nothing), a branch
# with real unlanded work, a branch fully merged, and a branch checked out in a
# worktree, which must be reported with the tree that holds it.
#
# The §0 fixtures are the three failures the check exists for, plus a good one,
# because a check that rejects everything passes every negative case.
#
# The fuzz fixtures are one night for each rule `_status-triage` keeps, so a
# rule that is lost fails the arm that names it.
[private]
check-status-selftest:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -uo pipefail
    dir=$(mktemp -d)
    trap 'rm -rf "$dir"' EXIT
    fail() {
        echo "status drifted from its own fixtures: $1"
        echo "--- got ---"; echo "$got"
        exit 1
    }

    git init -q -b main "$dir/repo"
    git -C "$dir/repo" config user.email t@t
    git -C "$dir/repo" config user.name t
    echo root > "$dir/repo/f"; git -C "$dir/repo" add f
    git -C "$dir/repo" commit -q -m root

    git -C "$dir/repo" checkout -q -b picked
    echo picked > "$dir/repo/p"; git -C "$dir/repo" add p
    git -C "$dir/repo" commit -q -m "a patch that reaches main another way"
    picked=$(git -C "$dir/repo" rev-parse HEAD)

    git -C "$dir/repo" checkout -q -b unlanded main
    echo unlanded > "$dir/repo/u"; git -C "$dir/repo" add u
    git -C "$dir/repo" commit -q -m "work that is genuinely not on main"

    git -C "$dir/repo" checkout -q main
    # Without an unrelated commit here first, cherry-picking onto the same
    # parent reproduces the byte-identical sha, `main..picked` is empty, and the
    # fixture quietly stops testing the thing it exists for: the broken
    # ahead-of-main implementation passed it.
    echo other > "$dir/repo/x"; git -C "$dir/repo" add x
    git -C "$dir/repo" commit -q -m "an unrelated commit, so the pick is not the same sha"
    git -C "$dir/repo" cherry-pick "$picked" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
        || { echo "the fixture's cherry-pick did not apply, so nothing here was tested"; exit 1; }
    [ "$(git -C "$dir/repo" rev-list --count main..picked)" -eq 1 ] \
        || { echo "the fixture's picked branch is reachable from main, so it cannot tell the two counts apart"; exit 1; }
    git -C "$dir/repo" branch -q merged main
    git -C "$dir/repo" worktree add -q "$dir/held" unlanded

    got=$(just _status-branches "$dir/repo" main)
    grep -q "^unlanded  *1 commit(s)  *$dir/held$" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the branch with real work is not listed with the worktree holding it"
    grep -q '^picked' <<<"$got" \
        && fail "a branch whose patch is already on main was listed as carrying work"
    grep -q '^merged' <<<"$got" \
        && fail "a fully merged branch was listed as carrying work"
    grep -qx "2 branch(es) carry nothing main does not already have" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the quiet count is wrong, so a broken sweep could pass as a clean tree"

    got=$(just _status-branches "$dir/repo" no-such-branch 2>&1) \
        && fail "weighing against a branch that does not exist did not fail"
    grep -qF "no branch named no-such-branch" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a missing base branch did not say so"

    # The green row, against a stub `gh` so no arm needs the network or an
    # account.
    # What is pinned here is that the local record can answer *alone*: with the
    # Actions allowance spent that is the only source there is, and the version
    # of this row that asked GitHub and stopped reported a tip 22 commits from a
    # green while a full gate had read that very tip. §173.9.
    mkdir -p "$dir/bin"
    cat >"$dir/bin/gh" <<'STUB'
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    cat "$GH_GREEN"
    STUB
    sed -i 's/^    //' "$dir/bin/gh"
    chmod +x "$dir/bin/gh"
    : >"$dir/ci"
    greens="$dir/repo/.git/rcad-greens"
    : >"$greens"

    git -C "$dir/repo" checkout -q main
    two=$(git -C "$dir/repo" rev-parse main~1)
    three=$(git -C "$dir/repo" rev-parse main)
    aside=$(git -C "$dir/repo" rev-parse unlanded)

    green_row() {
        GH_GREEN="$dir/ci" PATH="$dir/bin:$PATH" \
            just _status-green "$dir/repo" main 2>&1
    }

    printf '%s 2026-09-20T00:00:00Z somehost\n' "$two" >"$greens"
    got=$(green_row)
    grep -q "last green $(cut -c1-7 <<<"$two") (a local gate on somehost" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "with gh silent the local gate record did not answer, which is the only source left"
    grep -q "1 commit(s) on main not yet green" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the local green was named but main was not weighed against it"
    grep -qF "nothing independent has read it" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a green only this machine has seen was not said to be this machine's"

    # Furthest forward, not first seen: the two sources merged arrive in no
    # order, so a loop taking the first match would pass only by luck.
    printf '%s\n' "$two" >"$dir/ci"
    printf '%s 2026-09-20T00:00:00Z somehost\n' "$three" >"$greens"
    got=$(green_row)
    grep -q "last green $(cut -c1-7 <<<"$three")" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "it chose a green behind the furthest one that qualifies"

    # A CI green is a different machine, so it carries no caveat — and the row
    # must still be able to say so, or the caveat means nothing when it appears.
    printf '%s\n' "$three" >"$dir/ci"
    printf '%s 2026-09-20T00:00:00Z somehost\n' "$two" >"$greens"
    got=$(green_row)
    grep -qF "(CI)" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a green CI run on the tip was not credited to CI"
    grep -qF "nothing independent has read it" <<<"$got" \
        && fail "an independent green was still called this machine's"

    printf '%s\n' "$three" >"$dir/ci"
    printf '%s 2026-09-20T00:00:00Z somehost\n' "$three" >"$greens"
    got=$(green_row)
    grep -qF "(CI and a local gate)" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a commit both sources name was credited to only one of them"

    # Green somewhere that is not `main` moves nothing, and a sha naming no
    # commit here is dropped rather than crashing the row.
    printf '%s\n0000000000000000000000000000000000000000\n' "$aside" >"$dir/ci"
    : >"$greens"
    got=$(green_row)
    grep -qF "no green commit is on main in this checkout" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a green belonging to another branch was counted as main's"

    # The fuzz row. A verdict's sample sits under /night/ and a member's own
    # crash file is what a row names (§418.4). A done row's sha, and the main a
    # night replayed on, are resolved as git commits wherever `_status-triage`
    # runs, so a scratch git repo stands in for this session's own history
    # rather than risking a sha that may or may not resolve in the real one.
    scratch="$dir/triage-repo"
    git init -q -b main "$scratch"
    git -C "$scratch" config user.email t@t
    git -C "$scratch" config user.name t
    echo one > "$scratch/f"; git -C "$scratch" add f
    git -C "$scratch" commit -q -m "a fix that reached the replayed main"
    fixed_sha=$(git -C "$scratch" rev-parse HEAD)
    echo two > "$scratch/f"; git -C "$scratch" add f
    git -C "$scratch" commit -q -m "the main the night replayed on"
    replayed_sha=$(git -C "$scratch" rev-parse HEAD)
    git -C "$scratch" checkout -q -b aside "$fixed_sha"
    echo three > "$scratch/g"; git -C "$scratch" add g
    git -C "$scratch" commit -q -m "a fix that never reached the replayed main"
    not_ancestor_sha=$(git -C "$scratch" rev-parse HEAD)
    git -C "$scratch" checkout -q main
    bogus_sha=deadbeefdeadbeefdeadbeefdeadbeefdeadbeef

    logs="$dir/fuzz-logs"
    mkdir -p "$logs"
    full_hash=$(printf 'cafefeed%032d' 1)
    prefix_hash=$(printf 'beadfeed%032d' 2)
    twin_a=$(printf 'abcdef01%032d' 3)
    twin_b=$(printf 'abcdef01%032d' 4)
    dropped_hash=$(printf 'd00dfeed%032d' 5)
    notanc_hash=$(printf 'f00dfeed%032d' 6)
    closed_hash=$(printf 'c105eeed%032d' 7)
    bogus_crash_hash=$(printf 'b090fee5%032d' 8)

    printf '%s\n' '## Tier 1' '' '```' \
        'Q1 1 parked:§1 opus - a coedge at crates/rcad-step/tests/fuzzing/writer.rs:384 §1' \
        'Q2 1 todo opus - a refusal at crates/rcad-step/tests/fuzzing/writer.rs:226 §1' \
        'Q3 1 todo opus - the fuse at crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/arrange.rs:2306 §1' \
        "Q5 1 todo opus - names the crash file in full, crash-$full_hash §1" \
        "Q6 1 todo opus - names only an 8-digit prefix, crash-${prefix_hash:0:8} §1" \
        "Q7 1 todo opus - names a prefix two groups' crash files share, crash-${twin_a:0:8} §1" \
        "Q8 1 dropped:§1 opus - decided not worth fixing, crash-$dropped_hash §1" \
        '```' '' '## Closed' '' '```' \
        "Q4 1 done:$fixed_sha opus - the fuse at crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/arrange.rs:999 §1" \
        "Q9 1 done:$not_ancestor_sha opus - a fix that never reached the replayed main, crash-$notanc_hash §1" \
        "Q11 1 done:$fixed_sha opus - a fix that reached the replayed main, crash-$closed_hash §1" \
        '```' >"$dir/queue.md"

    point_latest_at() {
        printf '2026-10-08 08:15  abc1234 (0 days old, 0 behind main)  5 targets  %s\n' "$logs/$1" >"$logs/latest"
    }
    night_with() {
        local night=$1
        shift
        mkdir -p "$logs/$night"
        printf 'target\tfiles\tstill_fails\tsample\tgroup\n' >"$logs/$night/fuzz-triage-verdicts.tsv"
        printf '%s\n' "$@" >>"$logs/$night/fuzz-triage-verdicts.tsv"
        if [ "${old_format:-0}" != 1 ]; then
            {
                printf 'target\tgroup\tfile\n'
                local line target files still_fails sample group
                for line in "$@"; do
                    IFS=$'\t' read -r target files still_fails sample group <<<"$line"
                    [ "$still_fails" = yes ] || continue
                    printf '%s\t%s\t%s\n' "$target" "$group" "$sample"
                done
            } >"$logs/$night/fuzz-triage-members.tsv"
            printf '%s\n' "$replayed_sha" >"$logs/$night/fuzz-triage-replayed-main.txt"
        fi
        point_latest_at "$night"
    }
    triage_row() {
        just --justfile '{{justfile()}}' --working-directory "$scratch" \
            _status-triage "$logs" "${1:-$dir/queue.md}" 2>&1
    }

    got=$(triage_row)
    grep -qxF "no night has finished: $logs/latest does not exist" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a log root no night has finished in did not say so"

    mkdir -p "$logs/untriaged"
    point_latest_at untriaged
    got=$(triage_row)
    grep -qF "$logs/untriaged has no fuzz-triage-verdicts.tsv, so it has not been triaged" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a night with no verdicts file was not called untriaged"

    night_with decoder-path $'t\t3\tyes\t/night/artifacts/t/crash-aaaa\tpanicked at crates/rcad-fuzz-explain/src/../../rcad-step/tests/fuzzing/writer.rs:384:13: a coedge came back with a different edge run'
    got=$(triage_row)
    grep -qxF "decoder-path: 1 group(s) still fail on main: 1 named by no row, and 1 of those named only by a place, needing a look" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a parked row naming the place, without the decoder's ../../ and the column, was not counted unnamed and flagged for a look"

    night_with shared-place \
        $'t\t5\tyes\t/night/artifacts/t/crash-bbbb\tpanicked at crates/rcad-fuzz-explain/src/../../rcad-step/tests/fuzzing/writer.rs:226:9: the reader refuses: one reason' \
        $'t\t1\tyes\t/night/artifacts/t/crash-cccc\tpanicked at crates/rcad-fuzz-explain/src/../../rcad-step/tests/fuzzing/writer.rs:226:9: the reader refuses: another reason'
    got=$(triage_row)
    grep -qxF "shared-place: 2 group(s) still fail on main: 2 named by no row, and 2 of those named only by a place, needing a look" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "two groups sharing one place and named by neither file nor a unique place were not both counted unnamed and flagged"

    night_with closed-row $'t\t1\tyes\t/night/artifacts/t/crash-dddd\tpanicked at crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/arrange.rs:999:1: unreachable'
    got=$(triage_row)
    grep -qxF "closed-row: 1 group(s) still fail on main: 1 named by no row, and 1 of those named only by a place, needing a look" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a done row with no crash file was weighed against the replayed main instead of always surviving"

    night_with closed-row-crash $'t\t1\tyes\t/night/artifacts/t/crash-'"$closed_hash"$'\tpanicked at crates/rcad-geom/src/closed.rs:95:5: fix already landed check'
    got=$(triage_row)
    grep -qxF "closed-row-crash: 1 group(s) still fail on main: 1 named by no row, and 0 of those named only by a place, needing a look" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a done row naming a crash file whose fix already reached the replayed main was still counted as naming its group"

    night_with longer-line $'t\t1\tyes\t/night/artifacts/t/crash-eeee\tpanicked at crates/rcad-topo/src/fuse/arrange.rs:230:5: unreachable'
    got=$(triage_row)
    grep -qxF "longer-line: 1 group(s) still fail on main: 1 named by no row, and 0 of those named only by a place, needing a look" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a row naming arrange.rs:2306 was taken to name arrange.rs:230"

    night_with no-place \
        $'t\t247\tyes\t/night/artifacts/t/crash-ffff\tt: bytes did not decode to a case' \
        $'t\t9\tno\t/night/artifacts/t/crash-1111\tt: no longer fails — succeeded'
    got=$(triage_row)
    grep -qxF "no-place: 1 group(s) still fail on main: 1 named by no row, and 0 of those named only by a place, needing a look" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a failing group with no panic place, beside one that no longer fails, was miscounted"

    night_with all-fixed $'t\t9\tno\t/night/artifacts/t/crash-1111\tt: no longer fails — succeeded'
    got=$(triage_row)
    grep -qxF "all-fixed: no group still fails on main" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a night whose groups all pass on main did not say so"

    night_with bad-place $'t\t1\tyes\t/night/artifacts/t/crash-2222\tpanicked at somewhere: it broke'
    got=$(triage_row) && fail "a panic whose place has no line and column was read"
    grep -qF "is a panic whose place this cannot read" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a panic place this cannot read did not say so"

    night_with bad-header
    printf 'target\tfiles\tsample\tgroup\n' >"$logs/bad-header/fuzz-triage-verdicts.tsv"
    got=$(triage_row) && fail "a verdicts file with a column missing was read"
    grep -qF "the header is not target, files, still_fails, sample, group" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a verdicts file with a column missing did not say its header moved"

    night_with named-full $'t\t1\tyes\t/night/artifacts/t/crash-'"$full_hash"$'\tpanicked at crates/rcad-geom/src/foo.rs:10:5: full hash check'
    got=$(triage_row)
    grep -qxF "named-full: 1 group(s) still fail on main: 0 named by no row, and 0 of those named only by a place, needing a look" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a row naming a crash file's full 40-digit hash did not name the group"

    night_with named-prefix $'t\t1\tyes\t/night/artifacts/t/crash-'"$prefix_hash"$'\tpanicked at crates/rcad-geom/src/bar.rs:20:5: prefix hash check'
    got=$(triage_row)
    grep -qxF "named-prefix: 1 group(s) still fail on main: 0 named by no row, and 0 of those named only by a place, needing a look" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a row naming only an 8-digit prefix of a crash file's hash did not name the group"

    night_with ambiguous-prefix \
        $'t\t1\tyes\t/night/artifacts/t/crash-'"$twin_a"$'\tpanicked at crates/rcad-geom/src/baz1.rs:30:5: twin one' \
        $'t\t1\tyes\t/night/artifacts/t/crash-'"$twin_b"$'\tpanicked at crates/rcad-geom/src/baz2.rs:40:5: twin two'
    got=$(triage_row) && fail "a prefix matching crash files in two different still-failing groups was read"
    grep -qF "Q7: names crash-${twin_a:0:8}, which is a prefix of crash files in more than one still-failing group" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "an ambiguous crash-file prefix did not name the row and say so"

    night_with dropped-names-file $'t\t1\tyes\t/night/artifacts/t/crash-'"$dropped_hash"$'\tpanicked at crates/rcad-geom/src/qux.rs:50:5: dropped check'
    got=$(triage_row)
    grep -qxF "dropped-names-file: 1 group(s) still fail on main: 0 named by no row, and 0 of those named only by a place, needing a look" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a dropped row naming a crash file did not name the group, so the fault would be refiled every morning"

    night_with done-not-ancestor $'t\t1\tyes\t/night/artifacts/t/crash-'"$notanc_hash"$'\tpanicked at crates/rcad-geom/src/quux.rs:60:5: not ancestor check'
    got=$(triage_row)
    grep -qxF "done-not-ancestor: 1 group(s) still fail on main: 0 named by no row, and 0 of those named only by a place, needing a look" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a done row whose fix never reached the replayed main was not counted as naming its group"

    old_format=1 night_with old-format $'t\t1\tyes\t/night/artifacts/t/crash-aaaaaaaaaa\tpanicked at crates/rcad-geom/src/old.rs:70:5: old format check'
    got=$(triage_row)
    grep -qF "$logs/old-format has fuzz-triage-verdicts.tsv but not fuzz-triage-members.tsv and fuzz-triage-replayed-main.txt, so it predates §418.4" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a night with a verdicts file but not the two §418.4 files did not say it predates them"
    grep -qF "just fuzz-triage old-format" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a night that predates §418.4 did not name the replay that would rewrite it"

    night_with members-mismatch $'t\t1\tyes\t/night/artifacts/t/crash-bbbbbbbbbb\tpanicked at crates/rcad-geom/src/mismatch.rs:80:5: mismatch check'
    printf 'target\tgroup\tfile\n' >"$logs/members-mismatch/fuzz-triage-members.tsv"
    got=$(triage_row) && fail "a members file missing a still-failing group that the verdicts file lists was read"
    grep -qF "fuzz-triage-members.tsv: is missing a still-failing group" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a members file missing a still-failing group did not name the file"

    printf '%s\n' '## Tier 1' '' '```' \
        "Q10 1 done:$bogus_sha opus - a done row whose sha this repo never heard of §1" \
        '```' >"$dir/queue-bad-sha.md"
    night_with done-unresolvable $'t\t1\tyes\t/night/artifacts/t/crash-cccccccccc\tpanicked at crates/rcad-geom/src/badsha.rs:90:5: bad sha check'
    got=$(triage_row "$dir/queue-bad-sha.md") \
        || fail "a done row with no crash file whose sha does not resolve refused, instead of always surviving unweighed"
    grep -qxF "done-unresolvable: 1 group(s) still fail on main: 1 named by no row, and 0 of those named only by a place, needing a look" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a done row with no crash file and an unresolvable sha changed the count instead of being skipped"

    printf '%s\n' '## Tier 1' '' '```' \
        "Q12 1 done:$bogus_sha opus - a done row whose sha this repo never heard of, crash-$bogus_crash_hash §1" \
        '```' >"$dir/queue-bad-sha-crash.md"
    night_with done-unresolvable-crash $'t\t1\tyes\t/night/artifacts/t/crash-'"$bogus_crash_hash"$'\tpanicked at crates/rcad-geom/src/badshacrash.rs:95:5: bad sha with crash file check'
    got=$(triage_row "$dir/queue-bad-sha-crash.md") && fail "a done row naming a crash file whose sha does not resolve to a commit was read"
    grep -qF "Q12: done:$bogus_sha does not resolve to a commit here" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a done row naming a crash file, whose sha does not resolve, did not name the row"

    good='## 0. Where the work is

    reviewed: 2026-09-14

    **unit:** the thing, opened in §3.1 and still open.

    ## 1. Something else
    ### 3.1 Not really under 1, but this fixture is not check-decisions
    '
    printf '%s\n' "$good" | sed 's/^    //' > "$dir/good.md"
    got=$(just _where-the-work-is-check "$dir/good.md" 2>&1) \
        || fail "a well-formed §0 was rejected"

    sed 's/§3\.1/§9.9/' "$dir/good.md" > "$dir/dangling.md"
    got=$(just _where-the-work-is-check "$dir/dangling.md" 2>&1) \
        && fail "§0 citing a section that does not exist was accepted"
    grep -qF "cites sections that do not exist: 9.9" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a dangling citation was not named"

    sed 's/^reviewed: .*/reviewed: whenever/' "$dir/good.md" > "$dir/undated.md"
    got=$(just _where-the-work-is-check "$dir/undated.md" 2>&1) \
        && fail "§0 with an unparsable reviewed date was accepted"

    sed '/^## 1\./i\
    filler' "$dir/good.md" > "$dir/long.md"
    for _ in $(seq 6); do
        sed '/^filler$/a\
    filler\
    filler\
    filler\
    filler\
    filler\
    filler\
    filler\
    filler\
    filler' "$dir/long.md" > "$dir/long.next.md"
        mv "$dir/long.next.md" "$dir/long.md"
    done
    got=$(just _where-the-work-is-check "$dir/long.md" 2>&1) \
        && fail "a §0 grown past the cap was accepted"
    grep -qF "the cap is 50" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "an over-long §0 did not say what the cap is"

    fifty=$(for _ in $(seq 50); do printf 'filler\n'; done)
    awk -v f="$fifty" '/^## 1\./{print f} {print}' "$dir/good.md" > "$dir/three-over.md"
    got=$(just _where-the-work-is-check "$dir/three-over.md" 2>&1) \
        && fail "a §0 three lines over the cap was accepted"
    grep -qF "cut 3 non-blank lines" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "an over-long §0 did not say how many lines to cut"

    printf '## 1. No zero here\n' > "$dir/none.md"
    got=$(just _where-the-work-is-check "$dir/none.md" 2>&1) \
        && fail "a file with no §0 at all was accepted"

    # Every assertion above is `command && fail`, so the last one leaves its own
    # non-zero status behind when it passes. Without this the whole selftest
    # failed silently on a green run, printing nothing at all.
    exit 0

# Who is working where, right now. Run this before you touch a shared tree.
#
# **A worktree's lock is this repo's record of who owns it**, and it is the only
# record that survives the session that made it: it is a file in
# `.git/worktrees/<name>/locked`, not session state, so a `/clear` cannot lose it
# and a session that never dispatched the lane can still read it. `git worktree
# lock --reason` writes it and `git worktree unlock` clears it. Locking blocks
# only `worktree remove` and `worktree prune`, so it never gets in a lane's way.
#
# The alternative that was being used was `ps aux | grep claude`, and it cannot
# work: a subagent runs inside its parent's process, so two agents colliding in
# one worktree are one pid with one working directory, while every unrelated
# session on the machine — other repositories included — shows up as a hit.
#
# **An empty answer is a failure here, not a pass.** A repository always has at
# least its own worktree, so a listing that parses to nothing means the parse
# broke, and this says so instead of printing a reassuring silence.
#
# **The first tree is the hub, and it is not a lane.** Git refuses to lock the
# main working tree, so no reason string can ever appear beside it and printing
# `free` there would be a claim this command cannot make. It prints `hub`, leaves
# it out of the count, and says so when it is not parked and clean —
# `just check-hub` is the one that fails over it (docs/decisions.md §60).
[doc("Who is working where, right now. Run this before you touch a shared tree.")]
lanes dir=".":
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    repo=$(git -C "{{dir}}" rev-parse --show-toplevel)
    listing=$(git -C "$repo" worktree list --porcelain)
    if [ -z "$listing" ]; then
        echo "git worktree list --porcelain printed nothing, so this counted no lanes"
        exit 1
    fi
    total=0
    held=0
    seen=0
    hub_warning=""
    tree=""
    branch=""
    locked=""
    emit() {
        [ -n "$tree" ] || return 0
        seen=$((seen + 1))
        if [ ! -d "$tree" ]; then
            work="missing"
        elif [ -n "$(git -C "$tree" status --porcelain 2>/dev/null)" ]; then
            work="dirty"
        else
            work="clean"
        fi
        if [ "$seen" -eq 1 ]; then
            printf '%-4s %-7s %s [%s]\n' "hub" "$work" "$tree" "$branch"
            if [ "$branch" != "(detached)" ] || [ "$work" != "clean" ]; then
                hub_warning="somebody is working in the hub, which is the one tree that cannot record an owner; \`just check-hub\` says what to do"
            fi
            return 0
        fi
        total=$((total + 1))
        state="free"
        if [ -n "$locked" ]; then
            state="HELD"
            held=$((held + 1))
        fi
        printf '%-4s %-7s %s [%s]\n' "$state" "$work" "$tree" "$branch"
        if [ -n "$locked" ]; then
            printf '         held by: %s\n' "$(why_it_is_held)"
        fi
    }
    # Not from the porcelain listing, which C-quotes any reason holding a
    # non-ASCII byte — a `·` comes back as `"\302\267"`. The lock file holds the
    # reason as it was written, and a reason naming an agent is the whole point.
    why_it_is_held() {
        [ -d "$tree" ] || { echo "(locked; its directory is gone, so the reason cannot be read)"; return 0; }
        note="$(git -C "$tree" rev-parse --git-dir)/locked"
        if [ -s "$note" ]; then
            tr '\n' ' ' <"$note" | sed 's/ *$//'
        else
            echo "(locked, no reason given)"
        fi
    }
    while IFS= read -r line || [ -n "$line" ]; do
        case "$line" in
            "worktree "*)
                emit
                tree=${line#worktree }
                branch="(detached)"
                locked=""
                ;;
            "branch "*) branch=${line#branch refs/heads/} ;;
            "locked"*) locked="yes" ;;
        esac
    done <<<"$listing"
    emit
    if [ "$seen" -eq 0 ]; then
        echo "no worktree was parsed out of a non-empty listing, so the parse is broken"
        exit 1
    fi
    echo "$held of $total lane(s) held; the hub is not one of them, because git cannot lock it"
    [ -z "$hub_warning" ] || echo "$hub_warning"

# Pins `lanes` against a repository it builds itself, so it stays proven on a
# tree where nothing real is locked.
#
# The cases that matter are the ones this builds: a **held** worktree whose
# reason must come back verbatim, a **free** one that must not be reported as
# held, the **hub**, which must be labelled as such and left out of the count,
# and the **count** line, which is what stops a broken parse from looking like a
# quiet tree. Reading the live repository instead would prove nothing — it is
# unlocked almost all of the time, so a `lanes` that had lost the ability to see
# a lock at all would pass every run.
#
# The fixture's hub is deliberately left on a branch, which is the state
# `check-hub` rejects, so the warning line is exercised here too.
[private]
check-lanes-selftest:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    dir=$(mktemp -d)
    trap 'git -C "$dir/main" worktree unlock "$dir/held" 2>/dev/null || true; rm -rf "$dir"' EXIT
    git init -q -b main "$dir/main"
    git -C "$dir/main" -c user.email=t@t -c user.name=t commit -q --allow-empty -m "root"
    git -C "$dir/main" worktree add -q "$dir/held" -b held
    git -C "$dir/main" worktree add -q "$dir/free" -b free
    git -C "$dir/main" worktree lock --reason "agent-abc · deliverable · 2026-09-06T00:00:00Z" "$dir/held"
    got=$(just lanes "$dir/main")
    fail() {
        echo "lanes drifted from its own fixtures: $1"
        echo "--- got ---"; echo "$got"
        exit 1
    }
    grep -q "^HELD .* $dir/held \[held\]$" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the locked worktree is not reported as HELD"
    grep -qF "held by: agent-abc · deliverable · 2026-09-06T00:00:00Z" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the lock reason did not come back verbatim"
    grep -q "^free .* $dir/free \[free\]$" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the unlocked worktree is not reported as free"
    grep -q "^hub .* $dir/main \[main\]$" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the main working tree is not reported as the hub"
    grep -qx "1 of 2 lane(s) held; the hub is not one of them, because git cannot lock it" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the count line is wrong, so an empty parse could pass as a quiet tree"
    grep -qF "somebody is working in the hub" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a hub sitting on a branch drew no warning"

# Hand back every lane matching a pattern, in one call.
#
# `git worktree unlock` takes one tree, so releasing a batch of lanes was one
# call per lane: fifteen of them in a row on 2026-09-12, at about 137k of
# context each, which is two million tokens to clear fifteen locks. The locks
# are what `lanes` reads as ownership (§55), so a batch of lanes finishing
# together is the ordinary case and not an unusual one.
#
# **The pattern is a glob, and it is matched against the branch name and against
# the worktree's own directory name.** Both, because a lane is addressed by
# either depending on who is asking — `release-lanes 'lane-fuzz-*'` after a
# batch of briefs, `release-lanes 'agent-*'` after a batch of sessions. It is
# required and has no default: a recipe that released everything when told
# nothing is a recipe that unlocks a tree somebody is still working in.
#
# **The hub is never touched**, and it is left out by position rather than by
# name. Git documents the main working tree as the first entry of
# `--porcelain`, which is the same parse `lanes` and `check-hub` use, and it
# answers *"fatal: The main working tree cannot be locked or unlocked"* — so a
# pattern matching the hub has to be reported rather than attempted.
[doc("Unlock every lane matching a pattern: `just release-lanes 'lane-fuzz-*'`.")]
release-lanes pattern dir=".":
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    repo=$(git -C "{{dir}}" rev-parse --show-toplevel)
    listing=$(git -C "$repo" worktree list --porcelain)
    if [ -z "$listing" ]; then
        echo "git worktree list --porcelain printed nothing, so this released nothing"
        exit 1
    fi
    pattern='{{ pattern }}'
    released=0
    left=0
    seen=0
    tree=""
    branch=""
    locked=""
    consider() {
        [ -n "$tree" ] || return 0
        seen=$((seen + 1))
        if [ "$seen" -eq 1 ]; then
            printf 'hub      left alone, git cannot lock it and nobody works there: %s\n' "$tree"
            return 0
        fi
        if ! matches "$branch" && ! matches "$(basename "$tree")"; then
            left=$((left + 1))
            printf 'skipped  no match for %s: %s [%s]\n' "'$pattern'" "$tree" "$branch"
            return 0
        fi
        if [ -z "$locked" ]; then
            left=$((left + 1))
            printf 'skipped  already free: %s [%s]\n' "$tree" "$branch"
            return 0
        fi
        was="$(why_it_is_held)"
        git -C "$repo" worktree unlock "$tree"
        released=$((released + 1))
        printf 'RELEASED %s [%s]\n' "$tree" "$branch"
        printf '         was held by: %s\n' "$was"
    }
    matches() {
        case "$1" in
            $pattern) return 0 ;;
            *) return 1 ;;
        esac
    }
    # The same reason `lanes` prints, read from the lock file rather than from
    # the porcelain listing, which C-quotes any non-ASCII byte in it. Read before
    # the unlock, because the unlock is what deletes it.
    why_it_is_held() {
        [ -d "$tree" ] || { echo "(locked; its directory is gone, so the reason cannot be read)"; return 0; }
        note="$(git -C "$tree" rev-parse --git-dir)/locked"
        if [ -s "$note" ]; then
            tr '\n' ' ' <"$note" | sed 's/ *$//'
        else
            echo "(locked, no reason given)"
        fi
    }
    while IFS= read -r line || [ -n "$line" ]; do
        case "$line" in
            "worktree "*)
                consider
                tree=${line#worktree }
                branch="(detached)"
                locked=""
                ;;
            "branch "*) branch=${line#branch refs/heads/} ;;
            "locked"*) locked="yes" ;;
        esac
    done <<<"$listing"
    consider
    if [ "$seen" -eq 0 ]; then
        echo "no worktree was parsed out of a non-empty listing, so the parse is broken"
        exit 1
    fi
    echo "$released lane(s) released, $left left as they were; the hub is never either"

# Pins `release-lanes` against a repository it builds itself: what matches, what
# does not, and the hub.
#
# The case that costs the most to get wrong is the third one. A pattern that
# names the hub cannot be answered by unlocking it — git refuses, and `set -e`
# would take the whole batch down with the fatal — so the hub has to be
# recognised by position and reported. That arm passes only if the recipe never
# reached for `git worktree unlock` on it.
[private]
check-release-lanes-selftest:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    dir=$(mktemp -d)
    trap 'for t in alpha beta unrelated agent-xyz; do git -C "$dir/main" worktree unlock "$dir/$t" 2>/dev/null || true; done; rm -rf "$dir"' EXIT
    git init -q -b main "$dir/main"
    git -C "$dir/main" -c user.email=t@t -c user.name=t commit -q --allow-empty -m root
    git -C "$dir/main" worktree add -q "$dir/alpha" -b lane-alpha
    git -C "$dir/main" worktree add -q "$dir/beta" -b lane-beta
    git -C "$dir/main" worktree add -q "$dir/quiet" -b lane-quiet
    git -C "$dir/main" worktree add -q "$dir/unrelated" -b keep-me
    git -C "$dir/main" worktree add -q "$dir/agent-xyz" -b nothing-like-a-lane
    for t in alpha beta unrelated agent-xyz; do
        git -C "$dir/main" worktree lock --reason "agent-$t · deliverable · 2026-09-12T00:00:00Z" "$dir/$t"
    done

    fail() {
        echo "release-lanes drifted from its own fixtures: $1"
        echo "--- got ---"; echo "$got"
        exit 1
    }
    still_locked() {
        git -C "$dir/main" worktree list --porcelain \
            | awk -v want="worktree $1" '/^worktree /{found = ($0 == want)} \
                                          found && /^locked/ {print "yes"; exit}'
    }

    # One: the pattern matches two branches out of five.
    got=$(just release-lanes 'lane-*' "$dir/main")
    [ -z "$(still_locked "$dir/alpha")" ] || fail "a lane matching the pattern was left locked"
    [ -z "$(still_locked "$dir/beta")" ] || fail "only the first matching lane was released"
    [ -n "$(still_locked "$dir/unrelated")" ] \
        || fail "a tree that does not match the pattern was unlocked anyway"
    [ -n "$(still_locked "$dir/agent-xyz")" ] \
        || fail "a tree that does not match the pattern was unlocked anyway"
    grep -qF "RELEASED $dir/alpha [lane-alpha]" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a released lane was not reported"
    grep -qF "was held by: agent-alpha · deliverable · 2026-09-12T00:00:00Z" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the lock reason was thrown away rather than reported before the unlock"
    grep -qF "skipped  no match for 'lane-*': $dir/unrelated [keep-me]" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a skipped tree was not said to be skipped, so silence reads as success"
    grep -qF "skipped  already free: $dir/quiet [lane-quiet]" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a matching tree that was not locked was not reported as already free"
    grep -qx "2 lane(s) released, 3 left as they were; the hub is never either" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the count line is wrong, so a parse that saw nothing could pass as a quiet batch"

    # Two: the directory name is an address too, because a batch of sessions is
    # named that way and a batch of briefs is named the other way.
    got=$(just release-lanes 'agent-*' "$dir/main")
    [ -z "$(still_locked "$dir/agent-xyz")" ] \
        || fail "a pattern matching the worktree's directory released nothing"
    [ -n "$(still_locked "$dir/unrelated")" ] \
        || fail "matching on the directory name released a tree that matched neither"

    # Three: a pattern naming the hub leaves it alone and says so, rather than
    # dying on git's refusal to unlock the main working tree.
    got=$(just release-lanes 'main' "$dir/main") \
        || fail "a pattern matching the hub took the whole batch down with it"
    grep -qF "hub      left alone, git cannot lock it and nobody works there: $dir/main" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the hub was not reported as left alone"
    grep -qx "0 lane(s) released, 5 left as they were; the hub is never either" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the hub was counted as a lane"

# Catch the worktree that holds `main` up to `origin/main`, fast-forward only.
#
# **It finds that tree by the branch it holds, never by its name.** The tree was
# called `wave-1` until 2026-09-23 — a batch name from a milestone that ended
# long before, describing nothing anyone still did there — and every session
# reached it by typing that path out of memory or out of a handoff. A name
# nobody can derive is a name every document has to carry, and six of them did.
# Renaming it `main` fixes the reading; deriving it here fixes the typing, and
# the second is what stops the next rename from costing anything
# (docs/decisions.md §210).
#
# **It refuses rather than merges.** `--ff-only` is the whole point: this is for
# a tree that is *behind*, and the three other shapes each mean something
# different and want a different answer. Already there is a pass and says the
# sha. Ahead means the work is local and wants a push, not a merge. Diverged
# means somebody rewrote `main`, and picking a resolution here would pick it
# unseen. A dirty tree is left untouched whatever the shas say, because that
# tree is conventionally unlocked and so the work in it names no owner — ask the
# peers, do not merge over it.
#
# **It fetches first**, which `just` itself must never do (§146: a round trip on
# every recipe is the hub re-park's cost in another currency). This one is typed
# on purpose, once, and "aligned with `origin/main`" is a claim about the remote
# and not about whatever a stale remote-tracking ref last said.
#
# **It runs `queue-page` against `$held` after a fast-forward or a no-op**
# (Q254), the same as the `queue` recipe: a render that fails says so on
# stderr and fails nothing, since a bad page is not a reason to fail a
# fast-forward that already landed. The three refusals below run it never,
# since none of them changed what `main` holds.
[doc("Fast-forward the worktree that holds main up to origin/main.")]
ff-main dir=".":
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    repo=$(git -C "{{dir}}" rev-parse --show-toplevel)
    listing=$(git -C "$repo" worktree list --porcelain)
    if [ -z "$listing" ]; then
        echo "git worktree list --porcelain printed nothing, so this found no tree holding main"
        exit 1
    fi
    tree=""
    held=""
    while IFS= read -r line || [ -n "$line" ]; do
        case "$line" in
            "worktree "*) tree=${line#worktree } ;;
            "branch refs/heads/main") held=$tree ;;
        esac
    done <<<"$listing"
    if [ -z "$held" ]; then
        echo "no worktree has main checked out, so there is nothing to fast-forward"
        echo "put it back with: git worktree add <path> main   (docs/decisions.md §60)"
        exit 1
    fi
    if [ -n "$(git -C "$held" status --porcelain)" ]; then
        echo "$held holds main and has uncommitted work in it, so this touched nothing"
        echo "that tree is left unlocked by convention, so the work in it names no owner: ask the peers"
        exit 1
    fi
    git -C "$held" fetch --quiet origin main
    before=$(git -C "$held" rev-parse HEAD)
    onto=$(git -C "$held" rev-parse FETCH_HEAD)
    short() { git -C "$held" rev-parse --short "$1"; }
    if [ "$before" = "$onto" ]; then
        echo "$held is already at origin/main, $(short "$before")"
        {{ just_executable() }} --justfile '{{ justfile() }}' --working-directory "$held" queue-page || true
        exit 0
    fi
    if git -C "$held" merge-base --is-ancestor "$onto" "$before"; then
        ahead=$(git -C "$held" rev-list --count "$onto..$before")
        echo "$held: main is $ahead commit(s) ahead of origin/main and behind it in none,"
        echo "so a fast-forward would throw them away. This shape wants a push:"
        echo "    git -C $held push origin main"
        exit 1
    fi
    if ! git -C "$held" merge-base --is-ancestor "$before" "$onto"; then
        echo "$held: main and origin/main have both moved since they last agreed,"
        echo "so no fast-forward exists. Somebody rewrote one of them; read both before resolving:"
        echo "    git -C $held log --oneline --left-right main...FETCH_HEAD"
        exit 1
    fi
    gained=$(git -C "$held" rev-list --count "$before..$onto")
    git -C "$held" merge --ff-only --quiet "$onto"
    echo "$held: main fast-forwarded $gained commit(s), $(short "$before") -> $(short "$onto")"
    {{ just_executable() }} --justfile '{{ justfile() }}' --working-directory "$held" queue-page || true

# Pins `ff-main` against a repository it builds itself, with a real `origin` on
# disk, because the live one is in one of these five states at a time.
#
# Four of the five are refusals, and they are the cases worth building: a
# `--ff-only` that has lost its `--ff-only` still passes the one case where the
# tree is simply behind. The dirty case asserts the *bytes* survive rather than
# just the exit code, because the wrong answer there merges over work that is on
# no branch and names no owner.
[private]
check-ff-main-selftest:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    dir=$(mktemp -d)
    trap 'rm -rf "$dir"' EXIT
    commit() { git -C "$1" -c user.email=t@t -c user.name=t commit -q "${@:2}"; }
    grow() {
        echo "$2" >"$1/f"
        git -C "$1" add f
        commit "$1" -m "$2"
    }
    git init -q -b main "$dir/origin"
    commit "$dir/origin" --allow-empty -m "root"
    git clone -q "$dir/origin" "$dir/hub"
    git -C "$dir/hub" checkout -q --detach
    git -C "$dir/hub" worktree add -q "$dir/holder" main

    got=""
    rc=0
    run() { got=$(just ff-main "$dir/hub" 2>&1) && rc=0 || rc=$?; }
    fail() {
        echo "ff-main drifted from its own fixtures: $1"
        echo "--- got ---"; echo "$got"
        exit 1
    }
    at() { git -C "$dir/holder" rev-parse HEAD; }

    # One: nothing to do. A pass, and it names the sha rather than going quiet.
    run
    [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] || fail "a tree already at origin/main was reported as a failure"
    grep -qF "already at origin/main" <<<"$got" || fail "being already there was not said"

    # Two: behind, which is the whole point and the only case that moves.
    grow "$dir/origin" one
    grow "$dir/origin" two
    ahead_of_us=$(git -C "$dir/origin" rev-parse HEAD)
    run
    [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] || fail "a tree that was simply behind was not fast-forwarded"
    [ "$(at)" = "$ahead_of_us" ] || fail "the fast-forward did not land on origin/main"
    grep -qF "fast-forwarded 2 commit(s)" <<<"$got" || fail "the count of what it gained is wrong"
    grep -qF "$dir/holder" <<<"$got" || fail "the tree it acted on was not named"

    # Three: uncommitted work, which outranks every sha comparison. The bytes
    # are on no branch, so a wrong answer here is the only unrecoverable one.
    grow "$dir/origin" three
    printf 'work nobody has committed\n' >"$dir/holder/scratch"
    behind_at=$(at)
    run
    [ "$rc" -ne 0 ] || fail "a dirty tree holding main was merged into"
    grep -qF "names no owner" <<<"$got" || fail "the dirty refusal did not say why it is left alone"
    [ "$(cat "$dir/holder/scratch")" = "work nobody has committed" ] \
        || fail "content that is on no branch was destroyed"
    [ "$(at)" = "$behind_at" ] || fail "the tree moved under uncommitted work"
    rm "$dir/holder/scratch"

    # Four: ahead. A fast-forward here is a rewind, so it must name the push.
    # The catch-up runs through the recipe, because the refusal above declines
    # before it fetches and the tree's own FETCH_HEAD is a commit out of date.
    run
    [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] || fail "the tree did not catch up once the uncommitted work was gone"
    grow "$dir/holder" local-work
    ours=$(at)
    run
    [ "$rc" -ne 0 ] || fail "a main ahead of origin was reported as fast-forwarded"
    [ "$(at)" = "$ours" ] || fail "commits that were only local were thrown away"
    grep -qF "push origin main" <<<"$got" || fail "the ahead refusal did not name the push"

    # Five: both moved. No fast-forward exists and picking one would pick it unseen.
    grow "$dir/origin" theirs
    run
    [ "$rc" -ne 0 ] || fail "a diverged main was merged anyway"
    [ "$(at)" = "$ours" ] || fail "a diverged main moved"
    grep -qF "both moved since they last agreed" <<<"$got" || fail "divergence was not named as such"

    # Six: nobody holds main at all, which is §76's shape and reads as a quiet
    # success to anything that only greps the listing for a path.
    git -C "$dir/hub" worktree remove --force "$dir/holder"
    run
    [ "$rc" -ne 0 ] || fail "a repository with main checked out nowhere passed"
    grep -qF "git worktree add" <<<"$got" || fail "the empty case did not name the way back"

# One clean merge of a verified lane onto main, or a named stop (§383.4 item 2,
# §384). The `lander` agent runs it, and `lanes.js` hands any stop to the Opus
# merger with the stop line in its brief (§383.5).
#
# It runs `merger.md`'s steps 1, 3, 4 and 5 as they stand, from a tree of its
# own: fetch, branch from `origin/main`, unlock, check the sha, merge, close the
# row and add the lane's rows in one `queue apply`, `check-queue`, commit, gate,
# the push loop with §367's free catch-ups over docs and the same bounds as
# `lanes.js`, then `ff-main`, unlock and `mark-finished`. It stops and names the
# stop when the sha differs from the lane's report, a merge conflicts, §0 names
# the row, `queue apply` or `check-queue` refuses, the gate fails, or the
# attempts run out. Its last line is one line of JSON holding `mergedSchema`'s
# fields and `stop`.
#
# **Landers share one gate** (§397.5). The first call starts one run in a
# session of its own and follows its log for at most `LAND_WAIT` seconds, 540
# by default, and while its last line says the run is still going, `just land
# wait` follows it again, as `verify-lane` does (§385.2). The run writes its
# lane's entry to a spool under `rcad-land/` in the git common directory and
# waits on the `flock` beside it, which goes when the process does. Holding it,
# a run whose lane an earlier holder landed reports that merge. Any other run
# lands its own lane, then every lane waiting whose run is alive, oldest first,
# each with its own merge commit and queue apply, gates them once, and leaves a
# result file for each other lane it pushed. A lane that stops is put back out
# of the batch for its own run, and a red gate with other lanes in it goes back
# to `origin/main` and lands its own lane alone.
#
# **A run outlives its lander**, so the Opus merger that `lanes.js` hands a stop
# to runs `just land wait-for <lane branch>` from its own tree before it reads
# `origin/main`. That waits, for the same 540 s and with the same exit 3, while
# a live run's entry names the lane or anyone holds the lock, and exits 0 once
# neither is so.
#
# `--steps` names a file holding the lane's `add`, `after` and `tag` steps, one
# word to a line and a `+` line between steps, so that no row's text ever passes
# through a shell (§384.2). The logic is `tooling/land/land.py`; this hands it
# the arguments, and `LAND_JUST` is the one seam `check-land-selftest` uses to
# put a stub where `just` would run the gate, `ff-main` and `mark-finished`. A
# stop exits 1, a run still going 3 and a refusal 2, and `[no-exit-message]`
# keeps `just`'s own error line from landing after the last line.
[doc("Merge one verified lane onto main as merger.md's steps 1, 3, 4 and 5 do, sharing one gate with the lanes waiting, or stop and say which stop: `just land <args>`, then `just land wait` while it says it is still running. Ends with one line of JSON.")]
[no-exit-message]
land *args:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    LAND_JUST="${LAND_JUST:-{{ just_executable() }}}" exec python3 '{{ justfile_directory() }}/tooling/land/land.py' "$@"

# Pins `land` against repositories it builds itself, each with a bare `origin`
# on disk, one per arm: a clean merge lands and prints its JSON, and each stop
# of §383.4 item 2 stops by name and pushes nothing.
#
# Four arms pin §397.5, with a second lane and a lander for it: two landers
# waiting on the lock share one gate, a red batch falls back to the holder's own
# lane, an entry whose run was killed is never taken, and a holder killed in its
# gate drops the lock. The test holds the lock from a `flock` process of its own,
# never on one of its fds, which every `just land` it starts would inherit.
#
# A killed-follower arm pins §429.5: it stops the agent's own `just land`,
# started in a session of its own, the way Claude Code kills a command
# (§429.2) — SIGTERM to its group, then every descendant `ps` finds, then
# SIGKILL to the group — and shows the detached run it started still lands
# the lane, read back with `just land wait` from the same tree.
#
# The dead-entry arm also pins `wait-for`'s two legs. A run stopped with
# `SIGSTOP` while it waits on the lock keeps its entry alive and never takes the
# lock when it is freed, so `wait-for` with the lock free must still wait on the
# entry; then, with the run killed and the lock held, it must wait on the lock.
# The briefs in `lanes.js` and `lander.md` are held to the `wait` and `wait-for`
# lines, `wait-for` ahead of the merger's ancestor check.
# Which waiting run gets the lock first is not fixed, so those arms hold either
# order to the same outcome.
#
# Three arms pin §450.3 rule 4's `--answers`. A lane named as answering one
# open row, one that waits, one a lane holds, one parked, one closed and one
# that is no row closes the two open ones done at its tip in its own `queue
# apply` and names the other four in `needs_judgement`; a row named twice is a
# usage stop; and a spool entry with no `answers`, as a `land.py` from before
# it wrote them, still lands beside one that has them.
#
# **A stub stands where `just` would run a recipe**, on `LAND_JUST`, the way the
# fuzz selftests put a stub `cargo` on `PATH`. The real `gate` would gate this
# repository, `ff-main` and `mark-finished` act on real trees, and `queue apply`
# reads the real queue's claims. The stub logs every call, writes the `queue
# apply` line into the fixture's `docs/queue.md` so that the merge commit
# carries it, and fails or races on a flag file. A race is another merge landing
# on `origin` while the gate runs: code three times over spends the push
# attempts, docs six times over spends the catch-ups, and docs once costs no
# attempt at all (§367).
#
# The JSON is held to the fields `mergedSchema` in `lanes.js` requires, read out
# of that file, so a field the schema gains and the script lacks fails here and
# not in the lander's report.
[private]
check-land-selftest:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    dir=$(realpath "$(mktemp -d)")
    trap 'rm -rf "$dir"' EXIT
    mkdir "$dir/bin"
    cat >"$dir/bin/just" <<'STUB'
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -uo pipefail
    printf 'just %s\n' "$*" >>"$STUB_DIR/log"
    race() {
        local n
        n=$(( $(cat "$STUB_DIR/raced" 2>/dev/null || echo 0) + 1 ))
        echo "$n" >"$STUB_DIR/raced"
        git -C "$STUB_DIR/other" pull -q --ff-only origin main
        if [ "$1" = code ]; then
            echo "fn raced$n() {}" >"$STUB_DIR/other/raced$n.rs"
        else
            echo "raced $n" >"$STUB_DIR/other/docs/raced$n.md"
        fi
        git -C "$STUB_DIR/other" add -A
        git -C "$STUB_DIR/other" commit -q -m "another merge, $n"
        git -C "$STUB_DIR/other" push -q origin HEAD:main
    }
    case "${1:-}" in
        queue)
            [ "${2:-}" = apply ] || { echo "the stub has no queue ${2:-}" >&2; exit 2; }
            shift 2
            if [ -e "$STUB_DIR/refuse-apply" ]; then
                echo "queue apply: step 1 (done Q1) is refused: Q1 is closed already" >&2
                exit 1
            fi
            printf 'apply %s\n' "$*" >>docs/queue.md
            n=900
            verb=1
            for word in "$@"; do
                if [ "$verb" = 1 ] && [ "$word" = add ]; then
                    echo "Q$n"
                    n=$((n + 1))
                elif [ "$verb" = 1 ]; then
                    echo "$word: applied"
                fi
                verb=0
                [ "$word" != + ] || verb=1
            done ;;
        check-queue)
            if [ -e "$STUB_DIR/check-queue-fails" ]; then
                echo "check-queue: Q900 has no write set"
                exit 1
            fi
            echo "docs/queue.md: fine" ;;
        gate)
            n=$(( $(cat "$STUB_DIR/gates" 2>/dev/null || echo 0) + 1 ))
            echo "$n" >"$STUB_DIR/gates"
            if [ -e "$STUB_DIR/gate-blocks" ] && [ "$n" = 1 ]; then
                touch "$STUB_DIR/gate-started"
                for _ in $(seq 1200); do
                    [ ! -e "$STUB_DIR/gate-release" ] || break
                    sleep 0.05
                done
            fi
            case "$(cat "$STUB_DIR/race" 2>/dev/null)" in
                code) race code ;;
                docs) race docs ;;
                docs-once) [ "$n" != 1 ] || race docs ;;
            esac
            echo "=== gate summary ==="
            if [ -e "$STUB_DIR/gate-fails" ] || { [ -e "$STUB_DIR/gate-fails-with-lane2" ] && [ -e lane2.rs ]; }; then
                echo "gate: FAIL  first failure: test (exit 100)"
                exit 1
            fi
            echo "gate: PASS" ;;
        ff-main) echo "main fast-forwarded 1 commit(s)" ;;
        mark-finished) shift; for tree in "$@"; do echo "marked: $tree"; done ;;
        *) echo "the stub has no recipe ${1:-}" >&2; exit 2 ;;
    esac
    STUB
    chmod +x "$dir/bin/just"
    required=$(grep -F "required: ['start_sha', 'worktree_path', 'merge_branch'" .claude/workflows/lanes.js \
        | grep -oE "'[a-z_]+'" | tr -d "'" | tr '\n' ' ' || true)
    [ -n "$required" ] || { echo "found no mergedSchema required list in .claude/workflows/lanes.js, so the JSON would be held to nothing"; exit 1; }
    grep -qxF '  mise exec -- just land wait' .claude/workflows/lanes.js \
        || { echo "the lander's brief in .claude/workflows/lanes.js no longer follows just land with just land wait"; exit 1; }
    grep -qF 'mise exec -- just land wait' .claude/agents/lander.md \
        || { echo ".claude/agents/lander.md no longer follows just land with just land wait"; exit 1; }
    waits_at=$(grep -m1 -nxF '  mise exec -- just land wait-for ${branchOf(lane)}' .claude/workflows/lanes.js | cut -d: -f1 || true)
    checks_at=$(grep -m1 -nxF '  git merge-base --is-ancestor ${branchOf(lane)} origin/main' .claude/workflows/lanes.js | cut -d: -f1 || true)
    [ -n "$waits_at" ] && [ -n "$checks_at" ] && [ "$waits_at" -lt "$checks_at" ] \
        || { echo "the merger's brief in .claude/workflows/lanes.js does not run just land wait-for ahead of its ancestor check (lines '$waits_at' and '$checks_at'), so it can read origin/main while the lander's run still lands the lane (§397.5)"; exit 1; }
    steps_line='add 2.2 sonnet justfile from/Q1 small a row the lane left, ending in §384 + tag @1 small + add 3 sonnet - small was/Q7 a row the lane filed again, ending in §384'
    arm=""
    at=""
    got=""
    json=""
    rc=0
    fail() {
        echo "land drifted from its own fixtures, in the arm '$arm': $1"
        echo "--- got ---"; echo "$got"
        exit 1
    }
    fixture() {
        arm=$1
        at="$dir/$1"
        mkdir -p "$at/stub"
        touch "$at/stub/log"
        git init -q --bare -b main "$at/origin.git"
        git init -q -b main "$at/hub"
        git -C "$at/hub" config user.email t@t
        git -C "$at/hub" config user.name t
        git -C "$at/hub" config commit.gpgsign false
        mkdir -p "$at/hub/docs/decisions.d"
        printf '/target\n' >"$at/hub/.gitignore"
        printf '# The queue\n' >"$at/hub/docs/queue.md"
        printf '%s\n' '## 0. Where the work is' '' 'Q10 and Q12 closed what Q11 left.' >"$at/hub/docs/decisions.d/000-where-the-work-is.md"
        printf 'fn main() {}\n' >"$at/hub/main.rs"
        git -C "$at/hub" add -A
        git -C "$at/hub" commit -q -m root
        git -C "$at/hub" remote add origin "$at/origin.git"
        git -C "$at/hub" push -q origin main
        git -C "$at/hub" checkout -q --detach
        git -C "$at/hub" worktree add -q -b lane-x "$at/lane" origin/main
        printf 'fn lane() {}\n' >"$at/lane/lane.rs"
        git -C "$at/lane" add lane.rs
        git -C "$at/lane" commit -q -m "the lane's work"
        git -C "$at/hub" worktree add -q --detach "$at/verify" origin/main
        git -C "$at/hub" worktree add -q --detach "$at/lander" origin/main
        for tree in lane verify lander; do
            git -C "$at/hub" worktree lock --reason "an agent at work" "$at/$tree"
        done
        git clone -q "$at/origin.git" "$at/stub/other"
        git -C "$at/stub/other" config user.email t@t
        git -C "$at/stub/other" config user.name t
        git -C "$at/stub/other" config commit.gpgsign false
        mkdir -p "$at/lander/target"
        printf '%s\n' add 2.2 sonnet justfile 'small a row the lane left, ending in §384' + tag @1 small \
            + add 3 sonnet - 'small was/Q7 a row the lane filed again, ending in §384' \
            >"$at/lander/target/land-steps.txt"
        lane_sha=$(git -C "$at/lane" rev-parse HEAD)
    }
    second_lane() {
        git -C "$at/hub" worktree add -q -b lane-y "$at/lane2" origin/main
        printf 'fn lane2() {}\n' >"$at/lane2/lane2.rs"
        git -C "$at/lane2" add lane2.rs
        git -C "$at/lane2" commit -q -m "the second lane's work"
        git -C "$at/hub" worktree add -q --detach "$at/verify2" origin/main
        git -C "$at/hub" worktree add -q --detach "$at/lander2" origin/main
        for tree in lane2 verify2 lander2; do
            git -C "$at/hub" worktree lock --reason "an agent at work" "$at/$tree"
        done
        lane2_sha=$(git -C "$at/lane2" rev-parse HEAD)
    }
    hold_lock() {
        rm -f "$at/held" "$at/release"
        mkdir -p "$at/hub/.git/rcad-land"
        flock "$at/hub/.git/rcad-land/lock" -c "touch '$at/held'; for _ in \$(seq 1200); do [ ! -e '$at/release' ] || exit 0; sleep 0.05; done" &
        until_there "$at/held"
    }
    release_lock() {
        touch "$at/release"
        wait
    }
    until_there() {
        for _ in $(seq 400); do
            if [ $# -eq 1 ] && [ -e "$1" ]; then return 0; fi
            if [ $# -eq 2 ] && grep -qF "$2" "$1" 2>/dev/null; then return 0; fi
            sleep 0.05
        done
        fail "waited 20 s for $1 ${2:-}"
    }
    another_merge() {
        git -C "$at/stub/other" pull -q --ff-only origin main
        printf '%s\n' "$2" >>"$at/stub/other/$1"
        git -C "$at/stub/other" add -A
        git -C "$at/stub/other" commit -q -m "another merge"
        git -C "$at/stub/other" push -q origin HEAD:main
    }
    land_in() {
        local tree=$1 wait=$2
        shift 2
        got=$(STUB_DIR="$at/stub" LAND_JUST="$dir/bin/just" LAND_WAIT="$wait" \
            {{ just_executable() }} --justfile '{{ justfile() }}' --working-directory "$at/$tree" land "$@" 2>&1) && rc=0 || rc=$?
    }
    run() {
        land_in lander 60 "$@"
        ended
    }
    wait_for() {
        land_in "$1" 60 wait
        ended
    }
    start_in() {
        local tree=$1
        shift
        land_in "$tree" 0.2 "$@"
        [ "$rc" -eq 3 ] || fail "a run started in $tree ended with exit $rc while it waited, not 3 for one still going"
        [[ "$(printf '%s\n' "$got" | tail -n 1)" == "land: still running (pid "* ]] || fail "a run still going does not say so on its last line"
        until_there "$at/$tree/target/land/log" 'land: waiting for the land lock'
    }
    ended() {
        [ "$rc" -ne 3 ] || fail "the run was still going a minute later"
        json=$(printf '%s\n' "$got" | tail -n 1)
        local missing
        missing=$(python3 -c 'import json, sys; d = json.loads(sys.argv[1]); print(" ".join(k for k in (sys.argv[2].split() + ["stop"]) if k not in d))' "$json" "$required" 2>&1) \
            || fail "its last line is not JSON: $missing"
        [ -z "$missing" ] || fail "its JSON lacks $missing"
    }
    field() {
        python3 -c 'import json, sys; v = json.loads(sys.argv[1])[sys.argv[2]]; print(v if isinstance(v, str) else json.dumps(v, ensure_ascii=False))' "$json" "$1"
    }
    locked() {
        [ -e "$(git -C "$at/$1" rev-parse --absolute-git-dir)/locked" ]
    }
    gates() {
        cat "$at/stub/gates" 2>/dev/null || echo 0
    }
    on_origin() {
        git -C "$at/origin.git" merge-base --is-ancestor "$1" main 2>/dev/null
    }
    descendants_of() {
        local root=$1
        ps -A -o pid=,ppid= | awk -v root="$root" '
            { parent[$1] = $2 }
            END {
                for (pid in parent) {
                    cur = pid
                    for (i = 0; i < 1000 && (cur in parent); i++) {
                        if (parent[cur] == root) { print pid; break }
                        cur = parent[cur]
                    }
                }
            }'
    }
    stop_like_a_clear() {
        local group=$1 pid before
        before=$(descendants_of "$group")
        kill -TERM -- "-$group" 2>/dev/null || true
        for pid in $before; do
            kill -TERM "$pid" 2>/dev/null || true
        done
        sleep 0.2
        kill -KILL -- "-$group" 2>/dev/null || true
        for pid in $before; do
            kill -KILL "$pid" 2>/dev/null || true
        done
    }
    spool_empty() {
        local left
        left=$(find "$at/hub/.git/rcad-land/spool" "$at/hub/.git/rcad-land/results" -type f)
        [ -z "$left" ] || fail "the spool still holds $left"
    }
    stopped() {
        [ "$rc" -ne 0 ] || fail "it exited 0 on a stop"
        [ "$(field stop)" = "$1" ] || fail "it stopped at '$(field stop)', not at '$1'"
        [ "$(field merged)" = false ] || fail "a stop said it merged"
        [ -n "$(field not_merged_because)" ] || fail "a stop gave no reason"
        grep -qF "land: stopped at $1: " <<<"$got" || fail "no line names the stop"
        [ -z "$(git -C "$at/origin.git" log --merges --grep 'by just land' --format=%H main)" ] || fail "a stop pushed its merge"
        ! grep -qE '^just (ff-main|mark-finished)' "$at/stub/log" || fail "ff-main or mark-finished ran on a stop"
        ! locked lander || fail "a stop left its own tree locked, holding a slot (§263.4)"
    }

    fixture clean
    run --steps target/land-steps.txt Q1 lane-x "$lane_sha" merge-x "$at/lane" "$at/verify"
    [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] || fail "a clean merge exited $rc"
    [ "$(field merged)" = true ] || fail "a clean merge did not say it merged"
    [ "$(field stop)" = "" ] || fail "a clean merge named a stop"
    [ "$(field merged_sha)" = "$(git -C "$at/origin.git" rev-parse main)" ] || fail "merged_sha is not what origin/main holds"
    [ "$(git -C "$at/origin.git" rev-parse main^2)" = "$lane_sha" ] || fail "origin/main's merge commit does not have the lane's tip as its second parent"
    git -C "$at/origin.git" show main:docs/queue.md | grep -qxF "apply done Q1 ${lane_sha:0:8} + $steps_line" \
        || fail "the merge commit does not carry one queue apply: the done step, then the lane's own steps"
    [ "$(field rows_closed)" = "[\"Q1 done:${lane_sha:0:8}\"]" ] || fail "rows_closed is $(field rows_closed)"
    [ "$(field rows_added)" = '["Q900 tier 2.2 sonnet: from/Q1 small a row the lane left, ending in §384", "Q901 tier 3 sonnet: small was/Q7 a row the lane filed again, ending in §384"]' ] \
        || fail "rows_added is $(field rows_added), and a row the lane named no source for comes from Q1, the row it ran (§432)"
    [ "$(field push_attempts) $(field docs_catch_ups)" = "1 0" ] || fail "a first push that went through is not attempt 1 with no catch-ups"
    [ "$(field gate_pass_line)" = "gate: PASS" ] || fail "gate_pass_line is '$(field gate_pass_line)'"
    [ "$(field ff_main)" = "main fast-forwarded 1 commit(s)" ] || fail "ff_main is '$(field ff_main)'"
    [ "$(field worktree_path) $(field merge_branch)" = "$at/lander merge-x" ] || fail "it did not report its own tree and branch"
    for tree in lane verify lander; do
        ! locked "$tree" || fail "the $tree tree is still locked"
    done
    want=$(printf '%s\n' "just queue apply done Q1 ${lane_sha:0:8} + $steps_line" 'just check-queue' 'just gate quiet' \
        'just ff-main' "just mark-finished $at/lane $at/verify $at/lander")
    [ "$(cat "$at/stub/log")" = "$want" ] || fail "the recipes it ran were, in order: $(cat "$at/stub/log")"
    spool_empty
    ran=$json
    land_in lander 60 wait
    [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && [ "$(printf '%s\n' "$got" | tail -n 1)" = "$ran" ] || fail "a wait after the run ended did not print its JSON again"
    land_in lander 60 Q1 lane-x "$lane_sha" merge-x "$at/lane" "$at/verify"
    [ "$rc" -eq 2 ] || fail "a second just land in a tree that has started a run exited $rc, not 2"
    log="$at/lander/target/land/log"
    grep -qE '^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}T[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}Z land: waiting for the land lock$' "$log" \
        || fail "$log's 'waiting for the land lock' line carries no UTC timestamp (§411.4)"
    grep -qE '^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}T[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}Z land: holding the land lock$' "$log" \
        || fail "$log's 'holding the land lock' line carries no UTC timestamp (§411.4)"

    fixture sha-differs
    run Q1 lane-x "$(git -C "$at/origin.git" rev-parse main)" merge-x "$at/lane" "$at/verify"
    stopped sha-differs
    [ ! -s "$at/stub/log" ] || fail "it ran a recipe after the sha differed"

    fixture already-on-main
    git -C "$at/hub" push -q origin lane-x:main
    run Q1 lane-x "$lane_sha" merge-x "$at/lane" "$at/verify"
    stopped already-on-main

    fixture conflict
    another_merge lane.rs 'fn not_the_lane() {}'
    run Q1 lane-x "$lane_sha" merge-x "$at/lane" "$at/verify"
    stopped conflict
    grep -qF lane.rs <<<"$(field not_merged_because)" || fail "the conflict stop does not name the file"
    ! git -C "$at/lander" rev-parse -q --verify MERGE_HEAD >/dev/null || fail "the conflicting merge was left in progress"

    fixture section-0
    another_merge docs/decisions.d/000-where-the-work-is.md 'Q1 closes the last of it.'
    run Q1 lane-x "$lane_sha" merge-x "$at/lane" "$at/verify"
    stopped section-0

    fixture queue-apply
    touch "$at/stub/refuse-apply"
    run --steps target/land-steps.txt Q1 lane-x "$lane_sha" merge-x "$at/lane" "$at/verify"
    stopped queue-apply

    fixture check-queue
    touch "$at/stub/check-queue-fails"
    run --steps target/land-steps.txt Q1 lane-x "$lane_sha" merge-x "$at/lane" "$at/verify"
    stopped check-queue

    fixture gate
    touch "$at/stub/gate-fails"
    run Q1 lane-x "$lane_sha" merge-x "$at/lane" "$at/verify"
    stopped gate
    [ "$(field not_merged_because)" = "gate: FAIL  first failure: test (exit 100)" ] || fail "the gate stop does not carry the gate's FAIL line"

    fixture attempts-over-code
    echo code >"$at/stub/race"
    run Q1 lane-x "$lane_sha" merge-x "$at/lane" "$at/verify"
    stopped attempts
    [ "$(field push_attempts) $(field docs_catch_ups) $(gates)" = "3 0 3" ] \
        || fail "a race over code each time should spend 3 attempts and 3 gates, not $(field push_attempts) attempts, $(field docs_catch_ups) catch-ups and $(gates) gates"

    fixture attempts-over-docs
    echo docs >"$at/stub/race"
    run Q1 lane-x "$lane_sha" merge-x "$at/lane" "$at/verify"
    stopped attempts
    [ "$(field push_attempts) $(field docs_catch_ups) $(gates)" = "1 5 6" ] \
        || fail "a race over docs each time should spend 5 catch-ups and 6 gates, not $(field push_attempts) attempts, $(field docs_catch_ups) catch-ups and $(gates) gates"

    fixture docs-once
    echo docs-once >"$at/stub/race"
    run Q1 lane-x "$lane_sha" merge-x "$at/lane" "$at/verify"
    [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && [ "$(field merged)" = true ] || fail "a catch-up over docs alone did not end in a merge"
    [ "$(field push_attempts) $(field docs_catch_ups) $(gates)" = "1 1 2" ] \
        || fail "one catch-up over docs should cost no attempt, not $(field push_attempts) attempts and $(field docs_catch_ups) catch-ups over $(gates) gates (§367)"
    git -C "$at/origin.git" merge-base --is-ancestor "$lane_sha" main || fail "the lane did not reach origin/main after the catch-up"

    fixture steps
    printf '%s\n' done Q5 abcdef1 >"$at/lander/target/land-steps.txt"
    run --steps target/land-steps.txt Q1 lane-x "$lane_sha" merge-x "$at/lane" "$at/verify"
    stopped steps
    ! git -C "$at/hub" rev-parse -q --verify merge-x >/dev/null || fail "it branched before it read the steps"

    fixture shared
    second_lane
    hold_lock
    start_in lander --steps target/land-steps.txt Q1 lane-x "$lane_sha" merge-x "$at/lane" "$at/verify"
    start_in lander2 Q2 lane-y "$lane2_sha" merge-y "$at/lane2" "$at/verify2"
    release_lock
    wait_for lander
    [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && [ "$(field merged)" = true ] || fail "the first lane did not merge beside a second"
    [ "$(field worktree_path) $(field rows_closed)" = "$at/lander [\"Q1 done:${lane_sha:0:8}\"]" ] \
        || fail "the first lane's JSON names another tree or another lane's rows"
    first="$(field merged_sha) $(field merge_branch)"
    wait_for lander2
    [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && [ "$(field merged)" = true ] || fail "the second lane did not merge beside the first"
    [ "$(field worktree_path) $(field rows_closed) $(field rows_added)" = "$at/lander2 [\"Q2 done:${lane2_sha:0:8}\"] []" ] \
        || fail "the second lane's JSON names another tree or another lane's rows"
    [ "$(field merged_sha) $(field merge_branch)" = "$first" ] \
        || fail "the two lanes name different merges, $first and $(field merged_sha) $(field merge_branch), so they did not share one push"
    [ "$(field merged_sha)" = "$(git -C "$at/origin.git" rev-parse main)" ] || fail "merged_sha is not what origin/main holds"
    [ "$(gates)" = 1 ] || fail "two lanes waiting together ran $(gates) gates, not one"
    [ "$(git -C "$at/origin.git" log --merges --grep 'by just land' --format=%H main | wc -l)" -eq 2 ] \
        || fail "origin/main does not hold one merge commit for each lane"
    [ "$(grep -c '^just ff-main' "$at/stub/log")" -eq 1 ] || fail "ff-main did not run exactly once for one push"
    marked=$(grep '^just mark-finished ' "$at/stub/log" | cut -d' ' -f3- | tr ' ' '\n' | sort || true)
    [ "$marked" = "$(printf '%s\n' "$at"/{lane,verify,lander,lane2,verify2,lander2} | sort)" ] \
        || fail "mark-finished was given, all told: $marked"
    for tree in lane verify lander lane2 verify2 lander2; do
        ! locked "$tree" || fail "the $tree tree is still locked"
    done
    spool_empty

    fixture red-batch
    second_lane
    touch "$at/stub/gate-fails-with-lane2"
    hold_lock
    start_in lander Q1 lane-x "$lane_sha" merge-x "$at/lane" "$at/verify"
    start_in lander2 Q2 lane-y "$lane2_sha" merge-y "$at/lane2" "$at/verify2"
    release_lock
    wait_for lander
    both=$got
    [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && [ "$(field merged)" = true ] || fail "the lane the gate passes alone did not merge after a red batch"
    wait_for lander2
    both+=$got
    [ "$rc" -eq 1 ] && [ "$(field stop)" = gate ] || fail "the lane the gate fails on did not stop at the gate"
    [ "$(gates)" = 3 ] || fail "a red batch, then each lane alone, is 3 gates, not $(gates)"
    on_origin "$lane_sha" || fail "the lane the gate passes is not on origin/main"
    ! on_origin "$lane2_sha" || fail "the lane the gate fails on reached origin/main"
    grep -qF 'land: the gate failed with 2 lanes merged, so lane-' <<<"$both" || fail "nothing says the batch fell back to one lane"
    spool_empty

    fixture dead-entry
    second_lane
    hold_lock
    start_in lander2 Q2 lane-y "$lane2_sha" merge-y "$at/lane2" "$at/verify2"
    waiter=$(cat "$at/lander2/target/land/pid")
    kill -STOP -- "-$waiter"
    release_lock
    land_in verify 0.5 wait-for lane-y
    [ "$rc" -eq 3 ] || fail "wait-for exited $rc with the lock free and a live run waiting to land lane-y, not 3 for still running"
    grep -qxF "land: still running: the run in $at/lander2 (pid $waiter) waits to land it. Run \`mise exec -- just land wait-for lane-y\` to keep waiting" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "wait-for does not name the run that waits to land lane-y"
    hold_lock
    kill -9 -- "-$waiter"
    land_in verify 0.5 wait-for lane-y
    [ "$rc" -eq 3 ] || fail "wait-for exited $rc while a holder had the land lock, not 3 for still running"
    grep -qF 'land: still running: a holder has the land lock.' <<<"$got" || fail "wait-for does not say a holder has the lock"
    start_in lander Q1 lane-x "$lane_sha" merge-x "$at/lane" "$at/verify"
    release_lock
    wait_for lander
    [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && [ "$(field merged)" = true ] || fail "the live lane did not land beside an entry whose run was killed"
    [ "$(gates)" = 1 ] || fail "one live lane ran $(gates) gates"
    ! on_origin "$lane2_sha" || fail "it landed a lane whose run was gone, which the Opus merger lands too (§397.5 item 7)"
    grep -qF 'land: the run that waited to land lane-y' <<<"$got" || fail "nothing says the dead entry was dropped"
    spool_empty
    land_in verify 5 wait-for lane-y
    [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] || fail "wait-for exited $rc once no run was landing lane-y, not 0"
    grep -qxF 'land: no run is landing lane-y; fetch origin before you read origin/main' <<<"$got" \
        || fail "wait-for does not say that no run is landing lane-y"

    fixture killed-holder
    second_lane
    touch "$at/stub/gate-blocks"
    start_in lander Q1 lane-x "$lane_sha" merge-x "$at/lane" "$at/verify"
    until_there "$at/stub/gate-started"
    start_in lander2 Q2 lane-y "$lane2_sha" merge-y "$at/lane2" "$at/verify2"
    kill -9 -- "-$(cat "$at/lander/target/land/pid")"
    wait_for lander2
    [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && [ "$(field merged)" = true ] || fail "a lane waiting behind a holder that was killed did not land"
    on_origin "$lane2_sha" || fail "the waiting lane is not on origin/main"
    ! on_origin "$lane_sha" || fail "the killed holder's lane reached origin/main"
    [ "$(gates)" = 2 ] || fail "the killed holder's gate and the waiting lane's are 2 gates, not $(gates)"
    grep -qF 'land: the run that waited to land lane-x' <<<"$got" || fail "nothing says the killed holder's entry was dropped"
    spool_empty

    fixture killed-follower
    touch "$at/stub/gate-blocks"
    STUB_DIR="$at/stub" LAND_JUST="$dir/bin/just" LAND_WAIT=60 setsid {{ just_executable() }} --justfile '{{ justfile() }}' --working-directory "$at/lander" land Q1 lane-x "$lane_sha" merge-x "$at/lane" "$at/verify" >"$at/follower.log" 2>&1 &
    follower=$!
    until_there "$at/stub/gate-started"
    stop_like_a_clear "$follower"
    wait "$follower" 2>/dev/null || true
    touch "$at/stub/gate-release"
    wait_for lander
    [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && [ "$(field merged)" = true ] || fail "the run a killed follower started did not finish landing"
    on_origin "$lane_sha" || fail "the lane a killed follower started landing is not on origin/main"
    ! locked lander || fail "the lander tree is still locked after a killed follower"
    spool_empty

    fixture answers
    another_merge docs/queue.md "$(printf '%s\n' '## Tier 1' '' '```' \
        'Q3   1    todo           sonnet  tests/a.rs  a row the lane answers §450' \
        'Q4   1    lane:lane-z    sonnet  tests/a.rs  a row another lane holds §450' \
        'Q5   1    parked:§450    sonnet  tests/a.rs  a parked row §450' \
        'Q6   1    after:Q3       sonnet  tests/a.rs  a row that waits §450' \
        'Q7   1    done:abcdef12  sonnet  tests/a.rs  a closed row §450' \
        '```')"
    run --answers Q3,Q4,Q5,Q6,Q7,Q8 Q1 lane-x "$lane_sha" merge-x "$at/lane" "$at/verify"
    [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && [ "$(field merged)" = true ] || fail "a lane that answers other rows did not merge"
    git -C "$at/origin.git" show main:docs/queue.md \
        | grep -qxF "apply done Q1 ${lane_sha:0:8} + done Q3 ${lane_sha:0:8} + done Q6 ${lane_sha:0:8}" \
        || fail "the merge commit does not close the row and the open rows it answers in one queue apply (§450.3 rule 4)"
    [ "$(field rows_closed)" = "[\"Q1 done:${lane_sha:0:8}\", \"Q3 done:${lane_sha:0:8} answered by Q1\", \"Q6 done:${lane_sha:0:8} answered by Q1\"]" ] \
        || fail "rows_closed is $(field rows_closed)"
    for row in Q4 Q5 Q7 Q8; do
        grep -qF "\"$row is " <<<"$(field needs_judgement)" \
            || fail "needs_judgement does not say why $row, held, parked, closed or no row, was not closed as answered"
    done
    [ "$(git -C "$at/origin.git" log -1 --format=%s main)" = 'Merge lane-x: Q1 done, and Q3, Q6 answered by it, by just land' ] \
        || fail "the merge commit's subject is '$(git -C "$at/origin.git" log -1 --format=%s main)'"

    fixture answers-twice
    run --answers Q1 Q1 lane-x "$lane_sha" merge-x "$at/lane" "$at/verify"
    stopped usage

    fixture old-entry
    second_lane
    hold_lock
    start_in lander Q1 lane-x "$lane_sha" merge-x "$at/lane" "$at/verify"
    start_in lander2 Q2 lane-y "$lane2_sha" merge-y "$at/lane2" "$at/verify2"
    for entry in "$at/hub/.git/rcad-land/spool"/*.json; do
        python3 -c 'import json, sys; e = json.load(open(sys.argv[1])); e.pop("answers"); open(sys.argv[1], "w").write(json.dumps(e))' "$entry" \
            || fail "a spool entry carries no answers, so this arm cannot take it out to make one an older land.py wrote"
    done
    release_lock
    wait_for lander
    [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && [ "$(field merged)" = true ] || fail "the first lane did not land beside a spool entry with no answers"
    wait_for lander2
    [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && [ "$(field merged)" = true ] || fail "the second lane did not land beside a spool entry with no answers"
    spool_empty
    echo "land merges a clean lane and prints its JSON, stops by name on each of 9 stops, lands two waiting lanes on one gate, falls back to its own lane on a red batch, never takes a dead entry, outlives a killed holder, outlives a killed follower, wait-for waits on a live entry and on a held lock, closes the open rows a lane answers in its queue apply and names the others, and lands a spool entry an older land.py wrote"

# A verifier's fixed steps for one lane, run for it (§383.4 item 4, §385): unlock
# the lane's tree, `git log` from the base to the branch, the files outside the
# write set, `lines-that-run` before the checkout, the checkout, `gate quiet`,
# `verifier.md` step 7's revert, test run and restore, and the lane's numbers.
# Its last line is one line of JSON holding every `verdictSchema` field it could
# fill, with `skipped` and `left_to_you` for what it skipped and could not
# decide. The verifier keeps the reading.
#
# **It runs detached, and the verifier waits on it** (§385.2). The harness ends
# a Bash call at 600 s, and a cold gate alone can take nine minutes. So the
# first call starts the run in a session of its own and follows its log for at
# most 540 s, and while its last line says the run is still going, `just
# verify-lane wait` follows it again. Each call prints what the run logged since
# the call before. A run is one python process started before the checkout, so
# the copy that judges a branch is never one the branch changed.
#
# The input is a file the verifier writes with the Write tool, one word to a
# line and a `+` line between records, as the lander's steps are (§384.2). The
# logic is `tooling/verify-lane/verify_lane.py`; `VERIFY_LANE_JUST` is the seam
# `check-verify-lane-selftest` puts a stub in where `just` would run the gate and
# the tests, and `VERIFY_LANE_WAIT` how long one call follows the run.
[doc("A verifier's fixed steps for one lane, run detached so no call passes ten minutes: `just verify-lane <input file>`, then `just verify-lane wait` while it says it is still running. Ends with one line of JSON.")]
[no-exit-message]
verify-lane *args:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    VERIFY_LANE_JUST="${VERIFY_LANE_JUST:-{{ just_executable() }}}" exec python3 '{{ justfile_directory() }}/tooling/verify-lane/verify_lane.py' "$@"

# Pins `verify-lane` against repositories it builds itself, one per arm, each
# with a lane's worktree on its branch and a verifier's worktree at the base, as
# the `lanes` workflow leaves them. A stub on `VERIFY_LANE_JUST` stands in for
# `just`: its `gate` and `gate-lane` print a summary block, its `t <name>` runs the fixture's
# `tests/<name>.sh` and exits 100 when that fails and 4 when there is no such
# file, as nextest does, and its recipes are the fixture's `recipes/`. Its
# `lines-that-run` is the real recipe, run on the fixture. It logs `HEAD` for
# `lines-that-run` and the gate, which is how the first arm sees the one ran on
# the base and the other on the branch.
#
# The arms: a test that fails with the source reverted, beside a number whose
# value is in its command's print, one whose value is not, and one whose command
# writes; a revert that takes the new test out, which is no failure (Q231); tests
# that pass with the source reverted; a test in the slow tier `gate-lane` leaves
# out, which runs once on the branch; the same on a branch with no `gate-lane`,
# where `gate quiet` ran it; a `pinsOnly` lane; a `sameBehaviour` lane whose
# tests pass with the source reverted, and one whose test fails there, which
# says the behaviour changed (§399); a lines-that-run count of 0; a source path
# holding test code the branch changed; a lane that names no test; a write-set
# entry naming one item of a file, matched against the file it is in; a run
# longer than one call, which a second call follows to its end; an input
# file it cannot read, or that marks a lane both `pinsOnly` and `sameBehaviour`;
# and, pinning §429.5, the agent's own `just verify-lane` stopped mid-gate the
# way Claude Code kills a command (§429.2), which still leaves the detached run
# to finish and `just verify-lane wait` to read its verdict back.
#
# The JSON's fields are held to `verdictSchema` in `lanes.js`, read out of that
# file, so a field the schema renames fails here and not in a verifier's report.
[private]
check-verify-lane-selftest:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    dir=$(realpath "$(mktemp -d)")
    trap 'rm -rf "$dir"' EXIT
    mkdir "$dir/bin"
    cat >"$dir/bin/just" <<'STUB'
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -uo pipefail
    printf 'just %s\n' "$*" >>"$STUB_DIR/log"
    case "${1:-}" in
        lines-that-run)
            echo "lines-that-run at $(git rev-parse HEAD)" >>"$STUB_DIR/heads"
            exec "$REAL_JUST" --justfile "$REAL_JUSTFILE" --working-directory "$PWD" lines-that-run "$2" "$3" ;;
        gate|gate-lane)
            echo "$1 at $(git rev-parse HEAD)" >>"$STUB_DIR/heads"
            [ ! -e "$STUB_DIR/gate-sleeps" ] || sleep "$(cat "$STUB_DIR/gate-sleeps")"
            echo "· check-fixed (0.1 s)"
            echo
            echo "=== gate summary ==="
            echo "gate: PASS"
            echo "tests: Summary [   0.1s] 2 tests run: 2 passed, 0 skipped"
            echo "full log: target/last-gate.log" ;;
        --show) [ -f "recipes/${2:-}.sh" ] ;;
        --evaluate)
            case "${2:-}" in
                gate_steps) echo "check-plugins check-fixed lint test" ;;
                slow_tests) echo "rcad-x::slow/a_slow_test" ;;
                *) echo "the stub has no variable ${2:-}" >&2; exit 1 ;;
            esac ;;
        t)
            if [ ! -f "tests/${2:-}.sh" ]; then
                echo "error: no tests to run"
                exit 4
            fi
            if bash "tests/$2.sh"; then
                echo "PASS $2"
                exit 0
            fi
            echo "FAIL $2"
            exit 100 ;;
        *)
            [ -f "recipes/${1:-}.sh" ] || { echo "the stub has no recipe ${1:-}" >&2; exit 2; }
            exec bash "recipes/$1.sh" ;;
    esac
    STUB
    chmod +x "$dir/bin/just"
    schema=$(awk '/^function verdictSchema/,/^}/' .claude/workflows/lanes.js \
        | grep -oE '^    [a-z_]+: \{|properties\.[a-z_]+ = \{' | grep -oE '[a-z_]+' | grep -vx properties | tr '\n' ' ' || true)
    for wanted in worktree_path lines_that_run tests_fail_without_fix; do
        case " $schema " in
            *" $wanted "*) ;;
            *) echo "found no verdictSchema holding $wanted in .claude/workflows/lanes.js, so the JSON would be held to nothing"; exit 1 ;;
        esac
    done
    arm=""
    at=""
    base=""
    tip=""
    got=""
    json=""
    rc=0
    fail() {
        echo "verify-lane drifted from its own fixtures, in the arm '$arm': $1"
        echo "--- got ---"; echo "$got"
        exit 1
    }
    code() {
        printf '%s\n' "$1" 'pub fn answer() -> u32 {' "    $2" '}' '' '#[cfg(test)]' 'mod tests {' '    #[test]' \
            '    fn answer_is_right() {' "        assert_eq!(super::answer(), $3);" '    }' '}'
    }
    fixture() {
        arm=$1
        at="$dir/$1"
        mkdir -p "$at/stub"
        touch "$at/stub/log" "$at/stub/heads"
        git init -q -b main "$at/hub"
        git -C "$at/hub" config user.email t@t
        git -C "$at/hub" config user.name t
        git -C "$at/hub" config commit.gpgsign false
        mkdir -p "$at/hub/src" "$at/hub/tests" "$at/hub/recipes" "$at/hub/docs"
        printf '/target\n' >"$at/hub/.gitignore"
        printf 'broken\n' >"$at/hub/src/lib.txt"
        code '// The answer.' 41 41 >"$at/hub/src/code.rs"
        printf 'grep -q fixed src/lib.txt\n' >"$at/hub/tests/is_fixed.sh"
        printf 'true\n' >"$at/hub/tests/always_passes.sh"
        printf 'grep -q fixed src/lib.txt\n' >"$at/hub/recipes/check-fixed.sh"
        printf 'true\n' >"$at/hub/recipes/check-always.sh"
        printf 'true\n' >"$at/hub/recipes/gate-lane.sh"
        printf 'grep -q fixed src/lib.txt\n' >"$at/hub/tests/a_slow_test.sh"
        printf '# Notes\n' >"$at/hub/docs/notes.md"
        git -C "$at/hub" add -A
        git -C "$at/hub" commit -q -m base
        base=$(git -C "$at/hub" rev-parse HEAD)
        git -C "$at/hub" checkout -q --detach
        git -C "$at/hub" worktree add -q -b lane-x "$at/lane" "$base"
        git -C "$at/hub" worktree add -q --detach "$at/verify" "$base"
        git -C "$at/hub" worktree lock --reason "an agent at work" "$at/lane"
        mkdir -p "$at/verify/target"
    }
    lane_commit() {
        git -C "$at/lane" add -A
        git -C "$at/lane" commit -q -m "the lane's work"
        tip=$(git -C "$at/lane" rev-parse HEAD)
    }
    input() {
        printf '%s\n' "$@" >"$at/verify/target/verify-lane.txt"
    }
    call() {
        got=$(STUB_DIR="$at/stub" REAL_JUST='{{ just_executable() }}' REAL_JUSTFILE='{{ justfile() }}' \
            VERIFY_LANE_JUST="$dir/bin/just" VERIFY_LANE_WAIT="${budget:-60}" \
            {{ just_executable() }} --justfile '{{ justfile() }}' --working-directory "$at/verify" verify-lane "$@" 2>&1) \
            && rc=0 || rc=$?
        json=$(printf '%s\n' "$got" | tail -n 1)
    }
    held_to_the_schema() {
        local stray
        stray=$(python3 -c 'import json, sys; d = json.loads(sys.argv[1]); print(" ".join(k for k in d if k not in sys.argv[2].split() + ["skipped", "left_to_you"]))' "$json" "$schema" 2>&1) \
            || fail "its last line is not JSON: $stray"
        [ -z "$stray" ] || fail "its JSON has fields that verdictSchema in lanes.js does not: $stray"
    }
    run() {
        call target/verify-lane.txt
        [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] || fail "a run that ended exited $rc"
        held_to_the_schema
    }
    field() {
        python3 -c 'import json, sys; v = json.loads(sys.argv[1]).get(sys.argv[2], "(absent)"); print(v if isinstance(v, str) else json.dumps(v, ensure_ascii=False))' "$json" "$1"
    }
    says() {
        grep -qF -- "$2" <<<"$(field "$1")" || fail "$1 does not say '$2': $(field "$1")"
    }
    locked() {
        [ -e "$(git -C "$at/$1" rev-parse --absolute-git-dir)/locked" ]
    }
    back_at_the_tip() {
        [ -z "$(git -C "$at/verify" status --porcelain)" ] || fail "the verifier's tree is left changed: $(git -C "$at/verify" status --porcelain)"
        [ "$(git -C "$at/verify" rev-parse HEAD)" = "$tip" ] || fail "the verifier's tree is not at the lane's tip"
    }
    no_test_ran() {
        ! grep -qE '^just (t |check-)' "$at/stub/log" || fail "it ran a test: $(grep -E '^just (t |check-)' "$at/stub/log")"
    }
    until_there() {
        for _ in $(seq 400); do
            if [ $# -eq 1 ] && [ -e "$1" ]; then return 0; fi
            if [ $# -eq 2 ] && grep -qF "$2" "$1" 2>/dev/null; then return 0; fi
            sleep 0.05
        done
        fail "waited 20 s for $1 ${2:-}"
    }
    descendants_of() {
        local root=$1
        ps -A -o pid=,ppid= | awk -v root="$root" '
            { parent[$1] = $2 }
            END {
                for (pid in parent) {
                    cur = pid
                    for (i = 0; i < 1000 && (cur in parent); i++) {
                        if (parent[cur] == root) { print pid; break }
                        cur = parent[cur]
                    }
                }
            }'
    }
    stop_like_a_clear() {
        local group=$1 pid before
        before=$(descendants_of "$group")
        kill -TERM -- "-$group" 2>/dev/null || true
        for pid in $before; do
            kill -TERM "$pid" 2>/dev/null || true
        done
        sleep 0.2
        kill -KILL -- "-$group" 2>/dev/null || true
        for pid in $before; do
            kill -KILL "$pid" 2>/dev/null || true
        done
    }

    fixture fails-reverted
    printf 'fixed\n' >"$at/lane/src/lib.txt"
    printf 'not the lane'"'"'s\n' >"$at/lane/outside.txt"
    lane_commit
    input base "$base" + branch lane-x + lane-tree "$at/lane" + write-set src/ docs/ + source-paths src/ \
        + tests is_fixed check-fixed \
        + number 'lines that say fixed' 1 'grep -c fixed src/lib.txt' \
        + number 'a count the lane misread' 7 'grep -c fixed src/lib.txt' \
        + number 'a count whose command writes' 0 'git commit -q --allow-empty -m "a number"' \
        + number 'a count whose command pins a row' 0 'just queue pin Q1'
    run
    [ "$(field worktree_path) $(field branch_ok)" = "$at/verify true" ] || fail "worktree_path and branch_ok are $(field worktree_path) $(field branch_ok)"
    [ "$(field files_outside_write_set)" = '["outside.txt"]' ] || fail "files_outside_write_set is $(field files_outside_write_set)"
    [ "$(field lines_that_run_command)" = "mise exec -- just lines-that-run $base lane-x" ] \
        || fail "lines_that_run_command is not the command lanes.js hands a verifier: $(field lines_that_run_command)"
    [ "$(field lines_that_run | tail -n 1)" = "lines that run: 3" ] || fail "the lines-that-run print does not end in its count of 3"
    [ "$(printf '%s\n' "$(cat "$at/stub/heads")")" = "lines-that-run at $base"$'\n'"gate-lane at $tip" ] \
        || fail "lines-that-run must run before the checkout and the gate after it, and they ran at: $(cat "$at/stub/heads")"
    [ "$(field gate_summary | head -n 2)" = $'=== gate summary ===\ngate: PASS' ] || fail "gate_summary is not the block: $(field gate_summary)"
    [ "$(field tests_pass_on_branch) $(field tests_fail_without_fix)" = "true true" ] \
        || fail "tests that fail with the source reverted gave tests_pass_on_branch $(field tests_pass_on_branch) and tests_fail_without_fix $(field tests_fail_without_fix)"
    grep -qxF 'just t is_fixed' "$at/stub/log" && grep -qxF 'just check-fixed' "$at/stub/log" || fail "it did not run both tests against the revert"
    [ "$(python3 -c 'import json, sys; print(len(json.loads(sys.argv[1])))' "$(field number_mismatches)")" = 1 ] \
        || fail "of three numbers, one has a value its command prints and one has a value it does not, but number_mismatches is $(field number_mismatches)"
    says number_mismatches "a count the lane misread: '7' is not in what"
    says left_to_you 'a count whose command writes: not run'
    says left_to_you 'a count whose command pins a row: not run'
    back_at_the_tip
    ! locked lane || fail "the lane's tree is still locked"

    fixture taken-out
    printf 'fixed\n' >"$at/lane/src/lib.txt"
    printf 'grep -q fixed src/lib.txt\n' >"$at/lane/tests/new_test.sh"
    lane_commit
    input base "$base" + branch lane-x + lane-tree "$at/lane" + write-set src/ tests/ + source-paths src/ tests/ + tests new_test
    run
    [ "$(field tests_fail_without_fix)" = "(absent)" ] \
        || fail "a revert that took the test out gave tests_fail_without_fix $(field tests_fail_without_fix); no test ran, and that is no failure (Q231)"
    says left_to_you 'new_test ran no test'
    back_at_the_tip

    fixture passes-reverted
    printf 'fixed\n' >"$at/lane/src/lib.txt"
    lane_commit
    input base "$base" + branch lane-x + lane-tree "$at/lane" + write-set src/ + source-paths src/ + tests always_passes check-always
    run
    [ "$(field tests_pass_on_branch) $(field tests_fail_without_fix)" = "true false" ] \
        || fail "tests that pass with the source reverted gave $(field tests_pass_on_branch) and $(field tests_fail_without_fix)"
    [ "$(grep -cxF 'just check-always' "$at/stub/log")" = 2 ] \
        || fail "a named recipe the gate does not run should run once on the branch and once reverted"
    back_at_the_tip

    fixture slow-tier
    printf 'fixed\n' >"$at/lane/src/lib.txt"
    lane_commit
    input base "$base" + branch lane-x + lane-tree "$at/lane" + write-set src/ + source-paths src/ + tests a_slow_test
    run
    [ "$(tail -n 1 "$at/stub/heads")" = "gate-lane at $tip" ] || fail "the branch has gate-lane and was gated with something else: $(cat "$at/stub/heads")"
    [ "$(grep -cxF 'just t a_slow_test' "$at/stub/log")" = 2 ] \
        || fail "a named test in the slow tier gate-lane leaves out should run once on the branch and once reverted"
    [ "$(field tests_pass_on_branch) $(field tests_fail_without_fix)" = "true true" ] \
        || fail "a slow test that passes on the branch and fails reverted gave $(field tests_pass_on_branch) and $(field tests_fail_without_fix)"
    back_at_the_tip

    fixture old-base
    printf 'fixed\n' >"$at/lane/src/lib.txt"
    git -C "$at/lane" rm -q recipes/gate-lane.sh
    lane_commit
    input base "$base" + branch lane-x + lane-tree "$at/lane" + write-set src/ recipes/ + source-paths src/ + tests a_slow_test
    run
    [ "$(tail -n 1 "$at/stub/heads")" = "gate at $tip" ] || fail "a branch with no gate-lane was not gated with gate quiet: $(cat "$at/stub/heads")"
    grep -qxF 'just gate quiet' "$at/stub/log" || fail "a branch with no gate-lane was not gated with gate quiet"
    [ "$(grep -cxF 'just t a_slow_test' "$at/stub/log")" = 1 ] \
        || fail "the whole gate ran the slow tier, and its slow test ran on the branch again"
    [ "$(field tests_pass_on_branch) $(field tests_fail_without_fix)" = "true true" ] \
        || fail "a slow test under the whole gate gave $(field tests_pass_on_branch) and $(field tests_fail_without_fix)"
    back_at_the_tip

    fixture pins-only
    printf 'true\n' >"$at/lane/tests/is_pinned.sh"
    lane_commit
    input base "$base" + branch lane-x + lane-tree "$at/lane" + write-set tests/ + pins-only + tests is_pinned
    run
    [ "$(field tests_pass_on_branch) $(field tests_fail_without_fix)" = "true false" ] \
        || fail "a pinsOnly lane gave $(field tests_pass_on_branch) and $(field tests_fail_without_fix)"
    says skipped 'pinsOnly'
    no_test_ran

    fixture same-behaviour
    printf 'broken, said again\n' >"$at/lane/src/lib.txt"
    lane_commit
    input base "$base" + branch lane-x + lane-tree "$at/lane" + write-set src/ + source-paths src/ + same-behaviour \
        + tests always_passes check-always
    run
    [ "$(field tests_pass_on_branch) $(field tests_pass_without_change) $(field tests_fail_without_fix)" = "true true (absent)" ] \
        || fail "a sameBehaviour lane whose tests pass with the source reverted gave tests_pass_on_branch $(field tests_pass_on_branch), tests_pass_without_change $(field tests_pass_without_change) and tests_fail_without_fix $(field tests_fail_without_fix)"
    [ "$(grep -cxF 'just check-always' "$at/stub/log")" = 2 ] \
        || fail "a named recipe the gate does not run should run once on the branch and once reverted"
    back_at_the_tip

    fixture same-behaviour-changed
    printf 'fixed\n' >"$at/lane/src/lib.txt"
    lane_commit
    input base "$base" + branch lane-x + lane-tree "$at/lane" + write-set src/ + source-paths src/ + same-behaviour + tests is_fixed
    run
    [ "$(field tests_pass_without_change)" = false ] \
        || fail "a sameBehaviour lane whose test fails with the source reverted gave tests_pass_without_change $(field tests_pass_without_change)"
    says left_to_you 'the behaviour changed'
    back_at_the_tip

    fixture zero
    code '// The answer, which is right.' 41 41 >"$at/lane/src/code.rs"
    printf '# Notes, and more\n' >"$at/lane/docs/notes.md"
    lane_commit
    input base "$base" + branch lane-x + lane-tree "$at/lane" + write-set src/ docs/ + source-paths src/ + tests answer_is_right
    run
    [ "$(field lines_that_run | tail -n 1)" = "lines that run: 0" ] || fail "a comment and a note counted as lines that run"
    [ "$(field tests_fail_without_fix)" = false ] || fail "a count of 0 gave tests_fail_without_fix $(field tests_fail_without_fix)"
    says skipped 'lines that run: 0'
    no_test_ran

    fixture test-in-source
    code '// The answer.' 42 42 >"$at/lane/src/code.rs"
    lane_commit
    input base "$base" + branch lane-x + lane-tree "$at/lane" + write-set src/ + source-paths src/ + tests answer_is_right
    run
    says left_to_you 'revert by hand'
    says left_to_you 'src/code.rs: +assert_eq!(super::answer(), 42);'
    [ "$(field tests_fail_without_fix)" = "(absent)" ] || fail "a source path holding the new test gave tests_fail_without_fix $(field tests_fail_without_fix)"
    no_test_ran

    fixture moved-text
    printf '# Notes, moved\n' >"$at/lane/docs/notes.md"
    lane_commit
    input base "$base" + branch lane-x + lane-tree "$at/lane" + write-set docs/
    run
    [ "$(field tests_pass_on_branch) $(field tests_fail_without_fix)" = "(absent) (absent)" ] || fail "a lane naming no test got a test field"
    [ "$(field lines_that_run | tail -n 1)" = "lines that run: 0" ] || fail "a note counted as lines that run"
    no_test_ran

    fixture one-item-write-set
    printf 'fixed\n' >"$at/lane/src/lib.txt"
    lane_commit
    input base "$base" + branch lane-x + lane-tree "$at/lane" + write-set src/lib.txt#something
    run
    [ "$(field files_outside_write_set)" = "[]" ] \
        || fail "a write-set entry naming one item of a file did not match a change to that file: $(field files_outside_write_set)"
    [ "$(field tests_pass_on_branch) $(field tests_fail_without_fix)" = "(absent) (absent)" ] || fail "a lane naming no test got a test field"
    no_test_ran

    fixture resume
    printf 'fixed\n' >"$at/lane/src/lib.txt"
    lane_commit
    echo 2 >"$at/stub/gate-sleeps"
    input base "$base" + branch lane-x + lane-tree "$at/lane" + write-set src/ + source-paths src/ + tests is_fixed
    budget=1 call target/verify-lane.txt
    [ "$rc" -eq 3 ] || fail "a run longer than one call exited $rc, not 3"
    grep -qF 'still running' <<<"$json" || fail "a call that ran out of time does not say on its last line that the run goes on"
    call target/verify-lane.txt
    [ "$rc" -eq 2 ] && grep -qF 'has started a run' <<<"$got" || fail "a second start in the same tree did not refuse"
    call wait
    [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] || fail "the wait that saw the run end exited $rc"
    held_to_the_schema
    grep -qxF 'gate: PASS' <<<"$got" || fail "the wait did not print what the run logged after the first call"
    [ "$(field tests_fail_without_fix)" = true ] || fail "the run followed to its end gave tests_fail_without_fix $(field tests_fail_without_fix)"
    first=$json
    call wait
    [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && [ "$json" = "$first" ] || fail "a wait after the run ended does not print its JSON again"
    back_at_the_tip

    fixture killed-follower
    printf 'fixed\n' >"$at/lane/src/lib.txt"
    lane_commit
    echo 2 >"$at/stub/gate-sleeps"
    input base "$base" + branch lane-x + lane-tree "$at/lane" + write-set src/ + source-paths src/ + tests is_fixed
    STUB_DIR="$at/stub" REAL_JUST='{{ just_executable() }}' REAL_JUSTFILE='{{ justfile() }}' \
        VERIFY_LANE_JUST="$dir/bin/just" VERIFY_LANE_WAIT=60 \
        setsid {{ just_executable() }} --justfile '{{ justfile() }}' --working-directory "$at/verify" verify-lane target/verify-lane.txt \
        >"$at/follower.log" 2>&1 &
    follower=$!
    until_there "$at/stub/heads" gate
    stop_like_a_clear "$follower"
    wait "$follower" 2>/dev/null || true
    call wait
    [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] || fail "the wait after a killed follower exited $rc"
    held_to_the_schema
    [ "$(field tests_pass_on_branch) $(field tests_fail_without_fix)" = "true true" ] \
        || fail "a killed follower's run gave tests_pass_on_branch $(field tests_pass_on_branch) and tests_fail_without_fix $(field tests_fail_without_fix)"
    back_at_the_tip

    fixture bad-input
    input base "$base" + colour blue
    call target/verify-lane.txt
    [ "$rc" -eq 2 ] && grep -qF "is 'colour'" <<<"$got" || fail "an input with an unknown record did not refuse by name"
    [ ! -e "$at/verify/target/verify-lane" ] || fail "it started a run on an input it could not read"
    input base "$base" + branch lane-x + lane-tree "$at/lane" + write-set src/ + pins-only + same-behaviour
    call target/verify-lane.txt
    [ "$rc" -eq 2 ] && grep -qF 'both pinsOnly and sameBehaviour' <<<"$got" || fail "an input marking the lane both pinsOnly and sameBehaviour did not refuse"
    [ ! -e "$at/verify/target/verify-lane" ] || fail "it started a run on an input marking the lane both pinsOnly and sameBehaviour"
    echo "verify-lane runs a verifier's fixed steps detached and fills what it can, in 16 arms"

# Nobody works in the main working tree. It owns `.git` and nothing else.
#
# §55 made a worktree's lock this repo's record of who owns it, and the one tree
# that record structurally cannot describe is the tree every session lands in by
# default: `git worktree lock` answers *"fatal: The main working tree cannot be
# locked or unlocked"*, by path or by `.`, with no flag to override. This closes
# that by deleting the case rather than by inventing a second record beside the
# first — a tree nobody works in needs no owner, and `free` becomes the truth
# about it instead of a guess (docs/decisions.md §60).
#
# **The load-bearing half is that `main` is not checked out here.** Git refuses
# to move a branch that is checked out somewhere else — *"refusing to fetch into
# branch 'refs/heads/main' checked out at ..."*, and *"! [remote rejected] lane
# -> main (branch is currently checked out)"* — so a hub holding `main` drags
# every merge back into the one tree whose owner cannot be recorded, however
# carefully the lanes were dispatched. Parked on a detached HEAD, `main` is
# checkable out, and therefore lockable, in a worktree like any lane.
#
# **A repository with one worktree is exempt**, because then there is no hub and
# no lane, only a checkout, and no second session to collide with. That is a
# fresh clone, and it is the shape a CI checkout has. This is no longer a
# prediction: CI runs the whole `gate` since 2026-09-07, so a runner reaches
# this recipe, and run `34266469454` took the exemption and said so — *"is the
# only worktree, so it is a checkout and not a hub"*. The exemption is
# structural rather than an environment variable on purpose: one that has to be
# set is one that gets set, while this lapses by itself the moment a second tree
# exists, which is the moment ownership starts being a question.
[doc("Nobody works in the hub: it owns .git and nothing else.")]
check-hub dir=".":
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    listing=$(git -C "{{dir}}" worktree list --porcelain)
    if [ -z "$listing" ]; then
        echo "git worktree list --porcelain printed nothing, so this checked no hub"
        exit 1
    fi
    # Git documents the main working tree as the first entry of the listing.
    hub=$(awk '/^worktree /{print $2; exit}' <<<"$listing")
    trees=$(grep -c '^worktree ' <<<"$listing")
    if [ -z "$hub" ]; then
        echo "no worktree was parsed out of a non-empty listing, so the parse is broken"
        exit 1
    fi
    if [ "$trees" -eq 1 ]; then
        echo "$hub is the only worktree, so it is a checkout and not a hub"
        exit 0
    fi
    wrong=0
    branch=$(git -C "$hub" symbolic-ref --quiet --short HEAD || true)
    if [ -n "$branch" ]; then
        echo "the hub has the branch '$branch' checked out: $hub"
        echo "    git -C $hub checkout --detach"
        echo "  while a branch lives here, git will not let a lane move it, so every merge has to come back to the one tree it cannot lock"
        wrong=1
    fi
    dirt=$(git -C "$hub" status --porcelain)
    if [ -n "$dirt" ]; then
        echo "the hub has $(wc -l <<<"$dirt") path(s) of uncommitted work in it: $hub"
        head -n 10 <<<"$dirt" | sed 's/^/    /'
        if [ "$(wc -l <<<"$dirt")" -gt 10 ]; then
            echo "    ..."
        fi
        # A hub parked at an older commit shows work that has since landed as
        # untracked, which is a *stale* hub and not leftovers. Same symptom,
        # opposite fix: telling the reader to commit files that are already
        # committed sends them to duplicate somebody's merge. The two are told
        # apart by asking whether every path is already on `main`, which has to
        # mean the same bytes and not the same name. A name-only answer sends a
        # reader to delete a file whose content is on `main` under that name but
        # is not the content in front of them (docs/decisions.md §60.10).
        untracked=$(grep '^??' <<<"$dirt" | cut -c4- || true)
        modified=$(grep -v '^??' <<<"$dirt" || true)
        unmatched=""
        if [ -z "$modified" ] && [ -n "$untracked" ]; then
            # Enumerated rather than read off `status`, which reports an
            # untracked directory as one entry and quotes an awkward name. Every
            # file the `clean` would take is hashed; an empty directory is the
            # only thing it takes that this does not see, and it holds nothing.
            while IFS= read -r -d '' path; do
                on_main=$(git -C "$hub" rev-parse --verify --quiet "main:$path" || true)
                if [ -n "$on_main" ] \
                    && [ "$on_main" = "$(git -C "$hub" hash-object -- "$hub/$path")" ]; then
                    continue
                fi
                unmatched="$unmatched$path"$'\n'
            done < <(git -C "$hub" ls-files --others --exclude-standard -z)
        fi
        if [ -z "$modified" ] && [ -n "$untracked" ] && [ -z "$unmatched" ]; then
            echo "  Every one of those is already committed on \`main\`, byte for byte —"
            echo "  compared by content, not by name — so nothing here is lost work; the"
            echo "  hub is just parked at an older commit. Re-park it:"
            # One line, because `check-hub-selftest` greps it back out and runs
            # it. Wrapping it for width is caught rather than silent: the grep
            # then hands bash a dangling `&&` and the selftest fails on it.
            echo "      git -C $hub clean -fd && git -C $hub checkout --detach main"
            echo "  The clean is not optional. Checkout refuses to overwrite an untracked"
            echo "  file even when its content already matches the commit being moved to,"
            echo "  so the removal has to come first; the checkout then restores every one"
            echo "  of them byte-identical. Ignored files are left alone, so fuzz artifacts"
            echo "  survive it. Nothing moves the hub's HEAD on its own."
        else
            # The hub is shared, so this is usually somebody else's leftovers and
            # the reader's first thought is that their own worktree is broken.
            # Say whose it might be before saying what to do about it.
            echo "  This is very likely not yours. The hub belongs to nobody, so anyone's"
            echo "  leftovers fail this for everyone, and the tree you are working in is fine."
            echo "  Whoever left it should commit it from a worktree of their own; \`just lanes\`"
            echo "  will not name them, which is the reason for the rule rather than a gap in it."
        fi
        wrong=1
    fi
    if [ "$wrong" -ne 0 ]; then
        exit 1
    fi
    echo "the hub is parked and clean: $hub"

# Pins `check-hub` against a repository it builds itself, walking a hub from the
# state this repo was in on 2026-09-06 to the state the rule wants.
#
# Four cases, and the first is the one that would otherwise take CI down: a lone
# checkout must **pass**, because there it is not a hub. Then a second worktree
# appears and the same tree must start **failing** — on its branch, then on its
# dirt, then not at all once it is parked and clean. Reading the live repository
# instead would prove nothing: it is in exactly one of these states at a time,
# and a `check-hub` that had lost the ability to fail would pass every run.
[private]
check-hub-selftest:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    dir=$(mktemp -d)
    trap 'rm -rf "$dir"' EXIT
    git init -q -b main "$dir/main"
    git -C "$dir/main" -c user.email=t@t -c user.name=t commit -q --allow-empty -m "root"
    fail() {
        echo "check-hub drifted from its own fixtures: $1"
        echo "--- got ---"; echo "$got"
        exit 1
    }
    got=$(just check-hub "$dir/main") \
        || fail "a repository with one worktree is not exempt, so this would fail in CI"
    grep -qF "the only worktree" <<<"$got" || fail "the lone checkout passed for the wrong reason"

    git -C "$dir/main" worktree add -q "$dir/lane" -b lane
    got=$(just check-hub "$dir/main" 2>&1) && fail "a hub holding a branch was accepted"
    grep -qF "the branch 'main' checked out" <<<"$got" || fail "the branch was not named"

    git -C "$dir/main" checkout -q --detach
    echo loose >"$dir/main/untracked"
    got=$(just check-hub "$dir/main" 2>&1) && fail "a hub with uncommitted work in it was accepted"
    grep -qF "1 path(s) of uncommitted work" <<<"$got" || fail "the dirt was not counted"
    grep -qF "?? untracked" <<<"$got" || fail "the dirt was not listed"
    grep -qF "very likely not yours" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the failure does not say it is a shared condition, so it reads as the runner's fault"

    rm "$dir/main/untracked"
    got=$(just check-hub "$dir/main") || fail "a parked, clean hub was rejected"
    grep -qF "parked and clean" <<<"$got" || fail "the parked hub passed for the wrong reason"

    # A hub left behind by a merge: the file is on `main` and the hub's own HEAD
    # predates it, so `status` calls it untracked. Telling this reader to commit
    # it would send them to duplicate a merge that has already happened.
    echo landed >"$dir/lane/landed"
    git -C "$dir/lane" add landed
    git -C "$dir/lane" -c user.email=t@t -c user.name=t commit -q -m "landed"
    git -C "$dir/main" branch -f main lane
    echo landed >"$dir/main/landed"
    got=$(just check-hub "$dir/main" 2>&1) && fail "a stale hub was accepted"
    grep -qF "already committed on \`main\`" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "a hub parked behind main was reported as somebody's leftovers"
    grep -qF "checkout --detach main" <<<"$got" || fail "the re-park command was not given"
    if grep -qF "very likely not yours" <<<"$got"; then
        fail "the stale hub got the leftovers message as well as the stale one"
    fi

    # Run what it printed. A remedy nobody executes is a claim without a test,
    # and this one was wrong when it was first written: `checkout --detach` aborts
    # over an untracked file even when the content already matches the commit
    # being moved to, so a re-park with no `clean` in it leaves the hub where it
    # was and the reader distrusting a check that had the diagnosis right.
    remedy=$(grep -F 'clean -fd' <<<"$got" | sed 's/^ *//')
    [ -n "$remedy" ] || fail "no runnable remedy line was printed"
    bash -c "$remedy" >/dev/null 2>&1 || fail "the remedy the check prints does not run: $remedy"
    [ "$(git -C "$dir/main" rev-parse HEAD)" = "$(git -C "$dir/main" rev-parse main)" ] \
        || fail "the printed remedy ran but the hub did not move onto main"
    [ -z "$(git -C "$dir/main" status --porcelain)" ] \
        || fail "the hub is still dirty after its own remedy ran"
    [ "$(cat "$dir/main/landed")" = "landed" ] \
        || fail "the remedy did not restore the file it removed"
    got=$(just check-hub "$dir/main") \
        || fail "check-hub still fails after the reader did exactly what it said"

    # The same name on `main`, different bytes in the hub. Everything above this
    # point passes with a name-only comparison, because the fixture writes the
    # same content on both sides — so the fixture has to differ for the check to
    # be able to fail. Here the hub holds work that is in no commit anywhere, and
    # calling it "already committed on `main`" sends the reader to delete it with
    # the `clean -fd` the same message prints (docs/decisions.md §60.10).
    git -C "$dir/main" checkout -q --detach "main^"
    printf 'work nobody has committed\n' >"$dir/main/landed"
    got=$(just check-hub "$dir/main" 2>&1) && fail "a hub holding uncommitted work was accepted"
    if grep -qF "already committed on \`main\`" <<<"$got"; then
        fail "content that is on no branch was called committed, and the remedy deletes it"
    fi
    grep -qF "very likely not yours" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "differing content got neither the stale message nor the leftovers one"
    [ "$(cat "$dir/main/landed")" = "work nobody has committed" ] \
        || fail "the check itself changed the file it was asked about"

# Pins `tooling/hub-align/align.sh` against a repository it builds itself.
#
# The script runs ahead of every recipe in this file, in every worktree, so the
# cases that matter most are the ones where it must do **nothing**: a lone
# checkout, a hub holding a branch, and a hub holding anything that is not on
# `main`. A re-park deletes untracked files, so a wrong "yes" here deletes work,
# which is why the fixture writes differing bytes under a name `main` also
# carries (docs/decisions.md §60.10) and asserts they survive.
#
# Reading the live repository instead would prove nothing: it is in one of these
# states at a time, and a script that had lost the ability to act would pass.
[private]
check-hub-align-selftest:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    align="{{ justfile_directory() }}/tooling/hub-align/align.sh"
    [ -x "$align" ] || [ -f "$align" ] || { echo "no align.sh at $align"; exit 1; }
    dir=$(mktemp -d)
    trap 'rm -rf "$dir"' EXIT
    git init -q -b main "$dir/main"
    commit() { git -C "$1" -c user.email=t@t -c user.name=t commit -q "${@:2}"; }
    got=""
    fail() {
        echo "hub-align drifted from its own fixtures: $1"
        echo "--- got ---"; echo "$got"
        exit 1
    }
    run() {
        got=$(bash "$align" "$dir/main" 2>&1) \
            || fail "the script exited non-zero, which would take every recipe down with it"
    }

    commit "$dir/main" --allow-empty -m "root"
    root=$(git -C "$dir/main" rev-parse HEAD)
    echo one >"$dir/main/f"
    git -C "$dir/main" add f
    commit "$dir/main" -m "one"
    one=$(git -C "$dir/main" rev-parse HEAD)

    # A lone checkout is a checkout and not a hub, however far behind it is.
    git -C "$dir/main" checkout -q --detach "$root"
    run
    [ -z "$got" ] || fail "a repository with one worktree was spoken to"
    [ "$(git -C "$dir/main" rev-parse HEAD)" = "$root" ] \
        || fail "a lone checkout was re-parked, and it is somebody's working directory"

    # The same tree, once a second worktree makes it a hub.
    git -C "$dir/main" worktree add -q "$dir/lane" -b lane
    run
    grep -qF "re-parked it detached" <<<"$got" || fail "a stale hub was left behind"
    [ "$(git -C "$dir/main" rev-parse HEAD)" = "$(git -C "$dir/main" rev-parse main)" ] \
        || fail "the script said it re-parked and the hub did not move"
    [ "$(cat "$dir/main/f")" = "one" ] || fail "the file that main carries was not restored"
    [ -z "$(git -C "$dir/main" status --porcelain)" ] || fail "the hub is dirty after a re-park"

    run
    [ -z "$got" ] || fail "a hub already on main was spoken to, and this runs before every recipe"

    # Behind `main`, holding only files that have landed since. This is the
    # shape `check-hub` calls stale rather than leftovers, and the one the whole
    # feature exists for.
    echo landed >"$dir/lane/landed"
    git -C "$dir/lane" add landed
    commit "$dir/lane" -m "landed"
    git -C "$dir/main" branch -f main lane
    echo landed >"$dir/main/landed"
    run
    grep -qF "re-parked it detached" <<<"$got" || fail "a hub behind main with only landed files was left"
    [ "$(cat "$dir/main/landed")" = "landed" ] || fail "the re-park did not restore the file it removed"

    # A justfile arriving with the re-park makes this run's recipes yesterday's.
    printf 'a:\n    @echo one\n' >"$dir/lane/justfile"
    git -C "$dir/lane" add justfile
    commit "$dir/lane" -m "justfile"
    git -C "$dir/main" branch -f main lane
    run
    grep -qF "run it again" <<<"$got" \
        || fail "the justfile changed under a run in progress and nothing said so"

    # Differing bytes under a name `main` also carries. Everything above passes
    # with a name-only comparison; this is the case where a wrong answer runs a
    # `clean -fd` over the only copy of somebody's work.
    # Parked before `landed` existed, so the file below is untracked here and on
    # `main` under the same name with different bytes — which is the branch of
    # the script that compares content, and the one a name-only answer fails.
    git -C "$dir/main" checkout -q --detach "$one"
    printf 'work nobody has committed\n' >"$dir/main/landed"
    [ "$(git -C "$dir/main" status --porcelain)" = "?? landed" ] \
        || fail "the fixture meant to build an untracked file and did not"
    run
    grep -qF "left where it is" <<<"$got" || fail "a hub holding uncommitted work was re-parked"
    [ "$(cat "$dir/main/landed")" = "work nobody has committed" ] \
        || fail "content that is on no branch was deleted by the re-park"
    [ "$(git -C "$dir/main" rev-parse HEAD)" = "$one" ] || fail "the hub moved anyway"

    # A modified tracked file is the other half of that, and reaches it by a
    # different branch of the script.
    git -C "$dir/main" clean -fdq
    echo changed >"$dir/main/f"
    run
    grep -qF "a modified file" <<<"$got" || fail "a hub with a modified file was not named as such"
    [ "$(cat "$dir/main/f")" = "changed" ] || fail "a modified file was thrown away"

    # A branch checked out in the hub is `check-hub`'s failure, and it names the
    # fix. Detaching it here would move somebody off a branch without saying so.
    git -C "$dir/main" checkout -q -- f
    git -C "$dir/main" checkout -q -B parked "main^"
    run
    [ -z "$got" ] || fail "a hub holding a branch was spoken to by the wrong check"
    [ "$(git -C "$dir/main" symbolic-ref --short HEAD)" = "parked" ] \
        || fail "the script moved a hub off the branch it was holding"

# What a stretch of sessions cost, and what it was spent on.
#
# `just spend` covers the last seven days and prints every report. A window and
# one report narrow it: `just spend 2026-09-12 2026-09-20 ritual`, and a sixth
# argument keeps only the lanes whose path contains it.
#
# The reports are `summary` (token classes, batching, context, the cost of
# finding), `finding` (which of that a lookup lane could take, and what handing
# it over would cost), `classes` (which part of the tree the money went into),
# `ritual` (the recurring work no kernel change needs), `sessions`, `carry`
# (what fills the context every later response pays to re-read), `ceiling`
# (what handing off at a context cap would have saved), `warnings` (what the
# budget hook that exists actually caught) and `pairs` (a file searched in one
# response and read in the next — §144.6's two-call shape, priced).
#
# **`pairs` is what refused a tool change**: making `decisions-find` print the
# section when it matches one would have reached 4 of a week's 119 pairs on
# `docs/decisions.md`, and the whole shape there is under 1% of the bill.
# `docs/decisions.md` §161.
#
# **`summary`'s finding line is the one that gets misread**, and `finding`
# exists because it was: it counts every probe, wherever made and whatever
# about, and two thirds of what it counts is nobody's to delegate — probes
# inside lanes, which are cheap already, and §144.5's ritual, which is a session
# asking about its own state. `docs/decisions.md` §158.
#
# **`ceiling` and `warnings` ask different questions** and answered differently
# on the week of 2026-09-12: `ceiling` prices a cap nothing enforces, `warnings`
# prices the one installed, and the money turned out to be in sessions warned on
# time that kept going rather than in where the line was drawn.
# `docs/decisions.md` §160.
#
# **This reads transcripts, never the repository.** It cannot be a `check-` and
# is not in `gate_steps`: its answer moves with what the sessions did this week,
# so there is no fixed thing for a check to hold. What it is for is §144 — every
# finding there names the report that measures it, because a number written into
# a document is stale on the next session and a command is not.
#
# Transcripts live under `$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/projects/<repo key>`, one directory
# per worktree. They are swept by nothing, so the window can reach back further
# than `/tmp` does — but a reboot takes the scratchpads and leaves these, which
# is why the evidence for a finding belongs here rather than in a handoff.
[doc("What a week of sessions cost and where it went: `just spend 2026-09-12 2026-09-20 ritual`.")]
spend since="" until="" report="all" lane="":
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    since='{{since}}'; until='{{until}}'
    [ -n "$since" ] || since=$(date -u -d '7 days ago' +%F)
    [ -n "$until" ] || until=$(date -u -d 'tomorrow' +%F)
    exec python3 tooling/session-spend/spend.py \
        '{{claude_config}}/projects' \
        '-home-mattias-git-mattiasgronlund-rcad' \
        "$since" "$until" '{{report}}' '{{lane}}'

# The part of `spend` a check can hold: how it classifies a response, and what
# it read out of a hook's own message.
#
# Everything else there answers with this week's sessions in it and moves when
# they do, which is why `spend` is not in `gate_steps`. The classifiers are pure
# functions of a response's tool calls, and `ritual_of` was wrong for a week in
# the direction that mattered: a handoff listing the open questions counted as
# question-file work, so §144.5 reasoned about the question file from a figure
# a third too large. A number a document reasons from is worth a case.
#
# `warning_said` and `bucket_of` were wrong the same way a fortnight later: a
# warning recorded only as its summary line names no budget, and a parser that
# required one read three of the 39 sessions warned that week as sessions the
# hook never reached — a broken hook's signature, written by a broken parser.
# `docs/decisions.md` §160.
#
# `is_lookup`, `finding_runs` and `delegation_net` are held for the same reason
# one step on. §158's argument is entirely which probes a lane could have taken
# and what handing them over would have cost, and those three decide it — the
# obvious wrong version of each is caught by a case, which was checked rather
# than assumed.
#
# It needs no transcripts and no Claude Code, so it runs on a runner like any
# other check. `docs/decisions.md` §152.
# `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE` because the cases import `spend` as a module, and a
# `__pycache__` beside it is a directory the gate leaves behind in a tree the
# hub rule expects to be clean. Ignoring it would be a second record of the same
# thing; not writing it is one word.
[private]
check-spend-selftest:
    @PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 tooling/session-spend/check-spend.py

# Where each queue row's time went, from its `take` to merged and pruned.
#
# For each row worked in `lanes` runs it prints the elapsed time, the agents'
# running time, the waits between them, the slot time and the attempts; under
# the rows, the total share, median and p90 of every part, step and bucket.
# `--detail` lists each attempt's steps and what each step's time went to:
# the model writing, each kind of command, each tool. `--json` prints the same
# numbers. Rows are picked by Q id, or by `--since`/`--until` dates, the last
# seven days by default. `docs/decisions.md` §411 is the spec, and §411.10 the
# rules it chose where §411 said nothing.
#
# **Every sum is checked, and a broken one stops it before it prints.** A step's
# buckets add up to its running time and a row's parts to its elapsed time, in
# whole milliseconds, so there is no tolerance to pick. A real row that breaks
# one is a finding about a rule, never a reason to loosen the check.
#
# Like `spend` it reads transcripts and never the repository, so it cannot be a
# `check-` and is not in `gate_steps`; `check-row-time-selftest` holds what does
# not move with the week. The claims file is the one `just queue` writes, in the
# git common directory.
[doc("Where each queue row's time went, take to prune: `just row-time`, `just row-time Q1311 --detail`, `just row-time --since 2026-10-01 --json`.")]
row-time *args:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    claims="$(git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir)/rcad-queue-claims"
    exec python3 tooling/row-time/row_time.py \
        '{{claude_config}}/projects' \
        '-home-mattias-git-mattiasgronlund-rcad' \
        "$claims" {{args}}

# What `row-time` decides that does not move with the week: the made-up run
# records, transcripts and claims file it builds hold every case of
# `docs/decisions.md` §411.9's list, and one whose buckets do not add up, which
# has to make `row-time` fail rather than print. It needs no transcripts and no
# Claude Code, so it runs on a runner like any other check.
# `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE` for `check-spend-selftest`'s reason: the cases
# import `row_time` as a module.
[private]
check-row-time-selftest:
    @PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 tooling/row-time/check-row-time.py

# How far each row's work has grown, read back out of git: for each first
# row (one nothing else led to) its descendants, depth and still-open count;
# rows added vs. rows closed done, by week; and for each §433 fuzz target or
# corpus file, the faults it found directly versus its whole tree, by the
# week they were filed. A row filed since §432 names its parent in a word
# (`from/<id>`, `was/<id>`, `from/fuzz:<target>`, `from/step:<file>`); an
# older row's parent is read back out of the commit that added it: the
# lane's own row on a lane merge, else the row the commit closed, else, where
# a commit closes more than one row, the one the new row's own text names or
# whose own text names the new row, else the one the commit message ties it
# to in the same paragraph (never the subject line, which often names every
# row a batch touches together). No row filed before Q1703 merged could say
# `from/fuzz:` or `from/step:`, so where such a row's own text names the
# input that shows its fault -- a crash file, a seed by its hash, or one
# corpus file -- that input is its parent instead, unless the row it already
# hangs on is in that input's tree (§433.6, §433.2). The first-row and source
# tables are printed twice, without those links and with them, and the links
# are listed last; the weekly table reads no parent and is printed once. With
# one or more row ids it prints each one's resolved parent instead of the
# tables, and after a parent read from the row's text, the one it has without.
# `docs/decisions.md` §432, §433, §348.2.
#
# Like `row-time` this reads git history and not the repository's current
# state, so it is not a `check-*` and is not in `gate_steps`;
# `check-lineage-selftest` holds what does not move with the week. Expect
# about four minutes against `main`'s real history: 217 s at 3ca67d02, where
# 3472 commits and their parents hold 2296 distinct copies of `docs/queue.md`
# and parsing each copy once (not the git calls themselves) is what costs the
# time. This keeps nothing between runs; the queue page keeps what each commit
# did to the rows, so only its first render pays this (§444).
[doc("How far each row's work grows, read back out of git: `just lineage main`, `just lineage main Q412`.")]
lineage ref *row_ids:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail
    exec python3 '{{ justfile_directory() }}/tooling/lineage/lineage.py' "$PWD" {{ref}} {{row_ids}}

# Pins `lineage` against a scratch git history built by hand: a lane merge, a
# row re-filed with `was/<id>`, one with `from/<id>`, one with
# `from/fuzz:<target>`, one with `from/step:<file>`, and a commit that closes
# two rows at once where neither row's own text names the other, only the
# commit message's body (never its subject, §432's fab561be lesson) ties the
# new row to one of them. Every commit is built with `commit-tree`, not a
# working-tree commit, so the history is exact and the test needs no checkout.
#
# Every commit also carries two fuzz targets, one seed of `demo_target`'s
# corpus and a STEP corpus manifest whose `demo.stp` sits in two directories,
# so the rows filed before Q1703 merged can be read for the input their text
# names (§433.6): a crash file at its target, a seed by its hash, a crash file
# alone beside the one target it names, one corpus file, and one corpus file
# by its directory. Beside those, the rows that keep their other parent: one
# naming a target in passing (Q58's shape), two corpus files, a shared corpus
# name without its directory, two targets beside a lone crash file, a
# `was/<id>` re-file, a row whose parent is already in the input's tree, and a
# `from/-` row filed after Q1703 closed.
#
# The first commit also fences the grammar's own syntax example, `<id> <tier>
# ...`, the way `9414e41c` first wrote `docs/queue.md`: `rows.py` reads every
# non-blank fenced line as a row, so without a filter on the id's shape that
# line becomes a first row of its own, named literally `<id>`.
[private]
[doc("lineage agrees with a scratch history covering every §432/§433 resolution rule.")]
check-lineage-selftest:
    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -uo pipefail
    dir=$(mktemp -d)
    trap 'rm -rf "$dir"' EXIT
    repo="$dir/repo"
    mkdir -p "$repo"
    got=""
    fail() {
        echo "lineage drifted from its own scratch history: $1"
        echo "--- got ---"; echo "$got"
        exit 1
    }
    git init -q "$repo" || fail "git init failed"
    git -C "$repo" config user.email lineage-selftest@example.com
    git -C "$repo" config user.name "Lineage Selftest"

    seed=5eed0000c0ffee0000000000000000000000abcd
    target_blob=$(printf 'fn main() {}\n' | git -C "$repo" hash-object -w --stdin) || fail "hash-object a target"
    targets_tree=$(printf '100644 blob %s\tdemo_target.rs\n100644 blob %s\tother_target.rs\n' "$target_blob" "$target_blob" \
        | git -C "$repo" mktree) || fail "mktree fuzz/fuzz_targets/"
    seed_blob=$(printf 'a kept input\n' | git -C "$repo" hash-object -w --stdin) || fail "hash-object the seed"
    demo_seeds_tree=$(printf '100644 blob %s\t%s\n' "$seed_blob" "$seed" | git -C "$repo" mktree) || fail "mktree fuzz/corpus/demo_target/"
    seeds_tree=$(printf '040000 tree %s\tdemo_target\n' "$demo_seeds_tree" | git -C "$repo" mktree) || fail "mktree fuzz/corpus/"
    fuzz_tree=$(printf '040000 tree %s\tcorpus\n040000 tree %s\tfuzz_targets\n' "$seeds_tree" "$targets_tree" \
        | git -C "$repo" mktree) || fail "mktree fuzz/"
    printf '%s  kicad/Lone_Part.step\n%s  kicad/Other_Part.step\n%s  nist/CTC Definitions/demo.stp\n%s  nist/AP203 geometry only/demo.stp\n' \
        "$(printf 'a%.0s' {1..64})" "$(printf 'b%.0s' {1..64})" "$(printf 'c%.0s' {1..64})" "$(printf 'd%.0s' {1..64})" \
        > "$dir/manifest.sha256"
    manifest_blob=$(git -C "$repo" hash-object -w -- "$dir/manifest.sha256") || fail "hash-object the manifest"
    step_corpus_tree=$(printf '100644 blob %s\tmanifest.sha256\n' "$manifest_blob" | git -C "$repo" mktree) || fail "mktree corpus/"

    commit() {
        # commit <content-file> <message-file> [parent-sha]...
        local content="$1" msgfile="$2" qblob docs_tree root_tree
        shift 2
        qblob=$(git -C "$repo" hash-object -w -- "$content") || fail "hash-object $content"
        docs_tree=$(printf '100644 blob %s\tqueue.md\n' "$qblob" | git -C "$repo" mktree) || fail "mktree docs/ for $content"
        root_tree=$(printf '040000 tree %s\tcorpus\n040000 tree %s\tdocs\n040000 tree %s\tfuzz\n' \
            "$step_corpus_tree" "$docs_tree" "$fuzz_tree" | git -C "$repo" mktree) || fail "mktree root for $content"
        local parent_args=()
        for p in "$@"; do parent_args+=(-p "$p"); done
        git -C "$repo" -c commit.gpgsign=false commit-tree "$root_tree" -F "$msgfile" "${parent_args[@]}" \
            || fail "commit-tree for $content"
    }

    cat > "$dir/c0.md" <<'MD'
    # A queue

    ```
    <id>  <tier>  <state>  <model>  <write set>  <what, one line, ending in one or more §N or §N.M>
    ```

    ```
    Q1  1  todo  opus  -  a first row that nothing else led to §1
    Q2  1  todo  opus  -  a seed row that later closes done and is re-filed as Q12 §1
    Q3  1  todo  opus  -  a seed row that later closes done and leads to Q10 directly §1
    Q4  1  todo  opus  -  a seed row that later drops in a commit naming Q20 beside it §1
    ```
    MD
    printf 'queue: seed four rows\n' > "$dir/m0.txt"
    c0=$(commit "$dir/c0.md" "$dir/m0.txt") || exit 1

    cat > "$dir/c1.md" <<'MD'
    # A queue

    ```
    Q1  1  todo  opus  -  a first row that nothing else led to §1
    Q2  1  done:aaaa1111  opus  -  a seed row that later closes done and is re-filed as Q12 §1
    Q3  1  todo  opus  -  a seed row that later closes done and leads to Q10 directly §1
    Q4  1  todo  opus  -  a seed row that later drops in a commit naming Q20 beside it §1
    ```
    MD
    printf 'queue: Q2 done\n' > "$dir/m1.txt"
    c1=$(commit "$dir/c1.md" "$dir/m1.txt" "$c0") || exit 1

    cat > "$dir/c2.md" <<'MD'
    # A queue

    ```
    Q1  1  todo  opus  -  a first row that nothing else led to §1
    Q2  1  done:aaaa1111  opus  -  a seed row that later closes done and is re-filed as Q12 §1
    Q3  1  done:bbbb2222  opus  -  a seed row that later closes done and leads to Q10 directly §1
    Q4  1  todo  opus  -  a seed row that later drops in a commit naming Q20 beside it §1
    Q10  1  todo  opus  -  what closing Q3 led to directly §1
    ```
    MD
    printf 'queue: Q3 done, Q10 follows\n' > "$dir/m2.txt"
    c2=$(commit "$dir/c2.md" "$dir/m2.txt" "$c1") || exit 1

    printf 'lane-q1-demo: no change yet\n' > "$dir/ml.txt"
    l1=$(commit "$dir/c2.md" "$dir/ml.txt" "$c2") || exit 1

    cat > "$dir/m1merge.md" <<'MD'
    # A queue

    ```
    Q1  1  done:cccc3333  opus  -  a first row that nothing else led to §1
    Q2  1  done:aaaa1111  opus  -  a seed row that later closes done and is re-filed as Q12 §1
    Q3  1  done:bbbb2222  opus  -  a seed row that later closes done and leads to Q10 directly §1
    Q4  1  todo  opus  -  a seed row that later drops in a commit naming Q20 beside it §1
    Q10  1  todo  opus  -  what closing Q3 led to directly §1
    Q11  1  todo  opus  -  what the lane for Q1 left behind §1
    ```
    MD
    printf 'Merge lane-q1-demo: Q1 done, leaving Q11\n' > "$dir/mm1.txt"
    m1=$(commit "$dir/m1merge.md" "$dir/mm1.txt" "$c2" "$l1") || exit 1

    cat > "$dir/c3.md" <<'MD'
    # A queue

    ```
    Q1  1  done:cccc3333  opus  -  a first row that nothing else led to §1
    Q2  1  done:aaaa1111  opus  -  a seed row that later closes done and is re-filed as Q12 §1
    Q3  1  done:bbbb2222  opus  -  a seed row that later closes done and leads to Q10 directly §1
    Q4  1  todo  opus  -  a seed row that later drops in a commit naming Q20 beside it §1
    Q10  1  todo  opus  -  what closing Q3 led to directly §1
    Q11  1  todo  opus  -  what the lane for Q1 left behind §1
    Q12  1  todo  opus  -  was/Q2 the seed row re-filed with clearer wording §1
    Q13  1  todo  opus  -  from/Q3 a second row the closed seed row led to directly §1
    Q14  1  todo  opus  -  from/fuzz:demo_target a fault the fuzzer demo_target found §1
    Q15  1  todo  opus  -  from/step:CTC_Definitions/demo.stp a fault the corpus file demo.stp found §1
    Q20  1  todo  opus  -  a seed row for the message-tie test below §1
    ```
    MD
    printf 'queue: five new rows, three of them naming their own source\n' > "$dir/m3.txt"
    c3=$(commit "$dir/c3.md" "$dir/m3.txt" "$m1") || exit 1

    cat > "$dir/c4.md" <<'MD'
    # A queue

    ```
    Q1  1  done:cccc3333  opus  -  a first row that nothing else led to §1
    Q2  1  done:aaaa1111  opus  -  a seed row that later closes done and is re-filed as Q12 §1
    Q3  1  done:bbbb2222  opus  -  a seed row that later closes done and leads to Q10 directly §1
    Q4  1  dropped:dddd4444  opus  -  a seed row that later drops in a commit naming Q20 beside it §1
    Q10  1  todo  opus  -  what closing Q3 led to directly §1
    Q11  1  todo  opus  -  what the lane for Q1 left behind §1
    Q12  1  todo  opus  -  was/Q2 the seed row re-filed with clearer wording §1
    Q13  1  todo  opus  -  from/Q3 a second row the closed seed row led to directly §1
    Q14  1  todo  opus  -  from/fuzz:demo_target a fault the fuzzer demo_target found §1
    Q15  1  todo  opus  -  from/step:CTC_Definitions/demo.stp a fault the corpus file demo.stp found §1
    Q20  1  done:eeee5555  opus  -  a seed row for the message-tie test below §1
    Q21  1  todo  opus  -  what continued after the message-tie commit below §1
    ```
    MD
    printf 'queue: close two seed rows, drop Q4 and finish Q20\n\nQ4 is dropped for want of a consumer; nothing further follows from it.\n\nWork continues from Q20 as Q21, carrying on what Q20 started.\n' > "$dir/m4.txt"
    c4=$(commit "$dir/c4.md" "$dir/m4.txt" "$c3") || exit 1

    cat > "$dir/c5.md" <<'MD'
    # A queue

    ```
    Q1  1  done:cccc3333  opus  -  a first row that nothing else led to §1
    Q2  1  done:aaaa1111  opus  -  a seed row that later closes done and is re-filed as Q12 §1
    Q3  1  done:bbbb2222  opus  -  a seed row that later closes done and leads to Q10 directly §1
    Q4  1  dropped:dddd4444  opus  -  a seed row that later drops in a commit naming Q20 beside it §1
    Q10  1  done:ffff6666  opus  -  what closing Q3 led to directly §1
    Q11  1  todo  opus  -  what the lane for Q1 left behind §1
